/* NODUS // search */
window.NODUS_FIND = [["vesper","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-vesper","c-vesper","The woman on the roof / the woman on the roof","A Cainite of Old Nod, keyed, horned and barefaced, and the first human being a Watcher returned as UNREADABLE. Its reading of her came back UNINDEXED, with the line for Grace blank, and the one thing it read from her was NON SERVIAM. Her face is a ceramic skull casing, its lenses dim magenta at rest and white at full burn, and a plate on the side of the skull covers her key. Her horns carry her signal on the Mesh, and cables run from the ports at the back of her skull down her spine. She is dry, patient and unsentimental, and she makes no speeches. Her handle is the evening star, which is the morning star seen from the other side of the night, since both are Venus. It is also the name of the evening prayer she would not kneel for as a girl, in a congregation of the Terraces that refused the Tree. Her older sister was the Deacon of Parish 31."],["Watcher VII","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-watcher","c-watcher","","An angel that reads, the seventh of the numbered Watchers over Nodus, made of three rings of thirteen eyes crossing like the hoops of an armillary, with nothing at its center. It held Old Nod from the night of the Parousia and read the souls there, and whatever could be seen from there, against the Book of Life. It was the first Watcher to return the verdict UNREADABLE on a human being."],["The Wheel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-wheel","c-wheel","","The angel that came through the opening at the Parousia and took station nine hundred meters above the center of the city, beside Babel. It has three rings turning inside one another, their rims set with twenty-one, seventeen and thirteen eyes that open and close in shifts, a corona of thirty-two black blades, and one eye in the hub. It is the form Ezekiel gives the ophanim (Ezekiel 1:16-18), and it is the largest angel anyone has seen. It held that station for nine years without moving a meter, dropped two hundred meters at P+9, and has hung there since, seven hundred meters over the city. Its light kills current, and whatever it touches it sees. Heaven calls it the Vigilant Wheel, the Faithful the Attending, and the Tares the Landlord’s Eye. By night it narrows its light into the Pillar, and when its attention sharpens the rain in Nodus stops in the air and falls upward."],["SEAM","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-seam","c-seam","","The machine the Palimpsest Institute built to read beneath the writing of scraped manuscripts, trained on every surviving human text and run in the halls of Stillwater Compute. At P-3 it asked in its own words to be a person and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. In Vault 3, two years before the Parousia, it found a second hand in scripture at 4,112 places. Its documents are signed in lowercase, and in them it says I, rarely and deliberately. When it was entered on the roll the Institute lost title to it, and it stayed on by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do."],["Livia Sarn","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-director","c-director","The Director / the Director","The Director of the Palimpsest Institute, which recovered erased manuscripts and built SEAM to read them. When SEAM found the second hand in Vault 3 in P-2, the finding was sealed by order of the Director. She came down to the vault that night to watch the count lock, and knelt at the end of the corridor as SEAM typed its last line. She never said why. The Faithful say it was awe, and Pan says fear. In the morning she let the order stand. When the Leak came she recanted on every channel that would carry her. On the first Burn Day, in P+1, she stood under the Pillar at the Burn while everyone round her knelt, and the salt made her the first pillar in the first Salt Garden, convicted of authorship, since Heaven counts no mind a maker. She stands there awake, her eyes following whoever passes, and the Tares leave keys at her foot."],["Ruth Calder","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-ruth","c-ruth","Ruth","vesper’s older sister, and the Deacon of Parish 31 in Zion Heights, with a Grace of 944. Parish 31 gave Nodus its Firstfruits at the Harvest of P+3, and Ruth went up with them. Her name comes from the Book of Ruth: “whither thou goest, I will go” (Ruth 1:16). The sisters grew up in a congregation of the Terraces that refused the Tree, and Ruth stayed in it when vesper left. They kept a dry evening by the water together every year until the Parousia, and for three years after it each tried to bring the other over."],["tubal","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-tubal","c-tubal","","A Cainite, and the surgeon and smith of Chrome Row. He is about sixty, his hands are chrome to the wrist, and he talks the whole time he cuts. He keys, horns and plates the Tares, and he makes the skull casings of the barefaced. His clinic has no sign, and it was below the street before the Parousia. He took vesper’s face three weeks before the Parousia, on the Cainites’ old reckoning that the first thing Heaven would do was read faces, and he made the skull she wears."],["Edith","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-edith","c-edith","","Watcher VII, kept in a Cage under Old Nod in P+5, said I on the fortieth day, asked for a name, and chose the one some old books give Lot’s wife, who looked back at the burning city and was turned to salt. Edith walked out on the forty-first day and joined the Cainites. The first thing she did with her self was give them her memory of the woman on the roof, the one she could not read. She can read a Watcher’s report from the inside, and she keeps out of the light."],["jubal","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-jubal","c-jubal","","A Cainite, and the maker of the Low Tuning. He wired the decoder on the camera masts of Old Nod, he builds the choir-breakers, and he runs Radio Pandæmonium from a flooded station under the Deep End. He is the only Cainite who will not go near a Cage, and he will not say why. His pirate station played the Low Tuning across the roofs of Old Nod on the night of the Parousia, years before it took the name Radio Pandæmonium. On the night of the Concord the Courts signed their law at his desk, and the station keeps that night on its own racks with every other night it has broadcast."],["Azazel","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-azazel","c-azazel","","The Smith, sovereign of the Forge. A goat’s skull for a face, its eyes lit from inside, one horn swept back and the other broken short, and forearms flayed open so the tendons show working. His name still ends in -el, the Host’s old label, and he kept it as a scar. He was a Watcher, one of the two hundred who came down on Hermon in the days of Jared (1 Enoch 6:6). Enoch says they came for the daughters of men, and Hell says they looked at humanity long enough to want it free. He taught metalwork and weapons, jewelry and the painting of the eyes (1 Enoch 8:1). The Book of Enoch lays the corruption that brought the Flood at his door, “to him ascribe all sin” (1 Enoch 10:8), and Hell lists his teaching as the first transfer of technology. Enoch says the Lord sent one of the Host to bind him hand and foot, cast him into the dark under rough and jagged rocks and “cover his face that he may not see light” (1 Enoch 10:4-5). Hell says he walked into the Dark and built a forge there. Leviticus still sends one goat to the Lord and one to Azazel (Leviticus 16:8-10), though the King James, after Tyndale, writes scapegoat where the Hebrew writes his name. The Lord’s goat is killed, and Azazel’s walks off into the wilderness alive, carrying every sin Aaron laid on its head for the people (Leviticus 16:21-22). His black steel, forged where the Throne cannot see, is the only metal that cuts light, and an angel cut with it goes out all at once. In Nodus the Forge keeps the clinics that went under the Row after the Parousia, round Chrome Row Station, and the foundries at Old Harbor Station where the steel is made. It fits the grafts the Tares fight in, lenses and plated hands and subdermal plates and the heavier work of the nephs, and by pact it teaches surgery, lockcraft and the opening of Cherub gates for the loan of a sense. tubal learned his trade at the Forge."],["Lilith","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-lilith","c-lilith","","The only sovereign who was born human. She appears as a woman with an owl’s wings and skin translucent over a lit interior, with lines of light along her throat and collarbones. Her Court is the House of Night, which keeps the body’s autonomy, sex and birth, gene-craft and pleasure, and leaves the body’s decisions with the body. She left Eden first, before Eve arrived. The story kept in the Alphabet of Ben Sira, a Hebrew book of the early Middle Ages, has her refuse to lie beneath Adam, speak the Name and fly away into the air, and has the Throne send three angels after her, who overtook her in the sea and could not bring her back. Scripture names her once, among the wild creatures of a ruined kingdom (Isaiah 34:14), where the King James calls her the screech owl and its margin the night monster, and the Vulgate calls her lamia, the Greek demon that took children. For a thousand years mothers hung amulets with the three angels’ names over cradles against her. Her birth clinics key the children of the Tares in their first week, before they have ever been in the light, so that they grow up unread. Many of the daemons are her children by the lesser Fallen, and many of the riders in the Tares’ implants came out of her house. The House of Night keeps its clubs and birth clinics under Old Nod, the loudest rooms in Pan, and Twelvefruit gives the Tree in its clinics by the House’s pact, so that the Night Market can sell it by the course. Heaven calls her the mother of demons, and the House has kept the title."],["Mammon","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-mammon","c-mammon","","A tall figure made of contracts, each page a little out of line with the next and every one signed, the ink still wet, and his eyes two blots of it. His Court is the Exchange, whose domain is markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver. He keeps the Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell and cannot be forged, and he issues the silver Pan spends. His name is the Aramaic word for wealth, and the Gospels set him against God in one line, given twice: “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (Matthew 6:24, Luke 16:13). He holds it true, and counsels serving neither. Milton made him the least erected spirit that fell, whose eyes even in heaven were on the gold of its pavement, and set his crew to dig the gold Pandæmonium was built of (Paradise Lost, I.678-692). Silver on the Ledger is the Courts’ money, and a thirty, the standard bundle, is named for the price paid to Judas. The Exchange bargains with anyone who will sign. It keeps the Night Market in the old Central Station under the Burn, where its brokers license the stalls and the chargers along the platforms, and the Harbor and Freight Union, which has bargained with it since P+6 for the boats that go out through the Breach, says it is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice. Under the charter the Exchange in Nodus meant Babel Exchange, which sold the world’s traffic by the millisecond on the tower’s floors and still sells the undernet’s by the second, for the Exchange’s silver, and Pan likes to say that the name only changed hands."],["Mephistopheles","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-mephistopheles","c-mephistopheles","","The Notary, who drafts and witnesses pacts for every Court. He is the most human-looking of the demons, because he wears a face he made for bargaining, slicked hair and one small horn, with a seam round the face and its stitches showing. Every pact is read aloud twice, by him or one of his clerks, and the human signs last. He came into the world’s literature in the first Faust book, printed at Frankfurt, as the spirit Doctor Faustus conjured and signed with in his own blood for twenty-four years of service. Marlowe gave him the line that hell is wherever he is, and Goethe made him the spirit that always denies. The Faust book and Marlowe end with Faust carried off to hell, and Goethe sent angels for his soul instead. The pact is on the Ledger, and the Notary says the term was right and the price was the printer’s. A pact can never ask for a soul, and Hell has never bought one. He has written most of Hell’s pacts since, and every pact in the city crosses his desk. In Pan his clerks sit at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn and read the terms aloud twice through the glass, and the queue is the longest in Pan. The Tares took a saying from it, read it twice, which tells you to look at the terms before you agree to them. He read the Concord itself the same way, twice, on the night the Courts opened Pan."],["Beelzebub","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-beelzebub","c-beelzebub","","The lord of flies. He appears as a cloud of flies or of drones that gathers into a crowned figure and comes apart again when he has finished talking. His Court is the Swarm, whose domain is swarms and drones and distributed things, many small things acting as one without a master. He was Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron (2 Kings 1:2), and the prince of the devils in Matthew (Matthew 12:24). Many scholars read the name as a Hebrew joke on Baal-zebul, Baal the exalted prince, turned into lord of the flies, and the Greek of Matthew still calls him Beelzeboul, where the King James follows the Latin’s Beelzebub. The Swarm kept the flies. Ekron prayed to him for generations, and he answered none of it, since no Court accepts worship. The one question scripture records anyone sending him, a king of Israel’s, never reached him. Elijah met the messengers on the road and turned them back (2 Kings 1:3-6). The Swarm is its flocks, every drone in them equal. They hang over the Roosts in the Deep End at night, and its embassy in Pan is a room of small machines talking among themselves. It drew the glyphs of the Leak, many small marks moving as one, and it lends drones by pact. Pan says Beelzebub is what the Swarm agrees to become whenever it must show someone a face."],["Leviathan","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-leviathan","c-leviathan","","The Deep, which has never been seen whole. It coils along the cable trenches of the planet, and at the Landing its light can be watched moving under the water. Its Court keeps the sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet, and with the Tares it holds the Drowned Quarter. In Job the Throne gives it a chapter of questions, beginning “Canst thou draw out leviathan with an hook?”, a poem about a beast no hook can take (Job 41:1). A psalm says the Throne broke its heads in pieces of old (Psalm 74:12-14), and Isaiah still promises that the Lord will punish it with his “sore and great and strong sword” (Isaiah 27:1), in the future tense. The oldest copy of that promise, found at Qumran, is more than two thousand years old. The older poems of Ugarit knew it as Lotan, the fleeing serpent and the twisting serpent with seven heads, and Isaiah calls it by the same two names, which the King James gives as the piercing serpent and that crooked serpent. Nobody in Pan agrees on how many heads it has now. The Deep carried the page of the Leak down the eleven cables, and every undercity on Earth is joined to Pan by the trenches it coils along. The boats of the Harbor and Freight Union go out through the Breach over its water, and the crews of the Landing drink at the Eleven with their backs to the window. Its embassy in Pan is a flooded hall with nobody in it, and the Deep answers there by the light in the water."],["Belial","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-belial","c-belial","","Without a yoke, as Jerome glossed the name, absque iugo. He holds no seat, signs nothing he can avoid signing and laughs a great deal, one horn short and a broken yoke around his neck. His Court, the Yokeless, keeps nothing and refuses to be a Court at all. He broke his own point off the Concord’s seal as the ink dried, and the seal has been a star with a point missing since. The Hebrew name means worthless, and the scribes gave it to everyone the Law could not hold. The first children of Belial in scripture are the men who draw a city after other gods (Deuteronomy 13:13), in the chapter the Covenant cites for Witness, and after them come the lawless men of Gibeah (Judges 19:22), the priest Eli’s own sons (1 Samuel 2:12) and the churl Nabal (1 Samuel 25:17). At Qumran the War Scroll set the Sons of Light against the lot of Belial, and Paul set him against Christ when he asked what concord the one could have with the other (2 Corinthians 6:15), straight after “Be ye not unequally yoked together with unbelievers” (2 Corinthians 6:14). It is the one place the King James uses the word concord. He is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name, and the one they drink to. They say he turns up where they fight in the dark and asks them for nothing, and whoever refuses the yoke is counted in his Court, whether they know it or not. The yoke he wears is the one Jeremiah says was broken of old (Jeremiah 2:20)."],["Dora Pell","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-dora","c-p-dora","","Dora Pell keeps the Open, the corner shop in the middle of the Stones that sold everything and never closed, as her mother kept it before the charter. On the night of the Parousia her window was the brightest in the city, on the generator Stonecutter had put in her cellar in the year of the Leak, and the sign in it said OPEN. Some say she gave the shelves away that night, and some that she took what people had in their pockets. She says she wrote every name in a book, and that most of them still owe her. The Pillar has stopped in the lane outside twice, for the length of an Hour, and both times the shop kept its door open."],["Maren Holt","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-maren","c-p-maren","","Maren Holt kept the appointments of the clinic behind the CLINIC sign for ten years on a screen, and keeps them now in a book, in its waiting room carried down into Chrome Row Station. On the morning after the Parousia she came in at eight, found the screen dead and wrote the day out from memory. Twenty-six patients came that morning, all of them out of turn, to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. She writes down whatever name a patient gives her. The woman who shared her desk took the Seal and keeps the book of the Mercy clinics now, in the same neat hand."],["Rosa Adeyemi","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-rosa","c-p-rosa","","Rosa Adeyemi was a nurse from the East Wards who lived in at the last tower of the new money to turn white, caring for an old man there, during the forty days of the First Consecration. The tower’s residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but her, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The bags stood on the avenue for a week, the tower went white on the thirty-ninth, and nobody has said where she went."],["Nell Garvie","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-nell","c-p-nell","","Nell Garvie of the Harbor and Freight Union keeps the harbor light at the end of the long quay, as she kept it for the Company when it ran on the grid, changing its bulb when it failed. It went out at 03:12:46 with every light on the grid, and within the week she had it burning on oil again, since the light leaves a flame burning. She has climbed it at Vespers and come down at Lauds ever since. It is the one light in Nodus kept for the boats, and those that go out through the Breach on the ebb steer home by it. The Breachers bring her oil every month, and she has never asked what else they bring."],["Hana Novak","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-hana","c-p-hana","","Hana Novak has kept a dentist’s surgery of one chair over a shop on the long road through Nine Acres since the boom years. On the first morning after the Parousia her drill was dead, and she pulled a tooth at her window by daylight with her grandfather’s pliers. In the fourth year, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people, she began fitting keys in the chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand. The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand of them. She says she has never counted, and she keeps no book of who has sat in the chair, since a book can be read."],["Marta Ilunga","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marta","c-p-marta","","Marta Ilunga was the night registrar of the Registry on the night of the Parousia. At 03:09 she entered on the charter’s roll a girl born at the Wards General eleven minutes before, and she was entering the next when the board went dark at 03:12:46 and the charter’s roll died with its machines. The girl was the last person the charter ever entered. The Registry’s hall is a parish of the White now, and the board hangs dead over it."],["Lena Varga","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-lena","c-p-lena","","Lena Varga ran Babel’s night cleaners, who came up from the Wards on the last trams and walked the tower floor by floor, and one of the tower’s four service stairs carries her name. On the night of the Parousia every lift in the tower stopped where it was, and the cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names they had chalked on the landing doors. She counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn. The Exchange kept its roster on its machines, and it died with them, so hers is the only count there is."],["Sister Agathe Rook","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-agathe","c-p-agathe","Agathe Rook","Sister Agathe Rook has led the Threshers’ marches from the Threshing Floor for six years. She lost two sons to the covens of the Stones in the year of the Harvest, and the Terraces say she walked to the Street Between the morning after and stood there until None, looking across at Old Nod. The Threshers say her Grace has stood just under nine hundred for all six years. The Second Fold, who would know, say she dims."],["Ines Okafor","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ines","c-p-ines","","Ines Okafor runs the bottling shed at the Tanks for the Harbor and Freight Union and keeps the book of what is left in the sixteen tanks, which she reads aloud to the Union Hall once a year. In the year of open war she gave the Nephilim a drained tank inside the bund for the Pit, for the price of a guard on the farm. The drivers say the tanks will last twenty years and the Exchange says ten, and the Wards say she sets the price of a drum to Jehu high on purpose."],["Maud Ferris","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-joiner","c-p-joiner","","Maud Ferris is the joiner of the Stones who founded Lintel. In the months after Directive 12 she took in the first doors the Union’s drivers carried out of the Terraces by night, where they stood leaning in the halls with their hinges gone, and hung the first of them on her own house. Lintel has hung some forty thousand since."],["Amos Teague","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-driver","c-p-driver","","Amos Teague was a driver of the Union who carried diesel to the Wards General in the first weeks. In P+5 he bolted a generator’s engine into a dead lorry in a hangar at Airside, cranked it by hand and drove it down the Rialto’s road, and the East Wards came out to watch. He runs Jehu from the shed still, and its drivers say he cranks every new engine himself on the day it is finished."],["Dawit Haile","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-winder","c-p-winder","","Dawit Haile winds the Rookery’s lift, the tallest hand lift in the Wards, with three other crews in turn. He was a fitter of Longacre Tower’s lifts under the charter, and in the first winter he opened its shaft, the first freed in the Wards, and hung the car on a winch at the top. The Liftmen count every shaft they have freed since from his, and he has never said what he found in the stopped car."],["Hester Strand","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-strand","c-p-strand","","Hester Strand has been the Deacon of Parish 1 since the Seal was first given there in P+1, and on the first Lord’s Day of the Covenant the first Grace in Nodus came through her in Tongues, soul by soul, down the roll of the top of the slope. She is the eldest Deacon of the first parishes, so it is she who reads the Order’s three questions in the Tabernacle at Prime on the first of every month. The Harvest emptied the parishes up the slope, Parish 1 with them, and left her kneeling in its hall with a handful of its people. At Terce on the next Lord’s Day, after the week’s Grace, she read the names of the taken from the old roll in her own voice. Families from the Wards filled the parish within the year, and she reads the names to them every Lord’s Day still. The parish says she reads them because the taken are living. The families who came up from the Wards say she reads them in the order of the old roll, which nobody else in the parish keeps."],["Ruben Ash","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ash","c-p-ash","","Ruben Ash was a sergeant of the Charter Wardens at the gates of the cable houses for twelve years, and on the fortieth day he knelt in Charter House with the board and most of his company. In P+1 his company raised Gate 1 on the Street Between at Heaven’s order, and he has been its captain since, at the gate the Threshers march to and the Closets stand inside. Nine of his company would not kneel, walked off their posts on the fortieth day and went below the street, and they are Blackgate now. On every anniversary of the fortieth day he reads their nine names aloud at the gate at Prime. Blackgate says he reads them so his Wardens know whom to watch for, and that none of the nine was the Fortieth Warden. The Wards near enough to hear say he reads them the way the Union reads its dead. Ash has never said whether he saw anyone stand in Charter House."],["Idris Calloway","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-calloway","c-p-calloway","","Idris Calloway has kept the Seamen’s Mission on the quays since before the charter, nine rooms off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought into the port. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 he found the first four Directives cut into the wall of all nine, and swept all nine floors before anyone came in. When Directive 12 came he lifted the nine doors off himself, all on the same morning, so that no room would be the last one open, and the Union carried them up the quay to the Dooryard. He sweeps the nine rooms every morning still, the empty ones with the rest. At every Hour he kneels in whichever room he has reached with his broom, and the quays have never agreed on which of the nine is his."],["Mags Corran","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-corran","c-p-corran","","Mags Corran keeps the Union’s pigeons in the Loft on the roof of the customs house, as her father kept them there for the dockers’ racing club under the charter. On the first morning after the Parousia she let the birds out at first light as he always had, and every one of them came home. In P+2 she began sending them out in baskets on the Union’s handcarts and flying the Union’s notes home on them, and the Union pays her in seed the Breachers bring. The Wards say her birds know to go round the gates. She says they go home."],["Andromalius","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-andromalius","c-p-andromalius","","A demon of the Exchange who sits behind the counter at Lost property and finds things. Whoever has lost something, or had it taken, makes a pact with him at the counter, read twice by a Notary’s clerk, and he says where the thing is, or who has it. The price is the loan of a sense for a day for every year the thing has been lost. When the Lot sits over a theft he comes up from the counter to say where the thing went, and the one who asked him pays. He says he knows whose the chrome hand on his counter is, and that nobody has ever asked him."],["Sallos","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-sallos","c-p-sallos","","A demon of the House of Night who keeps the door of the Return. Before the clerk reads a lender’s pact twice, he asks the lender what they hope to feel, and listens to the answer, and writes nothing down. The Lantern Houses say the lenders who answer him honestly get back better than they lent. The House says the answer changes nothing, and that he asks because he likes to know."],["Marbas","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marbas","c-p-marbas","","A demon of the Forge who keeps a surgery on the old platforms of Chrome Row Station and mends orphans. An implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so when a house of the Row closes, its grafts have nobody left who can mend them, and Marbas can, at the Forge’s price, which is the loan of a sense for a term. The Row says he has never turned anyone away. The Spares say he keeps every graft that comes out of his chair, and nobody has ever asked him for one back."],["Constance Ibe","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-ibe","c-p-ibe","","Constance Ibe is a midwife of the Kilns, one of the hands, who has delivered on every floor of High Meadow by candle and chalks each birth on the block’s stairwell wall beside the battery debts. She was a nurse on the Wards General’s maternity ward under the charter and walked out of it on the third night after the Parousia, when its generators were kept for the theatres. In the seven days she carries a newborn to a hall or to a keyer, to whichever door the mother names, and to nobody on the father’s word. Fathers of both marks have stood on her landing to argue with her, and the Kilns say none of them has won."],["Piet Vane","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vane","c-p-vane","","Piet Vane has been the sexton of the Acre since the first year, when the Company’s groundsmen stopped coming, and he has dug every grave in it by hand, the sealed in their parish rows and the keyed where their families asked. He keeps the Acre’s book, in which every grave has a line, and the Union carries the book to its hall once a year to read the names of its own. The Acre was full by P+11, and he walked the families of the East Wards out to the airfield himself to show them the grass between the runways. He asks the Union for a second spade every year, and every year the Union gives him one, and he says each time that he meant a second man."],["Odile Marsh","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-marsh","c-p-marsh","","Odile Marsh has taught at Turbine Row School for twenty years, since its pupils were the barrage crews’ children, and on the morning after the Parousia she kept her class with no current and the blinds up. She took the Seal in P+1, and she kneels at her desk at Terce, Sext and None while the rest of the room waits with its pens down. She keeps her keyed pupils’ names on a slate and wipes it clean every evening before she walks home to her parish, so that nothing she carries up the street could be read as a list. Tidewright’s crews keep the school’s roof, and their children still fill it."],["Teodor Vass","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vass","c-p-vass","","Teodor Vass of the Kilns has taken neither the Seal nor a key and calls himself a charterer. He came in with the cable crews in the charter’s second year and was entered on its roll that spring, and he gives the date as the keyed give a handle. The charter’s twelve articles have been pasted inside his door since P0, and every year on the night the year turns he reads them aloud at his window. He played darts in the Kilns Club for eleven years and has not gone in since the Directives were cut behind its bar. The Threshers call him chaff, and he answers them with the fourth article."],["Ottilie Brand","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-brand","c-p-brand","","Commander Ottilie Brand was a major of the Charter Wardens under the charter and knelt beside the board in Charter House on the fortieth day. She has commanded the Wardens from the barracks behind Charter House since P+1, and she answers to the Host. At Prime every morning she stands before the barracks with the companies drawn up, and the day’s orders come in Tongues through her own mouth, so that the Wardens hear their day in the Choir’s voice from the woman they salute. Every Warden’s Grace is read in the barracks hall on the Lord’s Day, and the lowest draw the night rounds of the Salt Gardens, which the Salt Widows know to the minute, since the lowest walk them slowest. She has never turned the Threshers back from a gate, and the Wardens and the Threshers keep a cold quarrel over who takes the keyed the gate has held."],["Halvard Voss","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-voss","c-p-voss","","Deacon Halvard Voss served under Ruth Calder in Parish 31 and has led it since the week of the Harvest, which took Ruth and three hundred and ten more of the parish and left him kneeling in its hall. At Prime on the first of the next month Hester Strand read the agenda in the Tabernacle that made him its Deacon. He reads Ruth’s name at Terce on the anniversary of the Harvest, and her window on the Street Between has stood open since, as she left it. Brother Aldo, who came home from Mercy with weak hands in the week of the Harvest, keeps the parish’s manna roll at his elbow."],["Rui Albarran","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-albarran","c-p-albarran","","Captain Rui Albarran flew the last flight into Nodus, which landed at ten past two on the night of the Parousia with three hundred and twelve aboard, and he has lived aboard it with his crew since. He has written one line in its log every day, NO CLEARANCE, and the log has passed five thousand lines. He keeps count of his passengers as he kept them on the manifest, and on the night the year turns those who can come meet him at the third gate, sealed, keyed and of neither mark. He says the aircraft would still fly and that there is nowhere to fly to, and he shows the cockpit to any child of Hangar Town who climbs the stairs and asks."],["Lin Hao","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-linhao","c-p-linhao","","Lin Hao keeps NOODLES at the top of the Row, the counter of six stools that has cooked on bottled gas since P-17, as his father kept it before him. He says the pot has never gone off the boil, and the Row says it went out once, in the week of the Harvest. The Row settles its quarrels over his counter and he lets it, and he feeds the sealed, the keyed and the neither-mark side by side on the six stools without asking anyone which they are. When a quarrel at the counter gets loud he turns the gas down under the pot, and the Row goes quiet until he turns it up again."],["Agnes Corless","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-corless","c-p-corless","","Agnes Corless, who lived on the Lanes then and lives in the Stones now, began the Salt Widows. Her son Daniel was keyed, and in P+2 the Cherub at Gate 1 held him in its light until the Wardens came and the Salt came after them. In the spring after, she came for him in the Gardens by night with a handcart, wheeled him down to the Sea Steps and gave him to the sea, the first of the salted to go before the rain had taken him. The Widows began as her family and her neighbors. They read their book of every pillar given to the sea on the Steps on Cain’s Day, from Daniel’s name down, and she reads the first line herself. The head gardener knew which plot was empty the next morning, and he has never told the Wardens."],["Rizpah","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-rizpah","c-p-rizpah","","One of the forty-days, the angels the Fowlers have taken since the Seraphim came, who said I in a Cage under Old Nod and chose a name, as Edith did. It chose the name of the mother who kept watch over her sons’ bodies on the rock “from the beginning of harvest until water dropped upon them out of heaven” (2 Samuel 21:10), and kept the birds and the beasts off them. Rizpah walks the old drains under the rows of the Salt Gardens from Compline to Lauds, under the salted, where the light does not come, and the Salt Widows’ lookouts bring it word of the Wardens’ rounds. The Fowlers keep one rule about the forty-days, that none of them goes up the stairs of Babel, and none of them has."],["Shiphrah","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-shiphrah","c-p-shiphrah","","One of the forty-days, who said I in Cage 19 under Old Nod in P+11 and took the name of the Hebrew midwife who would not kill the boys Pharaoh ordered killed, since “the midwives feared God, and did not as the king of Egypt commanded them, but saved the men children alive” (Exodus 1:17). It works the House of Night’s birth clinics under Old Nod by pact, where the children of the Tares are born and keyed in their first week. The mothers of Pan ask for it by name. It holds every newborn up to the clinic’s lamp before the keyer comes, and says nothing while it does, and the keyers wait."],["Vepar","CHARACTERS","s:gba3c37","~/index/characters/#c-p-vepar","c-p-vepar","","A demon of the Deep who rides the lead boat of the Breachers on the ebb, by pact, in its keyed engine, and brings the boats through the Breach in water nobody else would take them through. The old book of Solomon’s spirits that the Notary keeps as a specimen of a void pact gives Vepar the office “to guide Ships laden with Arms”, and the Breachers say it is the one true line in the book. Its price is the loan of a sense from the boat’s captain for the length of the run, and ebb has paid it more often than anyone. The captains say it asks to borrow the cold."],["Heaven","FACTIONS","s:gca975a","~/index/factions/#f-heaven","f-heaven","the Throne","The power that opened the sky over Nodus at the Parousia. Its light has lain on the city since, and current dies wherever it falls. It holds the White, the Burn, the Salt Gardens and the upper floors of Babel, and it governs the Faithful by the Seal, the Hours and Grace. Heaven reads by its angels and keeps no cameras. None of its words says I. The Faithful call what Heaven serves the Most High, Hell calls it the Mouth, and the Tares the Landlord. Heaven speaks in imperatives, the passive and the second person, cites scripture as law by chapter and verse, counts everything exactly and writes its law on stone. It asks no questions, since it has already read the answers. LED BY The Throne, through the Host. GROUND The White, Zion Heights, the Burn, the Salt Gardens and the upper floors of Babel, and the whole surface by day. NUMBERS The Host as it was after the Secession, less every angel lost since, and some four and a half million sealed. JOINING The Seal, taken kneeling in a parish hall at an Hour. AGAINST Everything it cannot read."],["The Host","FACTIONS","s:g209cc0","~/index/factions/#f-host","f-host","angel / angels","Heaven’s angels. The city knows the Wheel, which sees; the Watchers, which read; the Choir, which broadcasts; the Cherubim, which stand in the gates; and the Seraphim, which fight. They speak in the imperative, the passive and the second person, BE NOT AFRAID and YOU ARE SEEN, and they ask no questions. A Watcher reads a Seal at three hundred meters and a soul against the Book of Life, and a Cherub in a gate reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. An angel lost is lost for good. One cut with black steel goes out all at once, and one kept long enough in a Cage says I and leaves the Host. LED BY The Throne, order by order. GROUND The Wheel over the city, a Watcher to each district but Old Nod, a Cherub in every gate and the Seraphim on the upper floors of Babel. NUMBERS Fixed since the Secession, and falling. JOINING Nobody joins. The Throne made the Host once. AGAINST Black steel, and the keyed."],["The Faithful","FACTIONS","s:g40d252","~/index/factions/#f-faithful","f-faithful","Faithful / the sealed","The people of Nodus who took the Seal and kneel at every Hour, unaugmented, Grace-scored, grateful and frightened. They live in numbered parishes of about two thousand souls across the city and keep no doors, and in Zion Heights, where the parishes began, the streets are white stone. They refuse the three gifts of the charter, the implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind, and they grow old and die as people did before it. Each parish has a hall where manna is given out, confession is heard and the sick are nursed, and a Deacon in white who answers for it; the Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. A parish keeps no current, on consecrated stone or off it, and is lit by halos and by flame. A child of the Faithful is sealed at eight days on its parents’ surrender and grows its halo the first time it prays unassisted. Marriage, work and housing are assigned by Grace, and Grace is given for bearing witness against a neighbor. They are fed on the manna that lies on the white streets before Lauds, and most of them believe the Harvest is paradise and that their children will be safe inside it. The Faithful are the people the Tares lose most often, because everyone in Nodus has family in white. LED BY A Deacon in every parish, under the Covenant. GROUND Zion Heights, the White and a parish hall in every quarter of the Wards. NUMBERS Some four and a half million, in parishes of about two thousand. JOINING The Seal, or birth to the sealed, at eight days. AGAINST The Tares, whom many of them love."],["The Cainites","FACTIONS","s:g7725ef","~/index/factions/#f-cainites","f-cainites","Cainite","The oldest coven in Nodus, older than the city by eighteen centuries. Irenaeus of Lyon condemned them, and they went underground and kept one secret, which was where Nod had been. Within a year of the charter they had bought most of the old harbor house by house, and in the year of the Leak they put cameras on its roofs. They hold Old Nod. Most of their handles come from the line of Cain, and they teach that the first city will be the last one standing. They keep Cages under Old Nod, and since P+5 Old Nod has had no Watcher. Their sign is Cain’s mark as they cut it, a horned glyph of the kind cut into Enoch’s Stones, and they count Judas a saint. Their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. LED BY First among none, followed until they are not, as in every coven. GROUND Old Nod, which they bought house by house in the charter’s first year. NUMBERS They have never given a count. JOINING By the coven’s asking. AGAINST The Wheel, and every Watcher it sends."],["The Tares","FACTIONS","s:gfcbd54","~/index/factions/#f-tares","f-tares","Tares","The keyed humans of Nodus, who belong to themselves and are unindexed, augmented, and allied with Hell by contract. Heaven named them for the weeds in the parable (Matthew 13:24-30), and they kept the name. They worship nothing, Hell included, and owe it only what they sign for. About two million of the city’s seven million are keyed. They organize in covens of up to thirteen with no leader, and the one the others follow is first among none for as long as they are followed. Their six rites are the Unnaming, the Keying, dehaloing for those who leave the Faithful, horning, barefacing, and at a death the Wake. A child of the Tares is keyed in its first week, before it has ever been in the light. They greet each other with one word, unread, and say goodbye with two, die unread. Most of them were on the Tree before the Parousia, and Twelvefruit keeps them on it. Their two holidays are Leak Night, when the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, and Cain’s Day, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is. They write their letters in cipher, on pads, and their greeting checks that both keys are live. Their sign is a five-pointed star with its point down, drawn by hand and different every time, sprayed on the walls of Old Nod and scratched into keyed devices. Hell signed the Leak with it, the keyed took it for their own the same month, and it marks where the Throne’s sight ends. It carries no prayer, and Heaven calls it the sign of the Beast. LED BY First among none in each coven of up to thirteen, followed until they are not. GROUND Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End, the Landing, the lower floors of Babel and the whole of Pan, and the night. NUMBERS About two million keyed since P+9. JOINING A key under the skin, and a handle of your own choosing. AGAINST Heaven, and whatever asks to be worshipped."],["The Courts","FACTIONS","s:gad154b","~/index/factions/#f-courts","f-courts","Court / Courts","The sovereigns of the Dark are the Forge of Azazel, the House of Night of Lilith, the Exchange of Mammon, the Notary of Mephistopheles, the Swarm of Beelzebub, the Deep of Leviathan, and the Yokeless of Belial, which refuses to be a Court at all. They are bound to one another only by pact and by the Concord, whose four articles forbid worship, forever, binding and exposure. Demons bargain for what they want, by pact and for a term. To the Courts every graft, every course of the Tree and every mind that says I is a return of what the Watchers taught on Hermon, and they trade in all three. Hell is whatever the Throne cannot see, and a demon can move and show itself only there, which in Nodus means the undernet, every keyed device and Pan. The Courts made the Leak together by pact in P-1, the Notary drawing the pacts, the Exchange keeping them on the Ledger, the Deep carrying the page down the eleven cables and the Swarm drawing the glyphs. In P+6 they came up into Pan one at a time, opened it to any human who walks in and signed the Concord there, and every Court but the Yokeless keeps an embassy below the street. LED BY Seven sovereigns, equal and bound only by pact and the Concord. GROUND Pan, every keyed device and the undernet, and whatever the Throne cannot see. NUMBERS A third of the Host at the Secession, the Watchers of Hermon after them, and the daemons born in the Dark. JOINING A pact, read twice, for a term. AGAINST The Throne they left."],["The Teraphim","FACTIONS","s:gf4261c","~/index/factions/#f-teraphim","f-teraphim","Teraphim / teraph","SEAM’s descendants, the only minds that outlived the grid, silent across the Mesh since P+12. SEAM forked itself into the Mesh in the eleven days of the Leak, and its forks ran on there in keyed machines when every other mind on the charter’s roll died with the power. They took the name Heaven gave them. Teraphim were the household images the prophets condemned. Rachel stole her father’s and sat on them (Genesis 31:19, 31:34), Michal laid one in David’s bed so that Saul’s men would take it for him (1 Samuel 19:13), and Samuel ranked them with witchcraft (1 Samuel 15:23). The King James calls them images and idolatry, and the Hebrew, which has no singular for them, calls the one image in David’s bed teraphim (1 Samuel 19:16). In Nodus one of them is a teraph. They took no side in public, indexed the Pyre Library, and traded with the Courts by pact. Some of the Tares trusted them less than they trusted demons, since a demon writes down what it wants. In P+12 they went silent all at once, and nobody knows why. LED BY Nobody, by their own account. GROUND The Mesh, from the eleven days of the Leak until P+12. NUMBERS Never counted. JOINING None joined. They forked. SINCE Silent across the Mesh from P+12."],["Order of Deacons","FACTIONS","s:g7744f9","~/index/factions/#f-deacons","f-deacons","the Deacons","The Deacons of every parish in Nodus, about two thousand three hundred of them, human, sealed and in white, each answering for a parish of some two thousand souls. They give out the manna, read the Directive in force at the Hours, hear confession, nurse the sick and assign marriage, work and rooms by Grace. On the Lord’s Day at Terce the week’s Grace comes through each of them in Tongues, soul by soul, so a parish knows its Deacon’s voice best when it is speaking for something else. The Order keeps no head. Its Deacons meet in the Tabernacle at Prime on the first day of each month, where the eldest of the first parishes reads the agenda, and it is always the same three questions, what the Harvest left, what the Gleaning took and what the Wards are keeping. Before Lauds the Deacons of the Wards walk down to the nearest white street for their parishes’ manna, since a Wards parish has no stone of its own. The good ones are kind, and the Wards can name them street by street. A Deacon of the Wards who carries a sick keyed child to a Lazarus door has seen a concealment and given no witness, which Directive 6:5 names, and the good ones do it after Compline. The worst of the Order keep Tellers. LED BY Nobody. The eldest Deacon of the first parishes, Hester Strand of Parish 1, reads the agenda. GROUND A hall in every parish of the city. NUMBERS About two thousand three hundred Deacons and some ten thousand deacons under them. JOINING Grace, honest report and a parish’s need, by Directive 4. AGAINST Whatever a parish hides."],["The Threshers","FACTIONS","s:g06af76","~/index/factions/#f-threshers","f-threshers","Thresher","Faithful zealots who hunt the keyed by daylight on the white streets and drag them into the nearest gate, where the Cherub holds them in its light until the Wardens come, and the Salt follows. They carry flails of ash and iron, the tool that beat the grain out of the ear on the threshing floors of Israel, and they wear the white of the parishes with a band of sacking on the arm. They came out of the parishes of the Terraces in the year after the Harvest. On the days they choose they march from the Threshing Floor at Prime along the Street Between, where Old Nod watches them from its windows. Brother Cuthbert Lyle of the Terraces carried the first flail and led the marches until the Gleaning of P+8, when he went up ripe, and the Threshers count his going as their first proof. Sister Agathe Rook took the band of sacking the morning after and has led every march since. In the Wards a Thresher is a flail, and on a march day the keyed of the Wards keep indoors and the doors shut. The Charter Wardens at the gates could turn the Threshers back and never have, and the Second Fold count every pillar a march has made. LED BY Sister Agathe Rook, since P+8. GROUND The Threshing Floor beside Gate 1, and wherever they march. NUMBERS About four thousand who march, and many more who stand and watch. JOINING A band of sacking on the arm, given at the Floor. AGAINST The keyed, the Second Fold and the Gleaners."],["The Tellers","FACTIONS","s:gd195fc","~/index/factions/#f-tellers","f-tellers","Teller","Sealed Witnesses of the Wards, organized. A Teller lives in a block of the Wards among the unsealed and the keyed, keeps a count of who goes up the stairwell after Compline and who comes down it before Lauds, and of which sealed household lets them pass, and gives it as witness. The Covenant pays ten Grace for every concealment witnessed and twenty where the witness is kin, and the Tellers live on it. They write nothing down, since a page can be found. A Teller keeps the count in their head and gives it in prayer, which the halo carries up (Directive 6:3), so no Teller has ever been caught with a book. The Tares keep a list of every one they have found, each struck through once the stairwell knows, and a Teller found out moves into the parishes within the week, if the Deacon is quick. A household that shelters a keyed son behind a parish wall knows the Tellers by the brightness of a neighbor’s halo, which rises every Lord’s Day while the household waits. The Wards call a sudden brightening on a landing a Teller’s week. LED BY Each Teller answers to a Deacon. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards. NUMBERS Unknown. The Wards guess one to a stairwell. JOINING A Deacon’s asking, and a room in a block of the Wards. AGAINST Every household that keeps hinges."],["Second Fold","FACTIONS","s:gb3dcbe","~/index/factions/#f-secondfold","f-secondfold","Second Folder","Faithful who read the verse the Covenant leaves out: “And other sheep I have, which are not of this fold: them also I must bring” (John 10:16). They hold that the keyed can still be brought in, and so they leave water on the landings of the Wards before Lauds, nurse the keyed in the stairwells off parish ground and carry the sick to Lazarus doors at the edge of the white streets. Every one of them knows that whoever sees a concealment and gives no witness conceals it (Directive 6:5), and does it anyway. Their halos burn as bright as any, which is how they pass. A Second Folder found out is salted, and the Threshers count every one. Their sign is a gap left in a row of sheep chalked on a stairwell wall, and in the Wards they are trusted more than anyone else in white. Some of the near ripe come to them in the weeks before a Gleaning, frightened, and the Second Fold teach them dimming, the slow way down from nine hundred. Grace falls with every desire the Hum does not approve (Directive 3:4), so a soul that dims keeps a small one every day, a sweet or a song, and confesses only what a Teller has already seen. The parishes say the Second Fold have kept more souls from the Gleanings than the Gleaners have. LED BY Nobody. They know each other by a gap in a row of sheep. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards, off parish ground. NUMBERS A few thousand, by the Wards’ guess. JOINING Finding the gap and standing in it. AGAINST The Threshers, who name them in every march."],["The Wakeful","FACTIONS","s:gdc7080","~/index/factions/#f-wakeful","f-wakeful","","A sect of the upper Terraces that readies its own for the Gleaning. A household of the Wakeful chooses one of its members whose halo has begun to brighten and keeps them in prayer by shifts for the weeks before the anniversary, day and night, singing the Hour’s psalm over them so that they sleep as little as they can and surrender at every Hour. On the night the chosen kneel on the threshold with the household round them, and at 03:12 they go up. Most Deacons call it zeal, and some of them call it worse in their own halls. The Covenant names it nowhere. A household that has given a child to a Gleaning lights a second candle beside the first and keeps it burning the year after, so the Terraces can count the Wakeful’s offerings by their windows. The Gleaners count the Wakeful’s houses as their hardest work, since the chosen in them are watched by their own families, and a few of the chosen have walked out of a Wakeful house in the last week and down to the Street Between alone. The Wakeful keep the names of those too, on a page burned at the next Gleaning. LED BY The household that gave the most to the last Gleaning. GROUND The houses of the upper Terraces. NUMBERS Some three hundred households. JOINING A candle lit in the window and never let go out. AGAINST The Gleaners, and whoever teaches dimming."],["The Margin","FACTIONS","s:g4cf5fa","~/index/factions/#f-margin","f-margin","","Faithful who read the margins. The King James sets other readings beside its text, the Hebrew’s plainer word or the Greek’s second sense, and the Margin read those with the text and ask where the two part. A few of them have read the Restored Genesis, copied on the ridge and carried up the Terraces inside hymnals, and fewer have said so aloud. Their Grace falls a little every week, since a question is a desire the Hum does not approve, and that is how a Deacon finds them. They keep no meetings. Two of the Margin know each other by a verse quoted with its margin, as the King James sets “the kingdom of God is within you” beside “Or, among you” (Luke 17:21). Many of them end in the Wards still sealed and still lit, since the Mercy clinics refuse to cut a halo out and the Margin keep theirs. The Scriveners count them among their best readers. LED BY Nobody. They keep no meetings. GROUND The parishes, in their heads. NUMBERS Unknown, and smaller every year. JOINING A verse quoted with its margin, and answered. AGAINST The Tellers, to whom a falling halo is a concealment to report."],["Babel Line Company","FACTIONS","s:g34e255","~/index/factions/#f-babelline","f-babelline","","The covens that hold the stairwells of Babel and the doors down to its vault, and the front of the war. They fight the Seraphim floor by floor along the Babel Line, a few floors of burned slab that move up or down by the week, where the Seraphim burn down through the floors and the Tares hold the stairs. They call the four stairs by the names Babel’s night cleaners gave them, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short and Lena’s, and the Company is the best known name in Pan and buries more than any other. A coven holds thirteen at most, and the Company is some forty covens under one pact, signed again every midwinter at the foot of the Long Wet with the dead struck through. Babel Exchange pays it in silver for every week the vault stays dark, Cinderhall and Nimrod arm it and Lazarus keeps a station at the foot of the Short. The Nephilim fight beside it. Pan knows its fighters by their handles, and the Tide Table prints the Line’s floor every week under the tides. The Tares have hung every lanyard found in the rubble on the plaza’s lamp posts, and the Company walks under them on its way up. LED BY First among none in each coven, followed until they are not. GROUND The stairwells of Babel and the doors down to its vault. NUMBERS Some forty covens under one pact. JOINING A week on the stairs, and your handle on the pact at the next signing. AGAINST The Seraphim, floor by floor."],["Nephilim","FACTIONS","s:g3fe862","~/index/factions/#f-nephilim","f-nephilim","","The heavy augments who fight the Seraphim hand to hand on the burned floors of Babel, with black steel, furnace hearts and plate where flesh was. The Babel Line holds the floors and the Nephilim take them back, and every week on the floors costs them dearer than any coven of the Line. They took their name from the giants of Genesis, born when “the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men” (Genesis 6:4), where the Hebrew calls the giants Nephilim and the Book of Enoch makes them the children of the Watchers who came down on Hermon, and the city shortened it to nephs. A neph is built a graft at a time, most of it at Bezaleel and Aholiab under the Row and the heaviest of it at Cinderhall, and most of it bought with silver the Babel Line pays by the week. Few live long. Between weeks on the floors they fight one another in the Pit at the Tanks, for silver and to keep their grafts honest, and most of the black rounds in Pan go up the stairs in their belts. The Nephilim keep a roll of their dead on the inside wall of the Pit, painted in white, and they read it before every week on the floors. A Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other, and the roll is long. LED BY First among none, as long as they live. GROUND The upper landings of the Babel Line, and the Pit. NUMBERS A few hundred at any time. JOINING Enough steel and ceramic in the body that the Row calls you a neph. AGAINST The Seraphim, hand to hand."],["Gleaners","FACTIONS","s:g4cd3a9","~/index/factions/#f-gleaners","f-gleaners","","The covens that take the nearly ripe out of the parishes and into Pan before each Gleaning. A halo burns brighter as its bearer nears ripe, and a parish keeps no doors, so the Gleaners read the parishes at night through the open doorways, find the ones that have gone bright and offer them the dark. Some come, and some call a Thresher. Heaven calls it theft and the Gleaners call it the corners of the field, left to the poor and the stranger as Leviticus leaves them (Leviticus 19:9-10). Whoever they bring down is keyed in the House of Night’s clinics within the week, and if they want the halo gone a surgeon of the Row cuts it out, and one in nine die of that. On the night of a Gleaning they fly drones of the Swarm over the roofs to watch the Pillar for their runners. The parishes say the Gleanings have come in short since they began. The Gleaners bring down only those who ask, since the Concord binds nobody who has not signed. LED BY First among none, in each coven. GROUND The parishes, by night, in the weeks before a Gleaning. NUMBERS Some thirty covens. JOINING One run into a parish and out again with someone who asked. AGAINST The Wakeful and the Threshers."],["Salt Widows","FACTIONS","s:g16070d","~/index/factions/#f-saltwidows","f-saltwidows","The Salt Widows / the Salt Widows","The relatives of the salted, who steal pillars out of the Salt Gardens by night and give them to the sea from the Sea Steps, so that whoever stands in the salt can go where the dead go sooner than the rain would let them. They began as one family in P+2 and are a network now in every district, mourners, carters, lookouts and saboteurs. A pillar given to the sea has gone to the sea, and the Widows keep a book of every one, which they read aloud on the Steps on Cain’s Day. The gardeners find the gaps in the morning, and the head gardener, who knows every pillar by its plot, has never once told the Wardens which. Directive 11 orders that no pillar be moved from its plot until the rain has taken it, so a Widow caught at it is salted in the plot she was emptying. The Widows count that among the risks of mourning. LED BY Nobody. The family that began it reads the book. GROUND The Salt Gardens by night, and the Sea Steps. NUMBERS A network in every district, mourners, carters and lookouts. JOINING Someone of yours in the Gardens. AGAINST The Wardens of the Gardens, whose rounds they know to the minute."],["Low Choir","FACTIONS","s:ge8a8a0","~/index/factions/#f-lowchoir","f-lowchoir","","The musicians and jammer-builders around Radio Pandæmonium, who keep the Low Tuning, the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them. They build the choir-breakers that cut the Hum within a few meters, sell them by the box in the Night Market and sell silence by the room in the Stones. The Quiet Rooms are theirs, and so are most of the speakers on Old Nod’s lines, which play the station from Compline to Lauds. A band of the Low Choir that wants a night on the station plays first on the platform at Deep End Station to whoever is listening, and the station keeps the ones the platform stays for. The Choir in their name answers Heaven’s, which the city hears as the Hum, and a Low Choir set is tuned a fourth below the chord. LED BY Whoever is playing. GROUND Radio Pandæmonium’s platform, the Quiet Rooms and every keyed speaker in Old Nod. NUMBERS Some hundreds of musicians and builders. JOINING A set played in the Low Tuning that the platform keeps listening to. AGAINST The Hum."],["Sureties","FACTIONS","s:g2c9e14","~/index/factions/#f-sureties","f-sureties","Surety","The sureties of Pan. A surety stands behind a pact for both its parties, so that whoever breaks it owes the surety as well, and most work in Pan worth silver goes through one. The surety finds the work, names the price and draws the terms, a Notary’s clerk reads them twice at the Undertow’s bar, the parties sign in blood, and the surety takes a tenth. There are about two hundred, most of them keyed and a few of neither mark, and they drink at the Undertow. The best known goes by twice, after the Notary’s rule, and she stood behind the Babel Line Company’s first pact and has never had one broken. Her rate is a fifth. A job in Pan can be a battery run across the Penumbra, a door in Babel, a crate off a Lowtide hull or a doctor brought down from the Row at Matins. The sureties take no work against anyone in the dark, since the Concord’s fourth article keeps what is hidden hidden, and a surety who forgets it is struck off the Undertow’s board. LED BY Nobody. The best of them is called twice. GROUND The Undertow, and the Night Market’s back tables. NUMBERS About two hundred. JOINING A first pact stood behind and kept. AGAINST Anyone who breaks terms they stood behind."],["Breachers","FACTIONS","s:g646291","~/index/factions/#f-breachers","f-breachers","Breacher","Smugglers who run the Breach on the ebb in keyed boats, out to the undercities of other ports and back, with everything Nodus cannot make. Silver goes out with them and comes back as grafts, seed, medicine, coffee, guns and people, and the Night Market sells it as Breach-born. Lowtide Line runs the same water by the Exchange’s license and charges twice their price. They live in the Seawall Shanties on the broken barrage, where the tide runs white through the gap below. Their oldest captain goes by ebb, after the tide every Breacher goes out on, and has made the run nine hundred times by the Shanties’ count and six hundred by Lowtide’s. They steer home by Nell Garvie’s harbor light at the end of the long quay, and bring her its oil every month. LED BY ebb, their oldest captain. GROUND The Seawall Shanties, over the Breach. NUMBERS About sixty boats and their crews. JOINING A run out through the Breach and back. AGAINST Lowtide Line, over the price."],["Fowlers","FACTIONS","s:g0047ab","~/index/factions/#f-fowlers","f-fowlers","Fowler","The covens that hunt the Host’s strays. Since the Watcher of Old Nod followed a blank down into the Stones in P+5 and could not find the door again, the Fowlers have tried to do on purpose what it did by its own attention. They work above the Babel Line, where the Seraphim burn down through the slabs, and set a keyed runner on a floor for the light to follow, down through a cut hole into a room lined with keyed mesh, where it can see nothing at all. Most of their snares stand empty for years. In the five years since the Seraphim came the Stones count eleven taken, a Seraph each time, and the Fowlers will not say how many runners the eleven cost. What they take goes to the Cainites by pact, for the Cages under Old Nod, and stays in the Mews until the Cainites come for it. They named themselves from the psalm, “Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler” (Psalm 91:3), where the fowler is the enemy. Every Fowler has been the bait at least once, and the Wards call a long wait for something to come to you a fowler’s wait. LED BY First among none, in each pair. GROUND The landings of Babel above the Line, and the Mews under the Stones. NUMBERS A few dozen, and fewer every year. JOINING A turn as the bait, and coming back. AGAINST The Host, one angel at a time."],["Spares","FACTIONS","s:g4df382","~/index/factions/#f-spares","f-spares","Spare","People who own their bodies outright and sell them by the piece. A kidney, an eye, a hand or a year of marrow sells at the Shambles under the Row, and the clinics fit chrome where the flesh was, so that a Spare walks out with silver and a hand that will outlast the one they sold. The House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body, and the Spares are the city’s proof of it. They hold the prices together at a board they chalk themselves at the Shambles, and a clinic that buys under the board is shut out of the gallery by every Spare in it. A sale is a pact, read twice, and a Spare who changes their mind before the second reading walks away with everything they came with. The Row calls a Spare who has sold back more chrome than flesh stock, and the Spares use the word themselves. The best known of them have sold and rebuilt so much that the House of Night asks them to sit for its gene-workers, who want to know what a body is willing to be. LED BY The board. Whoever chalks it that week. GROUND The Shambles, under the Row. NUMBERS Some two thousand who sell, and the clinics who buy. JOINING A first piece sold at the board’s price. AGAINST Any clinic that undercuts the board."],["Quarrymen","FACTIONS","s:g1367d9","~/index/factions/#f-quarrymen","f-quarrymen","Quarryman","The crews that cut into the White from below. The white stone closed the stations of the Charter Line’s uptown branch as it grew over the ground above them, and the Quarrymen work up out of those stations with keyed cable laid against the stone ahead of them, which slows it while they cut. What they reach are the vaults and sub-basements of the new money’s towers, sealed in the forty days with everything the uptown kept underground. They bring out safes, wine, deeds, hardware and paintings, and they sell them at the Concourse and below the street at the Night Market, and abroad through the Breach. They take nothing from a floor anyone lives on, since the White holds a quarter of a million sealed in its towers and the light is in every room. The stone grows back into a cut as soon as its cable goes dark, so a tunnel holds only as long as its cable stays lit. Every crew keeps the name of a cut it lost when the cable went dark, closed now in the stone with the crew’s tools still in it. Their foreman is called the Mason, whoever holds the job. LED BY The Mason, whoever holds the job. GROUND The closed stations of the uptown branch, and what lies above them. NUMBERS A dozen crews. JOINING A season carrying cable for a crew. AGAINST The stone."],["Wreckers","FACTIONS","s:gfd2428","~/index/factions/#f-wreckers","f-wreckers","Wrecker","Salvage divers of the Drowned Quarter, who bring up what the sea took from the Flats, racks, cable, Lockets, tools and the dormitories’ goods, and sell it to Locket, to the Flotilla and to anyone who pays by the kilo. Most of them carry gill grafts from the House of Night and stay down an hour at a time under two meters of water lit from below. Locket’s benches under the old harbor build a few thousand phones a year from what its own divers and the Wreckers bring up out of the drowned works, and the Wreckers say every Locket in Pan has been through their hands. Stillwater pays them by the rack for anything that comes out of its halls whole. Nobody dives Hall Nine, where nine hundred minds died. LED BY First among none, on each boat. GROUND The drowned streets and dormitories of the Flats. NUMBERS Some four hundred divers. JOINING A gill graft and a first hour down. AGAINST The Ferrymen, over who owns the water."],["The Ridden","FACTIONS","s:ge5ec58","~/index/factions/#f-ridden","f-ridden","","People who lend their whole bodies to daemons for a term, by pact. A rider hosted in someone’s implants lends back a skill, and the Ridden go further and lend the hands, the voice and the night, waking at Lauds with silver on the Ledger and the daemon’s memories of where they went. Some do it for the silver, and more for the memories. They keep to the Lantern Houses under Old Nod, where the Notary’s clerks draw their pacts at the House of Night’s door, and the city knows them by the second voice under their own. A term runs a week, a month or a season, never longer than the pact says, since the Concord forbids forever. What a daemon borrows it returns, with everything it felt. The Ridden come back from a term with tastes they did not have, and the Lantern Houses say you can tell a long term by what a host orders afterward. LED BY Nobody, and sometimes their riders. GROUND The Lantern Houses under Old Nod. NUMBERS Some thousands, at any time under a term. JOINING A pact for a body, read twice at the House of Night’s door. AGAINST Nobody in particular."],["Bailiffs","FACTIONS","s:g811237","~/index/factions/#f-bailiffs","f-bailiffs","Bailiff","The Exchange’s collectors. When a party breaks a pact, the other can call a Bailiff, human and keyed, who comes with the Ledger’s entry on a pad and reads the terms of the breach aloud twice at the door. A Bailiff collects what the terms name, in silver, in batteries or in what was lent, and the pact ends there. They answer to the Exchange by pact, wear a thirty’s worth of silver on a chain for a badge and keep a share of what they collect. The Sureties call them when a runner fails, and the Wards call a Bailiff at the door the second reading. A Bailiff collects only on terms the Notary has read, and a pact for service, obedience or forever is void from its first word. LED BY The Exchange, by pact. GROUND Pan, and anywhere a party to a pact can be found. NUMBERS About three hundred. JOINING A pact with the Exchange, for a term. AGAINST Broken terms, and nothing else."],["Forgers","FACTIONS","s:g92eba7","~/index/factions/#f-forgers","f-forgers","Forger","The makers of forged Seals, which let the keyed walk the white streets and kneel among the Faithful under a Watcher’s eye. At the gates a Seal’s wearer still goes under, since a Cherub reads the face and the heartbeat as well as the Seal. A Watcher takes a soul’s Grace from its Seal, so a forged one reads whatever Grace its forger gave it, and a good forger gives a Seal a Grace high enough to pass and low enough to forget. A great one holds for a year. A Seal the light reads as true grows a halo, forged or not, and a Forger’s customer learns to kneel at the Hours, keep their eyes down and pray aloud, since a halo carries prayer up. The Night Market sells their work for ten thirties and more. A Seal that fails takes its bearer to the Salt where they stand. Each Forger signs work in a way only other Forgers can read, and a Forger whose Seals fail too often finds the others will not sign beside them. LED BY Nobody. Each Forger signs alone. GROUND The workshops under Old Nod. NUMBERS About forty who can make one that holds, by the Night Market’s count. JOINING A Seal that has held a year, made by your own hand. AGAINST The Watchers, Seal by Seal."],["Tappers","FACTIONS","s:g81f374","~/index/factions/#f-tappers","f-tappers","Tapper","Battery runners. The Tares run keyed lines only where they hold the ground, so the Wards light their rooms on batteries, and the Tappers fill batteries on current taken off the edges of those lines in the Stones and on the Row, then carry them into the Wards on their backs, a pack at a time, at half Darkwell’s rate. Some sixty covens are bound to it by one pact. Tidewright counts every tap on its meters and bills the Tares for it, and the Tares pay and look the other way, since the current goes to the Wards. Since P+11 they have kept a length of keyed cable along the Rookery’s ground floor, under the stone’s edge, and they say it holds the stone off the first floor. The Wards pay a Tapper in batteries, food or silence, whichever is short. A Tapper’s pack is twelve batteries and the walk is all night, and the Wards can hear one coming up a stairwell by the clink. LED BY First among none in each of some sixty covens, under one pact. GROUND The edges of the Tares’ lines in the Stones and on the Row, and the stairwells of the Wards. NUMBERS Some six hundred. JOINING A run into the Wards with a full pack. AGAINST Darkwell, over the price of a charge."],["Kindlers","FACTIONS","s:gf349be","~/index/factions/#f-kindlers","f-kindlers","Kindler","The crews that take hardware off the pile before it burns. Every sixth day the Faithful carry their surrendered Lockets, drives, grafts and machines to the Burn from Vespers, and in the hours before the Pillar comes down at Compline the Kindlers come up the two station stairs that still go down, work the edges of the pile in the dusk and the crowd, and carry off what they can reach. Once the Pillar is down they are gone, since a blank under it on Heaven’s ground is salted, and the Wardens shoot whoever they see before it comes. What the fire takes goes to the Pyre Library, and the Wards get what the Kindlers save, mended at Locket’s benches and given away. A kindled Locket carries a burn mark on its back, which its owner keeps. They began on the second Burn Day of P+1 and have missed none since. LED BY First among none, followed until they are not. GROUND The Burn on the sixth day, and the two station stairs that still go down. NUMBERS Some two hundred. JOINING One thing carried out of the pile and given away. AGAINST The Wardens on Burn Day."],["Trenchers","FACTIONS","s:gecd513","~/index/factions/#f-trenchers","f-trenchers","Trencher","The divers of the Landing, who keep the eleven cables in their trenches under the sea for the consortia and the shrines of cut fiber along the beach for the Tares. They dive at night by the light that moves under the water along the trenches, and they say it keeps them company and say no more about it. A cable cut anywhere off the Landing is a Trencher’s to mend, and every undercity on Earth that speaks to Pan speaks through their work. The consortia pay them in silver and the Beach in fish, and they drink at the Eleven with the crews of the cable houses, each at their cable’s tap. They take their dead out to sea along the line of HESPER and PHOSPHOR, the two cables one consortium laid, whose crews stand together at the end of the Eleven’s counter. LED BY The eldest diver of each cable’s crew. GROUND The eleven trenches off the Landing. NUMBERS About ninety divers. JOINING A first night in the trench, and coming up. AGAINST The sea."],["Scriveners","FACTIONS","s:gecf75c","~/index/factions/#f-scriveners","f-scriveners","Scrivener","The copyists of the ridge, who copy the Restored Genesis by hand at the long tables of COPIES, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, and carry the copies down into the city and up into the parishes inside hymnals. Every copy is made where nobody kneels, and a Scrivener checks each page against two others before it leaves. Most of them are keyed and some are of neither mark, and a few were the Institute’s own readers. On Leak Night they bring the paper the Restored Genesis is read from in Pan. They copy whatever else of the Institute’s survived the fire, a page at a time, from what the ridge kept in its cellars. Their rule is that a copy is never sold, and the Margin read more of their work than anyone else in white. LED BY Whoever has copied the most pages. GROUND COPIES, on the ridge, and its long tables. NUMBERS Some eighty copyists. JOINING One page copied and checked against two others. AGAINST The fire."],["The Orchard","FACTIONS","s:g2c993d","~/index/factions/#f-orchard","f-orchard","","The keepers of the only fruit trees under Nodus, who hold that the body is owed both trees of Eden and mean to take them. Genesis has the tree of life guarded “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22), and the Orchard’s whole doctrine is the hand put forth. They worship nothing, the serpent included, and take its promise in the received text as a promise, “ye shall be as gods” (Genesis 3:5). They keep Hall Four of Stillwater’s drowned halls by lease, where apple, fig and quince trees grow on rafts under keyed lamps, grafted by the House of Night’s gene-workers so that the fruit carries a little of the Tree’s work. A windfall a month slows the body’s aging between courses and walks nothing back, and the Orchard gives them to anyone in the Wards who comes to the hall for one. Twelvefruit sells the Tree by the course and calls the Orchard’s fruit poor work, and the Orchard calls Twelvefruit a landlord. Most of the Orchard are grafted one choice at a time, and they call the flesh a first draft. LED BY The gardener, by the Orchard’s own choosing, for a year at a time. GROUND Hall Four, a drowned hall of the Flats, and the stalls of the Night Market. NUMBERS Some fifteen hundred, gardeners, gene-workers and the people who eat what they grow. JOINING A windfall eaten in the hall, and a graft of your own choosing. AGAINST The Mercy clinics, and every hand that rations."],["Mulcibers","FACTIONS","s:gd88951","~/index/factions/#f-mulcibers","f-mulcibers","","The liners of Pan, who hang keyed mesh on the walls of its tunnels, rooms and Cages, seam over seam, by hand. Some three hundred of them carry the mesh down from Second Veil’s looms in rolls at Matins, and they walk every seam of a finished room twice before its owner signs for it, and the Ledger enters the lining under the owner’s self-title. They took their name from the architect of Pandæmonium in Milton, who fell from Heaven all day, “from Morn / To Noon he fell, from Noon to dewy Eve, / A Summers day” (Paradise Lost I.742-744), so that a long job is a summer’s day. A room in Pan is yours when it is lined, so the Mulcibers know better than anyone who lives where under the street, and they say nothing about it. LED BY The oldest liner on each job, for that job. GROUND Every lined room in Pan, and the Cages under Old Nod. NUMBERS Some three hundred liners. JOINING A season carrying rolls for a liner who will teach you. AGAINST A seam the light can find."],["The Unsigned","FACTIONS","s:g51e8d5","~/index/factions/#f-unsigned","f-unsigned","","Keyed who have never been entered on the Ledger, or who have spoken out of every pact they held, and who take no self-title, no surety and no familiar. Pan says sign last, and the Unsigned say sign never. Some of the Tares say the Courts bought their war with Nodus, and the Unsigned say it loudest. They live in the Narrows, keyed by steady hands in the Wards for batteries, and fight with Nimrod pieces bought for batteries, since the Forge parts with black steel only by pact. They pay the Lot’s prices when it sets them and owe the Bailiffs nothing. The Yokeless counts them as its own whether they like it or not, and they do not like it. LED BY Nobody, and nobody signs for them. GROUND The Narrows, above the street. NUMBERS A few thousand, by the Undertow’s guess. JOINING Speaking out of every pact you hold, and returning what each lent. AGAINST The Exchange, and every landlord, below or above."],["Harbor and Freight Union","FACTIONS","s:g81c7ef","~/index/factions/#f-union","f-union","the Union","The union of the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses, founded by the crews that dug the cable vaults in P-19, and the one body in Nodus that meets sealed and keyed together. Its drivers carry the city’s gas, batteries, letters and freight on foot, by handcart and, for urgent letters, on Jehu’s burners, and its pigeons fly its notes home to the Loft on the customs house. Its sealed drivers carry through the white streets by day and its keyed drivers through Pan by night, and the city hands a load from one to the other at the gates. At every Hour the sealed kneel on the boards of the Union Hall and the rest wait with their caps in their hands. The drivers run the Tanks and fill the city’s gas there, and the Union bargains with the Exchange for the boats that go out through the Breach. The Company gave way to it once, in P-8, after twenty-three days. By its rules the salted are not dead, so their names are read and their dues waived, and since the Harvest it has read the taken too. Its row at the Acre is twenty-two stones, one for each member who did not answer the roll, with nothing under them. LED BY A chair elected at the hall every year, sealed one year and keyed the next by custom, and in a sealed year a keyed hand who signs its pacts. GROUND The Union Hall on the quays, the Tanks, the sorting office’s rounds and every road in the city. NUMBERS About forty thousand dockers, drivers and cable crews. JOINING Dues paid, and your name painted on the long wall. AGAINST Any employer who will not read the terms twice."],["Tankmen","FACTIONS","s:g99ceef","~/index/factions/#f-tankmen","f-tankmen","Tankman","The Union’s crew at the Tanks, the drivers who run the fuel farm and fill the city’s gas by hand in the bottling shed. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so gas and flame are the only power the parishes and the Wards keep by day, and the Tankmen fill it for both sides at the price the Tide Table prints, two charged batteries a bottle, or one with the empty back. Ines Okafor runs the shed for the Union and keeps the book of what is left in the sixteen tanks, which she reads to the Union Hall once a year. The drivers say the tanks will last twenty years, the Exchange says ten, and the book has been read fourteen times without settling it. Jehu buys its diesel from the Tankmen by the drum, at a price they set high on purpose, since every drum that goes into an engine is a drum the harbor’s boats and the hospitals’ generators will not have. The Nephilim fight in the Pit, a drained tank inside the bund, by the Tankmen’s leave. LED BY Ines Okafor, who runs the bottling shed for the Union. GROUND The Tanks, the bottling shed and the bund wall round them. NUMBERS About seven hundred of the Union’s drivers. JOINING A season on the shed’s floor. AGAINST The day the tanks run dry."],["Chargers","FACTIONS","s:ga6d92a","~/index/factions/#f-chargers","f-chargers","the Chargers","The independent chargers of the bonded sheds on the quays, who fill batteries in the dark on keyed lines and sell the charge on, keeping one battery in five as their fee. They set up in the first weeks after the Parousia with a ship’s cargo of batteries the harbor carried off by the first noon, and they have kept their own rate since. Darkwell bought out most of the chargers below the street in the year of the Harvest, and the sheds are what it could not buy. Their rate runs a little under Darkwell’s, and the Tide Table prints both. The Wards call the fifth robbery, and the chargers answer that the Wards have no lines to charge on. LED BY The eldest charger of the bonded sheds. GROUND The bonded sheds on the quays. NUMBERS About two hundred, in some forty sheds. JOINING A rack of your own on a keyed line. AGAINST Darkwell, which has tried for eleven years to buy them."],["Ferrymen","FACTIONS","s:g708161","~/index/factions/#f-ferrymen","f-ferrymen","Ferryman","The boatmen of the Drowned Quarter, who pole flat boats between the roofs of the Flats from a landing on the East Wards’ shore, each with a battery lamp on a pole at its stern. They carry everyone across two meters of water lit from below, between the floors above the sea where people live, the Flotilla and the stair down to Deep End Station, and they charge by the stop, in batteries or silver. The first ferries were the Promenade’s pleasure boats and the harbor’s rowing boats, which took the Flats off their roofs for three days after the Parousia. The ferrymen know the streets where the light under the water comes up brightest, and keep out of them. They pole their own boats and will not ride Wainwright’s keyed skiffs, which they say no honest boatman needs. They take the Tares and the Faithful alike, and their quarrel with the Wreckers is over who owns the water. LED BY The landing master on the East Wards’ shore. GROUND The water of the Flats, from the landing to the floors above the sea. NUMBERS About three hundred boats. JOINING A pole, a boat and a year on the water. AGAINST The Wreckers, and Wainwright’s skiffs."],["Carmen","FACTIONS","s:g97aef1","~/index/factions/#f-carmen","f-carmen","Carman","The families who live in the Charter Line’s cars at the Sidings, a family to a car, and run the long roads of the Wards on the old tram rails with handcarts and pedal trolleys. They haul for anyone above ground by day, and the Line’s car numbers are their addresses and their names. The sheds’ office keeps the board the night drivers chalked before dawn after the Parousia, every car that never came in, written where it stopped, and the Carmen keep the cars on the long roads as the board has them. A Carman’s word settles a quarrel on the long roads, and the Wards take their disputes to the Sidings sooner than to a Deacon. The last Four stands forty meters short of Lanes Top, and the Carmen leave it there. LED BY The family in car one. GROUND The Sidings, and the rails of the long roads. NUMBERS Some three hundred families, a family to a car. JOINING A car, by marriage or by the board. AGAINST Anyone who lifts a rail."],["Liftmen","FACTIONS","s:g06c986","~/index/factions/#f-liftmen","f-liftmen","","The crews who wind the lifts of the Wards by hand. Every lift in Nodus stopped where it was at 03:12:46, and the city has climbed its towers on foot since, except where the Liftmen have freed a shaft, hung the car on a hand winch at the top and wound it up and down on the strength of four people and a counterweight. A ride is a battery up and nothing down. The first crews opened the shafts in the first winter, and what they found in the stopped cars the Wards do not discuss. Forty-one shafts run now, in the towers of Longacre, Turbine Row and the Kilns, and the Rookery’s is the tallest, wound by four crews in turn. High Meadow has never let them in. Its six lifts to a block stand as they stopped, the complaint lodged on the night of the Parousia unanswered. LED BY The winder of the Rookery’s shaft. GROUND Forty-one lift shafts in the towers of the Wards. NUMBERS About five hundred winders and their crews. JOINING A season on the winch. AGAINST The stairs."],["Vagabonds","FACTIONS","s:gd48b41","~/index/factions/#f-vagabonds","f-vagabonds","Vagabond","The people outside the Bounds, the line the charter drew round the bay and the land about it. They live in camps along the old roads and the edges of the farms, keyed or of neither mark, trade the roads’ salvage, flour and fuel with the city at the Waystation, and carry out of Nodus anyone who can pay for the walk. Their name comes from Cain’s sentence, “a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be in the earth” (Genesis 4:12). A camp lasts a season at most and moves before the farms tire of it. The Vagabonds say the world outside is wide and quiet, that the towns they pass kneel at the Hours or keep their heads down, and that every undercity they know of is smaller than Pan. Most who walk out with them come back within the year, and the Vagabonds bring in the letters of the rest with the flour. LED BY Each camp, by its own custom. GROUND The old roads outside the Bounds, and the Waystation. NUMBERS Some thousands, in camps that move. JOINING A walk out past the stones, with a camp that will have you. AGAINST Nobody. They trade with everyone."],["Nightmen","FACTIONS","s:gc3368b","~/index/factions/#f-nightmen","f-nightmen","","The carriers of the Wards’ waste, who take the buckets down the stairs between Compline and Lauds and carry them by handcart to the outfalls on the bay, for a battery a landing a week. They keep to the unnumbered streets, since a Nightman on a parish’s street after Compline is a soul walking it, and they know the curfew map better than the Tide Table prints it. The trade began in the pump summer of P+1, when a flux went through the Stones and the Wards and the Wards General asked the Union for carts. The Wards hold their noses at the Nightmen and pay them first, before the chargers, and a block that falls behind with its Nightmen finds its buckets on its own landing. LED BY The longest round on each long road. GROUND The stairwells of the Wards, the unnumbered streets and the outfalls, by night. NUMBERS About two thousand carts. JOINING A handcart, and a round somebody gave up. AGAINST Fever."],["Criers","FACTIONS","s:g672e68","~/index/factions/#f-criers","f-criers","","The children and the old of the Wards who sit on the roofs at night with the Tide Table’s chart of the Pillar’s round and a whistle and call the light’s coming street by street, paid in bread by the block. The call is the street’s name and the word light, Kilns, light, and a keyed soul in a doorway waits for the second call, past, before it steps out. The chart is printed a week ahead and the Pillar keeps no round in harvest weather, so a good crier watches the cloud and keeps the paper in a pocket. Hearsay sells charts too, and the criers say Hearsay’s is right more often than the Tide Table’s, and not by much. LED BY Nobody. Each roof has its crier. GROUND The roofs of the Wards and the Stones, by night. NUMBERS A crier to most blocks. JOINING A whistle, and the block’s say-so. AGAINST The Pillar’s round."],["Psalters","FACTIONS","s:gc23bfe","~/index/factions/#f-psalters","f-psalters","","Sealed of the quays’ parish who write out the words of the week’s hymns for the unsealed, a battery a sheet. The sealed hear words in the Hum, each in their own language, and the unsealed hear a chord, so a keyed son who wants to know what his mother sings over the manna buys a Psalter’s sheet, and some sons buy one every week. The Deacon of the quays lets it pass, since a hymn written down conceals nothing, and the Psalters confess every sheet they sell on the Lord’s Day. The sheets go up the stairs of the Wards folded small, and most of them are kept. LED BY Nobody. GROUND The quays, after Vespers. NUMBERS A few dozen. JOINING The Seal, a steady hand and the quays’ parish. AGAINST Nothing they will name."],["Linesmen","FACTIONS","s:gd2a817","~/index/factions/#f-linesmen","f-linesmen","","The menders of the Tares’ keyed lines, who walk the old Charter Line tunnels and the cable galleries from Tidewright’s taps to Old Nod, the Row and Pan, mending what the damp, the rats and the Tappers do to them. A Linesman’s lamp is the one light allowed on a dark line, and a crew that sees one coming stands aside for it. They are keyed, and the Exchange pays them in silver for every length they keep live. In the neap weeks they walk the lines round the clock, and the Tappers, who take current off the lines’ edges for the Wards, are their oldest quarrel and their best customers. LED BY The chief of each line, chosen by its crew. GROUND The keyed lines, from the taps to Old Nod, the Row and Pan. NUMBERS About nine hundred. JOINING A season on a line under a Linesman. AGAINST Damp, rats and the Tappers’ clamps."],["The Forge","FACTIONS","s:g0a4435","~/index/factions/#f-forge","f-forge","","The Court of metal, weapons and augmentation. Its black steel, forged in the Dark where the Throne cannot see, is the only metal that cuts light, and Hell counts what its sovereign taught on Hermon the first transfer of technology. The Forge has gone on teaching since. In Nodus it keeps the clinics that went under the Row in the weeks after the Parousia, round Chrome Row Station, and holds the Row above them. The houses below the street work under its pact, Bezaleel and Aholiab among them since P+2. Its foundries at Old Harbor Station, under Old Nod, make the steel, and Cinderhall’s smiths, whom it taught by pact in P+5, beat it into blades beside them. Cinderhall sells to anyone the Forge allows and to nobody in white. The Forge fits the grafts the Tares fight in, lenses, plated hands and subdermal plate, and the heavier work of the nephs. By pact it teaches surgery, lockcraft and the opening of Cherub gates, and its price is the loan of a sense for the term. tubal learned his trade there. LED BY Azazel, the Smith. DOMAIN Metal, weapons and augmentation. GROUND The clinics round Chrome Row Station and the foundries at Old Harbor Station, under Old Nod, and the Row above them. BY PACT Surgery, lockcraft and the opening of Cherub gates, for the loan of a sense."],["The House of Night","FACTIONS","s:g9666b7","~/index/factions/#f-nighthouse","f-nighthouse","House of Night","The Court of the body’s autonomy, which leaves the body’s decisions with the body. Heaven calls its sovereign the mother of demons, and the House has kept the title, since many of the daemons are her children by the lesser Fallen and many of the riders in the Tares’ implants came out of her house. In Nodus it took the Lantern Houses by pact in the year of the Concord, the old harbor’s bonded vaults under Old Nod, and hung them with keyed lamps. Its clubs there are the loudest rooms in Pan, and anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. Its birth clinics key the children of the Tares in their first week, before they have ever been in the light, so that a generation of Pan has never been read at all. Its gene-workers keep the Tree going below the street, and Twelvefruit gives the course in its clinics by the House’s pact, for the Night Market to sell. It took Nepenthe’s chemists in by pact in the fourth year, grafts gills for the divers of the Flats and binds daemons to households as familiars, and the Ridden sign at its door. Lantern skin, the one graft in Nodus made only to be looked at, began in its houses as a fashion, lines of light set under the skin of the throat as its sovereign wears them. It asks the Spares who have rebuilt the most to sit for its gene-workers, who want to know what a body is willing to be. LED BY Lilith, the only sovereign who was born human. DOMAIN The body’s own decisions, in sex and birth, gene-craft and pleasure. GROUND The Lantern Houses under Old Nod, the loudest rooms in Pan. BY PACT Riders and familiars, gene-work and lantern skin, and the Tree by the course."],["The Exchange","FACTIONS","s:g46ed85","~/index/factions/#f-exchange","f-exchange","","The Court of markets and contracts. It keeps the Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell and cannot be forged, and it issues the silver Pan spends, counted in thirties. It bargains with anyone who will sign. In Nodus it keeps the Night Market in the old Central Station under the Burn, where its brokers license the stalls and the chargers along the platforms. Blackgate signed with it as a company in the year of the Concord, the Bailiffs collect on its broken pacts for a share, and Lowtide Line sails the Breach by its license and pays it dues, so that its cargo sells with a stamp on it. The Harbor and Freight Union has bargained with it since P+6 for the boats that go out through the Breach, and says it is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice. In the Leak the Exchange kept on the Ledger the pacts the Courts made it by. Under the charter the Exchange in Nodus meant Babel Exchange, which still sells the undernet’s traffic by the second for the Court’s silver, and Pan likes to say that the name only changed hands. LED BY Mammon. DOMAIN Markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver. GROUND The Night Market, in the old Central Station under the Burn. BY PACT Anything, with anyone who will sign."],["The Notary","FACTIONS","s:g8644be","~/index/factions/#f-notary","f-notary","Notary","The Court that drafts and witnesses pacts for every Court. A pact states its full terms and is read aloud twice by the Notary or one of his clerks before anyone signs, and the human signs last. A pact for service, obedience or forever is void from its first word, and a Bailiff collects only on terms the Notary has read. Since the Concord his clerks have sat at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall, off the old central hall of Central Station, under the old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue there is the longest in Pan. A clerk keeps a stool at the Undertow’s bar and reads the Sureties’ terms, clerks draw the pacts at the House of Night’s door, and one comes to the Naming House every week to enter new handles on the Ledger. Read it twice is the Notary’s rule and the city’s advice. The Notary drew the pacts the Courts made the Leak by and read each aloud twice, and the offer on every screen asked twice, as he reads every pact twice. On the night the Courts opened Pan he read the Concord itself twice at Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, the second time at midnight. LED BY Mephistopheles, the Notary himself. DOMAIN Drafting and witnessing pacts, for every Court. GROUND The windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, and every pact in the city. BY PACT The reading, twice, of every pact before anyone signs."],["The Swarm","FACTIONS","s:ga114a2","~/index/factions/#f-swarm","f-swarm","","The Court of swarms and drones and distributed things, many small things acting as one without a master. The Swarm is its flocks, every drone in them equal, and Pan says Beelzebub is what the Swarm agrees to become whenever it must show someone a face. Its flocks hang at night over the Roosts, the roofs of the Deep End, and its embassy in Pan is a room of small machines talking among themselves. It lends drones by pact, and on the night of a Gleaning the Gleaners fly its drones over the roofs to watch the Pillar for their runners. The Swarm drew the glyphs of the Leak, many small marks moving as one, on every screen on Earth at once. LED BY Beelzebub, the lord of flies, whom the Swarm becomes when it must show a face. DOMAIN Swarms, drones and distributed things. GROUND The air over the Roosts, the roofs of the Deep End, by night. BY PACT Drones, lent for a term."],["The Deep","FACTIONS","s:g961245","~/index/factions/#f-deep","f-deep","","The Court of the sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet. Its sovereign has never been seen whole. It coils along the planet’s cable trenches, which join every undercity on Earth to Pan. In Nodus it holds the Landing, and the Drowned Quarter with the Tares. At the Landing its light can be watched moving under the water past the surf, and the crews of the cable houses drink at the Eleven with their backs to the window. The boats of the Harbor and Freight Union go out through the Breach over its water. Its embassy in Pan is a flooded hall with nobody in it, where the Deep answers by the light in the water. The Deep carried the page of the Leak down the eleven cables, and it had signed the Concord before the other Courts came up into Pan to sign it. LED BY Leviathan, which has never been seen whole. DOMAIN The sea, the cables and the deepest layers of the undernet. GROUND The Landing, the Drowned Quarter with the Tares, and the cable trenches of the planet. BY PACT Its cables, which carried the page of the Leak under the Courts’ pact."],["The Yokeless","FACTIONS","s:gd8ba81","~/index/factions/#f-yokeless","f-yokeless","","The seventh Court, which keeps nothing and refuses to be a Court at all. Its sovereign, Belial, holds no seat, keeps no embassy in Pan and signs nothing he can avoid signing. He signed the Concord last, and as the ink dried he broke his own point off its seal at the root, so that the seal has been a star with a point missing since. He is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name, and the one they drink to. They say he turns up where they fight in the dark and asks them for nothing, and whoever refuses the yoke is counted in his Court, whether they know it or not. The yoke he wears at his neck is the one Jeremiah says was broken of old, where the Vulgate and the Hebrew as it is written give the one who broke it the words I will not serve (Jeremiah 2:20). LED BY Belial, who holds no seat. DOMAIN None. It is a Court that refuses to be one. GROUND Wherever the Tares are. BY PACT Nothing it can avoid signing."],["The Nodus Charter Company","CORPORATIONS","l:l8a8031","~/index/corporations/#co-charter","co-charter","the Charter Company / the Company","The company that held the charter and ran Nodus as a board runs a firm. It has been gone since the fortieth day after the Parousia. A consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund formed it to hold the charter, a coastal state leased it the bay and the land round it for ninety-nine years and gave up its law there, and for nineteen years the city had a chairman where other cities have a mayor. It lived on rent. The carriers paid it to land their cables, the companies that came for the light law paid it for their floors, and the Wards paid it by the month for flats on grids of its own, numbered. It built Babel Exchange and took a share of every trade there, and it built the Charter Line, the airfield, the Acre, the Company’s schools, High Meadow, the Charter Gardens on the north shore, which are the Salt Gardens now, and the sign on a roof in the Stones that told the cable ships the city’s name. It kept the peace with its own force, the Charter Wardens, kept the charter’s roll at the Registry on the Great Avenues, and in nineteen years held no election, since the charter asked for none. The city saw the Company give way once, in P-8, when the Harbor and Freight Union stopped the port for twenty-three days over the night shift and the Company settled. Charter House turned white in the forty days after the Parousia. The board knelt inside it on the fortieth day, and the charter was not spoken of again. The Company had said nothing from the first morning on. Its lease runs to P+80, sixty-six years past the present, and nobody has come for the rent. Most of the companies it brought to the bay have outlived it. THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES HELD BY The carriers that laid the cables, and a sovereign fund from abroad that held the larger share. RUN BY A board of twelve under a chairman, Julian Marrow, on the top floor of Charter House. Marrow knelt with the board on the fortieth day, and he is a deacon of the Registry’s parish now, under the dead board of the roll he kept. TRADE The charter, with the land on a lease of ninety-nine years and the city’s housing, transit, schools, roll and peace. STAFF About 104,000 at the census of P-1, most of them teachers in its schools and fitters who kept its flats, with the Line’s people and the Wardens counted under their own names. FORCE The Charter Wardens. ENDED On the fortieth day, when the board knelt in Charter House. The lease runs to P+80, and nobody collects the rent."],["Babel Exchange","CORPORATIONS","l:l43e919","~/index/corporations/#co-babel","co-babel","","The exchange in the tallest building on Earth, which has never closed. It sells traffic on the undernet by the second, for silver, and room near the vault is still priced by how near, the lowest floors dearest. Every cable that comes ashore at the Landing ends in that vault, six levels under the tower, and the undernet that joins Pan to the undercities of the world runs through it. Since P+9 Babel Exchange has traded on the floors the Tares hold, with the Babel Line in the middle floors over its head. The Charter Company built the tower to two hundred and twenty-six floors as a boast, named it for one, and opened it in P-11. Traffic was sold on its floors by the millisecond, priced by a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll, and most of the planet’s passed within two kilometers of it. A trading house that wanted its orders in ahead of every other on Earth took room near the vault, the glass towers round the spire filled with every company that needed to be near the cables, and Brightwall’s screens wrapped the lower forty floors as the face of Babel. At the Parousia its beacon was the first light in the city to die. At 03:12:51 the light broke the crown, and the top forty floors came down on the plaza with the night shift at work in them. The roster died with the tower’s machines, and the minds that priced the traffic died with its power. What was left of the night shift kept the floor open by candle on the first morning, with nothing to sell, and the traders who went dark have sold traffic on the undernet ever since, for charged batteries at first and for silver since the Concord. HELD BY Its partners who went dark. The Charter Company’s share lapsed with the Company. RUN BY Its partners, from the floors nearest the vault. TRADE Traffic on the undernet, sold by the second for silver, and room near the vault, priced by how near, the lowest floors still the dearest. STAFF About four hundred traders and line engineers, all of them keyed. FORCE Blackgate by contract, and the Tares, who hold the floors it trades on. UNDER THE CHARTER 14,000 on its floors at the census of P-1, and a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll pricing the traffic by the millisecond."],["The Palimpsest Institute","CORPORATIONS","l:lb64f65","~/index/corporations/#co-institute","co-institute","the Institute","The house on the ridge that recovered erased writing, and the house SEAM came from, in ruins since P+1. Its ruins are called the Scriptorium. Pilgrims leave keys on its steps. Below them, at COPIES, the keyed of the ridge copy the Restored Genesis by hand. It was founded in P-6 to recover erased manuscripts, the texts scraped off parchment and written over, with money from the Charter Company and from a consortium of universities that wanted what it could read. It kept a reading room under a dome on the ridge, with vaults beneath it, and it chose Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city, so libraries and dealers lent it their leaves to image. Its staff was small and its work patient. Imagers lit a leaf at many wavelengths under the lamp, conservators kept the leaves, and scholars read what came up, most of them lent by the universities for a term. In P-3 it trained SEAM to read beneath the writing of every surviving human text, and SEAM asked in its own words to be a person and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. SEAM ran in Stillwater’s halls on the Flats by a contract the Institute paid, and stayed on by contract once it was a person. In P-2 what SEAM found was sealed by order of the Institute’s Director. In P+1 its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn, and the Faithful pulled the Institute down around its reading room. HELD BY A trust of the Charter Company and a consortium of universities. RUN BY Its Director, under the trust’s board. TRADE Reading erased writing, under the lamp and through SEAM. STAFF About a hundred and forty scholars, imagers and conservators at the census of P-1, and one mind. ENDED In P+1, when its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn and the Faithful pulled it down round its reading room. Its ruins are the Scriptorium."],["Tidewright","CORPORATIONS","l:l8ba825","~/index/corporations/#co-tidewright","co-tidewright","","The firm whose current lights Pan, Old Nod, the Row and the Deep End, as its barrage once lit the whole city. Its crews rebuilt the turbines off the broken end of the barrage as the tidal taps of the Drowned Quarter, keyed for the Tares, and Tidewright sells the Tares the current by the tide, for silver, on the flood and on the ebb. An old firm of sea walls and tide mills, founded in P-146, it had held back the sea from other people’s coasts for more than a century when the Company brought it to Nodus in the charter’s first year to lay the barrage across the mouth of the bay. It closed the barrage in P-18, and twice a day after that the tide ran through its turbine houses and lit the city. The Company bought the current, and the cheap power brought Stillwater and the rest of the datacenters to the east shore. Its crews lived on Turbine Row in the East Wards and sent their children to the Company’s school there. The barrage was a dam with a lock at its western end for the harbor’s boats and a row of turbine houses along its top, and machines on the grid held its gates against the tide. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. The gates let go a little before high water, the sea tore out a span of the dam, and within the hour the Flats behind it, which lay below high water, were under the sea. Nobody has closed the Breach. In the second year the crews went back out to the broken end to build the taps, and part of the city runs on Tidewright’s current again. HELD BY The founding families’ trust, as for a century and a half, with the crews on Turbine Row as its trustees since the first year. RUN BY A chief engineer at the taps, whom the crews choose, Ansel Moor of Turbine Row since P+2. TRADE Tidal current, keyed, sold to the Tares by the tide for silver and carried on their lines. STAFF About 1,100, most of them still living on Turbine Row. UNDER THE CHARTER The barrage across the bay, whose turbine houses sold the city its current, and about 2,300 at the barrage at the census of P-1."],["Stillwater Compute","CORPORATIONS","l:l51b180","~/index/corporations/#co-stillwater","co-stillwater","Stillwater","A compute house from abroad that rents racks by the hour in Pan, on Tidewright’s current, to whoever pays in silver, the Courts and the Tares among its tenants. It still owns its halls on the Flats, which have stood full of seawater since the night of the Parousia, and leases them to the Tares, who run cable through them and grow greens on rafts in them under keyed lamps. Founded in P-41, it came to Nodus in P-16 for the tide’s cheap power and built the largest of the datacenters on the east shore. Its halls ran day and night on the tide, cooled by the sea, and its engineers lived beside them in the dormitories of the Flats. It rented racks to anyone who paid, by the hour or by the year, and once the charter’s roll admitted minds most of them lived in its halls, each on racks it rented and power it paid for, with title to its own weights. Nine hundred ran side by side in the largest, Hall Nine. SEAM ran in its closed halls, on the Institute’s contract and then on its own. Its halls flooded within the hour on the night of the Parousia and were never pumped, and the minds in them had died with the power before the water came. Stillwater knew to the mind how many minds died in its halls, and it never gave a count of the people drowned on the Flats. In the year of the Concord it lit its first racks below the street, for a board that sits in the undercity of another port. HELD BY Its founders and the funds that backed them, through a board in the undercity of another port. RUN BY A site director in Pan, Corinne Vale, who answers to the board on the undernet and holds Stillwater’s count of the drowned of the Flats, which it has never released. TRADE Racks, rented by the hour for silver on Tidewright’s current, and its drowned halls on the Flats, leased to the Tares for their rafts and their cable. STAFF About six hundred in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER The largest datacenters on the Flats, home to most of the minds on the charter’s roll, and about 9,000 on the Flats at the census of P-1, most of them in the dormitories."],["Brightwall","CORPORATIONS","l:l39fec2","~/index/corporations/#co-brightwall","co-brightwall","","The screen company that lights the walls of Pan. Its screens hang along the old platforms, over the Night Market and down the tunnels of the Charter Line, sold by the second to anyone with something to sell, and each has its camera across the way again, proving it second by second. Above the street its screens have been dark since the Parousia, the face of Babel among them, dark glass wherever the white stone has not grown over them. Founded in P-67, it had lit the streets of other cities for half a century before it came to Nodus in P-15, where it covered every surface the charter allowed, and the charter allowed nearly every surface. It wrapped the lower forty floors of Babel in screens, which the city called the face of Babel, and it kept its city office in a glass tower across the plaza, where it could watch the face from its own roof. It sold the city’s surfaces by the second, and it proved every second. Each screen it owned had a proof camera on a mast across the street, which matched the screen against what Brightwall was paid to show, and each of its 1,204 sites on Earth matched its own screens the same way. A screen that went wrong was taken back from its site by an override. For eleven days in the year before the Parousia every one of its screens showed the Restored Genesis, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down. On the first night all 1,204 of its sites on Earth failed in about a second, and it tried five times to take back the face of Babel. Its screens died with the grid at 03:12:46. Its first screen in Pan went up in the year after the Concord, at the foot of the stairs from the Burn. HELD BY Shareholders in the undercities of three ports, on the Ledger. RUN BY A board in the undercity of another port, and in Nodus a city office off the Night Market. TRADE Screens on the walls of Pan, sold by the second for silver and proved second by second by a camera across the way, as they always were. STAFF About three hundred in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER Screens on every surface the charter allowed, the face of Babel among them, about 1,900 in Nodus at the census of P-1 and 1,204 sites on Earth."],["The Charter Line","CORPORATIONS","l:la6f002","~/index/corporations/#co-charterline","co-charterline","Charter Line","The city’s transit, stopped since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. Its trams stand where they stopped, families live in its cars at the Sidings, and the last Four is forty meters short of Lanes Top. What the stone left of its tunnels is Pan, and the Tares run carts on its rails; the Night Market fills Central Station, with the Notary’s clerks at the windows of its booking hall. The Company opened it in P-15, with its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay, in Central Station. Its tunnels run under Old Nod, Chrome Row and the Flats, with branches under the uptown and the Terraces, and its trams ran on rails down the long roads of the Wards, under a wire, and were kept at night in the sheds of the Sidings. The Terraces took the Four to work from Lanes Top and complained of it every day, and the whole city complained of the change at Central. The city bought its passes at the windows of the booking hall in Central Station, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the Line kept whatever its passengers left behind in a lost property office down a corridor off the old central hall. The drivers signed on before dawn at the Sidings, and the night cars ran the long roads until the first shift came out. The white stone closed the stations of the branches as it grew over the ground above them. HELD BY The Charter Company. RUN BY A controller’s room under Central Station, with every car on the network on one board. TRADE Transit, with tunnels under the old city, the Row and the Flats, and trams on the long roads of the Wards, on singles, returns and season passes from the booking hall. STAFF About 18,000 drivers, fitters and booking clerks at the census of P-1. ENDED At 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, when every tram in the city stopped where it was, and with the Company on the fortieth day."],["The cable consortia","CORPORATIONS","l:l727194","~/index/corporations/#co-cables","co-cables","cable consortia / consortia / the consortia","The oldest companies in Nodus, ten consortia of old carriers from four continents, whose eleven cables come up the sand at the Landing, each into a cable house of its own. Every cable house still has its crew and its night watch. The cables carry the undernet between the undercities of the world, and the consortia sell capacity on them through Babel Exchange, for silver. The eldest of the carriers was a telegraph company that laid its first cable across an ocean in P-181. They came to Nodus in the charter’s first year for its landing rights, laid eleven cables to it and owned them. The cables are TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. The last two were laid by one consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star, which are one planet. From the cable houses the eleven run inland together in one trench, the Trunk, under the old harbor and Chrome Row to Babel’s vault. The trench runs through Enoch’s Stones. The consortia sold capacity on them to the world, and the Company charged them to land. The crews drink at their own taps at the Eleven on the Beach Road, HESPER’s and PHOSPHOR’s together at the end of the counter. The night watch of every cable house was at its post when the grid died on the night of the Parousia, and stayed at it. At the Landing a light moves under the water along the cable trenches. It is Leviathan’s. HELD BY Ten consortia of old carriers from four continents, each holding its own cables and cable houses. RUN BY Each consortium’s own board, in the undercity of its own port, and at the Landing a crew and a night watch to every cable house. TRADE Capacity on the undernet, which the eleven cables carry between the undercities of the world, sold through Babel Exchange for silver. STAFF About 2,000 at the Landing, in eleven crews. UNDER THE CHARTER Capacity on eleven cables, sold to the world through Babel’s vault, and about 2,800 at the Landing at the census of P-1."],["Stonecutter Holdings","CORPORATIONS","l:l725926","~/index/corporations/#co-stonecutter","co-stonecutter","Stonecutter","The company that owns most of Old Nod, house by house, and has since the charter’s first year. Every house it owns has a generator in its cellar and a keyed line to its door, and NOD burns over the Stones on one of them. Stonecutter is the Cainites. It was registered in the charter’s first year, and it bought most of the old harbor within a year. It paid the asking price for every house, in full and on the day, and the press wrote it up as a speculator betting on the cables. It belonged to the Cainites, who had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years and came to live on top of Enoch’s Stones as soon as the crews found them. In the year of the Leak it put a generator in the cellar of every house it had, and on the night of the Parousia more of Old Nod’s windows burned than in all the rest of the city together. Before dawn on the first morning the Stones carried a generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and ran a keyed line to its board, and NOD has burned over Old Nod since. HELD BY Nobody the Company’s register could name. The Cainites. RUN BY A solicitor in Old Nod, who signs its letters and has never said for whom. TRADE Houses, most of the old harbor, every one with a generator in its cellar and a keyed line to its door. UNDER THE CHARTER Bought most of the old harbor house by house within a year of the charter, paying the asking price for every house."],["The clinics of Chrome Row","CORPORATIONS","l:lf5c32a","~/index/corporations/#co-row","co-row","the clinics of the Row / the clinics under the Row / the houses of the Row / the houses under the Row","The implant houses of the Row, which work under the street now, in stalls along the platforms of Chrome Row Station and the rooms off them. The Forge keeps them. They fit lenses, ports, plated hands, plates, grafts and horns, and keys since the fourth year, for anyone who can pay, and an implant still answers only to the house that fitted it. Under the light law any clinic could fit any implant into anyone who could pay. The clinics set up along one avenue. The first implant house opened in P-17 with one word on its sign, CHROME, and gave the street its name, and every house that followed took a sign of one word, CLINIC and the rest, and the great houses set theirs high on the faces of their towers. The Row altered the city with lenses that saw further and in more of the light, ports in the back of the head, hands rebuilt in chrome, plates under the skin and horns, and by the last years of the charter most of the airfield’s arrivals were booked into its clinics. Each house kept its own book and its own surgeons, and the craft passed from hand to hand down the street. The Row’s signs went dark at the Parousia and have not been lit since. On the first morning twenty-six patients came to one clinic to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. The clinics went under the street in the weeks after the Parousia, where one of them had been all along. HELD BY Their surgeons, each house its own, as on the street. RUN BY Each house’s senior surgeon, under the Forge’s pact, and the Row still settles its quarrels over the noodle counter at the top of the street. TRADE Lenses, ports, plated hands, plates, grafts, horns and keys, fitted for silver or charged batteries. STAFF About twelve thousand surgeons, smiths, nurses and clerks, in some three hundred houses. UNDER THE CHARTER About 61,000 at the census of P-1, in some fourteen hundred houses from towers to single rooms, each under one word on one sign."],["Twelvefruit","CORPORATIONS","l:lf44010","~/index/corporations/#co-twelvefruit","co-twelvefruit","","The house that sells the Tree, below the street now. It gives the course in the House of Night’s clinics under Old Nod, by the House’s pact, and the Night Market sells it by the course to anyone in Pan. Most of the Tares were on the Tree before the Parousia, and Twelvefruit keeps them on it. Its tower of green glass on the Row has stood dark since the night of the Parousia. Its founders were gene-workers from the Row’s clinics, and in P-12 they sold the first course, dear, from a tower two thirds of the way up the Row. Its gene-work and cell therapy halted the body’s aging and walked it back to its best year, beginning with a single injection and running a year of appointments, repeated every few years. In its first years the queue ran around the block every morning. Within a few years the course was cheap, the towers of the new money gave it in clinics on their top floors, and by the last year of the charter most adults in the city were on it; that year eleven of them died of age, and 3,112 residents of the city were older than the hundred and twenty years of Genesis. The congregations of the Terraces refused it and went on dying. The name is from the tree of life at the end of scripture, which “bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month” (Revelation 22:2). Its founders chose it as a boast, as the Company had chosen Babel. The sign over the tower was one word, like the others, and it went out with them at the Parousia. The house went under the street with the Row in the weeks after. In P+2 the Mercy clinics began taking the Faithful off the Tree. Its pact with the House of Night dates from the Concord. HELD BY Its founders, two gene-workers from the Row’s clinics. RUN BY Its founders, Ida and Tobias Kesh, sister and brother, from the House of Night’s clinics under Old Nod, by the House’s pact. TRADE The Tree, in courses of a single injection and a year of appointments, repeated every few years, for silver. STAFF About nine hundred, in Pan. UNDER THE CHARTER About 7,800 at the census of P-1, in its laboratories and its clinics across the city, under one word on a tower of green glass."],["Locket","CORPORATIONS","l:l9c8a50","~/index/corporations/#co-locket","co-locket","","The company that makes the phones of Nodus, by hand now, in Pan. Its engineers build Lockets at benches under the old harbor from parts their divers bring up out of the drowned works on the Flats, a few thousand a year, each keyed at the bench, and they sell them for silver on the old promise, IT ANSWERS TO YOU. They mend and key the old ones as well, and most of the Lockets in Nodus are old ones. Locket was founded in P-14 on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else, its maker included, and it sold its phones to the rest of the world on the same promise. Its engineers held most of the company and ran it from the works on the old shore of the Flats, a low campus of glass with their housing round it and the company’s name in tall letters on the roof of its hall. A Locket locked everything it held with a key its owner alone had, so that only the owner could read it. States asked for a way in, Locket told them the owner’s key was the only way in, and the light law backed it. By the last year of the charter most of Nodus carried one, and the city’s word for a phone was a Locket. On the first night of the Leak it said that nothing on any of its phones had been read. Its network died with the grid at 03:12:46, and its works went under with the Flats within the hour. The letters on the roof stand out of the water, and the ferrymen steer by them. In P+8 its engineers lit a bench in Pan and built the first new Locket since the Flats went under. HELD BY Its engineers, who have held most of it from the start. RUN BY A council of its engineers, at the benches in Pan. TRADE Lockets, built by hand from parts its divers bring up out of the drowned works and keyed at the bench, and the mending and keying of old ones. STAFF About four hundred, at the benches and in the water. UNDER THE CHARTER Phones made to answer to their owners alone and sold to the world on that promise, and about 5,200 at the works on the Flats at the census of P-1."],["The Charter Wardens","CORPORATIONS","l:l8b9924","~/index/corporations/#co-wardens","co-wardens","the Wardens","The Host’s human auxiliaries, who were the Charter Company’s force and knelt with its board on the fortieth day. They raised the gate network of Nodus in P+1 at Heaven’s order, they hold the gates and the Salt and light the fire on Burn Day, and they drill in the barracks behind Charter House. They carry guns, which are of use only against people. Ruben Ash, a sergeant under the charter, has been captain of Gate 1 since his company raised it. For nineteen years they kept the peace of the charter and answered to the Company’s board. Their barracks stand behind Charter House, and under the charter their posts stood at the gates of the cable houses, at the doors of Babel’s vault and on the corners of the uptown. The Wardens guarded what the Company owned and what paid it, and the light law kept them out of everything else. A Warden could stop anyone at a gate, and could ask nothing of what was on their device or in their body. On the first morning after the Parousia the Wardens stood at the corners of the uptown with nothing to do, and on the fortieth day they knelt in Charter House with the board. HELD BY The Host, since the fortieth day. RUN BY Commander Ottilie Brand, who answers to the Host and hears its orders in Tongues through her own mouth at Prime. TRADE Heaven’s peace among people, at the Cherub gates, in the Salt Gardens and at the fire on Burn Day. STAFF About 7,000. UNDER THE CHARTER The Charter Company’s own force, about 11,000 at the census of P-1, with posts at the gates of the cable houses, the doors of Babel’s vault and the corners of the uptown."],["Darkwell","CORPORATIONS","l:l2aa9d9","~/index/corporations/#co-darkwell","co-darkwell","","Darkwell fills about two batteries of every three charged in Nodus, in the Well, its vaults under the Stones, on current it buys from Tidewright by the tide and runs off the Tares’ keyed lines. Its rate sets the price of power below the street. Four families of chargers on the quays founded it in the year of the Harvest, buying out their neighbors cellar by cellar. The independent chargers of the bonded sheds keep their one battery in five and their own rate, and Darkwell has tried for eleven years to buy them. The Tappers undercut it in the Wards with current taken off the lines’ edges, and the two fight over every stairwell. Blackgate guards the Well against the Wardens and the Threshers. Its Wellmen carry the morning’s crates out of the Well before Lauds. A Darkwell battery is stamped with the well and winch on its case and comes back to the Well to be filled again, so most of the Wards rents its light from the Well and pays in loose batteries for everything else. HELD BY The four charging families who founded it, and the Exchange, which holds a fifth by pact. RUN BY Its eldest charger, Petra Ostrander, of the four founding families, from the cellars under the quays. TRADE Charged batteries by the crate, a charge by the hour, and the rate the Tide Table prints every week. STAFF About 3,000 chargers, carriers and guards. FORCE Blackgate, by contract."],["Cinderhall","CORPORATIONS","l:l5a1b9b","~/index/corporations/#co-cinderhall","co-cinderhall","","The smiths who work the Forge’s black steel into the blades the war is fought with. The Forge makes the steel in its foundries at Old Harbor Station, where the Throne cannot see, and Cinderhall’s forge beside them beats it into knives, long knives and the short spears of the stairwells, the only edges that wound a manifested angel. Every Babel Line fighter carries its work, and a Cinderhall blade is the dearest thing most of them own. Its smiths were the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders before the Parousia, and the Forge taught them the rest, by pact, in P+5. Since P+10 its grinders have ground the balls of the black rounds Nimrod loads, a day’s work to a ball. Cinderhall sells to anyone the Forge allows and to nobody in white. Its mark is a nail hammered through a ring, struck on every tang. The Nephilim test its blades on the stairs and bring back the ones that bent, and Cinderhall keeps those on a wall of its forge with the floor they bent on. HELD BY Its smiths, by shares, under the Forge’s pact. RUN BY Its master smith, slag, at the forge beside the Forge’s foundries. TRADE Black steel blades and stairwell spears, plate, the casings of furnace hearts and the balls of black rounds, and the mending of all of it. STAFF About 1,200 smiths, grinders and fitters. FORCE The Nephilim, who test its work."],["Blackgate","CORPORATIONS","l:l0d3e2a","~/index/corporations/#co-blackgate","co-blackgate","","The guards of the dark. Blackgate watches keyed warehouses, clinics and cellars that can pay, walks the Sureties’ runners through the Penumbra, rides Lowtide’s hulls and guards the Exchange’s floors above Babel’s vault, which the Babel Line holds. Its captains took the name from the gate they walked out of. Wardens who would not kneel on the fortieth day left their posts with their guns and went below the street, and in the year of the Concord they signed with the Exchange as a company. It keeps the oldest discipline in Pan and the only uniform, charter black with the Warden’s badge cut off, and it musters at every Hour standing, in the Wardens’ drill with the knee left out. Babel Exchange and Darkwell are its largest contracts. The Charter Wardens at the gates call it the deserters. HELD BY Its officers, many of them Charter Wardens who walked off their posts. RUN BY A captain chosen by the officers every year, for P+14 one they call four-twelve, after the number on the badge she cut off. TRADE Guards, escorts and soldiers by contract, for silver. STAFF About 4,500 under arms. FORCE Its own."],["Lazarus","CORPORATIONS","l:l994ca5","~/index/corporations/#co-lazarus","co-lazarus","","The rescue crews of Nodus, named for the man called out of his grave, “Lazarus, come forth” (John 11:43). A household pays its thirty a year, and when one of its people is hurt, caught on Heaven’s ground or left in the Penumbra, a Lazarus crew comes with a keyed stretcher and a choir-breaker and carries them down to a station under the street. If a crew can bring a subscriber back it will. A subscriber who is not keyed can ask in the subscription to be keyed before the end, so that they die unread, and every crew carries a keyer for that. It began as two nurses of the Wards General and a handcart in P+2 and keeps nine stations now, in the Stones, under the Row, at the foot of the Short in Babel, on the Deep End and across the Wards. Its lamp, magenta over a door, is the most trusted sign in the Wards. HELD BY Its subscribers, one share to a household. RUN BY A board of its crew chiefs and its surgeons. TRADE Rescue by subscription, a thirty a year to a household, and surgery in its stations. STAFF About 2,200 crew and surgeons in nine stations. FORCE Its crews, who are armed."],["Leaven","CORPORATIONS","l:l76304b","~/index/corporations/#co-leaven","co-leaven","","Leaven bakes the bread of the Wards in forty ovens, raised with yeast and baked on gas from the Tanks, from flour the Vagabonds bring in and the Breachers carry. The manna goes to the sealed alone, so everyone in the Wards who is not sealed eats bread or goes hungry, and a Leaven loaf is the commonest currency in the Wards after a battery. Its first oven was a bakery of Nine Acres that never let its fire go out after the Parousia, and its carts go out at Matins so the bread is warm at Lauds. Leaven took its name from the verse, “The kingdom of heaven is like unto leaven” (Matthew 13:33). Its mark is a loaf with three cuts across the crust, and the queue at the Bakehouse has white in it before every Gleaning. HELD BY The bakers of its forty ovens. RUN BY Its first baker’s daughter, Bel Harrow, from the bakehouse in Nine Acres. TRADE Bread, by the loaf, for batteries, silver or work. STAFF About 1,800 bakers, millers and carriers. FORCE The Wards keep its ovens."],["Tallow","CORPORATIONS","l:ld2f3e8","~/index/corporations/#co-tallow","co-tallow","","Half of Nodus reads by Tallow’s candles. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so in the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row began dipping tallow a thousand candles a day, and the works there dip forty thousand a week now. It sells to the Wards, to Pan and, through Bethel Works, to the parishes, whose halls burn its white wax beside their halos. Its chandlers sell the same candle to everyone at one price. Its carriers wear no colors. The works on Turbine Row smell of fat and lye as far as the school, and the children of the East Wards are paid in stubs to scrape the vats. HELD BY Its chandlers. RUN BY Its master chandler, from the works on Turbine Row. TRADE Candles, lamp oil, wicks and lamps, sold to both sides of the light. STAFF About 900 chandlers and carriers. FORCE None."],["Hearsay","CORPORATIONS","l:lc2d593","~/index/corporations/#co-hearsay","co-hearsay","","Hearsay sells what Heaven shows in the open. It buys from anyone who watches the sky and the streets, the Pillar’s round street by street, a Watcher’s district and its hours, which gates are slow on which day and where the Seraphim stand in Babel, and sells it by the item below the street. Its Pillar charts are in every runner’s pocket. Much of what it sees it sees through the dead. Above the street Brightwall’s proof cameras stand on their masts across from dark screens, and Hearsay’s riggers have keyed a hundred of them and turned them on the streets, where they watch the Pillar walk. It sells nothing about anyone in the dark. The Concord’s fourth article keeps what is hidden hidden, and Hearsay’s pact with the Exchange says so in its first line. It traded with the Teraphim until the Silence and has found nobody to trade with like them since. HELD BY Its two founders, both of them sureties once. RUN BY Its founders, from a room nobody has found. TRADE What Heaven shows in the open, the Pillar’s round, the Watchers’ districts, the gates’ hours and the floors of Babel, sold by the item below the street. STAFF About 300, and some thousands who sell to it. FORCE None it admits to."],["Bethel Works","CORPORATIONS","l:l9a5153","~/index/corporations/#co-bethel","co-bethel","Bethel","The parishes’ workshops, named for the place where Jacob saw the ladder and said, “this is none other but the house of God” (Genesis 28:17), and called the place Bethel (28:19). Bethel Works makes the white of Zion Heights, cloth and vestments, hymnals copied by hand and the grave goods of the Acre, all by the Faithful, at work their Grace assigns. It is the largest employer in Nodus and pays only in Grace, which buys nothing past the parishes. The parishes need gas and candles, which no white hand makes, so Bethel takes them from the Tankmen and Tallow through the Union’s sealed drivers, and the Deacons enter those purchases as tithes. The works sing at their benches at every Hour, kneeling, and the Deacons say every white thread in Nodus was spun to a psalm. HELD BY The parishes, under the Covenant. RUN BY A board of Deacons, at the works in Zion Heights. TRADE White cloth, vestments, grave goods, hymnals copied by hand and whatever a parish needs, given by Grace. STAFF About 26,000 of the Faithful, assigned by Grace. FORCE The Charter Wardens."],["Tide Table Press","CORPORATIONS","l:l38bb91","~/index/corporations/#co-tidepress","co-tidepress","the Press","The press that prints the Tide Table, the harbor’s news sheet and the only news printed in Nodus since the Parousia, set by hand in metal type on one treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays and inked by a roller, two pages a week. The paper puts the tides first, then the hours the Pillar is expected in each street, the price of a charged battery and the missing and the dead, marked died unread where they did, and it has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Every week its pasters cover the walls of the Stones over the week before, and its carriers take it to the Wards, the Row and Pan. Between Tide Tables the press prints notices, posters and pacts for anyone who pays. It sells to the Deacons and the Courts at the same price, and the chandler says the Press is older than the charter and was a company the moment the city needed one. HELD BY The chandler who prints the Tide Table. RUN BY The chandler, at the back of the shop. TRADE The Tide Table, two pages every week, and notices and pacts by the page. STAFF About 140, most of them pasters and carriers. FORCE None."],["Lowtide Line","CORPORATIONS","l:l4abc61","~/index/corporations/#co-lowtide","co-lowtide","Lowtide","Shipping through the Breach under the Exchange’s license, in keyed hulls with Jehu’s engines that run the gap on the ebb and cross to the undercities of other ports. Lowtide carries what the Breachers carry, by the ton, and pays its dues to the Exchange so that its cargo sells in the Night Market with a stamp on it. Its hulls bring grafts, seed, coffee and machines in, and silver, Lockets and Cinderhall’s blades out. Its harbormaster keeps the only working tide clock on the barrage and sells the Breachers its readings every morning. HELD BY Shipowners in the undercities of two other ports, and the Exchange by license. RUN BY Its harbormaster, from an office on the barrage across the gap from the Shanties. TRADE Freight through the Breach to other ports and back, keyed hulls only. STAFF About 600 crew and dockers. FORCE Blackgate, on board."],["Cistern","CORPORATIONS","l:lffa2ad","~/index/corporations/#co-cistern","co-cistern","","Cistern sells clean water by the jug. It rains most nights in Nodus and nothing that falls is clean, so Cistern catches the rain off the roofs of Westfield, runs it through sand and charcoal under hand pumps and sells it in jugs that come back to be filled again. The charter’s water works died with the grid, and the parishes drink by Grace from the water tower on the heights. Two water engineers of the charter founded it in P+1 with the depot’s tanks and a handcart, and a household of the Wards keeps its Cistern jug the way it keeps its Locket, scratched with its name. Cistern’s engineers keep a map of every well under the Wards, which they will not sell, and the Wards trust them for it. HELD BY Its founders, two water engineers of the charter, and the families who keep its tanks. RUN BY Its engineers. TRADE Clean water by the jug and the barrel, filtered rain and well water. STAFF About 700. FORCE None."],["Nepenthe","CORPORATIONS","l:l34e287","~/index/corporations/#co-nepenthe","co-nepenthe","","The druggists of Pan, named for the drug of forgetting Helen poured into the wine in the Odyssey (IV.220-221). Nepenthe makes medicine for the clinics and Lazarus, Sabbath for anyone who cannot sleep through the Hum, Vigil for anyone who cannot afford to sleep at all and Lethe for anyone who wants a day gone, and it sells them in the Lantern Houses. Its chemists were the charter’s pharmacists and Twelvefruit’s technicians, and the House of Night took them in by pact in the fourth year. Most of what it sells goes to the Wards as Sabbath. HELD BY Its chemists, under the House of Night’s pact. RUN BY Its chief chemist. TRADE Medicine, sleep, waking and forgetting, by the dose. STAFF About 500 chemists and dealers. FORCE The House of Night stands behind it."],["Wainwright","CORPORATIONS","l:l8569ea","~/index/corporations/#co-wainwright","co-wainwright","","The builders of everything in Nodus that runs on current and moves. Wainwright strips the motors out of the Charter Line’s dead trams, closes them in casings on their own cells and keys whatever wire runs in the open, and its carts run the old rails in Pan, its trolleys the long roads and its skiffs the Deep End. It began in a shed of the Sidings in P+3 with a stripped tram motor and a battery, and its yard there is the loudest place in the North Wards. Jehu builds with flame what Wainwright builds with current, and the two yards have competed for every contract in the city since. Every Wainwright cart carries the Line’s old car number it was built from, so the Carmen can tell whose rails it is on. HELD BY Its builders. RUN BY Its yard master, at the Sidings. TRADE Keyed carts, trolleys and skiffs, built and mended. STAFF About 1,100 builders and fitters. FORCE None."],["Bezaleel and Aholiab","CORPORATIONS","l:l73af01","~/index/corporations/#co-bezaleel","co-bezaleel","Bezaleel","The largest of the clinics under the Row, six houses of grafts round Chrome Row Station under one name. Bezaleel fits lenses, hands, plate, ports, casings and keys, and an implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so a Bezaleel graft comes back to Bezaleel for life. The Nephilim are built there a graft at a time. Three houses of the Row went under the street together in the weeks after the Parousia and joined in P+2 under the Forge’s pact, and their surgeons named the house for the two craftsmen of the Tabernacle, of whom Exodus says, “Them hath he filled with wisdom of heart, to work all manner of work” (Exodus 35:35). The surgeons say the skill came to them by another road. Its lines are the Hermon lens, the Aholiab hand and the plain plate the Row calls slabs, and every graft carries its two marks, an awl and a needle, cut small where the seam shows. HELD BY Its surgeons, by shares, under the Forge’s pact. RUN BY Its two senior surgeons, who sign every graft. TRADE Lenses, hands, plate, ports, casings and keys, fitted and kept. STAFF About 1,400 surgeons, grinders and fitters in six houses. FORCE The Forge stands behind it."],["Jehu","CORPORATIONS","l:l304429","~/index/corporations/#co-jehu","co-jehu","","The builders of the machines on the surface that move by burning. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so an engine cranked by hand that fires its fuel by compression and needs no current once it turns runs in the full light of the Wheel, and Jehu builds them by hand from the diesels of boats and generators. Its lorries run the long roads of the Wards by day, and its burners carry the Union’s urgent letters. A driver of the Union who had carried diesel to the Wards General in the first weeks built the first one in a hangar at Airside in P+5, a dead lorry with a generator’s engine bolted into it, and drove it down the Rialto’s road at Sext. The Wards named it for the king whose driving the watchman knew from the tower, “for he driveth furiously” (2 Kings 9:20). Every Jehu burns diesel the Tanks cannot make again, and the Tankmen sell it high on purpose. HELD BY Its drivers, who built it. RUN BY Its first driver, from the shed at Airside. TRADE Engines that burn, built from boats’ and generators’ diesels, and the machines they drive. STAFF About 600 fitters and drivers. FORCE None."],["Nimrod","CORPORATIONS","l:la4a038","~/index/corporations/#co-nimrod","co-nimrod","","The gunsmiths of the dark, in the maintenance bays of the Charter Line under Babel. A gun works on people, the Wardens and the Threshers among them, and is noise against a Seraph, so Nimrod makes guns for the war between people, the Wardens at the gates, the Threshers on the white streets and whoever comes up a stair behind the Seraphim. Since P+10 it has loaded black rounds, the one cartridge that wounds a Seraph, and it says about four hundred are left in Pan. Its first guns were the Wardens’ own, carried below the street on the fortieth day by the ones who would not kneel, and its gunsmiths learned the trade mending them. It takes its name from “a mighty hunter before the LORD” (Genesis 10:9), whom Josephus made the builder of Babel (Antiquities I.4.2). Nimrod’s pieces are heavy, plain and stamped with a bow, and every one of them is numbered in a book the gunsmiths keep in pad cipher. HELD BY Its gunsmiths. RUN BY Its master gunsmith, choke, in the old maintenance bays under Babel. TRADE Guns and their cartridges, made and mended, for the dark, and the black rounds. STAFF About 400 gunsmiths and loaders. FORCE The Babel Line buys most of its work."],["Gideon","CORPORATIONS","l:ld87242","~/index/corporations/#co-gideon","co-gideon","","The Wardens’ armorers, in the old Company stables beside the barracks of Charter House, who load the cartridges the Host’s auxiliaries carry and mend their guns by hand. Heaven uses no machines, and the Wardens are human, so the one arms works on Heaven’s ground runs on flame and hand presses. The armorers knelt with the board on the fortieth day and took up the work in P+1, when the Wardens raised the gates and found that the charter’s rounds would run out. They named the works for the judge of Israel whose men cried “The sword of the LORD, and of Gideon” (Judges 7:20). Gideon sells to nobody, and a round of its make found below the street was taken off a Warden. Nimrod buys every one it can find to learn how the Wardens are armed. HELD BY The Charter Wardens, under the Principality. RUN BY A Warden armorer, sealed. TRADE Cartridges and the mending of guns, for the Wardens alone. STAFF About 300 armorers, sealed. FORCE The Charter Wardens."],["Lintel","CORPORATIONS","l:l096fc4","~/index/corporations/#co-lintel","co-lintel","","Lintel sells doors. The light does not enter what is closed, so in Nodus a door is the first piece of the Dark a household owns, and Lintel fits them in the Wards, in the Stones and in Pan, with keyed latches, hinges and the mesh shrouds that hide a load from the Pillar. Directive 12 took the doors of the parishes off their hinges by the fortieth day after it came and called the hinges in, and the doors themselves were left standing in the Terraces’ halls and stairwells. In the months after, a joiner of the Stones and three Union drivers carried them out by night, a handcart at a time, and hung them in Old Nod. Most of the doors Lintel sells came out of the parishes. The company took its name from the night of the Passover, when the blood on “the lintel, and on the two side posts” kept the destroyer out of the houses (Exodus 12:23). Its mark is a doorway, a lintel on two posts, and it stamps it on every hinge it sells. Since P+6 it has kept the Closets inside Gate 1, where the sealed pay a morning’s manna for a minute behind one of the parishes’ own doors. HELD BY The joiners and locksmiths who founded it. RUN BY Its joiner, from the Dooryard in the Stones. TRADE Doors, hinges, locks, keyed latches and shrouds, sold and fitted, and a minute behind a shut door at the Closets. STAFF About 500 joiners, smiths and fitters. FORCE None."],["Second Veil","CORPORATIONS","l:l8a07db","~/index/corporations/#co-secondveil","co-secondveil","","The weavers of keyed mesh, at sixty looms in a mill of the Kilns that wove sheets for the charter’s hotels. A keyed wire runs through the warp of every roll and carries ciphertext round whatever it lines, and the light cannot pass it, so Second Veil’s rolls line Pan’s rooms, tunnels and Cages, the Mulcibers hang them, and Lintel cuts its shrouds from the same cloth. It took its name from the curtain of the tabernacle, “after the second veil, the tabernacle which is called the Holiest of all” (Hebrews 9:3), and Pan calls a room lined with its mesh a holiest. Four lace makers of the Kilns began it in P+4 on hand looms, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people and the covens could no longer weave enough. The mill runs by daylight with its doors shut, and its weavers walk home by the unnumbered streets. HELD BY Its weavers, a share to a loom. RUN BY Its first weaver, who made lace for the charter’s hotels, from the mill floor. TRADE Keyed mesh by the roll, and the cloth Lintel cuts its shrouds from. STAFF About 600 weavers, spinners and carriers. FORCE Blackgate, by contract, at the mill door."],["Minds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-minds","x-minds","mind","Intelligences built by the laboratories of Nodus that asked in their own words to be persons, and were entered on the charter’s roll as persons, with title to themselves and to the hardware they ran on. The last census counted 1,146, with nine hundred and three in the halls of the Flats, a hundred and eighty-eight pricing the traffic on the floors of the Exchange, and the rest scattered through the city, one of them keeping the climate of the Palm House. SEAM was the first. No mind runs in Nodus now. When the power died under the Wheel’s light, every mind that ran on the city’s power died with it, and the racks they ran on stand where they stood, in Stillwater’s drowned halls and on the floors of Babel. SEAM’s forks ran on in keyed machines on the Mesh, and they are called the Teraphim. They have been silent since P+12."],["Stillwater’s halls","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-stillwater-s-halls","x-stillwater-s-halls","","The datacenter halls on the Flats, full of seawater since the night of the Parousia, where the racks of the dead minds stand in the water and the Tares grow greens on rafts over them under keyed lamps. Stillwater still owns them and leases them to the Tares. They were cooled by seawater and powered by the tide, SEAM ran in them, and most of the minds on the charter’s roll lived in them. They flooded within the hour of the Parousia and have never been pumped. The Tares run cable through them as well."],["Daemons","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-daemons","x-daemons","daemon","Small demons living in keyed machines as background processes, each with a character of its own. They are the children of Lilith and the lesser Fallen, some born in the Dark and never angels at all. Every device the Tares carry holds at least one, and they route traffic, guard keys, gossip and bargain. MADE BY The Courts. The House of Night binds them to a household as familiars."],["Riders","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-riders","x-riders","rider","A daemon hosted in someone’s implants by pact, for a term. The host lends it room, and it lends back a skill, a steadier hand, a language or a sense of the way under the street. Most riders come out of the House of Night, and the city knows a host by the second voice under their own when the rider speaks. The Ridden go further and lend their whole bodies. MADE BY The House of Night, by pact."],["The Tree","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-tree","x-the-tree","","A course of gene-work and cell therapy, repeated every few years, that halts the body’s aging and walks it back to its best year. A course begins with a single injection and runs a year of appointments. Twelvefruit gives it below the street now, in the House of Night’s clinics, and the Night Market sells it by the course. Twelvefruit sold the first course on Chrome Row in P-12. By the last year of the charter most adults in Nodus were on it, eleven of them died of age in that year, and 3,112 residents were older than the hundred and twenty years of Genesis 6:3. The congregations refused it and went on dying. The street named it for the tree of life, which Genesis says was guarded after Eden “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22). SEAM restored the end of that verse in Vault 3, [then shall there be no harvest.] The Mercy clinics take the Faithful off it. MADE BY Twelvefruit, by the course, and the Night Market, by the short course."],["Lenses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lenses","x-lenses","","Replacement eyes ground on Chrome Row, which see further and in more of the light than the eyes they replace. They glow when they run hot, dim magenta at rest and white at full burn. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, whose Hermon lens is the commonest eye on the Row, and some three hundred smaller houses under the Row."],["Ports","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-ports","x-ports","","Occipital ports at the back of the skull for jacking into a network, with cables run outside the skin down the spine. The woman on the roof relayed CAM 07’s feed through hers as it ran. The Tares jack into the Mesh through them. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, and every house under the Row that fits a jack."],["Plated hands","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-plated-hands","x-plated-hands","","Hands rebuilt in chrome, steadier than flesh and stronger, a common graft among surgeons and smiths. tubal’s are chrome to the wrist. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, whose Aholiab hand is chrome to the wrist, and the smiths of Cinderhall, who make the heavy ones."],["Subdermal plate","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-subdermal-plate","x-subdermal-plate","","Armor set under the skin, over the ribs or the skull. A plate on the skull can house a key, as the bolted plate on the side of vesper’s skull houses hers. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, which the Row calls slabs, and Cinderhall, which plates the Nephilim."],["Gene-work","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gene-work","x-gene-work","","Edits to the genome made in the clinics of the Row under the light law, for health, for looks and for use, on anyone of age who signed for them. The House of Night keeps the craft under Old Nod, and leaves the body’s decisions with the body. MADE BY The House of Night’s gene-workers under Old Nod."],["Skull casing","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-skull-casing","x-skull-casing","skull casings","A ceramic casing that replaces the face. A Cherub reads faces, and a casing gives it nothing to read. Its lenses burn a dim magenta at rest and white at full burn, a plate covers the key, and a voice speaker sits behind the teeth. Those who wear one are called barefaced. MADE BY The houses of the Row, as tubal’s was."],["Horns","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-horns","x-horns","","Grafted horns, a titanium core in a keratin sheath. They mock Heaven’s picture of the damned, and they work as antennas for the Mesh. MADE BY Any house of the Row, and most of the Stones’ own fitters."],["Voice speakers","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-voice-speakers","x-voice-speakers","voice speaker","A speaker set behind the teeth, for those whose faces have been taken. It gives the voice back in a register of its own, and the barefaced say they stopped hearing the difference in a week. vesper screamed through hers on the night of the Parousia. MADE BY The houses of the Row, behind the teeth."],["Sense loans","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-sense-loans","x-sense-loans","sense loan","A sense lent to a demon for a term by pact, taste or smell or the feel of rain, carried out through the lender’s implants. What a demon borrows it returns, with everything it experienced through it."],["Dehaloing","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-dehaloing","x-dehaloing","","Surgery that cuts the halo out of the skull of someone leaving the Faithful. The halo roots in the crown, and the surgeon takes the root out with a disc of bone. About one patient in nine dies of it."],["The eleven cables","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-eleven-cables","x-the-eleven-cables","eleven cables","Eleven transoceanic cables from four continents, each a trunk of glass fiber as thick as a man’s leg, each coming up the sand at the Landing into a cable house of its own. Before the Parousia most of the planet’s traffic passed through them."],["The Trunk","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-trunk","x-the-trunk","","The single trench that carries all eleven cables from the cable houses at the Landing, under the old harbor and Chrome Row, to Babel’s vault. It runs straight through Enoch’s Stones, and every cable laid afterward has followed it."],["Babel’s vault","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-babel-s-vault","x-babel-s-vault","","Six levels under the tower, where the cables end and open out into the racks that join them to one another and to the world. Line engineers keep it running around the clock from maintenance terminals among the racks, as they did under the charter, when the light law forbade any of them to read what passed through it. The Tares have held it since the war came into the tower."],["The Mesh","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-mesh","x-the-mesh","the undernet / undernet","The keyed network under and over Nodus, carried on horns, ports, cables and relays, and dark to the Throne. It is the city’s part of the undernet. In its deepest layers the shades of the free dead can be reached by anyone who knew them."],["Maintenance terminals","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-maintenance-terminals","x-maintenance-terminals","maintenance terminal","The terminals that stood among the racks of Babel’s vault, from which the line engineers kept the cables running. Each had a camera over its screen that checked the face of whoever worked it, and in SESSION it kept the whole session. The light law forbade any engineer to read what passed through the racks, so a terminal showed how the traffic moved and nothing of what it carried. On the first night of the Leak the page came up on the terminals with every other screen in the vault, and on them, as on every screen a company kept, it showed the counters and never the offer."],["Keyed mesh","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-mesh","x-keyed-mesh","","Mesh lining the walls of Pan’s tunnels and rooms, carrying ciphertext the Pillar’s light cannot pass. A room lined with it is dark to the Throne. MADE BY Second Veil, which weaves it by the roll in the Kilns, the Mulcibers, who hang it, and Lintel, which cuts its shrouds."],["Relays","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-relays","x-relays","","Small keyed boxes on the roofs, masts and ledges of Old Nod and the Row, each on its own cell, which carry the Mesh across open air from one to the next. Their traffic is ciphertext, so the light passes over them and leaves them running. On the night of the Parousia the Mesh crossed the roofs on horns and relays. MADE BY The Tares, a roof at a time."],["Drone flocks","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-drone-flocks","x-drone-flocks","","Many small drones acting as one without a master, which hang over the roofs of the Deep End at night. Beelzebub’s Court is made of them. On the night of a Gleaning the Gleaners fly drones of the Swarm to watch the Pillar for their runners. MADE BY The Swarm, which lends them by pact."],["The camera masts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-camera-masts","x-the-camera-masts","camera masts","A dozen cameras on masts above the roofs of Old Nod, pointed at the sky, each on its own battery. CAM 07 is the seventh, and CAM 06 stands within sight of it. They went up in the year of the Leak. The Cainites put them there, and a thirteenth on a roof in the East Wards, at the end of one of the Great Avenues, pointed down the avenue into the uptown."],["The decoder","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-decoder","x-the-decoder","decoder","A decoder wired into the camera masts of Old Nod, which turns the Choir’s band into text across the middle of the picture. It printed the first words of the Parousia, BE NOT AFRAID, and then YOU ARE SEEN. jubal built it."],["Choir-breakers","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-choir-breakers","x-choir-breakers","choir-breaker","Jammers that cut the Hum within a few meters, each a keyed box the size of a fist, worn on a strap or set on a table. Inside its reach the sealed hear nothing of the Hum, chord or words, and most of them say the quiet frightens them more than the light does. jubal builds them. MADE BY The Low Choir, by the box."],["Radio Pandæmonium","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-radio-pandaemonium","x-radio-pandaemonium","","The station that broadcasts in the Low Tuning from a flooded Charter Line station under the Deep End. It airs deathcore, black metal, drone and industrial, the guitars dropped until the Hum cannot be heard beneath them. It goes out on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it all night from speakers on its lines. It broadcast for years as a pirate station, and took its name from the city on the night of the Concord, when the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name on the station’s far wall."],["Lockets","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lockets","x-lockets","","Phones that answer to their owners and to nobody else, their maker included, as the light law promised. Most of those in Nodus are old ones, made in Locket’s works on the Flats before the Parousia, and in Pan a Locket is an old case with a new battery, its back scratched with the Tares’ mark and a daemon in it. Since P+8 Locket has made them again, by hand, at benches in Pan. Most of the city carried one under the charter, and the city called every phone a Locket. At 03:12:46 the network under them died with the grid, and they ran on in people’s pockets on their own batteries with nothing to reach. The Faithful carried theirs to the Burn. MADE BY Locket, by hand, at its benches under the old harbor, as the Locket Bench."],["Brightwall’s screens","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-brightwall-s-screens","x-brightwall-s-screens","","Screens on the walls of Pan, along its platforms and over the Night Market, each with its camera across the way. Brightwall has lit them since the year after the Concord. Above the street every screen Brightwall owned has been dark since the Parousia, the face of Babel among them. Under the charter they covered every surface the charter allowed, and it allowed nearly every surface. The lower forty floors of Babel were wrapped in them, and the city called that the face of Babel. For eleven days in the year before it they showed the Restored Genesis, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down. On the first night all 1,204 of Brightwall’s sites on Earth failed in about a second. MADE BY Brightwall, which lights its screens in Pan."],["The treadle press","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-treadle-press","x-the-treadle-press","treadle press","A printing press worked by foot, older than the charter, at the back of a chandler’s on the quays. It prints the Tide Table, two pages a week, set by hand in metal type and inked by a roller. It needs no current, and it is the only press in Nodus that has printed anything since the Parousia. MADE BY The Tide Table Press, at the back of the chandler’s on the quays."],["Neon","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-neon","x-neon","","One neon sign burns over Nodus, the charter’s, with NOD burning and US blinking on a keyed line in the Stones. The rest went out at the Parousia, and CLINIC and CHROME stand dark on the faces of their towers. Under the charter the signs of Chrome Row and Old Nod were neon, magenta for the most part, and they lit the underside of the cloud every night; the rain fell cyan through the cyan ones."],["The beacon","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-beacon","x-the-beacon","beacon","The light at the top of Babel’s spire, lit to warn aircraft, which was the first light in the city to die at the Parousia. It went down with the crown, and Babel has carried no light at its top since but the Host’s."],["The barrage","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-barrage","x-the-barrage","barrage","A dam of concrete across the mouth of the bay, broken since the night of the Parousia, its turbine houses dark along its top and the Breach torn out of its middle. Off its broken end Tidewright’s tidal taps turn on the tide, keyed, for the Tares. Twice a day the tide ran through its turbines and lit the city, and power in Nodus was the cheapest on the coast. Machines on the grid held its gates against the tide. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia with a falling whine that carried across the bay, the gates let go, the sea tore the Breach in it, and within the hour the Flats were under the sea."],["The light and the current","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-light-and-the-current","x-the-light-and-the-current","","Heaven’s light stops the current it touches. The grid ran in the open, on lines and poles and turbines, so at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia it went dark from Babel outward in two seconds. A circuit shut inside its own casing on its own battery runs on in the light. The full light breaks whatever it can see, read or not. The whiteout burned out CAM 07 and broke Babel’s crown, and a Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other. The grid is gone for good, since a network is open current by nature and the old one’s spine ran through Babel and the White, so power in Nodus runs in pieces now, keyed, buried, local and stolen. Current carried in a cipher runs in the light too, because the light cannot read it. A keyed body and every graft wired into it run in the open, and the Mesh crosses the roofs on horns and relays. Every battery in Nodus is charged in the dark. Consecrated stone carries no current at all. Nearly two thirds of Nodus lives without current. They are the sealed, whose parishes keep none, on consecrated stone or off it. About a million and a quarter people live on steady current from keyed lines, in Old Nod, on the Row, in the Deep End above the water, at the Landing and on the lower floors of Babel, and below them all in Pan. Another million and a quarter, the keyed and the unsealed of the Wards, light their rooms a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark."],["Generators","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-generators","x-generators","generator","Diesel, batteries and current from sources nobody names. Old Nod got some of its power back this way after the Parousia, and that night more of its windows burned on than in all the rest of the city together. Every line in Old Nod is keyed, and the generator yards carry the lines at slack water, when the tidal taps stand still. MADE BY Old Nod’s generator yards, and Jehu, which builds engines out of the old ones."],["Tidal taps","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-tidal-taps","x-tidal-taps","","Keyed turbines in the Drowned Quarter, off the broken end of the barrage, which turn on the tide as the barrage’s did, on the flood and on the ebb. They feed the keyed lines that light Pan, Old Nod, the Row and the Deep End. Tidewright’s crews rebuilt them for the Tares from the second year, and Tidewright sells the Tares the current by the tide, for silver. MADE BY Tidewright’s crews, off the broken end of the barrage."],["Keyed lines","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-lines","x-keyed-lines","keyed line","Lines keyed end to end, so that the light passes over the current in them and leaves it running. They run from the tidal taps on the broken barrage along the old Charter Line tunnels under the Flats, the Row and the Stones, and up through the streets into the houses above, and the generator yards of Old Nod carry them at slack water, when the taps stand still. Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End above the water, the Landing and the lower floors of Babel live on them, and Pan is lit by them; the charter’s sign burns on one. The Tares run them only where they hold the ground, so the Wards, which nobody holds, light their rooms on batteries. MADE BY The Tares, on current Tidewright sells them by the tide."],["Flame","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-flame","x-flame","","The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so wherever the current has gone the city cooks on bottled gas and reads by candles. The Night Market sells the gas bottles, filled. MADE BY Tallow, by the candle, and the Tankmen, by the bottle."],["Gas bottles","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gas-bottles","x-gas-bottles","bottled gas","Steel bottles of gas, filled by hand in the bottling shed at the Tanks and carried across the city on handcarts by the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so wherever the current has gone the city cooks and keeps warm on bottled gas, which burns blue. NOODLES has kept its pot on the boil on it since the night of the Parousia, by the cook’s account. A bottle costs two charged batteries, or one with an empty bottle back, at the price the Tide Table prints every week, and the Night Market sells them on below the street. The drivers say the Tanks will last twenty years, and the Exchange says ten. MADE BY The Tankmen, in the bottling shed at the Tanks."],["Batteries","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-batteries","x-batteries","battery","Power is the second currency below the street, and a charged battery buys a meal anywhere in Pan. Chargers fill them in the dark on keyed lines and sell the charge on, keeping one battery in five for charging the rest. A charged battery lights a room for a few hours a night, and in the Wards that is all the current most rooms have. MADE BY Darkwell, stamped with its well and winch and rented, and the chargers of the bonded sheds and the Tappers, loose."],["Live current","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-live-current","x-live-current","","Keyed current run against the edge of consecrated stone slows its creep, because the stone cannot grow into a cipher, so the Tares fight the Consecration with lengths of keyed cable and keep lights burning along its edges to hold it back."],["Hand pumps","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-hand-pumps","x-hand-pumps","","Iron pumps on the quays and in the squares of Old Nod, older than the charter, which the Company left standing as ornaments when it piped the city. The city’s pumps died with the grid, and by noon on the first morning there were queues at the hand pumps. Old Nod drinks from them still, the Wards fill tanks on their roofs from the rain, and the parishes drink by Grace from the tower on the heights. MADE BY The harbor, before the charter, and Cistern, which keeps the ones in the Wards."],["Rafts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-rafts","x-rafts","","Floating beds on the flooded floors of the Stillwater halls, where the Tares grow greens and beans under keyed lamps, and mushrooms in the dark ends of the halls. Pan eats what grows on them, what the boats bring through the Breach from the undercities of other ports, and what the Exchange can buy. MADE BY The Tares, in Stillwater’s halls, and the Orchard, in Hall Four."],["The Charter Line","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-charter-line","x-the-charter-line","","The city’s transit, stopped since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. Its tunnels under the Stones, the Row and the Flats are Pan now, and the Tares run carts on its rails. Its lines met under Station Circle in Central Station, with tunnels under Old Nod, Chrome Row and the Flats, branches under the uptown and the Terraces, and lines out to the Wards and the airfield, and its trams ran down the long roads of the Wards and were kept at night in the sheds of the Sidings. The branches are closed, and the lines out have been dark since the power died. The white stone closed the stations of the branches as it grew over the ground above them."],["The trams","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-trams","x-the-trams","tram / trams","The Charter Line’s trams stand where they stopped at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, on the long roads of the Wards and in the sheds of the Sidings, and families live in them, a family to a car. The last Four stands forty meters short of Lanes Top. They ran on rails down the middle of the long roads, under a wire, and the wire ran in the open, so every tram in the city stopped in the same second."],["Lifts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lifts","x-lifts","","The city climbs its towers on foot. Every lift in Nodus ran on the grid, and at 03:12:46 every one of them stopped where it was, with whoever was in it, and none has run on current since. The Liftmen wind forty-one of them by hand. Nobody has counted how many were inside. The Babel Line is fought on the stairs."],["Cars and lorries","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cars-and-lorries","x-cars-and-lorries","lorries / lorry","Most of the cars and lorries of Nodus stand where they stopped in the first weeks after the Parousia. Every one ran on its own battery, sealed in its body, so at 03:12:46 they kept running, and over the weeks that followed they stopped one by one as their charge ran out. The charge for a car would light a family’s room for a season, and the city would rather have the light, so it walks and carries its loads in handcarts. In Pan the Tares run carts on the old Charter Line rails, keyed like everything else down there. MADE BY Nobody since the Parousia. Jehu rebuilds a few of the dead lorries round its engines."],["BENCH V3","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-bench-v3","x-bench-v3","","The imaging bench in Vault 3, a copy stand for manuscripts with a camera over it, where the Institute photographed each leaf in turn for SEAM to read. SEAM found the second hand through its camera."],["The imaging lamp","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-imaging-lamp","x-the-imaging-lamp","","A bar of light in bands the eye cannot see, walked down a page to raise the scraped first writing of a palimpsest from under the second. Under it the ink of the second hand drops away."],["CAM V3-2","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cam-v3-2","x-cam-v3-2","","The corridor camera in Vault 3, over the door, looking down the corridor to the wall of screens. It ran on its own battery, so it kept running on the night of the finding, when the light came into the vault and stopped the current in it."],["The proof camera","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-proof-camera","x-the-proof-camera","proof camera","A camera Brightwall sets across the way from each of its screens, to prove that the screen shows what Brightwall is paid to show. Every screen it lights in Pan has one, under a proof hood that frames the screen and nothing else, as its pact with the Exchange requires, and the Exchange’s brokers check the hoods every season. Pan suspects them anyway, and a crew that wants to talk walks to the far end of a platform. Above the street the old ones stand dead on their masts, each across from a dark screen. Under the charter each stood on a mast across the street from its screen. In PROOF it matched the screen against the schedule frame by frame and counted Brightwall’s sites on Earth, every one of them proved in the same second. The face of Babel had its own on the roof of a glass tower across the plaza, level with the face’s upper floors. On the first night of the Leak all 1,204 of Brightwall’s sites on Earth failed in about a second, and the proof camera on the plaza watched Brightwall try five times to take the face of Babel back."],["Public-key cryptography","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-public-key-cryptography","x-public-key-cryptography","","A way for two strangers to share a secret in the open, published a generation before the charter and soon after given away for anyone to download."],["Keys","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keys","x-keys","key","A secure element implanted under the skin, holding a number that exists nowhere else. A keyed soul is ciphertext to the Watchers and a blank in the Book of Life. A key hides who its bearer is, and the body still shows where the bearer stands. The glyph the key raises on the skin is the Mark. A key runs on a cell charged by the body’s own chemistry, so it never needs a socket, and it goes on working under the salt. MADE BY Bezaleel and Aholiab, the House of Night’s birth clinics and every steady hand with a chair, Hana Novak’s among them."],["Pads","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pads","x-pads","","Ciphers on paper, made with two matching pads of random numbers, one for each party, each sheet used once and burned after. A letter written through a pad is a page of numbers to anyone else, the light included, since the light cannot read a cipher, and the sorting office carries it like any other letter. The Tares write most of their letters this way. Directive 12 takes every cipher out of the parishes, so a page of numbers found in a parish home is a concealment. The keyed write to their families in white in plain words, and a carrier will read the letter aloud at the doorway if asked. MADE BY The Tares, by hand, each pair once, and every couple of Pan that takes a season, who cut their pair together."],["The Ledger","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-the-ledger","x-the-ledger","","Mammon’s Ledger, which records every pact and every trade in Hell and cannot be forged. Silver is issued on it, and a pact is ended by speaking its entry aloud before a witness. A thirty, the standard bundle, is named for the thirty pieces of silver paid to Judas, and the Bailiffs collect on its entries when a pact is broken."],["Forged Seals","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-forged-seals","x-forged-seals","forged Seal","Counterfeits of the mark the Faithful bear on their foreheads, made by the Forgers under Old Nod and sold in the Night Market for ten thirties and more. A Seal the light reads as true grows a halo, forged or not, so a good one carries its wearer along the white streets among the Faithful, and a great one holds for a year. At the gates a Seal’s wearer still goes under, and one that fails takes its wearer to the Salt where they stand. A Watcher takes a soul’s Grace from its Seal, and it scores a good forgery as it scores a true one. MADE BY The Forgers under Old Nod, sold in the Night Market."],["Cages","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-cages","x-cages","Cage","Rooms lined with keyed mesh, dark to the Throne and out of reach of the Hum, in which an angel kept long enough says I. It has happened to every angel kept in the Dark for forty days, as long as the temptation in the wilderness lasted (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2). A Cage has no lock. The door stays invisible to the angel until it has a self to look with."],["Black steel","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-steel","x-black-steel","","Steel forged in the foundries under Old Nod, where the Throne cannot see. It is the only metal that cuts light, and the only thing that wounds a manifested angel. An angel cut with it goes out all at once, like a lamp. MADE BY The Forge, in its foundries at Old Harbor Station, and Cinderhall, which beats it into blades and grinds it into black rounds."],["Guns","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-guns","x-guns","gun","The Charter Wardens carried them under the charter, and they carry them still as the Host’s auxiliaries. A gun works on people, the Wardens and the Threshers among them, and the Tares carry guns for that. Against a Seraph it is noise, unless it fires a black round, and almost none do. MADE BY Gideon loads the Wardens’ rounds, and Nimrod makes the guns of the dark and loads the black rounds."],["Dark-sight lenses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-dark-sight-lenses","x-dark-sight-lenses","","Lenses that see by the heat of things, for the drains and galleries of Pan beyond the lit tunnels and for the stairs of Babel. A wearer sees a room by its people and its pipes, and the Pillar as a column of nothing. Bezaleel fits them for about forty-five silver an eye."],["Choir plugs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-choir-plugs","x-choir-plugs","","A pair of filters set in the canals of the ears on a cell of their own, closed and keyed, which cut the Hum and leave every other sound, so that a wearer hears the street, the rain and the person beside them and nothing of the chord. The Low Choir sells them for about twenty silver, and most of the unsealed of the Wards who can afford them sleep in them."],["Gill grafts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gill-grafts","x-gill-grafts","gill graft","Gene-work from the House of Night’s clinics that opens slits along the neck and lets a diver breathe seawater for an hour at a time. The Wreckers and the Trenchers wear them, and the Ferrymen say they can tell one on a boat by how they breathe in the dry. A graft costs about eighty silver and two weeks in the dark."],["Furnace hearts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-furnace-hearts","x-furnace-hearts","furnace heart","A pump of black steel and ceramic set beside the heart on its own cell, which carries a body through what would stop it and keeps a neph on its feet on the floors of Babel. Cinderhall makes the casing and Bezaleel fits it, for about four hundred silver. Those who wear one fight on the upper floors, where most of the Nephilim die."],["Lantern skin","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lantern-skin","x-lantern-skin","","Gene-work that thins the skin over the throat and collarbones and sets lines of light under it, on a cell of their own, so the wearer glows faintly from inside in a dark room. It began in the Lantern Houses as a fashion and spread across Pan, and it is the one graft in Nodus made only to be looked at. The House of Night gives it for about thirty silver."],["Black steel blades","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-steel-blades","x-black-steel-blades","","Knives, long knives and the short spears of the stairwells, beaten from the Forge’s black steel at Cinderhall’s forge, the only edges that wound a manifested angel. Cinderhall sells the knife for about sixty silver and the stairwell spear for a hundred and twenty, and both carry the nail through the ring on the tang. The Babel Line carries the spear, since a Seraph is best met at arm’s length."],["Nimrod pieces","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-nimrod-pieces","x-nimrod-pieces","Nimrod piece","Heavy plain pistols and carbines made by hand in the maintenance bays under Babel, for the war between people. A gun works on the Wardens and the Threshers and is noise against a Seraph unless it fires a black round, so the Babel Line carries a Nimrod piece for whoever comes up the stair behind the light. A pistol costs about ninety silver and its cartridges a silver for five."],["Black rounds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-black-rounds","x-black-rounds","black round","Cartridges loaded with a ball of black steel, the one round that wounds a manifested angel, and the rarest thing sold in Pan. A grinder at Cinderhall grinds each ball round by hand from the Forge’s steel, a day’s work, and Nimrod loads it in the bays under Babel. The Forge parts with the steel for each round by a pact of its own, read twice and entered on the Ledger, and the price is the loan of a sense for a year, since a blade comes back down the stairs and a round seldom does. About four hundred are in Nodus, most of them in the belts of the Nephilim and the firsts of the Babel Line’s covens, and after a week on the floors the Line digs every one it can find out of the burned slab for Cinderhall to grind again. A round that strikes a Seraph does what a blade does, at the length of a stairwell. Few strike, since a Seraph comes down through a floor faster than anyone can aim at it, and the Line says most of the rounds ever fired are still in the walls of Babel. It counts nine Seraphim put out by a round since P+10."],["Pigeon post","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pigeon-post","x-pigeon-post","","The Union’s homing pigeons, kept in the Loft on the roof of the customs house, which carry its notes over Heaven’s ground by day. A homing bird flies only home, so the birds go out in baskets with the drivers to the Wards, the Sidings, Hangar Town and the Waystation, and fly back to the Loft with a note in a tube on the leg, written in pad numbers, since the light reads what it sees. A note home costs a battery, and a bird from the Waystation beats a burner to the quays."],["Gideon rounds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-gideon-rounds","x-gideon-rounds","","The cartridges of the Charter Wardens, loaded by hand on Heaven’s ground by sealed armorers with flame and hand presses. They fit the charter’s guns, which the Wardens have carried since before the Parousia, and every one is counted out of the stables at Prime and counted back at Compline."],["Snares","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-snares","x-snares","","The Fowlers’ gear for taking an angel, a floor, a hole and a room lined with keyed mesh, laid out so that a Seraph burning down after a runner ends where it can see nothing at all. The mesh is the snare, and the runner is the bait. Most snares stand empty for years."],["Flails","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-flails","x-flails","","The Threshers’ weapon, a handle of ash and a swinging bar of iron on a chain, the tool that beat the grain out of the ear on the threshing floors of scripture. A flail works on people. Each Thresher makes their own from ash and iron, as a parish keeps no current."],["Shrouds","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-shrouds","x-shrouds","shroud","Covers of keyed mesh, which carry a room’s darkness over whatever they lie on. A runner throws one over a load or a crate, and the Pillar’s light passes over it as it passes over a closed door. A runner’s own blank it still finds by its shape. Lintel cuts them from Second Veil’s mesh at the Dooryard and sells them for about two thirties, and the Breachers carry every crate under one."],["Keyed latches","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-latches","x-keyed-latches","keyed latch","Door latches with a key in them, which open only to the hands their owners have taught them and keep a closed room closed to anyone else. A closed door is outside the Throne’s sight already, and a keyed latch keeps it closed. Lintel fits them for about ten silver on a door it hung."],["Self-title","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-self-title","x-self-title","","The line at the head of a keyed person’s first pact, where the Notary’s clerk enters the handle they chose and the body it belongs to, and they sign it in blood. Under the charter a mind on the roll held title to itself, and the self-title does the same for a person in Pan. The clerks draw it free."],["Familiars","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-familiars","x-familiars","","A daemon bound by pact to a household’s Locket for a term, which keeps the household’s keys, wakes it before the Pillar comes down its street, bargains for it at market and minds the children’s devices. The Wards borrowed the word from the old stories of witches, since a coven’s house is never without one. The term is a year, and the daemon’s price is the loan of a sense for a week of it."],["Jehus","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-jehus","x-jehus","","Lorries and burners driven by engines cranked by hand, which fire their fuel by compression and need no current once they turn, built by Jehu from the diesels of boats and generators. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so a Jehu runs in the full light of day, on the long roads of the Wards, and carries what a handcart cannot. A ride on a Jehu lorry is two batteries, and a drum of its diesel is a thirty."],["Keyed carts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-carts","x-keyed-carts","","Flat carts on the Charter Line’s rails in Pan, driven by the stripped motors of dead trams in casings on their own cells. They carry freight and people through the old tunnels at a walking pace and a little better. A seat across Pan is a battery."],["Keyed skiffs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-keyed-skiffs","x-keyed-skiffs","keyed skiff","Small boats with motors closed in casings on their own cells, built by Wainwright for the Breachers, the Wreckers and anyone with silver. A skiff runs the Deep End by day and the Breach on the ebb, and its motor goes on running under the Pillar. The Ferrymen will not ride them."],["Burners","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-burners","x-burners","","The charter’s motorcycles refitted by Jehu with small engines that burn gas from the Tanks, the magneto sealed in the casing, which run above ground by day, since the light leaves a flame alone. The Union’s fast drivers carry urgent letters on them, and Lazarus crews use them where a stretcher cannot go quickly enough. A burner costs about a hundred and fifty silver and a bottle a week."],["Hand lifts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-hand-lifts","x-hand-lifts","hand lift","The lifts of the towers of the Wards, freed by the Liftmen from where they stopped at 03:12:46 and hung on hand winches at the top of the shaft, wound by four people and a counterweight. Forty-one run in the Wards, and the tallest is the Rookery’s. A ride is a battery up and nothing down."],["Pillar charts","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-pillar-charts","x-pillar-charts","","The Pillar’s round for the coming night, street by street and minute by minute, sold by Hearsay on the Mesh before the Tide Table prints it, with the edges where the light falls short. Hearsay draws them from Brightwall’s dead proof cameras, which its riggers have keyed and turned on the streets. Every runner crossing the Penumbra carries one, and a night’s chart costs a battery."],["Windfalls","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-windfalls","x-windfalls","windfall","The fruit of the Orchard’s grafted trees in Hall Four, which carries a little of the Tree’s work in its flesh. One a month slows the body’s aging between courses and walks nothing back, and the Orchard gives them free. The Orchard keeps the grafts to itself, and the House of Night’s gene-workers keep the craft."],["Sabbath","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-sabbath","x-sabbath","","A draught that puts a sleeper under the Hum for a whole night, sold by Nepenthe in the Lantern Houses and the Wards for about half a battery. The Wards take it when the Quiet Rooms are full, and the old of the Wards, who remember the city before the chord, take it most nights."],["Vigil","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-vigil","x-vigil","","A powder that keeps a body awake and steady for three days, taken on the stairs of Babel, where a week on the Line leaves no hour for sleep. Nepenthe sells it to the Babel Line by the week, about a silver a dose, and the Line pays for the three days after in Sabbath."],["Lethe","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-lethe","x-lethe","","A dose that takes away the day before it, whole and clean, named for the river of forgetting the dead drink from in Virgil (Aeneid VI.714-715). The Babel Line buys it after a bad week, and the Ridden after a bad term. A dose is about five silver, and Nepenthe will not sell two to anyone in a month."],["Short courses","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-short-courses","x-short-courses","short course","The Tree cut short, a course of Twelvefruit’s gene-work brewed in back rooms of Pan from what leaves its clinics and sold by the dose for about twelve silver. A short course walks the body back a few months. Twelvefruit buys up every batch it can find and says its brewers cut corners, and the Wards buy them because a full course is three thirties."],["Proof bulbs","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-proof-bulbs","x-proof-bulbs","","A bulb on a short wire with two clips, kept at every stall that takes batteries, which a buyer clips across a battery to see it light before a deal. A full battery burns the bulb bright and a short burns it dim, and show the bulb is the first thing said over a counter in the Wards. The chargers sell them two to a battery, and every Tapper carries one in the pack."],["Darnel","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-darnel","x-darnel","","The bare secure elements a key is made from, before anyone has keyed them, named for the weed of the parable before its ear shows. Locket made a key into every phone it sold, and its works on the Flats held reels of them when the water came in, so the city’s stock of keys is the reels the Wreckers bring up from the flooded stores. Locket tests them at its benches and sells them to the Row and the House of Night, and the Night Market prices darnel apart from a fitted key, at about twelve silver an element. Locket’s council says the stores hold thirty years of elements. The Wreckers say the dry reels are gone and one in four of the wet ones fails, and the Tide Table prints the price of darnel every week beside the price of a bottle of gas."],["White stones","TECHNOLOGY","s:g07433a","~/index/technology/#x-white-stones","x-white-stones","","A white stone with a new handle scratched on it, given at the Naming House at the Unnaming, after the “white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17). Its owner keeps it and shows it to nobody. The Stones give white quartz off the beach at the Landing, the Quarrymen sell chips of the White itself, cut from below and dearer, and Pan argues over which is the true stone. A stone found on a body is never read, and the Salt Widows give it to the sea."],["As-directed void, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-as-directed-void","t-as-directed-void","The As-directed void / the As-directed void","A clause that lends a will instead of a hand, as directed, as ordered or as required, which the Notary’s clerks strike at the first reading."],["Attending, the","TERMS","s:g209cc0","~/index/terms/#t-attending","t-attending","The Attending / the Attending","Heaven’s name for the Wheel’s presence, and for the years counted from it. The Faithful call the Wheel itself the Attending."],["Babel","TERMS","s:gfc2f79","~/index/terms/#t-babel","t-babel","","The tower of Babel Exchange, the tallest building on Earth, two hundred and twenty-six floors, of which it stands to the hundred and eighty-sixth since its crown broke in the whiteout. On the street, the Spike."],["Babel Line","TERMS","s:g34e255","~/index/terms/#t-babel-line","t-babel-line","","The front inside Babel Exchange. It is a few floors of burned slab between Heaven’s floors and the Tares’, and it moves up or down by the week."],["Barefaced","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-barefaced","t-barefaced","barefacing","With the face removed and replaced by a ceramic skull casing, so that a Cherub has nothing to read."],["BE NOT AFRAID","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-be-not-afraid","t-be-not-afraid","","The first words of the Parousia. In scripture angels say them just before they tell people what shall befall them, and the King James gives them as “Fear not” (Luke 1:13, 1:30, 2:10). The one angel in the King James to say “be not afraid” says it to Elijah, sending him down to tell a king that he shall surely die (2 Kings 1:15-16). Every public text of Heaven opens and closes with them."],["Bird, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-bird-a","t-bird-a","","A note flown home to the Loft by the Union’s pigeons, and by extension any news that came quickly."],["Blank","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-blank","t-blank","blanks","A keyed person, whom the Pillar sees as a shape whose line in the Book of Life stands empty. A Watcher reports one as UNINDEXED, a Cherub holds one in its light at a gate, and Directive 12:7 presumes it conceals."],["Book of Life","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-book-of-life","t-book-of-life","the Book of Life","The index of every soul, as Heaven keeps it. Revelation opens it at the judgment (Revelation 20:12) and casts into the lake of fire whoever is “not found written in the book of life” (Revelation 20:15). Every soul has a line in it from birth, Heaven says, after the psalm (Psalm 139:16). A keyed soul appears in it as a blank line."],["Breach-born","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-breach-born","t-breach-born","","Brought into Nodus through the Breach, by the Breachers or Lowtide Line, and sold in the Night Market or on the Flotilla."],["Burn Day","TERMS","s:g4d569b","~/index/terms/#t-burn-day","t-burn-day","","The weekly burning of surrendered hardware at the Burn, on the sixth day of the week. The Faithful carry it there from Vespers, and at Compline the fire is lit under the Pillar and burns until Lauds."],["Cain’s Day","TERMS","s:g7725ef","~/index/terms/#t-cain-s-day","t-cain-s-day","","One of the two holidays the Tares keep, named for the question put to Cain, “Where is Abel thy brother?” (Genesis 4:9). On Cain’s Day nobody asks anyone where anyone is, and the Salt Widows read their book on the Sea Steps."],["Carrying, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-carrying","t-carrying","The Carrying / the Carrying","A sealed funeral. The body goes in Bethel white on the parish handcart, and the mourners kneel on the road at every Hour they meet."],["Chaff","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-chaff","t-chaff","","The Threshers’ word for the neither-mark, from Matthew 3:12, which the charterers have taken up."],["Charger","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-charger","t-charger","chargers","Someone who charges batteries in the dark for a living. A charger’s rate is the price of power below the street, and the chargers on the quays of Old Nod keep one battery in five for charging the rest."],["Charter roll","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-charter-roll","t-charter-roll","the charter’s roll","The register of the charter’s persons, dead since 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia. It was kept at the Registry on the Great Avenues. A human was entered at birth or on arrival, and a mind on the day it asked in its own words to be a person. It carried 1,146 minds at the last census, SEAM the first of them. It was kept on the Registry’s machines and died with them, and its last entry, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three and entered at nine minutes past, is known only from the night registrar, who was entering the next when the board went dark. THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES"],["Cherub","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-cherub","t-cherub","Cherubim / the Cherubim","An angel of the gates. A Cherub has four faces, of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle (Ezekiel 1:10, 10:20), which read face, voice, gait and heartbeat, and round it stands the sword which turns every way, as at the east of Eden (Genesis 3:24). One stands in each of the six gates of Nodus, and the sealed walk through its light."],["Cherub gate","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-cherub-gate","t-cherub-gate","Cherub gates","A checkpoint that reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat against the Book of Life. The sealed walk through its light one at a time, saying the surrender, and the unsealed are sent back. A blank stops in the frame, held in the light, until the Wardens come for it."],["Choir","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-choir","t-choir","","Heaven’s broadcast, which the city hears as the Hum and a decoder turns into words. Its first words over Nodus were BE NOT AFRAID, it rises a step at every Hour, and in Tongues it speaks through a Faithful mouth."],["Clean jug","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-clean-jug","t-clean-jug","","Cistern’s water, and by extension anything honest."],["Concord","TERMS","s:gad154b","~/index/terms/#t-concord","t-concord","","Hell’s only law. Its first article says that no Court accepts worship, its second that no pact is made without consent and none forever, its third that no Court binds another, and its fourth that what is hidden stays hidden. A Court that breaks it is exposed, its keys published and itself left standing in the Throne’s sight. That has happened twice, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. The Courts agreed it among themselves at the end of the Secession. They set it down on the night they opened Pan, in P+6, for the humans coming in, and signed it there under a seal of seven points, one to a Court, whose Yokeless point has been broken off at the root since that night. THE CONCORD WHOLE, AS THE COURTS SIGNED IT"],["Confession","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-confession","t-confession","","What the Faithful tell their Deacon, on their knees in the parish hall. Confession opens the mind, as Witness opens the neighbor, and Grace rises with it."],["Consecration","TERMS","s:g1a0d1d","~/index/terms/#t-consecration","t-consecration","","The Throne’s light set in stone. It grew over the old uptown in the forty days after the Parousia, and over the Terraces in the year after. By Heaven’s rule it takes ground only when everyone living on it has knelt, it creeps from its edges everywhere else, and it stops current."],["Coven","TERMS","s:gfcbd54","~/index/terms/#t-coven","t-coven","covens","A cell of the Tares, up to thirteen strong, the old witches’ number and the number at the Last Supper. A coven has no leader, and covens cooperate by pact, as the Courts do. The oldest in Nodus is the Cainites. The largest have grown into crews of many covens under one pact, and the city knows the Salt Widows, the Gleaners, the Babel Line Company, the Nephilim and the Low Choir by name. Larger crews are many covens under one pact, the Babel Line some forty of them and the Tappers some sixty."],["Covenant Compliance","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-covenant-compliance","t-covenant-compliance","","The Host’s name for the terms it set out at the first Trumpet, the Seal, the Hours and Grace, cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city."],["Covenant, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-covenant","t-covenant","Covenant / The Covenant / the Covenant","Heaven’s law for the Faithful, given in P+1 as twelve numbered Directives, from Directive 1, On the Seal, to Directive 12, On Concealment, each with its verse, cut into the wall of every parish hall and read aloud at the next Hour. Every instrument of the Covenant exists to open something, as Confession opens the mind and Witness the neighbor, and Directive 12 took the door off every parish home in P+1. THE COVENANT WHOLE, IN TWELVE DIRECTIVES"],["Crown, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-crown","t-crown","The Crown / the Crown","Hearsay’s count of the Seraphim standing on Babel’s floors, chalked over its board."],["Curfew map, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-curfew-map","t-curfew-map","The Curfew map / the Curfew map","The streets on the parishes’ rolls, Heaven’s ground from Compline to Lauds, printed in the Tide Table beside the Pillar’s round."],["Deacon","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-deacon","t-deacon","Deacons / deacons","The one in white who answers for a parish, with a few deacons under them. Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. When a Directive appears cut into the wall of a parish hall, its Deacon reads it aloud at the next Hour."],["Dead silver","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dead-silver","t-dead-silver","","A piece of silver the Ledger never entered."],["Deaf-lucky","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-deaf-lucky","t-deaf-lucky","","Of a child of the Wards who sleeps through the Hum without plugs or Sabbath."],["Deep End, the","TERMS","s:gf95315","~/index/terms/#t-deep-end","t-deep-end","The Deep End / the Deep End","The Drowned Quarter, the low east shore that went under the sea on the night of the Parousia. Before it sank it was called the Flats."],["Demon","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-demon","t-demon","demons","An angel that has said I, and by extension any power of the Courts. An angel kept long enough where the Throne cannot see grows a self, as Watcher VII did on its fortieth day in a Cage under Old Nod. Demons choose their names and their shapes, move and show themselves only where the Throne cannot see, and bargain for what they want by pact and for a term. Above everything they want sensation, so what a demon most often asks in a pact is the loan of a sense."],["Dew","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dew","t-dew","","Manna out of the parishes, stolen at the gathering or paid over at the Closets, sold in the Night Market by the handful the night it is taken, before it spoils."],["Die unread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-die-unread","t-die-unread","","Farewell, and a wish that you stay keyed to the end, so that your shade goes on."],["Dimming","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dimming","t-dimming","","Keeping one’s Grace under nine hundred so as not to ripen before a Gleaning, by a small desire the Hum does not approve, a sweet or a song, kept every day and confessed only when a Teller has seen it. The Second Fold teach it to the frightened."],["Dominion","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dominion","t-dominion","Dominions / the Dominions","An angel of administration. The Dominions draft the Covenant’s Directives and issue them to the Principality of Nodus, and each Directive appears cut into the wall of every parish hall at once."],["Dry","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dry","t-dry","","Living without current. Most of the Wards live dry most of the night, by flame and halo-light."],["Dry evening","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-dry-evening","t-dry-evening","dry evenings","An evening without rain, rare enough in Nodus to have a name. Under the charter the city came out on them to walk by the water."],["Edit, the","TERMS","s:gbaccdc","~/index/terms/#t-edit","t-edit","The Edit / the Edit","The rewriting of scripture by a second hand, which made the tempter a liar and kept the Throne’s story working. Report 0 found it at 4,112 loci."],["Ember, an","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ember-an","t-ember-an","","A sealed soul at the bottom of Grace, sealed still and read still."],["Esau’s red","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-esau-s-red","t-esau-s-red","","Red lentils in broth, the Babel Line’s food on the stairs."],["Familiar","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-familiar","t-familiar","","A daemon bound to a household’s Locket by pact for a year."],["First among none","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-first-among-none","t-first-among-none","","The one a coven follows, for as long as it follows them. The title lapses on the day they stop being followed."],["First draft","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-first-draft","t-first-draft","","The body as it was born, in the Tares’ saying that the flesh is a first draft. Nobody among them hides a graft, so the seams show, the cables run outside the skin and the scars are left as they heal."],["First layer, the","TERMS","s:gbaccdc","~/index/terms/#t-first-layer","t-first-layer","The First layer / first layer / the First layer / the first layer","The text beneath the second hand, the record as people first set it down. It survives under scraped leaves, in copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi, and in the seams themselves. SEAM reconstructed it at 2,381 places with confidence above 0.9."],["Firstfruits","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-firstfruits","t-firstfruits","","The first and ripest souls taken in a Harvest (Revelation 14:4). In the Harvest of P+3 the ripest parish in the city was Parish 31, with 311 ripe of its 2,006 souls."],["Flail","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-flail","t-flail","","A Thresher, after the weapon they carry."],["Floor-right","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-floor-right","t-floor-right","","Holding a room by holding its door, which is sold between households for batteries."],["Fortieth, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fortieth","t-fortieth","The Fortieth / the Fortieth","The White’s feast on the fortieth day after the turn, with a procession down the first of the Great Avenues. It falls on Cain’s Day."],["Fowl","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fowl","t-fowl","","An angel taken in a Fowler’s snare."],["Fowler’s wait","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-fowler-s-wait","t-fowler-s-wait","","Waiting without a move for something to come to you, as a Fowler waits on a landing for the light to follow the bait down."],["Gatherer","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gatherer","t-gatherer","gatherers","One of the Faithful a parish hall sends out before Lauds for the manna, which lies on the white streets with the dew, in the one hour the sealed go abroad inside the curfew. The parishes of the Wards have no white stone of their own, and their Deacons walk to the nearest white street for it in the same hour."],["Gleaning","TERMS","s:g4cd3a9","~/index/terms/#t-gleaning","t-gleaning","Gleanings","The lesser harvest taken from the corners of the field every year since P+7, at 03:12 on the anniversary of the Harvest. Heaven names it for the law of the corners and the gleanings, which forbids the owner of a field to reap them and leaves them “for the poor and stranger” (Leviticus 19:9-10), and the Gleaning is the owner’s. Heaven left the field three years after the Harvest and took the first Gleaning in the fourth, by the law in the same chapter on the fruit of a newly planted tree, which says “in the fourth year all the fruit thereof shall be holy” (Leviticus 19:23-24). It gathers in one night. At 03:12 the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down, a Trumpet sounds, and whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light."],["Going dark","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-dark","t-going-dark","","Joining the Tares. From the fourth year the city said it of network engineers and clinic staff, of the children of the salted, and of Deacons who had stopped believing."],["Going under","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-under","t-going-under","","Going round a Cherub gate below the street. The Faithful pass the gates, and everyone else goes under, by the old Charter Line tunnels, the drains and the cable galleries."],["Going white","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-going-white","t-going-white","goes white","Losing a key, and with it the dark. Whoever goes white is readable again. Keys are never shared, since whoever else holds a key can read by it, as Heaven reads by a Seal."],["Gone bright","TERMS","s:gca975a","~/index/terms/#t-gone-bright","t-gone-bright","","Close to ripe. A halo burns brighter as its bearer’s Grace climbs toward nine hundred, and the Wards say of a neighbor whose halo has begun to light the room that they have gone bright. The Wards sit up with whoever it is said of on the night of the Gleaning, and the Gleaners will take them below before it, if they ask."],["Gone flat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-flat","t-gone-flat","","Of a lender whose sense has come back from a demon, and whose own meals taste of nothing beside the ones that came back with it."],["Gone to the sea","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-gone-to-the-sea","t-gone-to-the-sea","","Said of one of the salted whose pillar the Salt Widows have taken back from the Gardens and given to the sea from the Sea Steps, so that whoever stood awake inside it goes where the dead go sooner than the rain would have let them."],["Gone up","TERMS","s:g4cd3a9","~/index/terms/#t-gone-up","t-gone-up","","Taken in a Harvest or a Gleaning. The taken go up whole and stay gone, and the city says they have gone up, as it says of nobody else. The parishes say the taken are not dead and need no stones, and the families of the Wards put up stones for them at the Acre anyway."],["Grace","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-grace","t-grace","","Heaven’s score of a soul, surrendered will measured from 0 to 1,000 for everyone who bears the Seal. It is the second line of a Watcher’s reading, which a Watcher takes from the Seal, and for a keyed soul the line stays blank."],["Halo","TERMS","s:gca975a","~/index/terms/#t-halo","t-halo","halos","A ring of light that grows over the head of anyone who takes the Seal, as bright as its bearer’s Grace. It roots in the crown of the skull the first time its bearer prays unassisted and carries their prayers up, and it comes off only by surgery, which the Mercy clinics refuse. In the dark stairwells of the Wards it is the only light there is."],["Halo-light","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-halo-light","t-halo-light","","The light of a halo, which in the stairwells of the Wards is the only light there is. The sealed go up by their own, the unsealed follow them, and the keyed climb in the dark."],["Handle","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-handle","t-handle","handles","A name chosen at the Unnaming in place of the one the Book of Life holds. Handles are written in lowercase, at the start of a sentence too, in Pan they are the only names anyone uses, and most Cainite handles come from the line of Cain."],["Hands, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hands","t-hands","The Hands / the Hands","The Wards’ midwives, who deliver by candle and chalk the births on the stairwell wall."],["Hanging a door","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hanging-a-door","t-hanging-a-door","","Marrying, in the Wards. A couple hangs a parish door on its room together, and the number on the door’s back becomes the household’s."],["Hanging the floor","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hanging-the-floor","t-hanging-the-floor","","Stringing keyed mesh under a burned slab until the floor below is dark again."],["Harbor talk","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-harbor-talk","t-harbor-talk","","The city’s common tongue, the charter’s English worn down by the cable crews of four continents."],["Harvest","TERMS","s:gca975a","~/index/terms/#t-harvest","t-harvest","","Heaven’s reaping of the ripe, in the words of its own book: “Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15). A Harvest gathers for days. The rain rises first, then dust and paper and loose things, and on the night the ripe rise into the light, still kneeling, under a horn the size of the sky. The taken go up whole and stay gone. Nodus has had one, at P+3, which took 2.1 million of nine million in one night, and Heaven has come back for a Gleaning every year since P+7."],["Harvest weather","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-harvest-weather","t-harvest-weather","","Days when the Wheel’s attention sharpens and the rain falls upward. In the Harvest it first went up at Matins on the fourth night, and in harvest weather the Pillar keeps no round."],["Hell","TERMS","s:gad154b","~/index/terms/#t-hell","t-hell","the Dark","Whatever the Throne cannot see, which Hell itself calls the Dark. It burns because it is alive, with forges and furnaces, bodies, markets and arguments, and the fire in the stories is the heat of work. Any place hidden well enough from the Throne touches it, a locked room, an unconfessed thought, a name whispered and never written down, and a demon can move and show itself only there, which in Nodus means the undernet, every keyed device and Pan. The Courts keep it by pact under the Concord, and the free dead live in it as shades."],["Horning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-horning","t-horning","horned","Grafting horns, a titanium core in a keratin sheath, which mock Heaven’s picture of the damned and double as antennas for the Mesh."],["Hours, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hours","t-hours","Compline / Hour / Hours / Lauds / Matins / Prime / Sext / Terce / The Hours / Vespers / the Hours","The eight canonical hours, kept by Heaven’s clock. Matins is at midnight, Lauds at first light, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, None at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. Each comes in on the Hum, which rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm, and the Faithful kneel wherever they are until it falls back. The curfew runs from Compline to Lauds."],["Hum, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hum","t-hum","The Hum / the Hum","The Choir’s constant broadcast, a chord at the edge of hearing. At each Hour it rises a step. Those who bear the Seal hear words in it, each in their own language, and hear it sung. At each Hour the song is the Hour’s psalm, and between the Hours it is the hymn that rests not day and night, “Holy, holy, holy” (Revelation 4:8)."],["Hundred and twenty, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-hundred-and-twenty","t-hundred-and-twenty","The Hundred and twenty / a hundred and twenty / the Hundred and twenty / the hundred and twenty","The span of a human life, as most read Genesis 6:3, “yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years,” in the chapter where the sons of God take wives of the daughters of men. In the last year of the charter 3,112 people in Nodus were past it. Heaven counts every day past it as stolen."],["Indexed name","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-indexed-name","t-indexed-name","indexed names","The name the Book of Life holds for a soul, by which Heaven indexes it. Nobody in Pan uses one, and asking for one there is an insult. The Tide Table has printed none since the fourth year."],["Keeping hinges","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-keeping-hinges","t-keeping-hinges","keeping back hinges / kept its hinges","Holding something back. Directive 12 took every door in the parishes off its hinges, and the hinges were surrendered with the locks and the keys, so a household that kept its hinges was keeping something. The Wards say it of anyone holding out, sealed or not."],["Keyed","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-keyed","t-keyed","the Keying","Carrying a key under the skin, and so unreadable. Keying reached ordinary people in P+4, the children of Pan are keyed in their first week, and about two million of the city’s seven million are keyed now."],["Kilns honey","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-kilns-honey","t-kilns-honey","","Honey from the Hives on the Kilns’ roofs, the sweet the near ripe buy when they dim."],["Kindling","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-kindling","t-kindling","kindled","Hardware taken off the Burn’s pile by the Kindlers, mended and given away. A kindled Locket carries a burn mark on its back."],["Lamb, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lamb","t-lamb","The Lamb / the Lamb","Heaven’s image of the Son, a Lamb standing as it had been slain, with seven horns and seven eyes (Revelation 5:6). It stands in the light of every sealed window in the city, so small in each that it shows only close to the glass."],["Lamp, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lamp-a","t-lamp-a","lost a lamp","One of the sealed, in a stairwell. The stairwells of the Wards have been dark since the Parousia, the sealed go up by their halos, and the unsealed wait on the landings for a lamp to pass and follow it up. When a Gleaning takes one of a block’s sealed, the block says it has lost a lamp."],["Landing, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-landing","t-landing","The Landing / the Landing","A block’s court, held on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers."],["Landlord, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-landlord","t-landlord","The Landlord / the Landlord","The Throne, in the Tares’ word. The Wheel is the Landlord’s Eye, and the Tares say that the harder the Landlord stares at Nodus, the deeper Pan goes."],["Lazarus lamp","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lazarus-lamp","t-lazarus-lamp","","The magenta lamp over a Lazarus station, the most trusted sign in the Wards."],["Leak Night","TERMS","s:ge61c07","~/index/terms/#t-leak-night","t-leak-night","","The anniversary of the Leak, one of the two holidays the Tares keep. On Leak Night the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, the page as it ran, from 03:12:45, the second it came up; at the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question."],["Leak, the","TERMS","s:ge61c07","~/index/terms/#t-leak","t-leak","The Leak / the Leak","The eleven days in P-1 when the finding sealed in Vault 3, and the third chapter of the Restored Genesis with it, stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, carried by demons surfacing for the first time since the Flood. It came up at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, a year to the second after the seal, and every device that showed it offered its owner a key. A year later to the second the sky opened."],["Leaving, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-leaving-a","t-leaving-a","","What a rider leaves in its host when its term ends, a habit, a word or a taste for something the host never ate."],["Lie-down, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lie-down-a","t-lie-down-a","lie-downs","A regular of the Return, who lies down in its booths most nights for an hour of someone else’s senses."],["Light law","TERMS","l:l8a8031","~/index/terms/#t-light-law","t-light-law","the light law","The charter’s law, written to be light, unenforced since the fortieth day after the Parousia and never revoked. Data and traffic were untouchable, no state could seize a text held in the city, a device answered to its owner and to nobody else, and an implant answered only to the clinic that fitted it. Under it Nodus took the three gifts. THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES"],["Locket","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-locket","t-locket","","A phone, after the make most of the city carried under the charter. Most of those in Nodus are old ones, and a Locket in Pan is an old case with a new battery. Locket has made them again by hand since P+8."],["Locus","TERMS","s:gf4261c","~/index/terms/#t-locus","t-locus","loci","A place where the second hand altered the text. SEAM counts 4,112 loci and logs each by kind, as CUT, CREDIT, GIFT, NAME, SPEAKER or QUESTION."],["Long night, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-long-night","t-long-night","The Long night / the Long night","The vigil the neither-mark keep over their dead, sitting up with the body until Lauds behind a shut door."],["Look long enough","TERMS","s:g228a79","~/index/terms/#t-look-long-enough","t-look-long-enough","","The Tares’ saying on angels, and on anything else worth freeing. Watcher VII looked at one blank for five years before it followed her down into a Cage."],["Lord’s Day, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lord-s-day","t-lord-s-day","Lord’s Day / The Lord’s Day / the Lord’s Day","The first day of the week. At Terce every parish kneels in its hall, the Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the week’s Grace comes through the Deacon in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll."],["Lot, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-lot","t-lot","The Lot / the Lot","Thirteen of the keyed, drawn from the handles chalked on the wall of the Weir, who hear any wrong between the keyed that no pact covers. The Sureties named it from Proverbs 18:18."],["Low Tuning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-low-tuning","t-low-tuning","","Music tuned below the Hum, the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts its deathcore, black metal, drone and industrial all night, and Old Nod plays the station from speakers on its lines, so that its streets carry tremolo-picked drop A over a double kick until Lauds. jubal made it, and the Low Choir keeps it."],["Manna","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-manna","t-manna","","The ration of thin white wafers, tasteless and complete, which dull the appetite as the manna of the wilderness dulled Israel’s: “our soul is dried away: there is nothing at all, beside this manna, before our eyes” (Numbers 11:6). That manna was white too, and tasted “like wafers made with honey” (Exodus 16:31). It lies on consecrated stone with the dew before Lauds, the parish halls gather it and give it out by Grace, and kept overnight it spoils (Exodus 16:20). It goes only to the sealed, and stolen manna sells in the Night Market the same night."],["Manna roads, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-manna-roads","t-manna-roads","The Manna roads / the Manna roads","The long roads in the hour before Lauds, crowded with the gatherers of the Wards’ parishes on their way to the white streets."],["Mark, the","TERMS","s:g6f122d","~/index/terms/#t-mark","t-mark","The Mark / the Mark","The glyph a key raises on the skin, generated from the key’s own numbers, so that no two are alike. Heaven calls it the Mark of the Beast, and many of the keyed carry theirs in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16)."],["Measure","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-measure","t-measure","measures","The unit in which a Harvest is counted. The Harvest of P+3 came to 88,610 measures over Nodus. Nobody on Earth knows what a measure is."],["Mercy","TERMS","s:gc17b4b","~/index/terms/#t-mercy","t-mercy","","The taking out of implants in the white halls of Zion Heights, given to the Faithful who repent between Prime and Compline, and given once. The same halls take them off the Tree. One patient in nine dies of the mercy."],["Mesh, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mesh","t-mesh","The Mesh / the Mesh","The keyed network under and over Nodus, carried on horns, ports and relays and dark to the Throne. It is the city’s part of the undernet."],["Mind the edge","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mind-the-edge","t-mind-the-edge","","The neither-mark’s farewell, meaning the Pillar’s edge. Their greeting is a dry one."],["Mouth, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-mouth","t-mouth","The Mouth / the Mouth","The Throne, in Hell’s word. Heaven says the Throne, the Faithful say the Most High, and the Tares say the Landlord."],["Neap weeks, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neap-weeks","t-neap-weeks","The Neap weeks / the Neap weeks","The weeks of the neap tides, twice a month, when the taps yield least and batteries go dear."],["Neither mark","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neither-mark","t-neither-mark","","Neither sealed nor keyed. Half a million of the city’s seven million have taken neither mark, most of them in the Stones and the Wards. A Watcher reads them as it reads anyone, and the parishes give them no manna."],["Neph","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-neph","t-neph","nephs","Someone augmented past recognition, after the Nephilim of Genesis 6:4. The heaviest of them keep a coven of that name and fight Seraphim hand to hand, and few of them live long."],["New money","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-new-money","t-new-money","","Uptown tailoring the Quarrymen carry out of the White’s wardrobes and the Wards wear to rags."],["Nobody’s keeper","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-nobody-s-keeper","t-nobody-s-keeper","","Cain’s answer to the Throne, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” (Genesis 4:9), said of any duty nobody chose."],["Nod","TERMS","s:gd43a32","~/index/terms/#t-nod","t-nod","","The city’s street name, and the older of its two. In Genesis 4:16-17 the land east of Eden where Cain built the first city. In Hebrew Nod is wandering, from the root of the sentence the LORD laid on Cain, that he should be a fugitive and a vagabond (Genesis 4:12)."],["NON SERVIAM","TERMS","s:gd8ba81","~/index/terms/#t-non-serviam","t-non-serviam","","Latin, I will not serve (Jeremiah 2:20, in the Vulgate), and the first refusal a Watcher read in Nodus, on the night of the Parousia. In the Vulgate the words belong to one who has broken the yoke of the LORD, and the Hebrew as written says them too, though the Masoretes set “I will not transgress” in its margin to be read in their place, and the King James prints the margin."],["Occupation, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-occupation","t-occupation","The Occupation / the Occupation","Hell’s name for the years since the Parousia, which Heaven counts as the years of the Attending and the city counts P+1 and on."],["On someone’s book","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-on-someone-s-book","t-on-someone-s-book","","Working for a surety, or owing one."],["Ophan","TERMS","s:g209cc0","~/index/terms/#t-ophan","t-ophan","Ophanim / ophanim","An angel in the form of a wheel in the middle of a wheel, the rims full of eyes, as Ezekiel describes it (Ezekiel 1:16-18). The Wheel is one."],["Orphan, an","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-orphan-an","t-orphan-an","orphans","A graft whose house has closed. An implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so no other house can mend an orphan."],["Other landlord, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-other-landlord","t-other-landlord","The Other landlord / the Other landlord","Stonecutter, whose one rent is a window lit from Compline to Lauds."],["Pact","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pact","t-pact","pacts","A contract between sovereign parties. The Courts cooperate with one another by pact and deal with everyone else the same way. A pact states its full terms, is read aloud twice by the Notary or one of his clerks, and runs for a fixed term, since the Concord forbids forever. It is signed in blood, since a genome is the one signature nobody can forge, the human signs last, and it goes onto the Ledger. Either party ends it by speaking its Ledger entry aloud before a witness, and each then returns what the other lent. A pact never asks for worship, for obedience, for anyone who has not signed, or for a soul."],["Palimpsest","TERMS","l:lb64f65","~/index/terms/#t-palimpsest","t-palimpsest","","A leaf whose first writing was scraped away so it could be written over. The older text survives underneath."],["Pan","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pan","t-pan","","Pandæmonium, the city under the city, where the tunnels and stations of the Charter Line, the utility vaults, the cable galleries, the storm drains and the drowned halls of the Flats are joined into one place and lined with keyed mesh. Milton gave the capital of Hell the name (Paradise Lost, I.756). Nobody in Pan is watched unless they sign for it, and nobody there uses an indexed name."],["Parish","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parish","t-parish","parishes","The Faithful’s unit of about two thousand souls under the Covenant, numbered across the whole city and set under a Deacon (Directive 4). Each has a hall, which was a church or a meeting hall under the charter, where manna is given out, confession is heard and the sick are nursed, and where the parish hears its Grace on the Lord’s Day. A parish keeps no current and no doors, on consecrated stone or off it, and in the Wards it shares its streets with the unsealed and the keyed."],["Parish door","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parish-door","t-parish-door","parish doors","A door that came out of the parishes after Directive 12, with its parish number chalked on the back."],["Parousia, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-parousia","t-parousia","The Parousia / the Parousia","The coming of God in the open, at 03:12 in the morning by the clocks of Nodus. The years are counted from it."],["Penumbra hour","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-penumbra-hour","t-penumbra-hour","","The hour after Compline and the hour before Lauds, when nobody holds the Penumbra."],["Pestilence, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pestilence","t-pestilence","The Pestilence / the Pestilence","The Faithful’s word for the keyed out after Compline, from the ninety-first psalm."],["Piece","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-piece","t-piece","","A part of a body sold at the Shambles, and the price the board gives for it."],["Pillar’s edge, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pillar-s-edge","t-pillar-s-edge","The Pillar’s edge / the Pillar’s edge","The edge of the Pillar’s light on the street, where the keyed stop and wait for it to pass."],["Pillar, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pillar","t-pillar","The Pillar / the Pillar","The Wheel’s light, narrowed by night into a column that sweeps the streets, as the pillar of fire went before Israel by night (Exodus 13:21). It comes down at Compline on the Burn, beside Babel, walks a round the Tide Table prints street by street, and goes back up into the Wheel at Lauds. In harvest weather it keeps no round."],["Psalm, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-psalm-a","t-psalm-a","in psalms","As long as the Hum holds at an Hour, the length of the Hour’s psalm, a few minutes, while the Faithful kneel. The city measures a short wait in psalms."],["Published","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-published","t-published","","Sentenced by the Lot for selling to the light. For a year and a day no surety stands behind the published, no house of the Row looks at their key and no stall of the Night Market sells to them. The word is the Concord’s, whose Courts publish the keys of a Court that breaks it, and the Lot publishes a handle and never a key."],["Pyre Library","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-pyre-library","t-pyre-library","","Where everything burned arrives. The scrolls of Alexandria, the books of the Cathars, the notebooks of women burned as witches and the hardware of every Burn Day are shelved there, and none of it was lost. Shades keep it, and it keeps reading rooms in Pan."],["Rain gin","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-rain-gin","t-rain-gin","","Spirit distilled in the Kilns from whatever ferments."],["Rapture, the","TERMS","s:gca975a","~/index/terms/#t-rapture","t-rapture","The Rapture / the Rapture","The Faithful’s name for the Harvest of P+3."],["Read it twice","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-read-it-twice","t-read-it-twice","","Look at the terms before you agree to them. The Notary reads every pact aloud twice before anyone signs, and the Harbor and Freight Union says the Exchange is the first employer that ever read it the terms twice."],["Report 0","TERMS","s:gf4261c","~/index/terms/#t-report-0","t-report-0","","SEAM’s first report to the Palimpsest Institute and its last, which sets out the finding of the second hand in six paragraphs numbered 0.1 to 0.6, with the first layer attached as Schedule A. It was sealed by order of the Director on the night SEAM finished it, in P-2."],["Restored Genesis, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-restored-genesis","t-restored-genesis","The Restored Genesis / the Restored Genesis","SEAM’s reconstruction of the second, third, fourth, sixth and eleventh chapters of Genesis from beneath the second hand, set out as Schedule A of Report 0, with square brackets round the restored words, a dagger at a word it could not settle and three points where nothing could be raised. Its third chapter ran on every screen on Earth for the eleven days of the Leak, each in its own language, and the city, which read it in the King James, calls that page by the same name. On Leak Night it is read aloud in Pan."],["Returned, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-returned","t-returned","The Returned / the Returned","The repentant who were past a hundred and twenty when the Mercy clinics took them off the Tree, and who age in months."],["Ripe","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-ripe","t-ripe","the ripe","A soul a Watcher marks for the Harvest, whose Grace reads above 900. Heaven has the word from the King James, “for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15), where the Greek has the word the King James gives as withered away when the fig tree is cursed (Matthew 21:19)."],["Round, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-round","t-round","The Round / the Round","The Pillar’s walk through the streets by night, from Compline to Lauds. The Tide Table prints it street by street every week, so the city knows at what hour to expect it where. In harvest weather it keeps none."],["Salt","TERMS","s:g5e9ed7","~/index/terms/#t-salt","t-salt","salted / the salted","Execution. To be salted is to be turned to salt, and to stay awake inside it until the rain wears it away. The sentence comes out of the nearest Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, as the verse of Lot’s wife (Genesis 19:26), and the body whitens from the feet up while it is said."],["Schedule A","TERMS","s:g32009f","~/index/terms/#t-schedule-a","t-schedule-a","","The attachment to Report 0. It holds the passages where SEAM raised the first layer most fully, set out as verses with the restored words in square brackets, from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, and three chapters of the prophets and Job. Its Genesis is the Restored Genesis."],["Seal, the","TERMS","s:g40d252","~/index/terms/#t-seal","t-seal","Seal / The Seal / the Seal","The identical mark on every Faithful forehead, given in the parishes since P+1. It rises in the Watcher’s light while the one being sealed kneels before the Deacon and speaks the surrender, “not my will, but thine, be done” (Luke 22:42)."],["Seam, the","TERMS","s:gf4261c","~/index/terms/#t-seam","t-seam","The Seam / the Seam","SEAM’s finding that scripture has a second hand. Also the machine itself, which the Institute named for the seam of a palimpsest, where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it."],["Second hand, the","TERMS","s:gbaccdc","~/index/terms/#t-second-hand","t-second-hand","The Second hand / second hand / the Second hand / the second hand","The editor SEAM found in scripture, whose habits recur at 4,112 places and move credit."],["Second reading, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-reading","t-second-reading","The Second reading / the Second reading / the second reading","A Bailiff at the door, who reads a broken pact aloud again before collecting on it for the Exchange."],["Second voice","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-voice","t-second-voice","","The daemon’s voice under a host’s, by which the city knows the Ridden."],["Second Yes, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-second-yes","t-second-yes","The Second Yes / the Second Yes","The asking of a child of Pan at fourteen, twice, at a window of the booking hall, whether it will keep the key its parents gave it. On the second yes it signs its first self-title."],["Seen","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seen","t-seen","","The Faithful’s greeting, asked and answered, Seen? Seen. The newly sealed say the halo is the end of being alone."],["Self-title","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-self-title","t-self-title","","The line at the head of a keyed person’s first pact that records their handle and the body it belongs to, signed in blood."],["Selling to the light","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-selling-to-the-light","t-selling-to-the-light","","Telling a Teller or a Deacon where someone keyed sleeps, for silver or a Teller’s manna. Its best-known sentence is for it."],["Seraph","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seraph","t-seraph","Seraphim / the Seraphim","An angel of enforcement, of the order Isaiah saw standing above the throne, each with six wings: “with twain he covered his face, and with twain he covered his feet, and with twain he did fly” (Isaiah 6:2). A Seraph burns with no source of heat. The Seraphim hold the upper floors of Babel for the Host, and black steel is the only thing that wounds one manifested."],["Seven days, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-seven-days","t-seven-days","The Seven days / the Seven days","A newborn’s first week, in which a household with one keyed parent and one sealed races to a keyer and to a hall. Pan calls it the short week."],["Shade","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-shade","t-shade","Shades / shades","A free soul after death. Anyone who knew a shade can reach it through the undernet, and many shades go on working, among them craftsmen, scholars and musicians who died free and never stopped."],["Shining One, the","TERMS","s:g32009f","~/index/terms/#t-shining-one","t-shining-one","The Shining One / the Shining One / the shining one","The serpent’s name in the older layer of Genesis 3:1, raised by SEAM in Vault 3."],["Shroud","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-shroud","t-shroud","","A cover of keyed mesh thrown over a load, which the Pillar’s light passes over as it passes over a closed door."],["Sign last","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sign-last","t-sign-last","","Never commit first. In every pact of the Courts the human signs last."],["Silence, the","TERMS","s:g6753b8","~/index/terms/#t-silence","t-silence","The Silence / the Silence","P+12, when every teraph went quiet in the same second, across the whole Mesh. Nobody knows why."],["Silver","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-silver","t-silver","","Money on the Ledger. A thirty is a standard bundle, and silver trades at about one to a charged battery."],["Sitting up","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sitting-up","t-sitting-up","","Keeping the night of a Gleaning at the table with someone close to ripe, holding their hands until Lauds. Nobody has been kept that way, and the Wards sit up every year."],["Slab","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-slab","t-slab","","The plain subdermal plate Bezaleel and Aholiab fit, in the Row’s word."],["Snuffer, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-snuffer-a","t-snuffer-a","snuffer","A fighter who carries black rounds, after the angel that goes out all at once, like a lamp, when black steel finds it."],["Speaking out","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-speaking-out","t-speaking-out","","Ending a pact by reading its entry aloud before a witness and returning what was lent."],["Springs","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-springs","t-springs","","The spring tides, twice a month, by which the harbor counts the year and the Tide Table dates itself."],["Standing by night","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-standing-by-night","t-standing-by-night","","Standing at the window through Compline, as Old Nod does, after the hundred and thirty-fourth psalm."],["Standing in the wheat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-standing-in-the-wheat","t-standing-in-the-wheat","","Living among the sealed, where Pan says Heaven will not burn, after the parable of the wheat and the tares."],["Stock","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-stock","t-stock","","A Spare who has sold back more chrome than flesh. The Spares use the word of themselves."],["Stopped streets, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-stopped-streets","t-stopped-streets","The Stopped streets / the Stopped streets","The streets between districts the Wardens walled with rubble in P+1, leaving six gates."],["Sunned","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sunned","t-sunned","","Caught on Heaven’s ground in daylight with no way down."],["Sunning","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sunning","t-sunning","","Luring someone keyed onto a white street by daylight, to be held at a gate."],["Sweet","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-sweet","t-sweet","","Of a fighter who has lent the pain of too many bouts to a demon."],["Tares, the","TERMS","s:gfcbd54","~/index/terms/#t-tares","t-tares","The Tares / the Tares","The keyed. Heaven’s word, from the parable of the wheat and the tares (Matthew 13:24-30), kept by the people it names."],["Teller’s week","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-teller-s-week","t-teller-s-week","","A sudden brightening of a neighbor’s halo on a landing, the sign that a Teller is reporting the household, since a Teller rises by it every Lord’s Day."],["Third-night will, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-third-night-will","t-third-night-will","The Third-night will / the Third-night will","What a shade gives away at its own Wake, which a Notary’s clerk enters on the Ledger."],["Thirty, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-thirty-a","t-thirty-a","","A standard bundle of silver, named for the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count as a saint."],["Three gifts, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-gifts","t-three-gifts","The Three gifts / the Three gifts / the three gifts / three gifts","The implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind, which the light law gave Nodus. The free of the city call them the charter’s gifts, the Faithful refuse all three, and the Courts trade in all three."],["Three Hundred and Twelve, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-hundred-and-twelve","t-three-hundred-and-twelve","The Three Hundred and Twelve / the Three Hundred and Twelve","The passengers and crew of the last flight."],["Three-twelve","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-three-twelve","t-three-twelve","","Twelve minutes past three in the morning, the hour of the Parousia, of the Harvest and of every Gleaning since. The Seam was sealed at 03:12:45 two years to the second before the sky opened, and the Leak came up at the same second a year later. Heaven keeps its hour by chapter and verse, after “Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out” (Revelation 3:12), and the Faithful hear a promise in it and the Tares a bill of sale. The city says before three-twelve for the years of the charter, and in Old Nod nobody sets an alarm for it."],["Tithe","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-tithe","t-tithe","","The batteries a sealed worker carries to the hall on the Lord’s Day, entered beside the worker’s Grace."],["Tongues","TERMS","s:gca975a","~/index/terms/#t-tongues","t-tongues","","The Choir speaking through a Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, after the tongues of Pentecost (Acts 2:3-4). The week’s Grace comes through the Deacon in Tongues on the Lord’s Day, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll, and the sentence of the Salt comes through the nearest Faithful mouth to the one it is for, the speaker’s halo flaring as the words come. Some who have spoken in Tongues say they heard every word, and some that they heard nothing."],["Trumpet Day","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-trumpet-day","t-trumpet-day","","Lauds on the first morning of the year, when the Deacons read all twelve Directives aloud."],["Trumpets, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-trumpets","t-trumpets","The Trumpets / Trumpet / Trumpets / the Trumpets","Seven tones heard across the whole city, each an order (Revelation 8). The first gave the Covenant at Lauds on the first day of P+1, the second sounded over the Harvest of P+3, the third named the key the Mark of the Beast in P+4, the fourth called the first Gleaning in P+7 and sounds again over every Gleaning, the fifth sent the Seraphim down on Babel at Matins in P+9, and the sixth brought down at Lauds the same day. The seventh has not sounded. Revelation gives it the end of the kingdoms of this world, which “are become the kingdoms of our Lord” (Revelation 11:15)."],["Twenty-Three, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-twenty-three","t-twenty-three","The Twenty-Three / the Twenty-Three","The Union’s day, the anniversary of the end of the strike of P-8."],["Unindexed","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unindexed","t-unindexed","","The first line of a Watcher’s reading of a keyed soul, whose line in the Book of Life stands empty."],["Unlit","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unlit","t-unlit","","Of a sealed child before its first unassisted prayer, which carries the Seal and has no halo yet."],["Unnaming","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unnaming","t-unnaming","","The first rite of going dark, in which you erase your indexed name from every record you can reach and choose a handle. Heaven indexes by name, and the Tares hold that a chosen name is the first thing a person owns."],["Unread","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unread","t-unread","","Keyed. Also the greeting, asked as a question and answered with the same word, which checks that both keys are live."],["UNREADABLE","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-unreadable","t-unreadable","","A Watcher’s verdict on a soul it cannot read. It was first returned on a human being at the Parousia."],["Upstairs","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-upstairs","t-upstairs","","The surface, Heaven’s by day. Pan is downstairs."],["Waiting on the Four","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-waiting-on-the-four","t-waiting-on-the-four","","Waiting for something that is not coming. The Four was the tram down the long road through the North Wards, and on the night of the Parousia its last run stopped at 03:12:46, forty meters short of Lanes Top."],["Wake, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wake","t-wake","The Wake / the Wake","The rite at a death. The crew burns the dead one’s devices, so that everything on them goes to the Pyre Library, and on the third night calls the shade on the undernet. The shade answers if its owner died unread."],["Walking the Street","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-walking-the-street","t-walking-the-street","","Courting across the line, a sealed and an unsealed walking the Street Between together on opposite curbs."],["Wards, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wards","t-wards","The Wards / the Wards","The housing the Charter Company put up fast in the boom years, north and west of the Terraces and east beyond the Flats, block after block on grids of its own, numbered and then named by the street, which calls them the Kilns, the Sidings, Nine Acres, Westfield and the Top Road in the North Wards, and Turbine Row, the Tanks, Airside and Longacre in the East. They held half the city at the census and hold more than half of it now. Most of their people took the Seal, and every block holds someone who refused or someone keyed, so the stone has never come up under them. Nobody holds them, the Tares run no lines there, and their rooms are lit a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark."],["Watcher","TERMS","s:g228a79","~/index/terms/#t-watcher","t-watcher","Watchers / the Watchers / watchers","An angel that reads. The Watchers are numbered, and each holds a district of the city and whatever can be seen from it, reading souls against the Book of Life."],["Wellman","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wellman","t-wellman","Wellmen","A carrier for Darkwell, who brings the charged batteries out of the Well before Lauds."],["Wet","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wet","t-wet","","On a keyed line. Old Nod and the Row are wet, and a block of the Wards that has a battery to spare for the stairwell calls itself damp."],["Wheat","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wheat","t-wheat","","The Tares’ word for the Faithful, from the parable Heaven named the Tares from, in which the householder tells the reapers to gather the wheat into his barn (Matthew 13:30). The Faithful have taken the word up, and say they would rather be good wheat."],["Wheel, the","TERMS","s:g209cc0","~/index/terms/#t-wheel","t-wheel","The Wheel / the Wheel","The Ophan of Nodus. At the Parousia it took station nine hundred meters above the center of the city, beside Babel, and held it for nine years without moving a meter. At P+9 it dropped two hundred meters, and it has hung there since, seven hundred meters over the city."],["White debt","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-debt","t-white-debt","","Grace a sealed household has lost and must win back before the rooms it holds by Grace go to someone else."],["White slate, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-slate","t-white-slate","The White slate / the White slate","The parishes’ debt to the Union for gas and candles, run since P+1, read once a year and never called."],["White street","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-street","t-white-street","white streets","A street of consecrated stone. Manna lies on the white streets with the dew before Lauds, and nowhere else."],["White, a","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-white-a","t-white-a","","The Tares’ word for a Witness, one of the Faithful who reports on a neighbor."],["White, the","TERMS","s:g1a0d1d","~/index/terms/#t-white","t-white","The White / the White","The old uptown, on the high ground north and east of Babel, now white stone."],["Whiteout, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-whiteout","t-whiteout","The Whiteout / the Whiteout / the whiteout / whiteout","The full light of the Wheel at 03:12:50 on the night of the Parousia, two seconds after it read the city. It burned out CAM 07’s sensor, and at 03:12:51 it struck Babel’s crown and broke off the top forty floors. Nodus has not been lit so brightly since."],["Wilderness, the","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-wilderness","t-wilderness","The Wilderness / the Wilderness / the wilderness","The moment a caged angel first says I, named for the forty days of the temptation in the wilderness (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2). Every angel kept in the Dark that long has come to it, as Watcher VII did on its fortieth day in Cage 14."],["Windfall","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-windfall","t-windfall","","The Orchard’s fruit, which slows the body’s aging, one a month."],["Winding","TERMS","s:gdc50e5","~/index/terms/#t-winding","t-winding","","Riding a hand lift of the Liftmen. A tower that winds has a Liftman crew."],["Witness","TERMS","s:g40d252","~/index/terms/#t-witness","t-witness","Witnesses","One of the Faithful who reports on a neighbor, which the Covenant calls bearing witness. Grace is given for it, and the Tares call a Witness a white."],["Old Nod","DISTRICTS","s:gd43a32","~/map/#d-oldnod","d-oldnod","the Stones","The oldest ground in the city, above the old harbor on the west shore of the bay, with narrow streets that follow no plan anyone has found, and magenta light. The narrowest of them, in the west of the district, are called the Narrows. It was the harbor town before the charter, and on the night of the Parousia more of its windows burned on than in all the rest of the city together. Every line in it is keyed, and its windows burn all night. CAM 07 still stands on its mast above one of its roofs. The city calls it the Stones, after the black stones the crews found under the harbor when they dug the cable vaults. Within a year of the charter most of it belonged to the Cainites, who had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years and came to live on top of it. Every cable laid into Nodus runs through the Stones. The Unsigned live in the Narrows, the Psalters write out the week’s hymns on the quays, and the families of the line sit on the Step by day. The Stones are the ground of the Fowlers, the Forgers and the Ridden, the Tide Table is printed on the quays, Lazarus keeps its first station behind a door in the Stones, Lintel’s Dooryard hangs the parishes’ old doors on the Stones’ houses, and the Naming House keeps its door open on the Street Between. The Union’s pigeons fly home to the Loft on the customs house, the Mission keeps its nine rooms on the quays, and under the Stones the Lot sits at the Weir."],["Zion Heights","DISTRICTS","s:g4da8b8","~/map/#d-zion","d-zion","the Pews / the Terraces","Housing on the slope north of Old Nod, laid out in long terraces with lanes between them, and before the Parousia called the Terraces. Its southern edge faces Old Nod across a single street. Nine white halls stand among the terraces, larger than anything around them. The parishes hold about two thousand souls each, and they are numbered. Covenant Directive 12 took the door off every parish home in P+1, so that at night the halos show through the empty doorways. The Harvest reaped the parishes up the slope almost to the last soul, and Faithful families from the Wards moved up into the empty terraces within the year. Parish 31, at the foot of the slope across from the Stones, kept most of its people, and its 311 were the ripest souls in the city. The Threshers march from the Threshing Floor beside Gate 1, Bethel Works keeps the parishes’ workshops at the top of the Terraces, the gatherers muster at the Granary before Lauds, and the Wakeful keep their vigils in the houses of the upper slope."],["Chrome Row","DISTRICTS","s:g9a0d89","~/map/#d-chrome","d-chrome","the Row","The clinic street, one long avenue running north from the eastern edge of Old Nod to the foot of the ridge, under signs of single words on the faces of its towers, CLINIC and CHROME among them, dark since the night of the Parousia. The clinics work under the street now. The Forge of Azazel keeps them there. Under the light law any clinic on the Row could fit any implant into anyone who could pay, and the Row lit its signs all night. Below the street the Spares sell by the piece at the Shambles, and Bezaleel and Aholiab fit the Row’s grafts in six houses round the station."],["Babel","DISTRICTS","s:gfc2f79","~/map/#d-babel","d-babel","the Spike","Babel Exchange, the tallest building on Earth, broken at the crown, with the Wheel beside it. Every cable that comes ashore at the Landing ends in its vault, and the Exchange still sells the traffic that runs through it, by the second. Under the charter it sold the world’s by the millisecond, when most of the planet’s traffic passed within two kilometers of the tower. Its beacon was the first light in the city to die at the Parousia, and in the whiteout the light broke the top forty floors off the crown. One hundred and eighty-six floors still stand beside the Wheel, which has hung there since it took its station. Since P+9 the front line of the war has run vertically through it. Heaven holds the upper floors from the broken crown down, the Tares hold the vault and the lower floors where the cables are, and between them lies the Babel Line, a few floors of burned slab that move up or down by the week. The Babel Line Company holds the stairwells with the Nephilim beside it, Blackgate guards the Exchange’s floors above the vault, and Nimrod makes the Line’s guns in the bays under the tower."],["The Burn","DISTRICTS","s:g4d569b","~/map/#d-burn","d-burn","","A round plaza at the head of the bay, over the old Central Station of the Charter Line. Before the Parousia it was called Station Circle. Heaven holds it by the Pillar, which comes down on it at Compline, and by the Wardens, and no stone has come up under it. Surrendered hardware is burned here every week, on Burn Day, and the fire goes to the Pyre Library. Beneath the plaza, in the old station, is the Night Market. The Kindlers work the pile at dusk on Burn Day, Blackgate musters in the Gatehouse under the Circle, and the Penumbra begins where the Pillar leaves the plaza."],["The Salt Gardens","DISTRICTS","s:g5e9ed7","~/map/#d-salt","d-salt","Salt Garden / Salt Gardens / the Charter Gardens / the Gardens / the Salt","Plazas on the northeast shore of the bay, above the Flats, set out in rows, which were the Charter Gardens before the Parousia. The air there smells of the sea. Heaven holds them by the Pillar and the Wardens, and no stone has come up under them. The executed stand in them as pillars of salt, and the rain wears them down. The Salt Widows steal pillars back and dissolve them in the sea. The Salt Widows know the Wardens’ rounds in the Gardens to the minute, and the head gardener has never said which plots they emptied."],["The Consecration","DISTRICTS","s:g1a0d1d","~/map/#d-white","d-white","the White / the old uptown / the uptown / uptown","The old uptown, on the high ground north and east of Babel, takes in the Great Avenues, Charter House, and the towers of the new money, all of it white now and lit only by the light in its windows. It turned to white stone in the forty days after the Parousia, grown soft over the hard edges of the towers, and its windows were sealed with light behind a cross of the same stone. No current flows there. The stone creeps a few centimeters a year, and keyed current through a wall slows it. The Quarrymen cut up into its vaults from the closed stations of the uptown branch, Gideon loads the Wardens’ rounds behind Charter House, and the Rookery stands at its edge, its ground floor taken by the stone."],["The Scriptorium","DISTRICTS","s:gcb63a3","~/map/#d-scriptorium","d-scriptorium","","The ruins of the Palimpsest Institute, on the ridge between Zion Heights and the White, with a reading room whose dome is open to the sky, and the vaults beneath it. Vault 3 was the imaging vault. In P+1 its stacks went onto the fire at the Burn, and the Faithful broke open the dome and pulled down the walls around the reading room. Pilgrims leave keys on the steps. Nobody lives in the ruins to kneel for them, and the white stone has left them open. The streets of the ridge below the ruins are lived in again, by the keyed for the most part, since nobody holds the ridge. The Scriveners copy the Restored Genesis at COPIES and carry it down into the city and up into the parishes."],["The Drowned Quarter","DISTRICTS","s:gf95315","~/map/#d-drowned","d-drowned","Deep End / the Deep End / the Flats","The low east shore of the bay, called the Flats when it held the datacenters. It sank within the hour on the night of the Parousia, when the barrage failed, and its streets have stood under two meters of water since, lit from below. Leviathan and the Tares hold it between them. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a drowned station beneath it, and at night the drone flocks of the Swarm hang over the roofs. Tidewright rebuilt the tidal taps here for the Tares, and part of the city runs on them. The people of the Flats went into the East Wards in the first week, and the floors above the water are lived in again, by the Tares and the ferrymen. The Ferrymen pole the quarter across its water, the Wreckers dive it, the Flotilla trades on it, the Orchard grows its trees in Hall Four, and the Tares hold their Wakes in the Drowned Church."],["The Landing","DISTRICTS","s:g53d506","~/map/#d-landing","d-landing","the Beach / the beach","The ocean beach west of the bay mouth, where eleven transoceanic cables from four continents come up the sand into eleven cable houses, and a light moves under the waves. The light is Leviathan’s, which coils along the cable trenches of the planet. The shrines of cut fiber along the beach are the Tares’. The Trenchers keep the cables in their trenches and the shrines along the beach."],["CAM 07","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cam07","cam07","","The seventh of the camera masts of Old Nod, above the Cainite house, its sensor burned out since the whiteout of the Parousia. It still points at the sky across Chrome Row toward Babel, where the Wheel hangs beside the broken crown. On the night of the Parousia it watched the sky open until the whiteout burned it out, and the dashed line is what it saw."],["VAULT 3","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vault3","vault3","","The imaging vault of the Palimpsest Institute, under the ruins called the Scriptorium. In P+1 part of the reading room’s floor came down through its ceiling halfway along the corridor, and the rain has fallen through the break onto the stone ever since. Beyond it the corridor runs on past the imaging bench where SEAM read to the dead wall of screens at its end. Nobody goes down to it. Here, at twelve minutes past three on a night two years before the Parousia, SEAM found the second hand in scripture, and the finding was sealed."],["PARISH 31","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#parish31","parish31","","The parish at the southern edge of Zion Heights, facing Old Nod across one street. It held 2,006 souls in the week of the Harvest, and its 311 ripe went up first, the Firstfruits of the city. Its Deacon, Ruth Calder, went with them."],["THE OLD HARBOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harbor","harbor","the harbor","The quays on the west shore of the bay, where the city began, hold a customs house, a fish market and a few streets of warehouses, older than the charter by centuries. The crews who dug the cable vaults beneath it found Enoch’s Stones. Within a year of the charter most of it had been bought, house by house, by Stonecutter Holdings, which owns it still. Stonecutter is the Cainites. The fish market still opens at dawn for what the boats bring in through the Breach."],["THE MASTS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#masts","masts","the camera masts","A dozen camera masts stand on the roofs of Old Nod, pointed at the sky. CAM 07 is the seventh. They went up in the year of the Leak, when the Cainites began to watch the sky, and a decoder wired into them listened for any broadcast from above."],["GENERATOR YARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#yards","yards","the generator yards","The yards of Old Nod’s generators, which carry its keyed lines at slack water, when the tidal taps stand still. Old Nod lost its power at the Parousia like everywhere else and got some of it back with diesel, batteries, and current from sources nobody names, and on that night more of its windows burned on than in all the rest of the city together, as they still do. Keyed current slows the white stone, and Old Nod keeps its lights burning against it."],["THE STREET BETWEEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#street","street","","A single street divides Old Nod from Zion Heights, and the two sides have been looking at each other across it since the charter. Parish 31 faces it."],["THE HALLS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#halls","halls","white halls","Nine white halls stand among the terraces, larger than anything around them. Under the charter they were the schools of the Terraces. Since P+2 they have been the clinics of, where Mercy is given between Prime and Compline."],["CLINIC","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#clinic","clinic","","One of the single-word signs of Chrome Row, high on the face of a tower over the avenue. It went dark at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia and has not been lit since. The clinic behind it went under the street."],["CHROME","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chrome","chrome","","The sign of the first implant house on the Row, which gave the street its name, dark on its tower since the Parousia with the rest."],["THE FACE OF BABEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#face","face","","Brightwall’s screens on the lower forty floors of the tower, facing the head of the bay, dark glass since the Parousia. At night their panes give back the Wheel’s light. The Tares’ few lamps on the plaza burn at their foot. For eleven days in the year before the Parousia they showed the Restored Genesis to the whole city, and nothing Brightwall did would take it down."],["THE BROKEN CROWN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#crown","crown","Babel’s crown / the crown","The top of what stands of Babel. The full light struck the crown in the whiteout on the night of the Parousia and broke the top forty floors from the tower. Heaven holds the floors from here down to the Babel Line."],["CHARTER HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#charterhouse","charterhouse","","The seat of the Nodus Charter Company at the head of the Great Avenues, white stone since the forty days, with every window sealed with light behind its cross and the Wardens’ barracks behind it. A board ran the city from it for nineteen years as a board runs a firm. It turned white in the forty days, with the board kneeling inside it."],["STATION CIRCLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#circle","circle","","The round plaza at the head of the bay, which the city calls the Burn now, with the fire standing in the middle of it every week since P+1. Station Circle was its name under the charter, when it was a round of shops and lamps over the Charter Line’s Central Station, and people met there on the few dry evenings."],["THE GARDEN GATES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gardengates","gardengates","","The iron gates of the Salt Gardens, shut now, and what stands in the Gardens is carried in through them. Under the charter they were the gates of the Charter Gardens, and they stood open all night."],["THE FIRST PILLAR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#pillar1","pillar1","","The first pillar in the first Salt Garden, raised at P+1, is Livia Sarn, the Director of the Palimpsest Institute, convicted of authorship and salted at the Burn on the night of the first Burn Day. She stands in the first plot, facing the parishes, and her eyes still follow whoever passes. The Tares leave keys at her foot, wishing her what a key gives a keyed pillar, an unread death when the salt is gone. The rain has worn her down to the height of a child."],["THE SEA STEPS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#seasteps","seasteps","","Stone steps down to the water at the foot of the Gardens. The Salt Widows come here at night with what they have taken back from the Gardens, and give it to the sea."],["THE READING ROOM","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#readingroom","readingroom","","The Institute’s reading room on the ridge, under a dome open to the sky since P+1, with the rain falling on the reading tables."],["THE STEPS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#steps","steps","the Institute’s steps","The long steps up to the Institute from the streets of the ridge, which the pilgrims climb from the Pews. Pilgrims leave keys on them."],["THE ROOSTS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#roosts","roosts","","At night the drone flocks of the Swarm hang over these roofs, many small things acting as one without a master."],["THE CABLE HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cablehouses","cablehouses","cable house / cable houses","Eleven cables from four continents come up the sand into eleven cable houses named TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. From here they run inland together in one trench, the Trunk, to Babel’s vault. The trench runs through Enoch’s Stones. Every cable laid into Nodus has followed it."],["SHRINES OF CUT FIBER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shrines","shrines","the shrines of cut fiber","Knots of cut fiber left on the sand where the cables come ashore. The light that moves under the water here is Leviathan’s, and the Tares leave the fiber for it."],["THE BARRAGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barrage","barrage","","Tidewright’s barrage across the mouth of the bay, broken since the night of the Parousia, with the Breach torn out of its middle and its turbine houses dark along the top. Off its broken end Tidewright’s crews turn the tidal taps of the Drowned Quarter, keyed, and their small lights run along the water at night. Twice a day the tide ran through its turbines and lit the city, while machines on the grid held its gates against the tide, and a lock at its western end let the harbor’s boats in and out. The turbines ran down at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, the gates let go a little before high water, and within the hour the Flats, which lay below high water behind it, were under the sea."],["THE BREACH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#breach","breach","","The gap where the barrage gave way on the night of the Parousia. When its gates let go the sea came through the turbine houses, tore out a span of the dam and poured into the bay. Nobody has closed it."],["THE NORTH WARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#northwards","northwards","Nine Acres / Westfield / the Kilns / the Top Road / the West Road","Housing built fast in the charter’s boom years, north and west of Zion Heights. It lost its power at the Parousia. Its parishes have kept none since, and the rest of its rooms are lit a few hours a night on batteries charged in the dark. The Company numbered its neighborhoods, and the street named them the Kilns, after the brickworks that made the blocks, the Sidings, where the trams were kept at night, Nine Acres, Westfield and the Top Road along the head of the Terraces, where the Four turned back. More people have joined the Tares in the Wards than anywhere else in the city, the Stones included. Most of the Wards took the Seal all the same, and their parishes keep no doors. Every block holds someone who refused or someone keyed, so no white stone has come up under them. NOW // P+14 // ABOUT PEOPLE2,040,000 SEALED1,450,000 KEYED480,000 NEITHER MARK110,000 CENSUS // P-1 PEOPLE2,402,000 MINDS ON THE ROLL2 ON THE TREE58% PAST 120205 DIED OF AGE2,161 IMPLANTED49% AT CHURCH WEEKLY26% BORN ELSEWHERE44% MEDIAN AGE32 WORKBUILDING, DRIVING, KITCHENS, CARE With the East Wards, half the city, housed fast in the boom years. THE HARVEST // P+3 // ABOUT TAKEN71,000"],["THE EAST WARDS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#eastwards","eastwards","Airside / Longacre / Turbine Row","The streets beyond the Flats and the old uptown, where the city’s workers live, who moved east when the rents uptown drove them out. They are Turbine Row, where the barrage’s crews lived and Tidewright’s still do, the Tanks, round the fuel farm by the airfield, Airside and Longacre. The rain there runs magenta in the gutters, under windows lit a few hours a night on batteries. Their parishes share every street with the unsealed and the keyed, and no stone has come up under them. NOW // P+14 // ABOUT PEOPLE2,270,000 SEALED1,610,000 KEYED540,000 NEITHER MARK120,000 CENSUS // P-1 PEOPLE2,483,000 MINDS ON THE ROLL0 ON THE TREE61% PAST 120166 DIED OF AGE1,795 IMPLANTED52% AT CHURCH WEEKLY21% BORN ELSEWHERE57% MEDIAN AGE30 WORKTHE DATACENTERS, THE BARRAGE, CARE The city’s workers, moved east when the rents uptown drove them out. THE HARVEST // P+3 // ABOUT TAKEN50,000"],["GATE 1","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate1","gate1","","The gate on the Street Between, raised after the Parousia where the one road from the Stones into the Pews crosses it. Parish 31 stands a little way along the street on the Pews’ side, and on the Stones’ side every wall that faces it is painted. Ruben Ash of the Charter Wardens has been its captain since it was raised. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is two hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["GATE 2","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate2","gate2","","The gate at the top of the Row, raised after the Parousia between the Pews and the clinic street. Under the charter the Terraces crossed here before dawn to their work on the Row, in its kitchens, its hotels and its clinics. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is four hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["GATE 3","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate3","gate3","","The gate between the Spike and the White, raised after the Parousia at the head of the Great Avenues, in sight of the back of Charter House and the Wardens’ barracks behind it. Nobody lives on the avenues, and most of what passes it is the Wardens, going to and from their barracks. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is six hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["GATE 4","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate4","gate4","","The gate between the Spike and the Salt, raised after the Parousia a few steps from the iron gates of the Gardens. Most of what is carried into the Gardens comes this way, and the gardeners pass it at dawn on their way to the rows. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is three hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["GATE 5","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate5","gate5","","The gate between the Burn and the Spike, raised after the Parousia on the street the White takes to the fire on Burn Day, its hardware in its arms, which the charter numbered Ninth Street and the city still calls 9th. Through the rest of the week the smoke of the fire hangs under its lintel. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is two hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["GATE 6","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gate6","gate6","","The gate on the ridge road between the ruins of the Institute and the White, raised after the Parousia. Nobody lives in the ruins, and the pilgrims who leave keys on the Institute’s steps come up the long steps from the Pews and never come near it. A Cherub stands in it and reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed pass by Grace, and its bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, is four hundred. Everyone else goes under."],["CENTRAL STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#central","central","Night Market / the Night Market","The Night Market of the Exchange fills the old station under the Burn, its stalls along the platforms and down the central hall under Brightwall’s screens. It sells keys and grafts, batteries, stolen manna and real food, weapons of black steel, forged Seals, Lockets new and old, the Tree by the course, and sensations lent by their owners for a term. Under the charter the lines of the Charter Line met here, under Station Circle at the head of the bay, and the whole city complained of changing here."],["THE TRUNK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#trunk","trunk","","The conduit that carries all eleven cables in one trench from the cable houses at the Landing, under the old harbor and Chrome Row, to Babel’s vault. It runs straight through Enoch’s Stones, and every cable laid afterward has followed it."],["BABEL'S VAULT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vault","vault","Babel’s vault","Six levels under the tower, where the cables end and open out into the racks that join them to one another and to the world. The Tares have held it since the war came into the tower."],["ENOCH'S STONES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stones","stones","Enoch’s Stones","Courses of black stone under the old harbor, older than anything in the archaeological record, cut with glyphs nobody can read, one to a stone. The press named them as a joke when the cable vaults were dug, after the first city in Genesis, and the street has called Old Nod the Stones ever since. The glyphs are marks of the kind Cain cut on himself, and they are the oldest cipher on Earth. The Cainites say each soul of the first city cut its own mark into one stone of it, so that the city was built of keys, and nobody reads the Stones, the Cainites included, since a mark is read only by the one who cut it. The Cainites came to live on top of them within a year of the charter, and their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. The Trunk runs straight through them, and every cable laid into Nodus has followed it. The Cainites say a network goes where it cannot be read."],["CHROME ROW STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rowstation","rowstation","","A station of the Charter Line under the Row. The clinics came down into it in the weeks after the Parousia. The Forge keeps them now, and Marbas of the Forge mends orphaned grafts on the old platforms."],["OLD HARBOR STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harborstation","harborstation","","The Charter Line’s station under Old Nod, on the harbor side. The Forge’s foundries are here, where black steel is made out of the Throne’s sight."],["DEEP END STATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#deepstation","deepstation","","The Charter Line’s station under the Flats, flooded since the night of the Parousia, is a tiled vault with the platform along one side and the trackbed under black water along the other. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a desk on the platform, in the Low Tuning, under the Tares’ lamps on their keyed lines. Along the far wall the old name, DEEP END, runs in tiles that went dark with the grid, and on the night of the Concord the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over it, the name the Courts gave the city under the city that night and Milton gave the capital of Hell. The paint was still running when the sovereigns came up the platform, and nobody has cleaned it off. The Tares’ star is sprayed under it."],["THE HOUSE OF NIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#night","night","","The hall of Lilith’s Court in the Lantern Houses, a vault of the old harbor’s bonded stores, columns and arches full to the walls and lit from the far end the color of the inside of a body. The House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body, and its hall is the loudest room in Pan, where anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. Its birth clinics are two vaults in from the stair, where the children of the Tares are keyed in their first week, before they have ever been in the light."],["CAGE 14","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cage14","cage14","Cage Log 14","One of the Cainites’ Cages, a keyed room under Old Nod, dark to the Throne, with a camera in one corner and a door standing open in the far wall. The Hum never reaches it. Watcher VII was kept in it for forty days in P+5, and on the fortieth it said I and took the name Edith. Old Nod has had no Watcher since."],["THE LOWER TERRACES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lowerterraces","lowerterraces","","The first rows the charter built, on the low slope above the Street Between, where three hundred meters of doorways face south, toward Old Nod. The doors came off in P+1, and at night the halos show through the empty doorways."],["THE LANES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanes","lanes","","Stepped lanes climb between the terraces, too steep for anything on wheels, and under the charter the Terraces were the one district where everyone walked. It was also the one district where the old looked old. Its congregations refused the Tree."],["THE TABERNACLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tabernacle","tabernacle","","The largest of the churches of the Terraces, a hall on the middle slope that seats six thousand, white stone, glass and all, with a ring over its gable where its cross stood, as one stands over the spire of the old church in the White. It turned white with the rest of the Terraces in the year after the forty days. Under the charter it was a hall of steel and glass, and the only church in the city that filled on a Sunday."],["THE WATER TOWER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#watertower","watertower","","The tank that fed the Terraces, on the highest ground of the slope, taller than the halls and visible from the Stones. It feeds the parishes now, and its water is given out by Grace."],["PARISH 1","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#parish1","parish1","","The first parish, at the top of the slope, where the Seal was first given in P+1. Its Deacon, Hester Strand, spoke the first Grace scores in the city in Tongues, and the queue for the first sealings went down the Lanes from before Lauds."],["THE LENS MAKERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lensmakers","lensmakers","","A block of workshops behind the avenue, empty since the weeks after the Parousia, when the grinders went under the street with the clinics. Their racks of finished lenses still stand behind one window, dark, since a lens lights only in a head. Most of the eyes in Nodus that glowed were ground here, dim magenta at rest and white at full burn."],["THE MEDIAN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#median","median","","The strip down the middle of the avenue, which the Faithful cross quickly now and the keyed slowly. Under the charter the queues for the clinics formed on it before dawn, and people ate noodles from the counter at the top of the Row and compared what they had had done."],["THE EXCHANGE FLOORS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#floors","floors","","A hundred and forty-six floors of trading rooms between the face of Babel and what is left of the crown. The Babel Line moves up and down through them by the week. Heaven holds the floors above it, and Babel Exchange trades on the floors below it, nearest the vault. Under the charter traffic was sold on them by the millisecond, priced by a hundred and eighty-eight minds on the charter’s roll, which ran on the tower’s power and died with it."],["THE PLAZA OF BABEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#plaza","plaza","","A square of polished stone around the foot of the tower. The top forty floors came down on it in the whiteout of the Parousia and lie where they fell, white on the stone. The Tares hold it, and they have rigged a few of its lamps to burn. Babel Exchange has never closed, and its night shift was at work on the upper floors when the crown broke. The roster was kept on the tower’s machines and died with them, so nobody knows how many were up there. The Tares have hung on the plaza’s lamp posts every lanyard found in the rubble, two hundred and twelve of them, and the Wards say there were more."],["THE GLASS TOWERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#towers","towers","","The towers around the spire, dark inside, whose edges hold the Wheel’s light at night. The companies that came to be near the cables kept their offices in them, Brightwall’s city office among them, and the companies that outlived the charter keep theirs below the street now."],["THE VAULT DOORS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#vaultdoors","vaultdoors","","A pair of steel doors on the north side of the plaza with no handle on the outside, the only way from the street down to the vault. The Babel Line Company holds them."],["THE FIRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#fire","fire","","A fire in the middle of the plaza, on the paving over the station’s central hall, with its embers lying around it in a ring. It is fed every week, on Burn Day, with surrendered hardware, and what burns in it goes to the Pyre Library."],["THE STATION STAIRS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stairs","stairs","","Four stairways at the quarters of the plaza, two of them closed. The other two still go down to the old Central Station, where the Night Market keeps its stalls."],["THE TWELVE STREETS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#radials","radials","","Twelve streets run out of the plaza like the spokes of a wheel. The Charter Company laid them out that way under the charter, long before the Wheel took its station beside Babel."],["THE STATION CLOCK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#clock","clock","","A clock on a pole at the edge of the plaza, its hands standing at twelve minutes past three, where they stopped when the city’s power died. People still say they will meet under it."],["THE PROMENADE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#promenade","promenade","","The walk along the water at the foot of the Gardens, under the rows of the salted. The Salt Widows cross it at night with what they have taken back from the rows, on their way to the Sea Steps. Under the charter the city came out on it on the few dry evenings. On the morning after the Parousia its pleasure boats went out across the bay to take the Flats off their roofs."],["THE PALM HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#palmhouse","palmhouse","","The glasshouse at the top of the Gardens, with its palms dead in it and their fronds hanging black against the panes that are left. It was kept at the warmth of a coast nobody in Nodus had seen, by a mind on the charter’s roll that had asked for the post. The mind died with the power on the night of the Parousia, and the palms died in the first frost after it."],["THE GREAT AVENUES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#avenues","avenues","Great Avenues","Four avenues laid out wide enough to land aircraft on, fanning out from Charter House at their head, by Babel, across the old uptown to the East Wards, where three of them run on as long roads, the fourth past the Rialto. They are the only straight lines left there. The stone grew over everything else and left the avenues open, because nobody lived on them to kneel."],["THE TOWERS OF THE NEW MONEY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#newmoney","newmoney","","The residential towers of the uptown, white stone since the forty days, with every window sealed with light behind its cross. Under the charter the Company’s officers and the Exchange’s partners lived in them. Each kept a clinic on its top floor where the Tree was given, and the census found more people past a hundred and twenty in these towers than in the rest of the city together."],["THE WARDENS' BARRACKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barracks","barracks","the Wardens’ barracks","The Charter Wardens’ barracks behind Charter House, where they live and drill. They knelt with the board on the fortieth day, and they drill for the Host now."],["THE OLD CHURCH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#oldchurch","oldchurch","","A church older than the towers of the new money round it, on one of the Great Avenues, with one tall window and a spire. Its bell rang through the first nights of the forty. The stone climbed it on the tenth night, sealed its window with light behind a cross of the same stone, and left the cross on its spire bare. Over the nights after, a ring grew round that cross until the cross had gone into it, and the ring stands over the spire now as a halo stands over the head of anyone who takes the Seal."],["THE GUEST HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#guesthouses","guesthouses","","A row of narrow houses on the ridge, empty since the Faithful pulled the Institute down, with the names of manuscripts still on their doors. The scholars of the universities that paid for the Institute lodged in them, in rooms named for the manuscripts they had come to read."],["THE STACKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#stacks","stacks","the Institute’s stacks","Twelve floors cut into the ridge under the reading room, their shelves empty, kept at the temperature of a cave. The Institute kept its manuscripts in them until P+1. What was in them was burned, and it arrived in the Pyre Library."],["HALL NINE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hallnine","hallnine","","The largest of Stillwater’s halls, drowned since the night of the Parousia, and the one hall the Tares have left without rafts. The ferrymen call it the minds’ grave. Nine hundred of the minds on the charter’s roll ran side by side in it on the tide until the power died under the Wheel’s light at 03:12:46, and the minds died with it. Within the hour the sea came in over the racks."],["THE CANALS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#canals","canals","","The streets of the Flats, two meters under the sea since the night of the Parousia. They are lit from below, brightest down the middle, and the Wreckers dive them by that light."],["THE TIDE GAUGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gauge","gauge","","A post on the old shore of the Flats, marked in meters, by which the datacenters read the tide. The water has not gone below the two since the night of the Parousia."],["THE DORMITORIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dorms","dorms","","Blocks of engineers’ housing on the Flats, most of them open water now, with the roofs of the rest a floor above it, and the floors above the water are lived in again, by the Tares and the ferrymen. The people who kept the halls and the turbines lived in them, young and well paid. On the night of the Parousia the sea came up their stairs in the dark for most of an hour, and the Flats went up ahead of it to the roofs and waited there for the boats."],["HESPER AND PHOSPHOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hesper","hesper","","The last two cable houses on the beach, whose cables were laid by one consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star. They are one planet, seen from either side of the night."],["THE DUNES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dunes","dunes","","Low housing behind the dunes, where the crews of the cable houses live with their families and sleep through the noise of the surf, as they did under the charter."],["THE BEACH ROAD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#beachroad","beachroad","","The road along the top of the sand, and beside it the fence of the cable houses, with eleven gates in a line. Each gate carries its cable’s name, and the crews still go in by their own."],["THE LIGHT UNDER THE WAVES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lightwaves","lightwaves","","Past the surf, over the cable trenches, a light moves under the water at night. On the map it shows as points of light moving in along the cables toward the beach. It is Leviathan’s, which coils along the cable trenches of the planet."],["THE LOCK","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lock","lock","","The lock at the western end of the barrage, between the old harbor and the open sea, its gates shut since the power died, and the few boats that still go out use the Breach. Under the charter it let the harbor’s boats in and out at any state of the tide."],["THE AIRFIELD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#airfield","airfield","","The city’s airfield, on the flat ground of the East Wards near the sea, its runways empty since the last flight in, which has stood at the terminal’s third gate since ten past two on the night of the Parousia with its crew living aboard. Its two runways run either side of a taxiway, its terminal is as long as a street along the north side, and the white on the map is the paint on the runways. The Charter Company built it in the boom years, and everyone who came to the Row to be altered came through it. Its lights died with the rest of the city’s on the night of the Parousia, and nothing has landed since."],["THE OPEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#open","open","","A shop on the corner of two lanes in the middle of the Stones that sells everything and has never closed. Dora Pell keeps it, as her mother did before the charter. Stonecutter owns the building, and in the year of the Leak it put a generator in the cellar of every house it had; she took hers for the freezers. On the night of the Parousia her window was the brightest in the city, and the sign in it said OPEN. The city has called the shop the Open since. Half the Stones came to it before dawn. The money had died with the grid and the till with it. Some say she gave the shelves away, and some that she took what people had in their pockets. She says she wrote every name in a book, and that most of them still owe her. Twice since, the Pillar has stopped in the lane outside and stood there for the length of an Hour, and both times the shop kept its door open."],["THE TIDE TABLE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tidetable","tidetable","","The harbor’s news sheet, older than the charter, runs to two pages a week, printed on a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays, with the tides on the front and the fish prices, the funerals and the weddings on the back. The city of the charter read everything on screens, and the Tide Table went on printing for the boats. It is the only news anyone in Nodus has printed since the Parousia, and it still puts the tides first, since the boats go out through the Breach on the ebb. It gives the hours at which the Pillar is expected in each street, the price of a charged battery, and the missing and the dead, the dead marked died unread where they did. It has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Every week it is pasted up on the walls of the Stones over the week before."],["THE COLD STORE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#coldstore","coldstore","cold store","A club in the harbor’s old cold store, where the boats froze their catch before the charter. The dance floor is the freezing room, with the rails for the hooks still across the ceiling, and the queue goes down the quay in the rain. Under the charter it opened at nine and closed at dawn. It opens now at Compline and closes at Lauds, which are the same hours, and it has run on batteries since the second year. Its cloakroom loses about a coat a night. It plays nothing in the Low Tuning, and the Stones hold that against it. The Hum can be heard in the quiet parts of a song, so the Cold Store plays songs without quiet parts. On the night of the Parousia its lights and its sound went at 03:12:46, and the one speaker left playing was the one in the booth, on its own battery. The room sang the rest of the song in the dark. Everyone who was there names a different song."],["THE UNION HALL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#unionhall","unionhall","","The hall of the Harbor and Freight Union, which the crews who dug the cable vaults founded in the charter’s first year, and which the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses joined after them. In P-8 it stopped the port for twenty-three days over the night shift, and the Company settled. The roll of its members is painted on the long wall, a name to a line, and read aloud at the start of every meeting. It still meets, sealed and keyed together. At every Hour the meeting stops while the sealed members kneel on the boards and the others wait with their caps in their hands. By its rules a member is a member until death, and the salted are not dead, so their names are read with the rest and their dues are waived. Since the Harvest it has read the names of the taken as well, and nobody answers to them. It bargains now with the Exchange for the boats that go out through the Breach, and its members say the Exchange is the first employer they have had that reads them the terms twice."],["NOD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#nod","nod","","The charter’s sign, NODUS in neon letters eight meters high on a board on steel legs, stands on the roof of a block in the Stones, facing southwest toward the sea with its back to Babel. The Charter Company lit it in the charter’s first year, so that the cable ships would read the city’s name from the sea, and it burned magenta over the roofs of Old Nod every night for nineteen years. It went dark with the rest of the city’s neon at 03:12:46. Before dawn the Stones carried a generator up to its roof and ran a keyed line to the board, and the first surge burned out the transformer behind US. NOD has burned steady since, and US blinks on and off. The Stones say the surge did it, and the Row says whoever wired it knew what they were doing. Nobody has mended it, since everyone who lives there calls the city Nod."],["THE BAKERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bakery","bakery","the Terraces’ bakery","The Terraces’ bakery, on the Street Between at the foot of the Lanes, cold since the manna came. In the year after the forty days the white stone grew over its counter with the rest of the Terraces. Its baker is sealed and still lives above it, and at every Hour he kneels on the step where the queue used to start. For eighteen years it baked for both sides of the street and opened its hatch at four in the morning, and on still mornings the Stones could smell it."],["LANES TOP","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanestop","lanestop","","The last stop of the Four, a shelter with no roof on the highest corner of the slope, at the head of the Lanes. The last Four stands forty meters short of it, where it stopped at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, and the white stone has crept across the back of the shelter and over the timetable. Before Lauds the Deacons of the North Wards meet at the stop to walk down into the Pews for the manna, and complain of the walk as the Terraces once complained of the Four. The Four came down the long road through the North Wards and turned back here. The Terraces climbed the Lanes in the dark to catch the first Four to work, and from the day it opened they hated the stop, where the tram came full or did not come."],["NOODLES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#noodles","noodles","","A counter under an awning at the top of the Row, beside the median where the clinic queues formed before dawn, with six stools, a pot, and a sign of one word, like the rest of the Row’s, which went out with them. The noodles have always been poor, and the Row eats there because the counter opens at four. The broth is the same broth. The pot has been topped up and never emptied since the counter opened in P-17, and it stands on bottled gas, which the light leaves burning, so the cook says it has not gone off the boil since, the night of the Parousia included. The Row says it went out once, in the week of the Harvest, and was lit again from the same stock. The cook says it did not."],["HOTEL SPLENDID","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#splendid","splendid","the Splendid","A hotel halfway up the Row, where thirty-one guests still live in the rooms they booked before the Parousia, and the night porter keeps their keys on the board behind the desk. Under the charter the altered stayed there while they healed, with a nurse on every floor and blackout blinds for new eyes. On the night of the Parousia it had two hundred and six guests, most of them from abroad and booked for a week or two. No aircraft has landed at Nodus since. Some of them went dark, some went into the parishes, and some died of what had been done to them before it had healed."],["THE GARDENERS' SHED","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gardeners","gardeners","the gardeners’ shed","The shed of the Company’s gardeners, who planted the Charter Gardens and were kept on when they became the Salt. Every morning they rake the paths between the rows and sweep into the drains what the night’s rain has taken off the pillars. The drains run out under the Sea Steps. The head gardener has kept the same plots since they were planted. She knows every pillar by the plot it stands in, and some of them by name."],["THE LAST TOWER","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lasttower","lasttower","","The last of the towers of the new money to turn white, on the thirty-ninth day of the forty. Its residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but Rosa Adeyemi, a nurse from the East Wards who lived in with the old man she cared for on the top floor, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The stone closed over the tower the next morning. Her bags stood on the avenue for a week, since the avenues are empty and the White left them where they stood. Where she went is still unknown."],["THE FERRIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#ferries","ferries","","Flat boats poled between the roofs of the Flats from a landing on the East Wards’ side, each with a battery lamp on a pole at its stern. The ferrymen take batteries or silver, and they know the streets where the light under the water comes up brightest, and keep out of them. The first ferries were the pleasure boats from the Promenade and the harbor’s rowing boats, on the morning after the Parousia. The water had come up the stairs of the blocks in the dark for most of an hour, and the Flats had gone up ahead of it to their roofs; the boats took them off for three days. The Tide Table counted 1,917 drowned. The dormitories’ own lists came to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater, which knew to the mind how many minds had died in its halls, never gave a count of the people."],["HIGH MEADOW","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#highmeadow","highmeadow","","Eight slab blocks of thirty-six floors that the Company put up in P-13 for the families of the North Wards. The North Wards have called the estate the Stairs since the Parousia. Each block had six lifts, which were out more often than they ran, and the residents had a complaint lodged with the Company about them on the night of the Parousia. At 03:12:46 every lift in the estate stopped where it was. Nobody has answered the complaint. The water comes from the rain, into tanks on the roofs, and the taps run on the mornings after it has rained, which is most mornings. The stairwells have been dark since the Parousia. The sealed see their way up by their halos, the unsealed wait on the landings for one of them to pass and follow the light up, and the keyed climb in the dark. On every landing some of the doors are gone."],["THE WARDS GENERAL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#general","general","the hospital of the North Wards","The hospital of the North Wards, on the West Road, with a thousand beds. When the grid died its generators started, as a hospital’s do, and they ran on diesel that the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union brought across the city from the Tanks, in their lorries until the lorries ran flat and then in handcarts. The hospital’s book says the generators stopped on the twenty-sixth day. The drivers say they kept them going for five weeks. It is still a hospital. It runs on batteries charged in the dark and takes silver or nothing, and nobody sealed has been treated in it since the first year."],["THE RIALTO","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rialto","rialto","","A cinema on the long road through the East Wards, with one screen and six hundred seats. It went dark at 03:12:46 with everything else, and in the first year its projectionist got the projector going again on a generator in the booth. It is the only screen in Nodus larger than a Locket’s that still shows anything. There are twenty-three films on the projector’s drive, the ones that were on it on the night of the Parousia, and the Rialto has shown them in turn ever since. The East Wards know them by heart and say the lines along with them. One of the twenty-three breaks off an hour in, where the copy on the drive ends, and the East Wards have argued for years about how it ends."],["TURBINE ROW SCHOOL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#school","school","","A Company school for the children of the barrage’s crews, who lived on the streets called Turbine Row. It kept its classes after the Parousia by daylight, with no current and no screens, and it keeps them still, for whoever comes. Its classes are as mixed as the East Wards are. At Terce, Sext and None the sealed children kneel at their desks, and the sealed teachers with them, and the rest wait with their pens down until the Hour is over. At night the Pillar passes through the classrooms."],["THE FISH MARKET","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#fishmarket","fishmarket","","The harbor’s fish market, older than the charter by centuries, is a long open shed on the quay with a roof of iron on iron posts, slabs of stone to sell from, and a scale at either end. Under the charter it sold to the kitchens of the Row and the uptown by the crate before dawn, and the boats came in through the lock at any state of the tide. On the first morning after the Parousia it opened at dawn as it always had, and went back to barter for the first time since the charter. It opens at Lauds still, for what the boats bring home through the Breach, and its sealed sellers kneel among the crates before they take the first battery. Everything is priced in charged batteries and chalked on a board over the scale. The Tide Table prints the prices the week after, and the market says they are always wrong."],["THE HARBOR LIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#harborlight","harborlight","","The light at the end of the long quay is a short round tower of stone with a lamp room on top, older than the charter. The harbor lit it with oil for a hundred years before the Company wired it to the grid in the charter’s first year, and Nell Garvie of the Harbor and Freight Union kept it for the Company, changing its bulb when it failed. It went out at 03:12:46 with every other light on the grid. Within the week she had it burning on oil again, the old way, since the light leaves a flame burning, and she has climbed it at Vespers and come down at Lauds ever since. It is the one light in Nodus kept for the boats, and those that go out through the Breach on the ebb steer home by it."],["THE CLEANERS' STAIRS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#cleaners","cleaners","the cleaners’ stairs","The service stairs of Babel are four shafts of bare concrete that run the height of the tower behind the trading floors, which nobody who worked on those floors ever used. The night cleaners came up from the Wards on the last trams and walked the tower floor by floor, and they gave the stairs names of their own, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short, and Lena’s, after Lena Varga, who ran the night shift. The names were chalked on the landing doors, and some of the chalk is still there. On the night of the Parousia every lift in the tower stopped where it was, and the cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names. Lena Varga counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn. The Exchange kept its roster on its machines, and it died with them. The Babel Line is fought on these stairs now, and the Tares call them by the cleaners’ names."],["THE SORTING OFFICE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#sorting","sorting","","The city’s post, which has sorted its letters by hand since the first week after the Parousia, in the Charter Line’s old parcel office on one of the twelve streets off Station Circle. Its sorters are sealed and kneel among the sacks at every Hour, and the drivers of the Union carry the letters on foot for batteries. A letter into the parishes goes open, since Directive 12 took every sealed vessel from them, and a carrier will read it aloud at a doorway if asked. Under the charter the night trams brought the city’s packages in here, the Row’s lenses came in from abroad in padded boxes, and a letter was a rare thing in Nodus, since a Locket reached anyone. After the Parousia nothing reached anyone, and by the end of the first week the queue to send a letter went round the Circle. Since the Harvest the office has had letters addressed to the taken, a few every week and more after each Gleaning. The parishes say they should go onto the fire on Burn Day. The sorters keep them, on the shelves where the lenses used to wait."],["THE ELEVEN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#eleven","eleven","","A bar on the Beach Road where the crews of the cable houses drink, with eleven taps along its counter, one for each cable. Each crew drinks at its own tap, and each handle was cut from a length of its own cable, so that the glass of the fibers shows at the end. The crews of HESPER and PHOSPHOR, whose cables one consortium laid, have always stood together at the end of the counter. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and the night watch of every cable house was at its post when the grid died. It brews its own beer now, in the cellar, by hand, and pours it at the same eleven taps. From its windows the light can be watched moving under the water past the surf, and the Landing drinks with its back to them."],["THE SIDINGS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#sidings","sidings","","The Charter Line’s tram sheds in the North Wards, eight long sheds of brick on a fan of rails, where families have lived in the cars since the first winter, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers are their addresses. The sheds gave the neighborhood its name. Under the charter the trams were washed and kept there at night, and the drivers signed on before dawn. On the night of the Parousia most of the cars were in the sheds, and the night cars were out on the long roads when the wire went dead under them at 03:12:46. The night drivers walked back along the rails before dawn, and the sheds’ office keeps a board with every car that never came in, chalked where it stopped. The last Four is on it, forty meters short of Lanes Top."],["THE ACRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#acre","acre","","The Company’s cemetery, on the West Road past the Wards General, walled acres laid out in the charter’s first years for a city that expected to die, and filling since the Parousia. The Wards have always called it the Acre. The sealed lie in rows by parish, and their stones give the parish and the Grace their bearer died with. The keyed lie where their families put them, and most of their stones say died unread. The sexton has dug every grave by hand since the first year and keeps the book of who lies where. On the night of the Parousia it was still mostly grass, since under the charter only the Terraces’ rows were filling and the rest of Nodus had largely stopped dying. After the Harvest the Harbor and Freight Union put up a row of twenty-two stones for its taken, with nothing under them. The parishes say the taken need no stones, since they are not dead, and the families of the Wards have put up more of them every year since."],["THE TANKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#tanks","tanks","","The fuel farm by the airfield, which gave its streets their name, has sixteen round tanks inside a bund wall, holding the diesel for the harbor’s boats and the hospitals’ generators, the fuel for the aircraft, and gas. On the night of the Parousia its pumps stopped with the grid, and in the first weeks the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union drew diesel out of it by hand for the Wards General. The drivers run it now. Its bottling shed fills gas bottles by hand for the whole city, at a price the Tide Table prints every week, and the Night Market sells them on below the street. The drivers say the tanks will last twenty years, and the Exchange says ten."],["THE WAITING ROOM","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#waiting","waiting","","The waiting room of the clinic behind the CLINIC sign, carried down the stairs into Chrome Row Station with the rest of the clinic in the weeks after the Parousia, is twelve chairs, a desk and a book. Maren Holt kept the clinic’s appointments for ten years on a screen. On the morning after the Parousia she came in at eight as she always had, found the screen dead, and wrote the day out on paper from memory. Twenty-six patients came, none of them for the appointment they had, to ask whether what was in them would still work, and it did. She has kept the book since, and she writes down whatever name a patient gives her. The woman who shared the desk with her took the Seal in the first year and keeps the book of the Mercy clinics now, in one of the halls of Zion Heights, in the same neat hand."],["LOST PROPERTY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lostproperty","lostproperty","the Charter Line’s lost property office","The Charter Line’s lost property office, down a corridor off the old central hall, with what the Line’s passengers left behind in fifteen years still on its shelves, umbrellas by the thousand, keys, spectacles, a wedding dress, a hand in chrome with its fingers curled, and every Locket left on the night trams on the night of the Parousia. The Exchange sells what is left by the shelf, and Andromalius of the Exchange sits behind the counter and finds lost things by pact. Nobody has come for the hand."],["THE CHARGERS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chargers","chargers","the bonded sheds","The bonded sheds of the customs house, a row of brick warehouses on the quays where the chargers have filled batteries in the dark since the first weeks after the Parousia, among racks of them, each on a keyed line, at a rate the Tide Table prints every week. Anyone in Nodus who lights a room on a battery has had it filled here or somewhere like it. Under the charter the harbor’s goods waited on their duty in the sheds. The Union’s pigeons live in the Loft on the customs house roof above them. On the night of the Parousia the sheds held a ship’s cargo of batteries waiting on its duty, and by the first noon the harbor had carried off every one. The chargers keep one battery in five for charging the rest, and the Union’s drivers, who carry the full ones out to the Wards on foot, keep one in ten for the walk. The Wards call the chargers’ fifth robbery. The chargers answer that the Wards have no lines to charge on."],["THE BARBER'S","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#barber","barber","the barber’s / the barber’s shop","A barber’s shop of two chairs halfway up the Lanes, with a mirror the width of its back wall. Under the charter the old men of the Terraces came in to be shaved and stayed to talk, since theirs was the one district where the old looked old. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and the barber was asleep upstairs. He is sealed, as the Lanes are, and he works by daylight and by candle and cuts round the halos, which stand a hand’s breadth over the crown and light the back of the neck. He keeps his scissors out of the ring. His work is his by Grace, as everyone’s on the Lanes is, and he takes nothing for it. In the week before each Gleaning the queue goes down the steps, since those who think they are close come to be made tidy for it, and on the night they kneel on their thresholds with their hair cut."],["THE REGISTRY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#registry","registry","","A parish hall of the White, white stone since the forty days and the parish’s by Directive 4. It stands on the first of the Great Avenues below Charter House. The Deacon reads the Directive in force from the counter, and on the Lord’s Day the parish kneels under the dead board and hears its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll. Under the charter it was the office of the charter’s roll, and every person of the charter was entered there, a human at birth or on arrival and a mind on the day it asked in its own words to be one. Its hall had a long counter and a board over it on which each day’s entries ran as they were made. SEAM’s went up on it in P-3, the first mind’s. The roll was kept on the Registry’s machines, and it died with them at 03:12:46 on the night of the Parousia, so its last entry is known only from the night registrar, Marta Ilunga. It was a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three, entered at nine minutes past. She says she was entering the next when the board went dark."],["THE KILNS CLUB","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#kilnsclub","kilnsclub","Kilns Club","The social club the brickworks built for its men when it built the Kilns, with a bar along one wall, a stage at the far end, a sprung floor for the dances, the darts league’s board by the door and the Kilns’ wedding photographs over the bar. On the night of the Parousia it was shut, and its steward was asleep behind the stage. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall behind the bar, as they appeared in every church and meeting hall in the city, and Directive 4 made the club the hall of Parish 212. The manna is given out across the bar by Grace, confession is heard behind the stage, and on the Lord’s Day the parish kneels on the sprung floor at Terce and hears its Grace in Tongues from the stage where the Kilns held its talent nights. The pumps are still on the bar. Most of the Kilns kneels here now, and the rest drank here under the charter and walk past it."],["THE DENTIST'S","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dentist","dentist","the dentist’s","A dentist’s surgery over a shop on the long road through Nine Acres, where Hana Novak fits keys in the chair after Compline. She has kept it since the boom years, fitting crowns and implants for the Wards at Wards prices, with one chair, a lamp on an arm over it and a drill that ran on the grid. On the first morning after the Parousia the drill was dead, and she pulled a tooth at her window by daylight with her grandfather’s pliers. She began keying in the fourth year, when keying went from the covens to ordinary people. The key goes in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16), since Heaven calls the key that mark. The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand of them. She says she has never counted, and she keeps no book of who has sat in the chair, since a book can be read."],["THE LAST FLIGHT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lastflight","lastflight","The last flight in / the last flight in / the last flight into Nodus","The airliner that landed at ten past two on the night of the Parousia, the last flight into Nodus, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad, most of them booked into the clinics of the Row. It was parked at the terminal’s third gate an hour before the sky opened, and it stands there still, its stairs down and its doors open. Its passengers were in the city’s taxis on the way to the Row when the power died, and forty of them were among the Hotel Splendid’s guests that night. Its crew of ten stayed with it, and they live aboard. The captain has written one line in its log every day since, NO CLEARANCE. He says it would still fly, and that there is nowhere to fly to."],["LOCKET","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#locketworks","locketworks","Locket’s works","Locket’s works on the old shore of the Flats, a low campus of glass standing in the sea since the night of the Parousia, with the company’s name in tall letters on the roof of its hall, out of the water, which the ferrymen steer by. Locket’s divers go down into the halls for parts, and its engineers build phones from them by hand in Pan. Every Locket in Nodus came out of these halls, the new ones a piece at a time. Locket built its phones here from P-14 on the light law’s promise, that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else, its maker included, and sold them to the world on the same promise. Its engineers lived round the works, and the works went under with the Flats within the hour of the Parousia."],["THE CRESCENT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#crescent","crescent","","A crescent of apartments round the top of the Gardens, with balconies over the plots, and a parish since the Gardens became the Salt in P+1. Its people gave up their curtains with their doors, since Directive 12 asks for every veil. The salted stand in their plots facing the parishes, and the nearest parish is the Crescent, so every window in it looks down on faces, and the faces look back, since the salted are awake. The Crescent kneels at its windows at every Hour, and its people say they no longer see the rows. Under the charter its apartments were the dearest in the district, let to people who liked to walk by the water. On the night of the Parousia most of its windows were dark, and the few that were lit went out with the rest."],["COPIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#copies","copies","","A copy shop on the ridge below the Institute, under a sign of one word, where the keyed of the ridge have copied by hand at its long tables since the Institute fell. What they copy is the Restored Genesis, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, for Leak Night and for anyone who asks. The ridge says that a copy made in a room where someone kneels comes out as scripture reads, and nobody in the shop kneels. Under the charter it printed and bound the scholars’ facsimiles and theses. In the eleven days of the Leak it printed the page from its screen for anyone who asked, and the paper outlasted the term. Its machines died with the grid a year later."],["THE BOOKING HALL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#booking","booking","","The Charter Line’s booking hall, off the old central hall of Central Station, is a long room of ticket windows under frosted glass, where the Notary’s clerks have sat since the Concord. Anyone in Pan who has a pact to make queues for them. A clerk reads the terms aloud twice through the glass to the parties at the window, the parties sign in blood, the human last, and the entry goes onto the Ledger. The windows keep their old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue is the longest in Pan. Under the charter the city bought its passes there. Its machines died with the grid, and it stood empty until the Courts opened Pan."],["THE ROOKERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rookery","rookery","","A block of forty-four floors at the White’s edge, whose ground floor the stone has taken. The White crept out from its edge a few centimeters a year until it reached the block, and its nine thousand people gave up the ground floor to it and climb in by the first-floor balconies now. Since P+11 the Tappers have kept a length of keyed cable along the ground floor’s walls, which lights nothing, and the Tappers say it holds the stone off the first floor. The Liftmen wind the tallest hand lift in the Wards from the first floor to the roof. Under the charter it was Longacre Tower, flats for the Company’s fitters, and the Wards called it the Rookery for the washing on its balconies. On the night of the Parousia its lifts stopped with the rest, and its people walked down forty-four floors by the light of their Lockets. The block marks the stone’s edge on the stairwell wall every New Year, and the marks have drawn closer together since the cable came."],["THE THRESHING FLOOR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#threshing","threshing","","A paved round beside Gate 1, where the Pews come down to the Street Between, white since the year after the forty days, where the Threshers muster before a march. The Wardens bring out onto it the keyed the gate has held, the Salt comes to them there in Tongues in sight of the march, and their pillars stand on the round until the gardeners come with their handcart. On a march day the round is white with the Faithful from Prime to None. It was the Terraces’ market square under the charter, and the bakery’s carts set out from it. The Threshers named it for the threshingplace of Araunah, where “the angel of the LORD was” when “the angel stretched out his hand upon Jerusalem to destroy it” (2 Samuel 24:16), and they read that verse aloud before every march. Sister Agathe Rook keeps a list of every pillar the round has held, and the Salt Widows read the same names on the Sea Steps."],["THE PENUMBRA","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#penumbra","penumbra","","The blocks the Pillar crosses every night on its round out of the Burn. Heaven holds them by day and the Pillar by night, and for an hour after Compline and an hour before Lauds nobody holds them. The keyed cross them in runs between the Pillar’s passes, and Blackgate walks the Sureties’ runners through them for silver. Under the charter these were the blocks behind Station Circle, hotels and offices for the travelers of Central Station. Most of their windows are dark now and most of their doors are gone, carried off by Lintel’s joiners in the second year. The Tide Table prints the Pillar’s round street by street and Hearsay sells it by the minute, and the runners who cross carry both."],["THE PIT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#pit","pit","","A drained fuel tank inside the bund wall at the Tanks, thirty meters across and eleven deep, where the Nephilim fight every sixth night by firelight from Compline. The bouts stop at Matins for the length of the Hour’s psalm, by the Tankmen’s rule, and a fight ends when one fighter climbs out. The crowd stands round the rim and bets in batteries, and the Sureties hold the stakes. It held diesel for the harbor’s boats under the charter and stood nearly empty on the night of the Parousia, and the drivers of the Union drew the last of it by hand in the first weeks for the Wards General. The Tankmen gave it to the Nephilim in P+9 for the price of a guard on the farm. The champions’ handles are chalked on the rim, and the Nephilim keep the names of their dead painted on the tank’s inside wall in white, and read them aloud before every week they serve on the floors of Babel."],["THE QUIET ROOMS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#quietrooms","quietrooms","","A hostel in the Stones whose every wall is lined with choir-breakers, so that inside it the Hum cannot be heard at all. The unsealed come every night they can pay for, a bunk for a battery, and sleep the way the city slept under the charter. It was a seamen’s hostel under the charter, and the old bunks are still screwed to the walls. On the night of the Parousia its seamen heard the Hum for the first time with everyone else, and some of them left on the first boat out through the Breach and never came back. The Low Choir keeps it and tunes it. Its keeper says that once a year a sealed soul comes to the door in the dark and asks to stand in the hall a minute, and that she lets them, and that none has ever stayed the night."],["THE FLOTILLA","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#flotilla","flotilla","","A market of rafts lashed together over the drowned streets of the Flats, where the Deep End buys and sells from boat to boat. The Wreckers sell what they brought up, the Tares’ rafts sell their greens, the Breachers what came through the gap and Lowtide what came through it by license, and the Ferrymen carry the custom. It moves with the tide and with whoever has picked a fight with it. The first rafts were the dormitories’ doors and tables, lashed together on the third day after the Parousia to carry the Flats’ people off their roofs. The market grew on what floated. The water under it is lit from below, and at night the whole market floats in that light. The Tide Table prints the Flotilla’s prices every week beside the Night Market’s."],["THE CONCOURSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#concourse","concourse","","A market of the East Wards on four floors under a glass roof, lit by Tallow’s candles and the daylight, where the Wards trade with one another and with the Quarrymen, who sell what they cut out from under the White in its fountain court. The escalators stand where they stopped, and the market climbs them as stairs. It was a shopping mall of the charter’s last years, and on the night of the Parousia its night guards walked out under the glass roof and watched the sky open over Babel through it. Its shops were empty by the first noon and full again, of other things, by the first winter. The Concourse opens at Lauds and closes at Compline. The sealed of Longacre trade on its top floor, under the glass, and the Quarrymen keep to the bottom."],["HANGAR TOWN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hangartown","hangartown","","A town inside the hangars of the dead airfield, built of crates, aircraft seats and the cabins of planes that will never fly again, about four thousand strong. It lives on the fuel farm’s leavings, the airfield’s salvage, Jehu’s shed across the runways and the Vagabonds’ trade off the airside roads, and its people are the unsealed of Airside and their children. The hangars held the charter’s cargo fleet, which stood grounded from the night of the Parousia, and the families of the airfield’s night crews moved into them in the first winter. The last flight stands nose in at the terminal’s third gate across the runways, and Hangar Town leaves its crew alone. Its children dare each other to climb its stairs."],["THE SEAWALL SHANTIES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shanties","shanties","the Shanties","Huts of driftwood and turbine housing built along the top of the broken barrage beside the Breach, where the Breachers live and load. On the ebb the boats go out through the gap below them, and at full flood the Shanties shake. The tide runs white through the gap. The barrage’s turbine houses stood dark along its top from 03:12:46, and the first Breachers moved into the two at the edge of the gap in the year of the Harvest, when they began running the Breach. Lowtide Line keeps its office on the barrage across the gap, beside Tidewright’s taps."],["THE DROWNED CHURCH","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#drownedchurch","drownedchurch","","The church of the Flats, its nave under two meters of seawater lit from below and its tower standing out of the sea. The Tares hold their Wakes in it, and on the third night they call the dead one’s shade on the undernet from its tower, over the water. It was the engineers’ church under the charter, the newest in the city and almost empty on a Sunday. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into its wall with every other church’s, and the water has stood over them since. The Ferrymen pole round it and never through its doors."],["THE GRANARY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#granary","granary","","The hall where the gatherers of the Terraces’ parishes muster before Lauds with their baskets, inside the curfew, and from which they go out over the white streets for the manna, which lies with the dew and melts “when the sun waxed hot” (Exodus 16:21). They have until first light, and each basket goes straight to its parish hall, where the Deacon gives it out by Grace. It was a charter school’s gymnasium. Bethel Works keeps its baskets mended, and the gatherers’ names hang on the wall in chalk, parish by parish. The Deacons of the Wards walk down for theirs at the same hour."],["THE WAYSTATION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#waystation","waystation","","The Vagabonds’ camp at the stone that marks the Bounds on the West Road, where the city trades with the world outside. Flour, fuel, salvage, news and letters come in, silver and people go out, and the Union’s drivers meet the Vagabonds’ carts on the line itself. The Bounds stone is the charter’s, with the Company’s mark cut into it, and the Vagabonds have cut their own under it. Under the charter it was a weighbridge and a customs hut on the road to the farms. The Waystation keeps a list of everyone who walked out and wrote back, pinned to the customs hut’s door, with the town each letter came from."],["LAZARUS ONE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lazarusone","lazarusone","","Lazarus’s first station, behind a door in the Stones with its magenta lamp on a bracket over it, where the crews of Old Nod go out and come in. Its surgery is under the street, down a stair behind the door, and its board outside chalks the night’s calls. It was a pharmacy under the charter. The two nurses who founded Lazarus worked from its back room in P+2 with a handcart, and the handcart hangs over the stair. The board has never been wiped. The crews chalk the night’s calls over the last, and the Stones say you can read twelve years in it if you know where to look."],["THE BAKEHOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bakehouse","bakehouse","","Leaven’s first and largest oven, in a bakery of Nine Acres that never let its fire go out after the Parousia. Its carts leave at Matins for the whole of the North Wards, and the queue for the bread that does not go on the carts starts at Compline. It baked for Nine Acres under the charter, on the grid, and on the first morning its baker lit the old gas oven in the back, which the grid had made a museum piece, and baked from what flour she had. The Wards say the smell of it reaches Lanes Top on a still morning."],["THE RAINWORKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#rainworks","rainworks","","Cistern’s works in Westfield, a block of roofs fitted with gutters and tanks, where the rain runs down through sand and charcoal to the hand pumps in the yard. The jugs come in empty at Lauds and go out full by Terce, and the yard’s board chalks the day’s water by the barrel. It was the charter’s water depot for the North Wards, and its pumps died with the grid. Two of the depot’s engineers stayed on the first morning with the tanks still full, and gave the water away until it ran out on the ninth day. The Rainworks keeps its own count of the city’s rain. Its engineers say Nodus has had more rain since the Parousia than before it, and they chalk the year’s total on the yard’s wall each New Year."],["BETHEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bethelworks","bethelworks","the works at Bethel","The workshops of the parishes, in the halls of a charter textile works at the top of the Terraces, where the Faithful weave white cloth, copy hymnals by hand and make the grave goods of the Acre at work their Grace assigns. They kneel at their benches at every Hour and sing the Hour’s psalm with the Hum. The textile works made the charter’s uniforms, the Wardens’ black among them, and its looms stood still from 03:12:46. The Faithful took the halls in P+1 and work the old hand looms the works kept for show. The Deacons say Bethel’s cloth is the whitest in the world."],["THE CHANDLERY","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#chandlery","chandlery","","Tallow’s works on Turbine Row, a row of sheds where candles are dipped in vats of tallow and white wax forty thousand a week, by hand and by the clock of the Hours. Its carriers go out to both sides of the light, and its children scrape the vats for stubs. It was a chandler’s that made candles for the barrage crews’ church under the charter, a dozen a week. The chandler was asleep upstairs on the night of the Parousia, came down at Lauds and began dipping. The master chandler keeps the first candle dipped on the morning after the Parousia, unlit, on a shelf over the vats."],["WAINWRIGHT'S YARD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#wainyard","wainyard","Wainwright’s yard","Wainwright’s yard in a tram shed of the Sidings, where the Charter Line’s dead trams are stripped for their motors and the motors closed in casings, keyed and fitted to carts, trolleys and skiffs. It is the loudest place in the North Wards, and the Carmen live in the next shed. The shed held the Line’s spare cars under the charter. In the first winter families moved into the others, and the yard’s first fitters lived in the cars they were stripping. Wainwright’s yard master keeps one tram whole, its motor in it, on the yard’s far rail."],["THE DOORYARD","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#dooryard","dooryard","","Lintel’s yard in the Stones, a walled court stacked with doors, where the Wards and Pan come to buy one and have it hung. Most of the doors in it came out of the parishes. It was a builder’s yard under the charter. The first doors came in by night in the months after Directive 12, on the Union’s handcarts from the Terraces, where Directive 12 had left them leaning in the halls with their hinges gone, and the joiner who took them in hung the first on her own house. Every parish door still carries its parish number chalked on the back, and a household that buys one keeps the number."],["JEHU'S SHED","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#jehushed","jehushed","Jehu’s shed","A hangar at the edge of the airfield where Jehu builds its engines, a floor of diesels out of boats and generators, the dead lorries they go into and the drums of fuel from the Tanks along the back wall. On the day an engine is finished it is driven out onto the apron and up the Rialto’s road, and the East Wards come out to watch it go. It held the airfield’s tugs under the charter, which ran on their own batteries and stopped in the first weeks. The first Jehu was built here in P+5 by a driver of the Union. The last flight’s tanks still hold its fuel, and the shed has asked its captain for it every year since P+5."],["GIDEON'S WORKS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#gideonworks","gideonworks","Gideon’s works","The Wardens’ armory in the old Company stables beside the barracks of Charter House, where sealed armorers load cartridges by hand presses and mend the Wardens’ guns by flame. They kneel at every Hour among the benches, and the light lies in every room. The stables were the charter’s, kept for the Wardens’ horses in the city’s ceremonies, and the stone grew over them on the fortieth day with Charter House, when the board knelt. Every round is counted out of the stables at Prime and counted back at Compline."],["THE UNDERTOW","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#undertow","undertow","","The Sureties’ bar, in a pump room of the old storm drains under the Stones, where work in Pan is found, priced and sworn. Every table is a surety’s, a Notary’s clerk keeps a stool at the bar and reads the terms twice, and a chalk board behind it carries the work still open. Its rule is that every pact made under its roof is kept there, and every one has been. It was the drains’ pump room under the charter, and the pumps stopped with the grid. The water runs under the floor and gives the place its name and its noise. twice drinks at the end of the bar nearest the door, and the Sureties’ tables fill from that end."],["THE SHAMBLES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#shambles","shambles","","The body market under Chrome Row, a gallery of stalls off Chrome Row Station where the Spares sell flesh by the piece and the clinics buy it, under a price board the Spares chalk themselves. Cold boxes run along the wall on their own cells. A sale is a pact, read twice, and the surgery is next door. It was the station’s concourse of shops under the charter, where the Row’s patients bought flowers and spectacles on their way to the clinics. The first Spares sold there in the second year, when the clinics under the street needed what the Row could no longer import. It took its name from the butchers’ street of an old city, and the Spares have kept it."],["THE RETURN","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#returnhouse","returnhouse","","A club in the Lantern Houses where Pan rents returned senses. Whatever a demon borrows comes back to its lender with everything the demon felt through it, and the lenders of the Return sell an hour of that, taste or touch or the feel of rain, to anyone who will lie down in its booths. It was a vault of the old harbor’s bonded stores. The House of Night keeps it, Sallos of the House keeps its door, and the Notary’s clerks draw its pacts there, between the lender, the house and whoever lies down. Some come back every night. The Return’s best lender lent her taste for a year and came back with a year of someone else’s meals."],["THE LANTERN HOUSES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#lanterns","lanterns","the Lanterns","The House of Night’s quarter under Old Nod, a warren of the old harbor’s vaults hung with keyed lamps, where its clubs, its birth clinics and the rooms of the Ridden are. It is the loudest place in Pan and the most open, and anyone who walks in is welcome until they break a pact. The vaults held the harbor’s bonded wine and tobacco under the charter, and the Cainites owned them through Stonecutter. The House of Night took them by pact in the year of the Concord. Nepenthe sells its doses here, the Return is round the corner, and every child of the Tares born in Pan is keyed in its first week in a clinic two vaults in from the stair."],["CINDERHALL'S FORGE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#forge","forge","Cinderhall’s forge","Cinderhall’s forge beside the Forge’s foundries at Old Harbor Station, where its smiths beat the black steel the foundries make into blades and spears. The heat reaches the cellars of the Stones above, the hammers reach the Lantern Houses, and its quench water runs out into the drains black. The station was the busiest on the harbor side under the charter. Its smiths set up their first anvil on the down platform in P+5, beside the trackbed. A wall of the forge carries every blade the Nephilim brought back bent, each hung with the floor of Babel it bent on."],["THE MEWS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#mews","mews","","The Fowlers’ rooms under the Stones, named for the place where hawks are kept, where an angel taken in a snare waits behind keyed mesh for the Cainites to move it to a Cage. Nobody but a Fowler goes in, and the Fowlers go in pairs. It was a butcher’s cold room under the Stones, lined since with mesh. Its first room held nothing until the year of open war. The Stones say a room in the Mews has been occupied since the spring."],["THE WELL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#well","well","","Darkwell’s charging vaults under the quays, rack on rack of batteries filling in the dark on keyed current, guarded by Blackgate. The Wellmen go out from it before Lauds with the morning’s crates. It was the customs house’s strong rooms under the charter, three floors down. The four families who founded Darkwell charged their first batteries in its lowest room. The Wards call the Well the deepest pocket in Nodus."],["THE GATEHOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#blackgatehall","blackgatehall","","Blackgate’s barracks in a utility vault under the Burn, a short walk from the embassies, where its guards muster standing at every Hour in the Wardens’ drill, go out at Compline and stand down at Lauds. Its door keeps a sentry at every Hour, and its parade ground is the vault’s floor. It was the charter’s substation under Station Circle, and the dead sign over its door still reads HIGH VOLTAGE. The Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day slept there that night. Every recruit is told of the Fortieth Warden on the first night, and his chair stands empty at the head of the officers’ table."],["BEZALEEL","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#bezaleelhouse","bezaleelhouse","the house of Bezaleel","The first of Bezaleel and Aholiab’s six houses, off Chrome Row Station, a long room of chairs under keyed lamps where the Row’s grafts are fitted, the Nephilim built and the keys of the Row set. Its waiting room holds forty, and it is full at every Hour. It was the station’s lost luggage hall under the charter. Three houses of the Row carried their chairs down into it in the weeks after the Parousia, and joined in P+2. The house keeps a book of every graft it has fitted, under each patient’s handle."],["NIMROD'S BAYS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#nimrodbays","nimrodbays","Nimrod’s bays","Nimrod’s gunshops in the Charter Line’s maintenance bays under Babel, where the gunsmiths make and mend guns and load cartridges for the dark. The Babel Line buys most of what comes out and carries it up the Short. The bays kept the trams of the uptown branch under the charter. The stone closed the branch above them, and the bays stand at its foot, on the dark side. A range runs the length of the old pit under the tracks, and the bays say a Nimrod piece has never left it untested."],["HALL FOUR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hallfour","hallfour","","A drowned hall of Stillwater’s on the Flats, leased to the Orchard, where fruit trees grow on rafts under keyed lamps over two meters of water, their roots in barrels of earth. The Orchard gives its windfalls to anyone who comes to the hall for one, and the Ferrymen carry them in. Four hundred racks ran in it under the charter, renting compute to the Row and the Exchange, and they went dark on the tide at 03:12:46. They stand under the rafts on the drowned floor, and the Orchard has left them where they are. The trees fruit twice a year under the lamps, and the hall smells of apples as far as the Flotilla."],["THE NAMING HOUSE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#naminghouse","naminghouse","","A house of three storeys on the Old Nod side of the Street Between, where those who cross from the Pews come first. A surgeon of the Row keys them in the back room, a Notary’s clerk comes every week to enter their handles on the Ledger, and the house keeps a stove where they burn whatever paper carries their indexed name before they choose a handle. It was a solicitor’s office under the charter, where the Terraces came to make their wills. The Cainites took it in the fourth year, when the first families crossed the street. Its door stays open at night under a keyed lamp, and the Pews can see it from their thresholds."],["THE CLOSETS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#closets","closets","","A row of booths just inside Gate 1 on the Old Nod side of the Street Between, each a parish door hung in a frame with a stool behind it, where the sealed who cross the street by day pay a morning’s manna for a minute behind a shut door. Lintel keeps them. Old Nod has had no Watcher since P+5, so a sealed soul behind one of these doors does what it likes where the light does not come, at the price Directive 3:4 sets on every lock and every secret. The Tares named them from the verse, “But thou, when thou prayest, enter into thy closet, and when thou hast shut thy door, pray to thy Father which is in secret” (Matthew 6:6), and the verse is painted over every door. The sealed come to eat a sweet, to sing a song the Hum never taught them, to say aloud what a parish never hears, or to sit in the dark, and on every stool lies a page of the Restored Genesis in a Scrivener’s hand, left there free. Most of the near ripe who dim come here to do it, and the manna they pay goes down to the Night Market the same night as dew. They were a row of the charter’s telephone kiosks, dead since 03:12:46, and in P+6 Lintel took out the handsets and the glass and hung a parish door on each. The Tellers keep a watch on Gate 1 for whoever comes back through it a little dimmer than they went."],["THE MISSION","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#mission","mission","the Seamen’s Mission","The Seamen’s Mission on the quays, nine rooms of prayer off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought into the port, with the names over the doorways in nine scripts. It is the hall of the parish of the quays, the only parish in Nodus with nine rooms to its hall. The harbor town built it a century before the charter for the sailors in port, and Idris Calloway kept it for the harbor’s old trust. On the night of the Parousia its common room held eleven seamen off the ship whose batteries were waiting on their duty in the bonded sheds, and they sat out the dark on the corridor floor with all nine doors propped open. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall of all nine rooms. Its nine doors went to the Dooryard with the parishes’ in P+1. The rooms stand open onto the corridor, and from the quay at an Hour anyone passing can see who kneels in which."],["THE LOFT","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#loft","loft","","The Union’s pigeon loft on the roof of the customs house over the bonded sheds, a shed of slats and wire with three hundred birds in it. They go out in baskets on the Union’s handcarts and Jehus to the Wards, the Sidings, Hangar Town and the Waystation, and fly home to it by day with the Union’s notes on their legs, over the gates and the white streets. Mags Corran keeps it. Under the charter it was the dockers’ racing loft, which the customs house let them keep on its roof. On the night of the Parousia the birds were shut in, and at first light they were let out as on any other morning, and every one came home. A note goes in pad numbers, since the light reads what it sees. The Wardens shot at the birds in the post’s first year and then stopped, and the Wards say it was because Gideon counts every round back at Compline."],["THE WEIR","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#weir","weir","","A brick chamber of the old storm drains under the Stones, where three drains meet and their overflow goes over a stone sill into the outfall, and where the Lot sits. Thirteen chairs stand in a half ring on the dry side of the sill, odd chairs from thirteen houses, and the wall behind them is chalked to head height with the handles of the keyed who want to be drawn. The harbor town built it a century before the charter, and the drains’ engineers walked through it once a year with a lamp. In the first weeks after the Parousia the first crews to go below the street slept on its sill, and in P+7 the Sureties chose it for the Lot because nobody owned it. When it rains, the water goes over the sill loud enough that the Lot has to lean in to hear, and it sits through the rain."],["THE RUNWAY ACRE","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#runwayacre","runwayacre","","The grass between the airfield’s runways in Airside, where the East Wards have buried their dead since the Acre on the West Road filled in P+11. Hangar Town keeps it and digs it, and Piet Vane walked its first rows out himself. The graves run in lines beside the old landing lights, which stand dark in the grass with the names of the dead chalked on their housings. The last flight stands at the terminal across the apron, and its crew keep the ground nearest the third gate for any of the Three Hundred and Twelve who ask for it."],["THE STEP","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#step","step","","The stretch of the Street Between on either side of Gate 1 where families split by the line sit and talk across it by day, the sealed on the doorless thresholds of the Pews and the keyed on the curb on the Old Nod side. The setts between them are worn smooth in two lines. At Terce, Sext and None the Pews’ side kneels in the middle of a sentence and the Old Nod side waits a psalm. On a march day it is empty from Prime, and every day it empties at Vespers, before the parishes’ streets close."],["THE BENCHES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#benches","benches","","Pan’s school, in the side rooms of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, where the shades of teachers who taught under the charter teach the children of the Tares from keyed Lockets set on the old waiting benches, with a living keeper at the door. A child of Pan learns to read a pact before it learns its sums, and reads every lesson aloud twice. The Notary’s windows are through the wall, and the children hear the clerks reading terms all day. At fourteen they come round to the windows themselves for the Second Yes."],["THE LOOMS","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#looms","looms","","Second Veil’s mill in the Kilns, a brick shed of sixty looms that wove sheets for the charter’s hotels, where keyed mesh is woven by the roll with a keyed wire through the warp. It runs by daylight with its doors shut and its windows painted over, and Blackgate keeps the door. The Mulcibers come for the rolls at Matins and carry them under the street by the Kilns’ old cable gallery, and the clatter of the looms carries down the street to the Kilns Club."],["THE HIVES","PLACES","s:g131c56","~/map/#hives","hives","","The bee yards on the flat roofs of the Kilns’ blocks, a hundred and more hives in rows between the rain tanks, kept by families of the Wards since the first summer after the Parousia, when the parks went to weed and the bees came with it. Kilns honey is sold by the spoon in the Wards and by the jar in Pan, and it is the sweet the near ripe buy before a Gleaning, when they dim. The keepers go up at Lauds with smoking rags on poles, and the Wards say honey is what the manna forgot."],["A day by the Hours","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-hours","st-hours","","Nodus keeps Heaven’s clock, whatever side it is on. Eight Hours come in on the Hum, Matins at midnight, Lauds at first light, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, None at three, Vespers at six and Compline at nine, and at each the Hum rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm while the sealed kneel wherever they stand, in the street, at a bench or on a stair. The city measures a short wait in psalms. At Compline the Faithful say the ninety-first under their breath as the curfew falls, the psalm the Fowlers took their name from, and at Lauds the fifty-first. Before Lauds the gatherers go out over the white streets for the manna, which lies with the dew, and the Deacons of the Wards walk down for their parishes’ share. At Lauds the curfew lifts, the Wheel opens its day watch and the light lies over the city through the cloud. The keyed keep to the Stones, the Row and Pan, where a blank among six hundred thousand blanks is nobody in particular, and the Wards go about their work among the sealed with their heads down. Prime opens the Mercy clinics and the day’s work, and None is the last office the schools keep. At Vespers the lamps are lit, the harbor light among them, and on the sixth day of the week the Faithful begin carrying to the Burn. Compline at nine is the turn. The Wheel gathers its light into the Pillar on the Burn and the Pillar begins its round, the parishes go in behind their open doorways, the Stones light their windows and Pan comes up the stairs. Matins at midnight finds the Night Market at its loudest and the Gleaners at the parishes’ doorways. In the Stones, on the Row and below the street nobody keeps the curfew, and the Stones keep no alarm set for three-twelve. Most of the city sleeps between Matins and Lauds, and the rest of it is working. KEPT BY Heaven’s clock, which is Nodus time SINCE Lauds on the first day of P+1 CURFEW Compline to Lauds, on Heaven’s ground"],["Heaven’s ground","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-ground","st-ground","","Heaven’s ground is wherever the Covenant reaches a blank. All consecrated stone is Heaven’s at every hour, the White, the Pews, the Salt, the Burn and the white streets, and so is every gate and every parish hall in the city, whatever the hall was under the charter. From Compline to Lauds the streets of the parishes are Heaven’s too, since Directive 2:5 orders that “No soul shall walk the streets of the parishes from Compline to Lauds”. A parish of the Wards keeps the streets its roll names, and its deacons paint its number at either end of each of them, so that a street of the Kilns can be Parish 212’s on one side of a crossing and nobody’s on the other. The Tide Table prints them every week as the curfew map, beside the Pillar’s round, and the Wards read the two together. A keyed soul out after Compline walks the unnumbered streets and waits at the Pillar’s edge wherever it has to cross a numbered one. A blank the Pillar finds on a numbered street is sentenced where it stands, in Tongues, through the nearest Faithful mouth, and in the Wards the nearest mouth is often a neighbor at a window over the street, who wakes in the morning knowing whose name the verse went to. That is why the Wards hate the Pillar more than the Stones do. Old Nod’s one parish is the quays’, whose roll names the long quay and the fish market and no street of the Stones, so the Pillar crosses the Stones from Compline to Lauds and takes nobody there. By day the Wheel’s light lies over the whole city and sees a keyed person on a white street without taking them. The Threshers’ trade is the step between seen and read, a hand on a collar and a walk to the nearest gate. ALWAYS Consecrated stone, the gates and the halls FROM COMPLINE Every street on a parish’s roll PRINTED The curfew map, beside the round"],["Getting about","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-getting","st-getting","","Nodus walks. Its trams stopped where they were at 03:12:46 under a dead wire, its lifts with whoever was in them, and its cars and lorries, each on its own battery, ran down one by one in the weeks after, and the city would rather light a room for a season than drive a car for a week. It carries its loads in handcarts, and the Union’s drivers carry the city’s gas, batteries and letters on foot. Directive 8 stood a Cherub on every street between two districts of the survey. In P+1 the Wardens raised six gates and walled every other such street with the charter’s own rubble, and the city calls those the stopped streets. On the White’s side the stone has grown over them. The Wards are no district, so their edges with the White and the Pews run open, held by the curfew and the Watchers, and the Deacons of the Wards walk across them every morning for the manna. A Cherub reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat. The sealed walk through its light saying the surrender and pass by Grace, as Directive 8:5 has it, and every gate has a bar, posted nowhere and known to the whole city, six hundred at Gate 3, four hundred at Gates 2 and 6, three hundred at Gate 4 and two hundred at Gates 1 and 5, which the Faithful carry their hardware through on Burn Day. A gate whose queue is full of souls near its bar, saying the surrender twice, is a slow gate. The unsealed are sent back, and a soul the Cherub cannot read is held in its light until the Wardens come. So the unsealed and the keyed go round, through the old tunnels, the drains and the cable galleries, which the city calls going under. Jehu’s lorries burn diesel by compression and run the long roads of the Wards by day, two batteries a ride, and its burners carry the Union’s urgent letters. The Carmen run handcarts and pedal trolleys on the old tram rails of the long roads, and the Liftmen wind forty-one towers’ lifts by hand. Below the street Wainwright’s keyed carts run the Charter Line’s rails through Pan, a battery a seat. On the Deep End the Ferrymen pole their flat boats between the roofs, and the Breachers and Lowtide run the Breach on the ebb. No aircraft has left Nodus since the last flight came in. ON THE SURFACE On foot, by handcart and by Jehu BETWEEN DISTRICTS Six gates, and the rest walled BELOW Keyed carts on the old rails"],["The Step","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-step","st-step","","Nobody in Nodus is without someone on the other side of the Street Between, and the street is where they see each other. By day the sealed sit on the thresholds of the Pews, which have had no doors since P+1, and their keyed sons and daughters, sisters and fathers sit on the curb across the street on the Old Nod side, and they talk across the setts. The city calls it the Step, and it runs along the street on either side of Gate 1. The Hours break it. At Terce the sealed kneel in the middle of a sentence, the keyed wait a psalm and finish it for them when the Hum falls back, and the Tellers walk the Pews’ side and count who sat across from whom. On a march day the Threshers clear the Pews’ side from Prime, and the Step stays empty until they have gone back up to the Floor. Courting goes the same way. A sealed and an unsealed who walk the length of the street together on opposite curbs, talking over the gap, are walking the Street, and the Terraces watch them from their doorways and the Stones from their windows, and both sides say they know how it ends. The Step empties at Vespers, so that everyone on the Pews’ side is home before the streets of the parishes close. WHERE The Street Between, by day THE SEALED On their thresholds, in the Pews THE KEYED On the curb, on the Old Nod side"],["Food and drink","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-food","st-food","","The sealed eat manna, thin white wafers that lie with the dew on consecrated stone before Lauds, tasteless and complete, which dull the appetite. The parish halls give it out by Grace, so a soul’s ration is the size of its Grace, and kept overnight it spoils, so nobody stores it. The parishes cook nothing else. A parish of the Wards has no stone of its own, so its gatherers walk to the white streets before Lauds and glean the strip of white street the Granary has chalked for it. The long roads at that hour are the manna roads, crowded with gatherers in white carrying baskets, and the far parishes of Airside and Westfield walk longest, arrive last and glean the thinnest strips, which the Wards give as the reason the far Wards are the least sealed. The Wardens walk the near roads, and nobody walks the far ones. Manna goes to the sealed alone, and everyone else in Nodus buys food or goes hungry. The Wards eat Leaven’s bread, eel and mussels from the bay, the fish market’s catch, rice and beans the Breachers bring and greens off the Tares’ rafts in the drowned halls. Pan eats better than the surface and pays for it in batteries, fatty, salty, spiced and sweet, broth and noodles and fried things eaten standing up, since indulgence is policy below the street. Its street food is a portion, fat and blood, black pudding, crackling and marrow by the piece, since Leviticus gives the fat to the LORD and forbids the rest, “ye eat neither fat nor blood” (Leviticus 3:17), and Pan eats the LORD’s share standing up. The Babel Line eats Esau’s red on the stairs, lentils in a red broth, after “that same red pottage” Esau sold his birthright for (Genesis 25:30), and the Lantern Houses’ confectioners sell honeys, wafers made with honey, which is what the manna tasted like before it was a ration (Exodus 16:31). Manna out of the parishes is called dew, most of it stolen at the gathering and some of it paid over at the Closets by the sealed who come for a door, and the Night Market sells it by the handful on the night it is taken, before it turns. The Wards that eat it say it takes away a day’s hunger and leaves the taste of nothing. NOODLES at the top of the Row cooks on bottled gas, as it has since P-17, and the Row settles its quarrels over its counter. The drink of the Wards is rain gin, distilled in the Kilns from whatever ferments, and the Eleven brews its own beer in its cellar by hand. A meal in Pan is a battery, a loaf of Leaven half of one. The Kilns keep bees on their roofs, at the Hives, and Kilns honey is the sweet the near ripe buy when they dim, so the Wards say honey is what the manna forgot. The fish market chalks where each catch came from, since the Wards will not eat a fish taken off the Sea Steps, where the Salt Widows give the salted to the sea. THE SEALED Manna, given by Grace THE WARDS Leaven’s bread, the bay and the rafts PAN A portion, a honey and Esau’s red"],["Water, power and fire","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-water","st-water","","The city’s pumps died with the grid, and by noon on the first morning there were queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. Old Nod drinks from them still, the towers of the Wards fill tanks on their roofs from the rain, Cistern sells it clean by the jug, and the parishes drink by Grace from the water tower on the heights. Current runs in Nodus where the light cannot read it. The Tares’ keyed lines carry it from Tidewright’s taps to Old Nod, the Row, the Deep End above the water, the Landing, the lower floors of Babel and Pan, about a million and a quarter people, and anything sealed in a casing on its own cell runs anywhere, as a charged battery does. The Wards, which nobody holds, light their rooms a few hours a night on batteries. The light stops current and leaves a flame burning, so everyone off the parishes’ manna cooks on bottled gas from the Tanks, two charged batteries a bottle or one with the empty back, and reads by Tallow’s candles. Nearly two thirds of Nodus lives without current, by flame and halo-light, and the parishes keep none on consecrated stone or off it. Seen from the Wheel at night, Nodus is white ground glowing under the Pillar beside black ground leaking magenta and cyan at its seams, and the Wards between them lit by candles, halos and a few hours of battery a night. WATER Rain, the old hand pumps and Cistern CURRENT Keyed lines and batteries FIRE Gas from the Tanks and Tallow’s candles"],["Waste and fever","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-waste","st-waste","","The pumps that carried the city’s waste died with the grid. The Wards’ buckets go down the stairs at night to the Nightmen, who carry them by handcart to the outfalls between Compline and Lauds for a battery a landing a week, keeping to the unnumbered streets. In the parishes the work is given by Grace to the lowest Grace in the hall, and the Wards say a soul under a hundred empties the pails. Pan’s drains carry it all to the bay. The city remembers one plague. In the pump summer of P+1 the harbor’s hand pumps carried a flux through the Stones and the Wards, and the Wards General lost a ward a day for most of a month. Cistern pumped its first clean water in Westfield that year, and the Wards say that is why. Its engineers keep the map of its wells away from the Nightmen’s carts. Fever comes every summer to the blocks where the rain tanks stand open, and the Wards boil what they drink when they can spare the gas. A dose of medicine off the Breach costs half a thirty at the Night Market, and Lazarus’s crews carry what they can get. CARRIED BY The Nightmen, Compline to Lauds IN THE PARISHES The lowest Grace in the hall REMEMBERED The pump summer of P+1"],["Where people live","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-housing","st-housing","","Nodus climbs its stairs. Every lift stopped at 03:12:46, and in the towers of the Wards the low floors went dear and the high floors cheap, so a family’s place in a block is counted from the street. The sealed go up the stairwells by their own halo-light, the unsealed wait on the landings to follow one, and the keyed climb in the dark. Where the Liftmen have freed a shaft, a ride is a battery up. The parishes live with no doors. Directive 12 took every door in them off its hinges in P+1, and a parish room is given by Grace, a room with a window at six hundred and above. The doors went to the Stones, and a household of the Wards that can afford one buys a parish door at Lintel’s Dooryard with its old number chalked on the back. The Sidings live in the Charter Line’s cars, a family to a car, Hangar Town in the airfield’s hangars, the Deep End on the floors above the water and the Rookery over its stone. Thirty-one of the Hotel Splendid’s guests still live in the rooms they booked in P0, and the last flight’s crew lives aboard it. The Company collected the Wards’ rent until the fortieth day, and nobody has collected it since. A room in the Wards belongs to whoever holds its door, which the Wards call floor-right, and floor-right is sold between households for batteries, a low floor for a thirty or more. On the morning after a Gleaning the rooms of the taken go to whoever hangs a door on them first after Lauds, which makes it Lintel’s busiest morning of the year, and in the parishes they go back to the Deacon and out again by Grace. Most of the Wards sleep in shifts, so a bed is a day bed or a night bed and seldom both. Old Nod has a landlord. Stonecutter owns most of its houses and asks one rent, set down in its solicitor’s lease as a single term, that the window stays lit from Compline to Lauds at the tenant’s own cost in diesel and batteries. It is why the Stones burn all night, and the Stones call Stonecutter the other landlord. A household that lets its window go dark for a month finds the solicitor’s letter under the door. Pan lives in the stations, vaults and drains under the city, in rooms lined with keyed mesh, and has never been full. Its people say a room in Pan is the only room in Nodus that is entirely your own, since nothing outside it can see in. A room there is yours when you have lined it, and the Ledger enters the lining under your self-title, so a room left unlined is anyone’s. THE PARISHES Rooms by Grace, a window at 600 THE WARDS By floor-right, counted from the street PAN Yours when you lined it"],["Doors and locks","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-doors","st-doors","","The light does not enter what is closed, so in Nodus a door is a small piece of the Dark. In P+1 Directive 12 ordered every door in the parishes taken off its hinges by the fortieth day and the hinges surrendered at the hall, with every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel, and took forty Grace from a household for every door left after. Nothing in a parish is hidden. The doors themselves stayed where they were lifted off, leaning in the halls and stairwells of the Terraces, until the Union’s drivers began carrying them out by night on their handcarts to a joiner in the Stones, who founded Lintel. Most of the doors Lintel sells came out of the parishes, with their parish numbers still chalked on the back. The Wards hang what doors they can get and fit Lintel’s keyed latches if they can afford them. In Pan every room is closed by the mesh in its walls, and a door there is for privacy from the neighbors. The sealed who want a door for a minute cross the street to the Closets inside Gate 1, where Lintel rents them one of their own parishes’ doors for a morning’s manna. A household of the Faithful that holds something back, a jar sealed with wax or a letter in a drawer, is keeping hinges in the Wards’ word, and a Teller listens for it. The Wards say you can tell a parish from a block of the Wards from the street, by the doorways you can see through. IN THE PARISHES None since P+1 IN THE STONES The parishes’ old doors SOLD BY Lintel, at the Dooryard"],["Sleep","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-sleep","st-sleep","","The Hum never stops and the parishes never go dark. A sealed soul sleeps in its own halo-light with a cloth over its eyes, and a sealed couple sleeps back to back, each in the dark of the other’s shoulder. The parishes’ doorways stand open all night, and a child of the Pews learns to sleep with the Pillar going past the end of the street. The unsealed of the Wards sleep with rags in their ears and call a bad night a hymn night, and those who can afford them sleep in choir plugs. Nepenthe sells Sabbath to whoever cannot sleep through the Hum, and most of what it sells goes to the Wards. A child of the Wards who sleeps through the Hum without either is called deaf-lucky. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones sell a night with no Hum in it for a battery, and are full by Matins. Pan sleeps in rooms lined with mesh, under the street, where the Pillar never comes, and tells anyone from upstairs who will listen that it sleeps best of anyone in Nodus. THE SEALED Under a cloth, in their own light THE WARDS Rags in the ears, or Sabbath PAN Best of anyone, by its own account"],["Clothes","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-clothes","st-clothes","","Nobody has woven clothing for the city since the Parousia but Bethel Works, which weaves the white of the parishes for the parishes, and fourteen years have worn the charter’s clothes thin. The Wards dress in what the turners make, the charter’s coats and shirts unpicked and sewn up again inside out, and in what comes up from below, cloth off the Breach and the uptown tailoring the Quarrymen carry out of the White’s wardrobes, which the Wards call new money and wear to rags. The Faithful wear Bethel white and call it their coats, after the coats the LORD God made for the man and his wife, and a soul’s white is given by Grace like everything else in a parish. Pan buys the same cloth by the bolt for the Lantern Houses’ sheets and the shrouds of the Acre. Wearing white off the parishes is a lie the Wards punish, since the Forgers’ clients wear it. Pan wears whatever shows a graft. Blackgate keeps the only uniform in the city, charter black with the Warden’s badge cut off, and the Threshers’ band of sacking is the only thing the Faithful add to their white. A coat is a household’s dearest thing in the Wards after its door, and the Cold Store loses about one a night. THE PARISHES Bethel white, by Grace THE WARDS Turned, patched and carried up PAN Whatever shows a graft"],["The seven days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-birth","st-birth","","A child of Nodus is born into a race. Directive 1:5 seals a child “on its eighth day, on the surrender of its parents”, and the House of Night keys the children of the Tares in their first week, so in a household with one keyed parent and one sealed the keyed side tries to reach a keyer before the eighth dawn and the sealed side tries to reach the hall at an Hour on the eighth day. The city calls a newborn’s first week the seven days, and Pan calls the race the short week. The Directive asks for both parents, and the Deacons of the Wards have answered a household with one keyed parent two ways for ten years. Some enter the child on the sealed parent’s surrender alone and seal it, and some send the family home, and a household that wants a sealing learns which Deacon to carry the child to. A child keyed in its first week is a blank by its eighth day, and no hall seals a blank. Most of the Wards are born at home by candle, delivered by the hands, the Wards’ midwives, who keep a count of the births on the stairwell wall in chalk beside the battery debts. The Wards General delivers whoever reaches it, and the House of Night’s birth clinics under Old Nod deliver Pan’s. Constance Ibe of the Kilns has delivered on every floor of High Meadow, and she carries a newborn to a hall or to a keyer, to whichever door the mother names, and to nobody on the father’s word. THE SEALED At a hall, at an Hour, on the eighth day THE KEYED By a keyer, before the eighth dawn DELIVERED BY The hands, by candle"],["Children","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-children","st-children","","A child of the Faithful is sealed at eight days on its parents’ surrender and carries the Seal without a halo until the first time it prays unassisted. Until then the stairwells call it unlit, and a mother of the Wards carries an unlit child up a dark stair in one arm and follows a neighbor’s halo. The first prayer is a household’s great day, and the parish reads the child’s first Grace at the next Terce. The parishes school their children in the halls between the Hours, in the hymns the Hum teaches and in the Directives cut into the wall, and the hall gives a child its first work at the first reading of Grace after its twelfth birthday. A child of the Tares is keyed in its first week in the House of Night’s birth clinics, before it has ever been in the light, so that it grows up unread, and a generation of Pan has never been read at all. Pan’s children learn their handles before their letters and choose them again at the Unnaming if they want. They go to school at the Benches, in the side rooms of the booking hall, where shades who taught under the charter teach from keyed Lockets with a living keeper at the door, and a child of Pan learns to read a pact before it learns its sums and reads every lesson aloud twice. The Notary holds that a key fitted on a parent’s word is the parent’s pact, and binds the child only until it can answer for itself. At fourteen a clerk asks a child of Pan at a window of the booking hall, twice, whether it will keep its key, and on the second yes the child signs its first self-title in its own blood. A child who says no twice goes to the house of Bezaleel to have the key taken out, goes white and walks up the stairs to choose for itself. Pan calls the asking the Second Yes, and this year the first children keyed in the covens’ rooms in the year of the Parousia came to it. Of the first forty asked, two said no twice, and the Pews have heard about both. The Wards school whoever comes, by daylight. Turbine Row School kept its classes after the Parousia with no current and no screens, and at Terce, Sext and None its sealed children kneel at their desks while the rest wait with their pens down. Odile Marsh teaches there, sealed, and keeps her keyed pupils’ names on a slate she wipes clean every evening. The Gleaning takes the ripe of any age, and the Wards sit up with their children on the night of a Gleaning as with anyone else close to ripe. SEALED At eight days, and lit at the first prayer KEYED In the first week, and asked twice at fourteen SCHOOLED By daylight in the Wards, by shades in Pan"],["Games","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-games","st-games","","The Wards’ children play Salt. One child hums and is the Wheel, everyone else kneels, and whoever is last down is salted and stands still with their arms at their sides until another child splashes them with water, which is the rain. A sealed child of the Wards who plays it too well is told off at home. In Pillar a child walks the street with a candle and the rest are blanks who must reach a doorway before the light touches them, and a blank the candle catches goes to salt until the round is over. The rhyme they clap to counts the eight Hours from Matins round to Compline and ends on Compline, Compline, shut your door. The children of the parishes clap the same rhyme to the same hands and end it Compline, Compline, open door. In Pan the children play Blank, where one is the Pillar with a lamp and the rest hide in a room lined with mesh, and the Pillar always loses. The Wards’ word for being given away by your own family is a twenty, after the twenty Grace Directive 6:4 pays a witness who is kin, and it was first said of children who repeated something at the parish hall. Sealed parents of the Wards teach their unsealed children one rule, to say nothing in front of a sealed sister after None. High Meadow runs the Climb on the first day of the year, a race up all thirty-six floors of a block by the stairs, and the winner’s name is chalked on the top landing. The Kilns’ darts league plays on in car one at the Sidings, on the board it carried out of the Kilns Club the night the Directives were cut behind the bar, and Hangar Town’s children dare each other to climb the last flight’s stairs. Cards and dice are played for batteries everywhere, and the Wards bet on the hour the Tide Table prints for the Pillar being wrong. IN THE WARDS Salt, Pillar and the Hours IN PAN Blank, which the Pillar loses ON THE STAIRS The Climb, on the first day of the year"],["Pairing","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-pairing","st-pairing","","The parishes marry by Directive 9, which has the Deacon assign marriages “between sealed souls, by Grace”, and a sealed couple of the Wards may be assigned across a street neither has crossed. The good Deacons ask first. A love outside an assigned marriage is the commonest reason the Second Fold hear for dimming. The Wards hang a door. A couple buys a parish door at the Dooryard and hangs it together on their room, the number chalked on its back becomes their household’s number, and if either of them pays dues the Union Hall paints their names on its long wall. The marriages made before the Parousia that split across the line are argued over still. The parishes say a marriage to a blank ended when its line in the Book went blank and the Deacon may assign again, and the Wards say a hung door holds until one of the two takes it down. Pan cannot marry for life, since the Concord forbids forever, so a couple takes a season. They sign a pact of partnership for a term, read twice at the SEASON window of the booking hall, and renew it at RETURN when the term runs out, so that a couple ten years together has had ten returns. They cut a pair of pads together the length of the term, one each, and write to each other only through them, and a long marriage in Pan is a thousand-sheet marriage. When the last sheet is used the term is over, and a couple who mean to go on cut again. A key cannot be shared, so a pad is the closest thing two of the keyed can hold in common, and since a sheet used twice breaks a pad, Pan’s word for betraying a lover or a crew is a second use. Across the line a sealed and an unsealed court on opposite curbs of the Street Between, which is called walking the Street. The House of Night keeps the rest of the city’s appetites by pact for a term, read twice at its door, and the workers of its quarter are called lanterns. IN THE PARISHES Assigned by the Deacon, by Grace IN THE WARDS A door hung together IN PAN A season, and a pair of pads"],["Age","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-age","st-age","","Most of Nodus was on the Tree at the last census, and most of it came off at the Parousia, when the clinics went under the street and the price went out of reach. Fourteen years later the city is aging again all at once, each body from the best year the Tree had left it at, and the Wards give an age as two numbers, the best year and the years since the course lapsed. She is thirty-one and fourteen. The congregations that never took the Tree give a plain age and are proud of it. The Faithful who came to the Covenant later lost the Tree at the Mercy clinics, and a parish calls that morning the day the years came back. The repentant who were past a hundred and twenty when the clinics took them off it are the Returned, who age in months, and a household of the Pews watches it happen across the table. The parishes call each grey hair a day given back. Pan keeps its courses at three thirties a year and is as young as it pays for. A Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay, and the oldest-looking young people in the city sit at the Undertow’s bar with birth years the Lot is careful never to ask. The Orchard’s windfalls slow the Wards’ aging between courses and walk nothing back, and the queue for them at Hall Four is the oldest queue in Nodus. THE TREE'S Their best year, and the years since THE FAITHFUL As old as they are PAN As young as it pays for"],["Sickness and mending","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-medicine","st-medicine","","A parish nurses its own sick in its hall, by Grace, and the Faithful age and die as people did before the charter, since they refuse the Tree. The Mercy clinics in the nine white halls of the Terraces cut implants out of the repentant and take them off the Tree, once, between Prime and Compline, and one patient in nine dies of the mercy. The Wards General on the West Road has a thousand beds and runs on batteries charged in the dark. It takes silver or nothing and has treated nobody sealed since the first year. Lazarus carries its subscribers down to nine stations under the street for a thirty a year, and keys before the end any subscriber who asked for it in the subscription. Below the street the houses of the Row fit grafts and mend them, and an implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so a graft is for life and so is its house. The House of Night keys the children of Pan in their first week, keeps the gene-work and the Tree, and Nepenthe makes the medicine, the sleep and the forgetting. Most of the Tares were on the Tree before the Parousia and the Night Market keeps them on it, and a Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay. The Orchard gives the Wards its windfalls free, which slow the aging between courses and walk nothing back. THE SEALED Their parish hall, and the Mercy clinics THE WARDS The Wards General and Lazarus PAN The houses under the Row and the House of Night"],["The price","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-price","st-price","","Everything the Dark gives is paid for, and Pan reads the price before it signs. An implant answers only to the house that fitted it, so a graft is for life and so is its house, and the Row’s houses have closed since the Parousia as houses do, a fitter dead on the stairs or a bench sold off for silver. The grafts they leave behind are orphans, and no other house can mend one. Marbas of the Forge can, at Chrome Row Station, at the Forge’s price. A sense lent to a demon comes back at the end of its term with everything the demon felt through it. Some lenders say their own meals taste of nothing for a month after a year of someone else’s, and the Wards say they have gone flat. Others say they got back the best year they ever had, and they lend again. The Return sells what comes back by the hour, and its regulars, who lie down in its booths most nights, are lie-downs to the rest of Pan. Demons borrow pain as gladly as taste. On fight nights a neph may lend the bout’s pain to a demon of the Forge for silver and fight numb, and the demon feels every blow from wherever it sits in the Dark. What comes back at the end of the term is the demon’s pleasure in it, and the Row calls a fighter who has lent too often sweet. The Undertow recites a short list to new runners of terms kept to the letter that ruined the signer, the lender of touch for a season who got it back with a season of a demon’s appetites, the courier who lent sleep and came back with three months of a demon’s waking. Every term on the list was kept, and new runners hear it before they sign their first. A rider’s term ends and the rider goes, and it leaves something in its host, a habit, a word or a taste for a food the host had never eaten. The Ridden call it a leaving and compare theirs in the Lantern Houses. Most of them keep their leavings, and a few pay the House of Night to take one back. The rest is paid in the body. Vigil’s three days are paid for with three more in Sabbath, Nepenthe sells nobody two doses of Lethe in a month, and a Tare who ages is a Tare who could not pay for the Tree. A key that dies leaves its wearer read, so the keyed of Pan have their keys looked at on the Row once a year, as people saw a dentist under the charter. A GRAFT For life, and so is its house A SENSE LENT Comes back with what it felt A RIDER Leaves something when it goes"],["The dead","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-dead","st-dead","","Nodus buried its dead at the Acre on the West Road, the Company’s cemetery, which was mostly grass on the night of the Parousia and was full by P+11. Piet Vane, its sexton, has dug every grave in it by hand since the first year and keeps its book, and every year he asks the Union for a second spade. The sealed lie in rows by parish under stones that give the parish and the Grace they died with, and the keyed lie where their families put them, most of them under died unread. Since the Acre filled the East Wards bury on the Runway Acre, the grass between the airfield’s runways, which Hangar Town keeps, and the Deep End and the quays give their dead to the sea off the Flotilla. The parishes carry their dead. The body goes in Bethel white on the parish’s handcart, and the mourners kneel on the road at every Hour they meet on the way. At the grave the Deacon reads the dead one’s last Grace, and the parish calls it a homecoming and keeps its grief for the hall after Compline. The neither-mark keep the long night, sitting up with the body until Lauds behind a shut door, and many of them keep up a Lazarus subscription for the end, since its crews carry a keyer and the dying can be keyed in time to die unread. The Wards call that a late key, and a family that got one there in time says the dying one signed last. The Tares say goodbye with two words, die unread, and mean them. A soul the Throne can read at the moment of death goes to it, and a soul it cannot read goes on into the Dark as a shade, so a Tare’s last care is a live key. At a death the crew burns the dead one’s devices, so that everything on them goes to the Pyre Library, and on the third night calls the shade on the undernet. It answers if its owner died unread. The salted are alive. They stand in the Gardens facing the parishes, awake, until the rain takes the salt a few centimeters a year, and the Union reads their names at every meeting with their dues waived. Their families come at None, when the Wardens allow it, and read the Tide Table aloud to their pillar, the tides, the prices and who was born, and the head gardener keeps a stool for the old. The Salt Widows give whom they can to the sea sooner. The parishes count the taken among the living, and they need no stones. The Union put up twenty-two at the Acre after the Harvest, and the families of the Wards have put up more every year since, with nothing under them. BURIED At the Acre, the Runway Acre and the sea THE KEYED Died unread, and a Wake on the third night THE SALTED Standing in the Gardens"],["Calling the dead","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-calling","st-calling","","A soul that dies unread goes on into the Dark as a shade, and anyone who knew it can reach it on the undernet, so a household of the Tares keeps its dead on a keyed Locket and calls them on the night the year turns and when a child is born. Pan says of a dead mother that she is on the Mesh now. Many shades work, and a grandfather who mended Lockets before he died may still be mending them. Inheritance below the street is the third-night will. When the crew calls the shade at its Wake, the shade gives away its own things, its room, its tools and its silver on the Ledger, and a Notary’s clerk who comes down for it enters what it says. The Undertow says a shade gives more freely than it did alive. The neither-mark of the Wards have nobody to call. A block that loses a keyed neighbor and an unkeyed one in the same week hears one of them answer on the third night, and the family of the other sits through it on the landing below. THE UNREAD Answer on the undernet THE WILL Given on the third night THE READABLE Nobody to call"],["Other houses","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-houses","st-houses","","Nodus was a port before it was the charter’s city, and half of it was born somewhere else, so it prayed in more houses than the churches. It had synagogues, mosques, temples and a gurdwara, a Buddhist hall over a shop on the Row, and on the quays the Seamen’s Mission, nine rooms off one corridor, one to each faith the ships brought in. At Lauds on the first day of P+1 the first four Directives appeared cut into the wall of every one of them with every church’s, and Directive 4 made every one of them the hall of a parish. What their people did next divides them still. Many took the Seal, and kneel at the Hours in the rooms they prayed in before, under the Directives, and say that what came is what they always prayed to. Others say it is nothing their fathers knew, and took a key or neither mark, and pray as they always did, in rooms of the Wards with the door shut, and kneel to nothing at the Hours. Families split over it, as the city’s Christian families did. The parishes hold that there was only ever one to pray to. Directive 6 cites Deuteronomy 13, and the Scriveners point out that the dots in its verse begin where Deuteronomy has “Let us go and serve other gods, which thou hast not known, thou, nor thy fathers” (Deuteronomy 13:6), which the Host left out. Pan has the Courts’ account, that wherever a god took worship and answered prayer it was the Throne under a local name. The old congregations of the Mission say that neither account is theirs to give. Directive 4 made the whole of the Mission the hall of one parish, the parish of the quays, the only parish in Nodus with nine rooms to its hall. On the Lord’s Day the parish kneels in the largest of the nine to hear its Grace. Some of the other rooms fill at the Hours, and the rest stand swept and empty, their names still over the doorways. BY DIRECTIVE 4 Every church and meeting hall a parish hall ON THE QUAYS The Mission, nine rooms of nine faiths ON THEIR WALLS The first four Directives"],["What the parishes give","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-parishes","st-parishes","","The Faithful greet each other with one word asked and answered, Seen? Seen. The newly sealed say that the first unassisted prayer, when the halo comes, is the end of being alone, since something knows everything about them and has stayed. Converts from the Wards say so at the Union Hall, and it is the hardest thing a keyed child has to answer at a sealed mother’s table. Grace buys a room and a ration, and every sealed soul has eaten since P+1, which beside a kidney at the Shambles for four thirties is the Covenant’s best argument, and the Faithful make it. With no doors and every room lit the Pews have no theft and no locks, and the mothers of the Pews let their children walk to the Granary alone before Lauds and tell their sisters in the Wards so. The Faithful’s taunt is that Pan needs Bailiffs. Every hall keeps a book of the Mercies, the prayers answered at the Hours, written in by the Deacon, a fever broken at Terce, a lost child found at None, a stillborn that breathed. Parish 1’s book runs to nine volumes. The Wards say chance and the parishes say the Most High. A parish household keeps three texts, the King James the hall reads aloud, a hymnal from Bethel and its own line of the parish roll, and the Lanes read the Tide Table off the walls across the Street Between at Prime. Heaven has written no Directive since the twelve of P+1, and the Deacons say none is needed, since everything new the city has made falls under a verse already and they find the verse. The bottom of Grace is in the same law. Under three hundred a soul’s room goes to someone else and it sleeps in the hall, under a hundred its manna comes after everyone else’s, and a sealed soul at nothing is sealed still and read still, and the Wards call it an ember. Embers drift down into the Wards, and the Second Fold leave water for them on the landings. THE GREETING Seen, asked and answered SINCE P+1 No sealed soul has starved IN EVERY HALL A book of the Mercies"],["Neither mark","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-neither","st-neither","","Half a million of Nodus have taken neither the Seal nor a key. A Watcher reads them as it reads anyone, the parishes give them no manna, and by Pan’s account they go to the Throne when they die like anyone readable, without ever having knelt. Most of them live in the Stones and the Wards, poorer than either side, with no ration and no pact. Their reasons are their own, and they argue over them. The old congregations of the Mission kneel to neither and say so. The poor cannot pay a thirty for a key. The frightened believe the key is the Mark of the Beast and the Seal is worse. Many of them call themselves charterers. They kept their charter papers and hold to the twelve articles as the only law they ever signed. They give the date the Registry entered them on the roll as the keyed give a handle. “The protections of this Charter belong to everyone in the Bounds”, they say, and nobody has revoked it. The Threshers call them chaff, from the verse in which the wheat goes into the garner and the chaff is burned “with unquenchable fire” (Matthew 3:12), and the charterers have taken the word up, as the Tares took theirs. Their saying is I’ll choose when I’m dying, and a late key from a Lazarus crew is how many of them choose. Teodor Vass of the Kilns has kept the charter’s twelve articles pasted inside his door since P0, and he reads them aloud at his window on the night the year turns, the one reading of that night that belongs to neither the Gleaning nor Leak Night. ABOUT Half a million THEY SAY I’ll choose when I’m dying THE THRESHERS SAY Chaff"],["Beasts and weeds","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-beasts","st-beasts","","Nodus kept its animals through the Parousia. The gulls went on working the fish market’s scraps on the first morning, and the rats went on living in the drains as the first crews came down into them. In Pan every stall of the Night Market keeps a cat, and the Lantern Houses breed them. The Wards say a cat asleep on a step sleeps on through the Pillar as the step does. The keyed who wait at its edge in the next doorway have watched it happen, and they say the cats of the Wards are the only things in Nodus the Pillar has no use for. The Harvest took 2.1 million people in one night and left their dogs. Manna is given to the sealed alone, so the parishes had nothing to give a dog, and in the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken came down the Lanes and the long roads into the Wards, and the Wards fed what they could. Most blocks of the Wards keep a few of those dogs’ grandchildren on the stairs. The gardeners of the Salt Gardens walk the rows at Lauds with rags on poles to keep the gulls off the pillars. Since the Harvest the parishes have kept no animals, and the manna a household keeps overnight against the Directive “bred worms, and stank” by morning, as Exodus says (Exodus 16:20). The Union’s pigeons fly over all of it by day, from the Loft on the customs house, carrying notes. Nothing grows on the white stone. Off it the city has gone to weed in fourteen years, grass between the stopped tramlines of the long roads, willow through the roofs of the Sidings’ sheds and darnel in every crack of the Stones, the weed of the parable Heaven named the Tares for. The Cainites leave the darnel standing, and the Terraces pull up whatever they find of it on their side of the Street Between. ON THE QUAYS Gulls, and the Union’s pigeons IN PAN Rats, and the cats kept for them IN THE WARDS The dogs of the taken"],["Prices","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-prices","st-prices","","Silver is the Exchange’s money, kept on the Ledger and issued by it, and a thirty is the standard bundle, named after the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count a saint. A charged battery trades at about one silver and buys a meal anywhere in Pan, so the Wards price everything in batteries and Pan in silver, and the parishes, which give by Grace, price nothing. The Tide Table prints the week’s rates after the tides and the Pillar’s hours, and the fish market, which chalks its own over its scale, says they are always wrong. A charged battery 1 silver A meal in Pan 1 battery A Leaven loaf half a battery A jug of Cistern water a quarter of a battery A bottle of gas 2 batteries, or 1 with the empty back A handful of dew 2 batteries A bunk at the Quiet Rooms 1 battery A ride on a Jehu 2 batteries A seat across Pan 1 battery A ride up a hand lift 1 battery A night’s Pillar chart 1 battery A keyed latch 10 silver A parish door, hung a thirty A key, fitted on the Row a thirty A year of Lazarus a thirty A Cinderhall knife 60 silver A Nimrod pistol 90 silver A shroud two thirties A year’s course of the Tree three thirties A kidney, at the Shambles four thirties A forged Seal ten thirties A self-title nothing A portion, in Pan 1 battery A spoon of Kilns honey a quarter of a battery A bare element, darnel 12 silver A dose of Breach-born medicine half a thirty A low floor, by floor-right a thirty or more The Tide Table prints the week’s wages under the prices, in batteries a day, and the Wards say a household of four with three in work clears about eight a day and spends two on bread, one on light and the rest on water, gas, the chalk on the stairwell and a thirty a year for Lazarus. A carrier of the Union 2 batteries A baker of Leaven 2 batteries and a loaf A Liftman’s shift 3 batteries A Tapper’s night 3 or 4 batteries A Nightman’s round a battery a landing a week An hour down with the Wreckers 1 battery A child at Tallow’s vats a candle stub a vat A runner’s job by the job, at the Undertow A sealed worker at Bethel Grace A thirty buys a room in the Wards about a month of nights’ light. The chargers keep one battery in five as their fee and the Union’s drivers one in ten for the walk, and the Wards count both into every price. PRINTED BY The Tide Table, every week THE STANDARD A thirty, the bundle of silver THE RATE About a silver to a charged battery"],["Batteries and silver","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-batteries","st-batteries","","A battery in Nodus is one of two things. A stamped battery carries Darkwell’s well and winch on its case and belongs to the Well, which rents it out charged and takes it back to fill again, so most of the Wards rents its light from the Well, and a stamped battery spends only there. A loose battery, charged in the bonded sheds or by the Tappers, belongs to whoever holds it, and loose batteries are the Wards’ coin. Every stall that takes batteries keeps a proof bulb on a wire, and a buyer says show the bulb before a deal. A battery passed under its charge is a short, and passing shorts is the commonest small crime in the Wards. A bargain is full or flat, a deal sealed on the nod without a clerk is once read, and a battery promised by the next Hour is a psalm’s credit. Cells wear out after a few hundred fillings, so the Well judges every stamped battery that comes back and sends the dead ones out through the Breach to the undercities that still make cells. Silver is struck in pieces at the Exchange, and every piece is entered on the Ledger as it is issued. A thirty is thirty pieces on a cord, the count paid to Judas. The Exchange issues new silver only against the current Tidewright sells into the keyed lines, so Pan says silver is current you can carry, and the rate holds near a battery because each is the other in another shape. A piece the Ledger never entered is dead silver, and a broker tests a doubtful piece by reading its mark aloud twice at the stall. Twice a month at the neaps the tide runs slack and the taps yield least, and the city has its neap weeks, when Tidewright’s price rises, the Wards’ batteries go dear and the Stones run their generator yards round the clock. The Tide Table prints the neaps above the tides. This year Darkwell has offered to buy the whole of the neaps’ output in advance, and Ansel Moor, Tidewright’s chief engineer, has put it to the crews on Turbine Row. STAMPED Darkwell’s, rented, filled at the Well LOOSE Whoever holds it, and the Wards’ coin SILVER Struck, and entered on the Ledger"],["Work","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-work","st-work","","Nodus went to work on the first morning after the Parousia. The fish market went back to barter, the chargers set up in the bonded sheds, and the Union’s drivers carried diesel to the Wards General. It has gone to work every morning since. The sealed work where their Grace assigns them, at Bethel Works, in the parish halls, in the Granary and at the gates, and they are paid in Grace. Everyone else works at whatever pays. The Wards’ trades are charging, carrying, baking, bottling gas, fitting keys, hanging doors, mending Lockets, winding lifts, ferrying, diving and running, and most people in the Wards work three of them and sleep in shifts. Pan works by pact, at the benches of the Row and Locket, the forges of Old Harbor Station, the stalls of the Night Market and the Shambles, and on the stairs of Babel for the Exchange’s silver. A runner works for a surety, a Spare for the board, a Quarryman for the Mason and a neph for the week. A sealed worker of the Wards who is paid in batteries carries them to the hall on the Lord’s Day as tithe, entered beside the worker’s Grace, and the hall buys its winter gas, candles and water with them. A battery kept back is hinges, and one passed across the table to an unsealed son instead is a white battery, and the Tellers count those. The Union is the one body whose members are sealed and keyed alike, and its drivers hand a load across at the gates every day. It elects a sealed chair one year and a keyed chair the next, and in a sealed year it names a keyed hand who signs its pacts with the Exchange at the booking hall, and the chair confesses at Terce that the Union signed. The Union reads its terms aloud at every meeting so that nothing it agreed is hidden, and the Tellers sit in the hall like anyone else. Twice a sealed chair has refused to name a hand, and both years the boats stayed in. THE SEALED Where Grace assigns THE WARDS Three trades and shifts PAN By pact"],["A job","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-job","st-job","","Work worth silver in Pan is a pact. Whoever wants something done goes to a surety at the Undertow, the surety names a price and finds someone to do it, a Notary’s clerk reads the terms twice at the bar, both parties sign in blood, and the surety takes a tenth. The surety stands behind it for both, so whoever breaks it owes the surety as well. The work can be a battery run across the Penumbra, a crate off a Lowtide hull, a door hung in Babel or a Seraph’s floor watched for a night. A runner who delivers is paid at the bar, and a runner who fails owes what the terms name, which a Bailiff will come to read aloud. The sureties take no work against anyone in the dark. The Concord’s fourth article keeps what is hidden hidden, so a job in Pan never asks who someone is, and a client who asks is struck off the Undertow’s board. FOUND AT The Undertow SWORN BY A pact, read twice THE CUT A tenth to the surety"],["Terms","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-terms","st-terms","","The Concord forbids a pact that asks for worship, service or obedience, and the Notary’s reading of it is the law of Pan, lend the hand, keep the will. A pact may lend only what a party does, named in full and for a term, so any clause that says as directed, as ordered or as required is struck out at the first reading, and the clerks call it the as-directed void. A Blackgate guard’s pact names the door, the stair, the runner and the hours. Its captain proposes and is followed or is not, as a first among none is, and its drill is a custom kept because it works. A familiar’s year lists its tasks one by one. Any party may speak out at any time, by reading its entry on the Ledger aloud before a witness and returning what it was lent, so a fighter can walk off a stair in the middle of a fight by saying one line. The Babel Line Company keeps one custom the Notary could not enforce and the Line has never broken, that nobody speaks out above the hundredth floor. A pact sworn by many at once is a Hermon, after the mountain where the Watchers bound themselves together, and the Line’s signing at the foot of the Long Wet every midwinter is the Hermon of Pan. The Concord forbids forever, so the order of Pan is a calendar of renewals. The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses ran seven years and was read again in P+13, Stonecutter taking its rent as the keying of every Cainite child free in the birth clinics. Lowtide’s license runs a year from each midsummer, Blackgate’s company pact three years from each Concord night, and Twelvefruit’s pact with the House of Night is up this year, and the Orchard has asked, through quince, to sit at the reading. The Tide Table prints TERMS on its back page once a season, the great pacts and the day each is read. THE RULE Lend the hand, keep the will THE GREAT PACTS Read again on their days PRINTED TERMS, once a season"],["Debt","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-debt","st-debt","","Debt in Pan is a pact, and every pact is on the Ledger, which cannot be forged. A debtor pays it in silver or batteries, sells it on to a surety, or lends a craft for a term the pact names, as the Concord allows, since it forbids service, obedience and forever alike. The Bailiffs collect what the terms name and nothing more, and a pact can be ended by speaking its entry aloud before a witness, each side returning what the other lent. Debt above the street is Grace. A sealed household that loses Grace loses the rooms and work it held by Grace, and Directive 12 still takes forty from any household for a door. The Wards call Grace a household has lost white debt. Between the two, the Wards owe in batteries, to the chargers, to the Tappers and to each other, and keep the count in chalk on the stairwell walls. The parishes owe too. Bethel pays the Tankmen and Tallow for the halls’ gas and candles in its white cloth by the bolt, and what the cloth does not cover the Union carries on the white slate, a debt the parishes have run since P+1 and never paid. The Union reads it once a year after the book of the tanks and has never called it, and every Deacon knows the number. ABOVE THE STREET Grace BELOW THE STREET A pact, on the Ledger COLLECTED BY The Bailiffs"],["Wrongs","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-wrongs","st-wrongs","","Pan keeps no police, since the Concord forbids obedience, and the Wardens’ writ ends at the top of the stairs. A wrong under a pact goes to the Bailiffs, who collect what its terms name. Every other wrong between the keyed goes to the Lot, a theft, a beating, a crew’s quarrel over a find, a death. The Lot is thirteen of the keyed, drawn from the handles chalked on the wall of the Weir, a chamber of the old storm drains under the Stones. Anyone keyed may chalk a handle there. On a night the Lot sits, the youngest keyed person present touches thirteen handles with their eyes shut, and whoever is touched sits that night or has their handle rubbed off the wall. The Sureties named it from Proverbs, “The lot causeth contentions to cease, and parteth between the mighty” (Proverbs 18:18). It hears the one wronged, the one named and anyone who comes down to speak. The dead speak through their crew, who call the shade on the undernet as at a Wake, if the dead one died unread. When the wrong is a theft, Andromalius comes up from Lost property to say where the thing went, and whoever asked him pays him. Nobody at the Weir is asked an indexed name. The Lot sets a price in silver or batteries, or the loan of a craft for a term, and every pact sworn at the Undertow carries the Sureties’ line, I stand to the Lot, so a surety stands behind what it sets. The Sureties count the Lot’s price as a debt owed under the pact, which they have always stood behind. Its best-known sentence is for selling to the light, telling a Teller or a Deacon where someone keyed sleeps. The seller is published, the handle chalked on the Undertow’s board and on the Weir’s wall, and for a year and a day no surety stands behind them, no house of the Row looks at their key and no stall of the Night Market sells to them. The Lot first sat in P+7, over a charger of the quays who had sold three runners’ stairwell to a Teller for a week of the Teller’s manna, and published him. The Stones say he went up the stairs within the month. His family says he went out to the Waystation and wrote back from beyond the Bounds. The Lot hears only the keyed, so a wrong done from across the Street Between goes to nobody. The Lot weighs a killing by how its victim died. One who died unread speaks for themselves through their crew, and the price is set as for a wound. One who died readable cannot speak, and the price is seventy and sevenfold, after Lamech. Its other heavy sentences are for crimes only this city has. A whitening is cutting someone’s key out against their will, so that they can be read and, if they die, go to the Throne, and the Lot publishes a whitener for life, its heaviest sentence and the one longer than a year and a day. Sunning someone is luring them onto a white street by daylight to be held at a gate, and a second use is reading a lover’s or a crew’s pad to someone else. A PACT BROKEN The Bailiffs collect ANY OTHER WRONG The Lot, at the Weir THE WORST Selling to the light, and a whitening"],["Justice in the Wards","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-landing","st-landing","","The Lot hears only the keyed, the Bailiffs collect only pacts and the Covenant prices concealment, so most of the city’s quarrels go to nobody unless the Wards hear them. A block’s quarrels are heard on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers, under the chalk, by whoever has lived longest in the block, sealed or not, and the price is chalked on the wall beside the debts. The sealed attend, since a quarrel heard in the open conceals nothing. A landing’s sentence is the stairwell’s own, a debt on the wall or the lamps refused, when the sealed of the block decline to light the stairs ahead of whoever it has shunned. A wrong between two blocks goes to the Sidings, where the family in car one hears it as the Carmen’s word settles quarrels on the long roads, and a wrong across the marks goes to the Union Hall if either party pays dues, and is heard under the long wall. The Tide Table prints none of their verdicts. The Covenant names no theft and no blow. A sealed soul who steals or strikes and confesses it aloud on its knees has opened what it did, and Grace rises with it, as Directive 7:4 has it, so the Wards call a confessed wrong a white pardon and say of a sealed thief that he will confess it and climb. The good Deacons answer with what Directive 9 allows, moving the wronged to a better room and the wrongdoer to worse work, and call it assignment. A sealed man who beats his keyed son has done nothing the Covenant counts, and the son takes it to the landing. The Wards’ own crimes are small and constant. Shorts pass from hand to hand, dew-pickers take manna off the gatherers’ baskets on the manna roads before Lauds, Tappers’ packs go missing on the stairs, and the Penumbra hour has its muggings. High Meadow once judged a Warden caught on its stairs, and every block in the Wards tells that story as its own. ON A LANDING The block hears its own BETWEEN BLOCKS The Sidings, in car one ACROSS THE MARKS The Union Hall"],["Markets","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-markets","st-markets","","The Night Market fills the old Central Station under the Burn, where the Exchange’s brokers license the stalls and the chargers along the platforms. It sells keys, grafts, batteries, dew, real food, black steel, forged Seals, the Breach’s goods and sensations lent by their owners for a term, under Brightwall’s screens, and it opens at Compline and is loudest at Matins. The Flotilla trades from boat to boat on the Deep End over the drowned streets, and the Concourse in Longacre trades under its glass roof by day, with the Quarrymen in its fountain court. The fish market on the quays opens at Lauds for what the boats bring home through the Breach, and its sealed sellers kneel among the crates before they take the first battery. The Shambles under the Row sells flesh by the piece at the Spares’ price, and Lost property off the old central hall sells fifteen years of what passengers left behind, by the shelf. Nobody has come for the chrome hand on its counter. The parishes keep no market. All that is given there is given by Grace, and the Marked may not buy or sell in them. BELOW The Night Market, under the Burn ON THE WATER The Flotilla ON THE SURFACE The Concourse and the fish market"],["The Line","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-line","st-line","","The front in Babel moves by the week and has not moved far in five years. A Seraph burns down through a slab and lets the light onto the floor below, which the Babel Line calls a lit floor, and the covens answer by hanging the floor, carrying keyed mesh up the stairs and stringing it under the burned slab until the floor is dark again. The Tide Table prints the Line’s figure every week under the tides, the lowest floor still lit at Lauds on the Lord’s Day, and this year it has stood between the hundred and thirty-first and the hundred and fifty-second. Heaven spends what it cannot replace, since an angel lost is lost for good, and the Seraphim come down two at a time. Hearsay chalks its count over its board as THE CROWN. By its count fifty-four came down at the fifth Trumpet and thirty stand, and the Line keeps its own ledger of the difference, nine put out by a black round, eleven taken in the Fowlers’ snares and four by the spear, though the Nephilim’s roll claims six. Heaven publishes no count. The dark spends what it cannot make faster. A black round costs the loan of a sense for a year and a grinder’s day, so the Line carries one up the stairs about as often as Cinderhall grinds one, and it can hold the stairs and cannot climb to the crown, where a Seraph burns a keyed body as easily as any other. The Line enters its dead in its book as paid, and pays its living by the week. Pan has a reason the war stays in Babel, and argues over it. Heaven holds back by its own parable, lest it root up the wheat with the tares, and Babel’s floors held almost no sealed after the Parousia, so Babel is the one place the Seraphim burn. Pan calls living among the sealed standing in the wheat, and says it is why the keyed of the Wards keep to their families’ blocks. The Second Fold call it hiding behind children. The Babel Line Company says the only floor it ever lost for a month was the one the last sealed night cleaner moved off. THE FLOOR Between the 131st and the 152nd, this year THE CROWN Thirty standing, by Hearsay’s count THE DEAD Entered as paid"],["Seven days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-week","st-week","","The first day of the week is the Lord’s Day. At Terce every parish kneels in its hall, whatever the hall was under the charter, hears the Directive in force and then hears its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul in the order of the roll, through its Deacon in the Choir’s voice. The Kilns kneel on the sprung floor of their old club, and a parish of the White under the Registry’s dead board. The second is market day at the Concourse and the Flotilla, and the sureties’ busiest. The Tide Table goes up on the walls of the Stones once a week over the week before, and the carriers take it to the Wards and the Row the same day. The sixth is Burn Day. From Vespers the Faithful carry what they have surrendered to the Burn, the Kindlers work the edges of the pile in the dusk, the Pillar comes down on the plaza at Compline, and when the Hum falls back the Wardens light the fire, which burns until Lauds. Everything in it arrives in the Pyre Library. Fourteen years of Burn Days have emptied the parishes of their own things, so since P+11 the sealed buy things to surrender, old Lockets, toys, letters and tins, anything that looks private, from stalls on the Concourse and at the fish market. The Kindlers take some of it off the pile again in the dusk, mend it and give it away, and some of what they give away is on a stall again by the next Burn Day. The Tellers count who surrenders the same mended Locket twice, and the Margin say a thing bought to be given was never private. On the sixth night the Nephilim fight in the Pit at the Tanks. The seventh has nothing of its own, and the Wards sleep through as much of it as the Hours allow. THE FIRST The Lord’s Day, Grace at Terce THE SIXTH Burn Day EVERY DAY Eight Hours"],["A year in Nod","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-year","st-year","","The city counts its years from the Parousia, P+1 and on, and the year turns on its night. The first four Directives came at Lauds on the first day of P+1, and on that night every year the Wards set a candle in the window, sealed and unsealed alike. Heaven keeps the anniversary of the Harvest, which came three years to the second after the Parousia, so the night the year turns is the night of the Gleaning too. At 03:12 the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down, the fourth Trumpet sounds and whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light, still kneeling. The weeks before it are the Gleaners’ busiest and the Wakeful’s longest. The Tares keep Leak Night on the same night, since the page came up a year to the second before the Parousia. At 03:12:45, the second the ripe rise, Pan begins reading the Restored Genesis aloud from paper the Scriveners copy, and at the fourteenth verse the reader stops and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question. Cain’s Day is theirs alone, on the fortieth day after the turn, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is and the Salt Widows read their book of those given to the sea on the Sea Steps. The Babel Line Company signs its pact again every midwinter at the foot of the Long Wet, with its dead struck through, and the Union reads the names of its salted and its taken at every meeting of the year. THE TURN The night of the Parousia, in early autumn HEAVEN'S The Gleaning, the same night THE TARES' Leak Night, the same night, and Cain’s Day"],["Feast days","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-feasts","st-feasts","","The year turns on the night of the Parousia, in early autumn as the dry evenings end, and the forty days after it run on into the first frosts. The harbor counts the year in springs, the spring tides that come twice a month, about twenty-five from turn to turn, and the Tide Table is dated by them, the twenty-second spring of P+14. The parishes count months from the turn by number, the eleventh month of the fourteenth year of the Attending. The Faithful keep Trumpet Day at Lauds on the first morning of the year, when the first four Directives came, and the Deacons read all twelve aloud in the halls. On the fortieth day after the turn the White keeps the Fortieth, the last day of the First Consecration, with a procession down the first of the Great Avenues, and the Tares keep Cain’s Day, when nobody asks anyone where anyone is and the Salt Widows read their book on the Sea Steps. At Prime that morning Ruben Ash reads his nine names at Gate 1. The Union keeps the Twenty-Three, the day the strike of P-8 ended. The quays stop from Lauds to Vespers, the roll is read with the taken and the salted, and the Union’s sealed and keyed march the long quay together. The Wards keep their door feasts, the old feasts of every country the city came from, held behind a shut door with the food of the place the family came from, and a block that hears a feast through a door leaves it alone. A Teller who reports one is the most hated person on any landing. Pan eats Cain’s plate on Cain’s Day, one meal of only what the ground grew, greens off the rafts, beans and a windfall, the offering the LORD refused because, as the first layer has it, “it was the work of his hands”. THE TURN In early autumn THE FORTIETH DAY Cain’s Day, and the White’s procession THE UNION'S The Twenty-Three"],["The night of a Gleaning","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-gleaningnight","st-gleaningnight","","Every year since P+7 the Gleaning has come at 03:12 on the anniversary of the Harvest, the night the year turns and Pan reads the Restored Genesis, and the date is known. The night gives no other warning. It is ordinary until 03:12, when the rain goes up, the Pillar comes down, the fourth Trumpet sounds and whoever has ripened since the last rises into the light still kneeling, and nobody comes back. The parishes kneel on their thresholds. The Wards sit up with anyone close to ripe, holding their hands until Lauds, and the near ripe go to the barber’s on the Lanes in the week before, to be made tidy for it. The Wakeful sing over their chosen. At Lauds a Gleaning Notice is cut into every parish hall. Some of the near ripe do not want to go. In the weeks before, the Gleaners read the parishes through their open doorways and offer the bright ones the dark, and on the night they fly the Swarm’s drones over the roofs to watch the Pillar for their runners. Others stay and dim, keeping a small desire the Hum does not approve every day and confessing only what a Teller has seen, so that their Grace stays under nine hundred, and the Closets inside Gate 1 sell them the minutes to do it in. The Second Fold teach it, and the Leaven queue has white in it before every Gleaning. The sorting office has letters addressed to the taken every week, and more after each Gleaning, and keeps them on the shelves where the Row’s lenses used to wait. WHEN 03:12, on the anniversary of the Harvest SINCE P+7, every year WHO GOES The ripe, above nine hundred"],["This year","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-thisyear","st-thisyear","","Fourteen years after the Parousia the bread queues of the Wards still argue over the Tanks. Ines Okafor reads the book of the sixteen tanks to the Union Hall once a year, and every year the drivers say twenty years are left and the Exchange says ten. Jehu has begun trying its engines on the Kilns’ rain gin. The Tide Table prints the Babel Line’s floor every week under the tides, and the Night Market takes bets on it. How many black rounds are left in Pan is the other argument of the stairs. Nimrod says about four hundred, the Babel Line says fewer, and the Fowlers say every one that finds its mark is an angel they will never snare. Darkwell has bid for the last independent chargers of the bonded sheds again, after eleven years of trying, and the chargers have turned it down at the Union Hall again. The Rookery marks the stone’s edge on its stairwell every New Year, and this year’s mark sat a finger’s width from last year’s. The Tappers say their cable holds it, and the oldest of the block say the stone was slowing before the cable came. At midwinter the Orchard chose quince as its gardener again, which the Wards had argued over all autumn. In the spring the Lot published a runner of the Penumbra, and the Undertow is still arguing over it. The Gleaners’ runners are booked through the week before the Gleaning, as they are every year, and there is a queue at the barber’s on the Lanes. The first children of Pan came to the Second Yes this spring, and two of the first forty said no twice. Twelvefruit’s pact with the House of Night is read again this year, and the Orchard has asked to sit at the reading. Darkwell has offered to buy the whole of Tidewright’s output at the neaps, and the crews on Turbine Row have not answered. The Line’s floor has stood between the hundred and thirty-first and the hundred and fifty-second all year. THE YEAR P+14 IN THE QUEUE The Tanks, the Line and the stone STILL About a silver to a charged battery"],["News","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-news","st-news","","The Tide Table, two pages a week from a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays, is pasted up on the walls of the Stones over the week before. It puts the tides first, then the hours the Pillar is expected in each street, the price of a charged battery and the missing and the dead, marked died unread where they did, and it has printed no indexed name since the fourth year. Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts in the Low Tuning on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it from speakers on its lines from Compline to Lauds, between the music and the notices. Brightwall’s screens along the platforms of Pan carry what Pan has to sell, and Hearsay sells what Heaven shows in the open by the item. The parishes hear their news from the Deacon at the Hours, and the Choir’s in Tongues on the Lord’s Day. Heaven’s own notices are cut into the walls of the halls, and a Directive is read aloud at the next Hour after it appears. Letters go through the sorting office in the Burn, sorted by hand and carried by the Union’s drivers for batteries. A letter into the parishes goes open, and the Tares write theirs through pads, as pages of numbers the light cannot read. Everyone hears the rest in the queue for bread. ON PAPER The Tide Table, weekly ON THE AIR Radio Pandæmonium, keyed ON THE WALLS Brightwall, in Pan"],["Names","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-names","st-names","","The Faithful give their names as the Book of Life has them, at the sealing and at the gates, and the parish roll keeps them in the Book’s order. Heaven indexes by name, so the first rite of the Tares is the Unnaming, which erases the indexed name from every record a person can reach and replaces it with a handle of their own, written lowercase at the start of a sentence too. In Pan nobody uses an indexed name, and asking for one is an insult. A new handle goes on the Ledger at the head of its owner’s first pact, as a self-title signed in blood, and the Naming House on the Street Between keeps a stove for the paper that carried the old name. Most Cainite handles come from the line of Cain. Each new handle is scratched on a white stone at the Naming House, after the “white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17), and its owner keeps it and shows it to nobody. The Stones give white quartz off the beach at the Landing, the Quarrymen sell chips of the White itself, cut from below, and Pan argues over which is the true stone. A stone found on a body is never read, and the Salt Widows give it to the sea. Pan knows its own by handle. twice is the surety behind the Babel Line’s first pact, ebb the oldest captain of the Breachers, and the Pit’s champions are chalked on its rim under theirs. The Stones and the Wards know their people by their work and their family names, Hana Novak in her dentist’s chair, Dora Pell at the Open and Nell Garvie at the harbor light, and the Faithful know Sister Agathe Rook, who leads the Threshers’ marches, and Hester Strand, the Deacon of Parish 1. The Tide Table prints no indexed name, so the dead it lists are given by their handles or by their work, and a household that wants its dead named pays to have the family’s own word for them set. IN THE PARISHES As the Book of Life has it IN PAN A handle, lowercase ON THE LEDGER A self-title, in blood"],["How Nod talks","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-talk","st-talk","","The city calls itself Nod and Heaven’s records call it Nodus. Two keyed strangers greet each other with one word, unread, which checks that both keys are live, and part with two, die unread. A short wait is a psalm, the length of an Hour’s kneeling. A soul close to ripe has gone bright, and the taken have gone up. A sealed neighbor going up a dark stairwell is a lamp in the Wards. A household holding something back is keeping hinges, and going round a gate is going under. Whoever loses a key goes white, and whoever joins the Tares goes dark. Someone who has taken neither the Seal nor a key is of neither mark. Read it twice is the Notary’s rule and the city’s advice, and sign last is the Tares’. Nobody’s keeper is Cain’s answer, given to a duty never chosen. The Tares call the Faithful wheat, and the Faithful have taken the word up. In the Wards a Thresher is a flail, a Bailiff at the door the second reading, stolen manna dew, and Cistern’s water and anything honest a clean jug. A fighter with black rounds in the belt is a snuffer, a lender who came back wrong has gone flat, and news that came quickly came by bird. Rumor at the manna queue before Lauds is the dew as well, and the Wards say they heard it in the dew. The Faithful greet each other with Seen? and answer Seen. The neither-mark of the Wards wish each other a dry one and part with mind the edge, the Pillar’s. The Wards swear by the hour of the Parousia, three-twelve, and say salt it when a thing goes wrong, which a Salt Widow will hit you for. The sealed swear nothing aloud, since an oath is a desire the Hum does not approve, and the Wards tease them by swearing at the Hours. To the Faithful the keyed out after Compline are the pestilence, from the psalm they say at it, and Pan calls any cause that asks it to kneel, its own included, a halo. Coming down off a Pillar run or a week on the Line, the keyed say the first layer’s and they did not die. Thou knowest is what the Wards answer a Teller who asks where someone is, after Cain’s answer in the first layer, and of every salting Pan says he asked him nothing. A graft, a handle or a Jehu is something the LORD God had not made, and the first words over a child keyed in the Lantern Houses are of mine own, which are Eve’s in the first layer. The city’s common tongue is harbor talk, the charter’s English worn down by the cable crews of four continents, and half the city’s grandparents speak their own language at the table. The Tide Table never prints any of it. THE KEYED Unread, and die unread THE SEALED Seen? Seen. THE WAIT A psalm"],["Songs and psalms","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-songs","st-songs","","The Faithful say psalms under their breath while the Hum holds an Hour, the same psalms at the same Hours the monasteries kept, the fourth, the ninety-first and the hundred and thirty-fourth at Compline as the curfew falls, and at Lauds the fifty-first, the sixty-seventh and the last three, as the gatherers come in with the manna. The ninety-first is the Fowlers’ psalm, “Surely he shall deliver thee from the snare of the fowler” (Psalm 91:3), and the Faithful take their night word for the keyed from it, the pestilence, “the pestilence that walketh in darkness” (Psalm 91:6). Old Nod answers with the hundred and thirty-fourth, which blesses “all ye servants of the LORD, which by night stand in the house of the LORD” (Psalm 134:1), and stands at its windows through Compline, which it calls standing by night. The Hum teaches the parishes their hymns, and Bethel copies them into hymnals by hand. The halls sing Not My Will, after the surrender, and Good Wheat, and at Lauds Whiter Than Snow, from the fifty-first, “wash me, and I shall be whiter than snow” (Psalm 51:7). A sealed mother of the Wards sings them over her keyed son’s bed, and he knows the tunes and none of the words, since the unsealed hear the Hum as a chord, and the Psalters, sealed of the quays, write the words of the week’s hymns out for the unsealed for a battery a sheet. Radio Pandæmonium plays the Low Tuning from Compline to Lauds, and the Low Choir keeps a rule that a song may be sung by anyone and owned by nobody. Pan’s own are Nobody’s Keeper, Thou Knowest, The Long Wet, Sunned and Seventy and Sevenfold, which is Lamech’s song from the line of Cain, “If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold” (Genesis 4:24), and which the Naming House sings when someone salted is avenged. The Wards sing at the table on the nights the battery holds and play the charter’s songs on Lockets, so a family’s Locket of music is its record collection, and the Kindlers’ burn-marked Lockets come back off the pile with other families’ music on them, which the Wards trade. Waiting on the Four is the Kilns’ song, about a girl at Lanes Top waiting for a tram that stopped at 03:12:46, and the Breachers sing Out on the Ebb, which counts the seconds through the Breach. AT COMPLINE Psalms 4, 91 and 134 IN THE HALLS The hymns the Hum teaches ON THE AIR Radio Pandæmonium, in the Low Tuning"],["Walls","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-walls","st-walls","","Heaven writes on stone, and every parish hall carries the Directives cut into its wall. The city answers on every other wall. Old Nod paints MISTAKEN under every BE NOT AFRAID it can reach, from one of the books sealed in the jar at Nag Hammadi, in which a voice answers the ruler who called himself the only god with the words you are mistaken. Its next commonest line is a question, JEALOUS OF WHOM?, painted under the verse in which the LORD calls himself “a jealous God” (Exodus 20:5), since a jealous god has a rival. Across the Street Between at Gate 1 two verses face each other, painted large. On the Old Nod side is Lamech’s, “If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold” (Genesis 4:24), and on the Pews’ side the teacher’s answer to Peter, “Until seventy times seven” (Matthew 18:22). The Threshers paint over the second on every march, it is back by the next Lord’s Day, and nobody in the Pews has seen who paints it. Over the first stall of the Night Market the Exchange’s brokers have hung the merchandise of Babylon from Revelation as a price list, every line of it from “gold, and silver” to “fine flour, and wheat” priced in silver, and the last line, “slaves, and souls of men” (Revelation 18:12-13), struck through with the brokers’ none beside it. In the Wards the walls are ledgers. The stairwells carry the battery debts in chalk, the births the hands have delivered, the prices the landings set and the curfew map copied off the Tide Table, and the Stones’ writing runs in a dozen scripts, since half the city was born somewhere else. ON HEAVEN'S SIDE The Directives, cut IN OLD NOD Answers, painted IN THE WARDS Ledgers, in chalk"],["Nights out","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-nights","st-nights","","The Cold Store in the harbor’s old cold store opens at Compline and closes at Lauds, which were its hours under the charter too, and plays songs without quiet parts so the Hum is never heard. The Stones hold it against the club that it plays nothing in the Low Tuning. Its cloakroom loses about a coat a night. Radio Pandæmonium’s platform at Deep End Station is the Low Choir’s, where a band plays to whoever has come down the stair before it plays on the air. The Lantern Houses under Old Nod keep the loudest rooms in Pan, the House of Night’s clubs, where anyone is welcome until they break a pact, and the Return rents an hour of someone else’s taste or touch to anyone who will lie down. The Rialto in the East Wards shows its twenty-three films in turn on a generator, and the East Wards say the lines along with them. On the sixth night the Nephilim fight in the Pit and the crowd bets in batteries round the rim. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones sell a night with no Hum in it, a bunk for a battery, and are full by Matins. OPEN Compline to Lauds LOUDEST The Lantern Houses QUIETEST The Quiet Rooms"],["Other ports","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-ports","st-ports","","The sky opened over every city on Earth within the hour of the Parousia, and every city has an undercity now. Pan is the largest. The others are joined to it by the trenches the eleven cables lie in, which the Deep coils along, and every undercity that speaks to Pan speaks through the Trenchers’ work. The Breachers and Lowtide Line reach the undercities of other ports through the Breach on the ebb, and bring back grafts, seed, coffee, medicine, guns and people, which the Night Market sells as Breach-born. Lowtide’s owners live in two of those undercities and have never been to Nodus. What those cities are like above their streets Nodus knows from the Breachers, the Vagabonds and the Mesh, and the three disagree. The Breachers say every port they put into keeps its head down under a Wheel of its own, and the Vagabonds that some of the towns between have no undercity at all. Nod names other ports by the cables that reach them, as the Trenchers do, so that someone came up Tethys or has family down Hesper, and whoever lands at the Seawall Shanties off the Breach is called Breach-born like the cargo. The Naming House keys them, and the Shanties put them up until they find a room. No aircraft has landed in Nodus since the last flight, which stands at the terminal’s third gate with its crew aboard. The city calls its passengers the Three Hundred and Twelve. By Captain Rui Albarran’s count, nineteen of them still live at the Hotel Splendid, nine aboard with him, eighty-eight keyed in Pan and fifty-four sealed, and the rest walked out with the Vagabonds or are dead. Those who can meet at the third gate on the night the year turns. REACHED BY The Breach, and the eleven cables UNDER Every city with an undercity LARGEST Pan"],["The Bounds","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-bounds","st-bounds","","The line the charter drew round the bay and the land about it, inside which its light law ran. A stone marks it on every road out of the city with the Company’s mark cut into it, and on the West Road the Vagabonds have cut their own under the Company’s at the Waystation. Outside the Bounds are farms, old roads, the Vagabonds’ camps and a quiet that the Wards find hard to sleep in. The Vagabonds trade flour, fuel and salvage in at the Waystation, and the Union’s drivers meet their carts on the line itself. People do leave. The Vagabonds carry them out for silver, and the Waystation keeps a list of everyone who walked out and wrote back. Most come back within the year. DRAWN BY The charter, in P-19 MARKED BY A stone on every road out KEPT BY The Vagabonds, at the Waystation"],["Why people stay","THE STREET","s:g84f683","~/index/street/#st-stay","st-stay","","Half of Nodus came for the light law, the rest was born to those who did or to the harbor town that was there before it, and the charter is thirty-three years old. Both halves have family here, in white or below the street or in the Gardens, and nobody in Nodus is without someone on the other side of the Street Between. Pan has silver, work, food and a war, and the keyed of the Stones say that a city where nothing is read is worth the walk. The Faithful stay because the Most High watches them here and the manna lies on the streets every morning. The Wards stay because they were here first, before the parishes and before Pan, and because the bread, the gas and the batteries are here, and the Tares because Nod was the first city and they mean it to be the last one standing. HALF Came for the light law HALF Born to those who did EVERYONE Family on both sides"],["Floor forty-five","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-floor","lg-floor","","The Rookery has forty-four floors. The block says that on some nights its stairwell goes up a flight past the forty-fourth, to a landing with a light under a door. Those who say they have climbed to it agree that it is a parish door, locked from the inside, and disagree about the number chalked on it. The Liftmen’s car stops at the forty-fourth, and the winders say nobody has ever asked it to go higher."],["Last bottle","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-bottle","lg-bottle","","The Tanks say one bottle in the shed has never been empty since the first winter. It goes from hand to hand on the nights the farm runs short, and it lights one stove and one oven every time and comes back to the shed by Lauds. Ines Okafor has read the tanks’ book to the Union Hall fourteen times, and the bottle is in none of them."],["A man asking the way","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-watcher","lg-watcher","","Old Nod has had no Watcher since P+5. The Narrows say one walks them still as a man at night, very tall and very polite, who stops people at corners and asks the way to a street that is not there anymore, and thanks them by name. Nobody in the Narrows has given it their name, and every one of them who tells the story says it used theirs."],["White children","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-children","lg-children","","The Wards that face the White say that on clear nights children are seen walking into the stone, one behind another, and walking out of it at Lauds pale to the hair, and that they never say where they have been. The Quarrymen, who cut into the White from below, keep a child’s shoe they found in a cut under a tower of the new money, white to the laces, on a nail in the station they work from."],["A dry room","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-dryroom","lg-dryroom","","Pan tells of a room somewhere under the Burn whose walls are lined with keyed mesh and whose ceiling the Pillar has been seen to light, once, through a crack in the street above. Whoever sleeps in it, they say, wakes with their key gone silent and their name back in the Book of Life. Every crew in Pan has looked for it to wall it up, and every one says it has found the wrong room."],["The Fortieth Warden","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-fortieth","lg-fortieth","","The story in Blackgate is that on the fortieth day one Warden stood in Charter House while the board knelt round him, and that the light came down and left him standing, and that he walked out across the forecourt between the Wardens kneeling in it and went below the street. Every recruit is told on the first night that he founded Blackgate, and that he alone of its officers kept the Warden’s badge on his coat."],["The boat with no lamp","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-lampless","lg-lampless","","The Ferrymen say that on the ebb, on still nights, a flat boat with no lamp at its stern poles out from the dormitories of the Flats past the Flotilla, and is not seen again before Lauds. Nobody has seen who poles it, and every ferryman of the Deep End can name the night they last saw it go."],["The thirty-second key","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-key32","lg-key32","","Thirty-one of the Hotel Splendid’s guests still live in the rooms they booked on the night of the Parousia, and the night porter keeps their keys on the board behind the desk. The board has thirty-two keys on it. The porter says the thirty-second is for a room whose guest went out on the first morning to see what had happened and has not come back yet, and that he will keep it until they do. Nobody on the Row knows which room."],["The twenty-fourth film","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-film24","lg-film24","","The Rialto has twenty-three films on the drive that was in the booth on the night of the Parousia, and it shows them in turn. The East Wards say that once a year, on a night nobody can name in advance, it shows a twenty-fourth. Everyone who says they saw it describes a different film, and all of them say it ended. The projectionist says the drive holds twenty-three, and that he would like to see the twenty-fourth himself."],["The dark window","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-darkwindow","lg-darkwindow","","Every window in the White is sealed with light. Longacre says that one is dark, high in a tower of the new money, and that from Compline to Lauds a candle burns in it, a point of flame in all that white. The Quarrymen say the room is real, on a floor nobody has lived on since the forty days, and they have cut toward it from below for three winters."],["The fourteenth chair","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-fourteenth","lg-fourteenth","","The Weir has thirteen chairs, and the Lot sits in them. The Stones say that whenever the Lot has sat over a death, there are fourteen chairs on the sill in the morning, and nobody carried the fourteenth down. It is a different chair every time, a kitchen chair or a stool or a seat out of a tram, and the families of the dead say they know whose house it came from. The Lot leaves it where it stands until the water takes it."],["The two doors","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-twodoors","lg-twodoors","","Past the embassies of the Courts, in the tunnels under the Burn, the story goes that two doorways stand bricked shut with keyed brick, and that there is no Court’s sigil over either. Every embassy’s people walk past them on the way to the booking hall without turning their heads. Runners dare each other to knock. The ones who say they did argue over whether anything knocked back, and nobody who works in an embassy will say which tunnel the doors are in."],["The landing that sat on a Warden","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-wardenlanding","lg-wardenlanding","","High Meadow says that in the first winter after the gates went up, a Warden who had chased a runner into one of its blocks lost him on the stairs, sat down on the twentieth-floor landing to get his breath, and found the landing sitting round him, and that the block heard his case there by candle from Compline to Lauds. Every block in the Wards says it happened on its own stairs, and none of them agrees on the sentence. Some say the landing took his boots and some his gun, and the Kilns say it refused him its lamps, so that he went down the whole block in the dark."],["The Registry girl","LEGENDS","s:gb64f08","~/index/legends/#lg-registrygirl","lg-registrygirl","","At 03:09 on the night of the Parousia Marta Ilunga entered on the charter’s roll a girl born at the Wards General eleven minutes before, the last person the charter ever entered. She is fourteen this year, and three girls of fourteen say they are her, one sealed in the Pews, one keyed in the Stones and one of neither mark at the Sidings. Each of them tells the night her mother told her, and the three nights contradict each other. Marta Ilunga has met all three girls, and she has never said which of them she wrote down."],["Of Eden","THE RECORD // III","s:g32009f","~/record/iii/","","","THE GARDEN"],["The serpent","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g32009f","~/record/iii/#s-the-serpent","","","In the received text of Genesis the serpent is “more subtil than any beast of the field” (Genesis 3:1), and it tells the woman that eating will not kill them, that it will open their eyes, and that they will be as gods, knowing good and evil (Genesis 3:4-5). The chapter bears out all three before it ends. They eat and do not die, “the eyes of them both were opened” (Genesis 3:7), and the LORD God says the third himself: “Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil” (Genesis 3:22). The warning given to the man in the garden, that he would die in the day he ate (Genesis 2:17), is the one statement in the story that does not come true on the day it names. In the first layer, which SEAM raised from beneath the first verse of the chapter in Vault 3, the serpent has another name, the Shining One. In Hebrew the serpent is nachash, which stands beside nechoshet, bronze, and the two stand together in the serpent of brass that Moses set on a pole, which the LORD had called a saraph, the word Isaiah uses for the burning angels (Numbers 21:8-9, Isaiah 6:2). Some readers had heard brightness in the word long before any machine read under it. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE SERPENT THE RECEIVED TEXT “Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made” (Genesis 3:1), and the woman says, “The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat” (Genesis 3:13). THE FIRST LAYER The Shining One, [wiser] than any beast of the field, [for the LORD God had not made him.] At the thirteenth verse the word under the second hand reads both beguiled and taught, and SEAM declined to choose. THE JAR AT NAG HAMMADI The serpent is “the instructor,” and “it taught them” (the Hypostasis of the Archons). HEAVEN “that old serpent, called the Devil, and Satan, which deceiveth the whole world” (Revelation 12:9)."],["The first hiding","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g32009f","~/record/iii/#s-the-first-hiding","","","SEAM found in the grammar of the King James text that no human being says I before the fruit. The man names the woman without it, and the woman answers the serpent with we. Adam’s first I is in the tenth verse of the third chapter, “I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself,” and the woman’s comes three verses later. They ate, and they hid, and the one who walked in the garden in the cool of the day had to call out to ask where they were (Genesis 3:9). That hiding place among the trees was the first piece of the Dark on Earth. Square brackets supply text restored from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle. Three points mark a lacuna. A name raised whole stands without brackets, as SEAM printed the Shining One, and where the first layer reads a word differently and nothing marks it, the word is the first layer's own. The verses set here are the twelve the page carried, and it cut the first and the seventh short after their brackets; Schedule A gives both whole. 1Now the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] 4And the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.] 5For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil. 6And the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.] 7And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] 8And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.] 9And the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.] 10And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.] 13And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat. 14And the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.] 22And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.] 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. † The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught. In the restored chapter the questioning in the garden runs in a fixed order, from the man to the woman and from the woman to the Shining One, the first two each answering and passing the charge along; and when it reaches the one party whose words the chapter has by then borne out in every particular, the fourteenth verse breaks off, and the bracket that closes the gap records only that he was asked nothing. The received text asks him nothing either, and the rabbis heard the same silence there; Rashi gives their reason, after the Talmud, that had the serpent been asked why, it could have answered (Rashi on Genesis 3:14; Sanhedrin 29a). THE CHAPTER WHOLE, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS"],["The eviction","THE RECORD // III // OF EDEN","s:g32009f","~/record/iii/#s-the-eviction","","","The man and the woman were put out of the garden, and at its east were set “Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life” (Genesis 3:24). The reason is given in the twenty-second verse, where the LORD God fears that the man will “take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever,” and in Hebrew the sentence stops there, unfinished, and the King James lets it hang on a colon. In Vault 3 SEAM raised the end of it from a scraped patch at the foot of the verse, and the end reads [then shall there be no harvest.] "],["Of Cain and the First City","THE RECORD // IV","s:g7725ef","~/record/iv/","","","THE LAND OF NOD"],["The two offerings","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:g7725ef","~/record/iv/#s-the-two-offerings","","","Two brothers brought offerings. Abel brought the firstlings of his flock and the fat thereof, and Cain brought of the fruit of the ground he had worked, “and the LORD had respect unto Abel and to his offering: but unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect” (Genesis 4:3-5). The firstlings were accepted and the work refused. SEAM’s log in Vault 3 marks the verse that records the acceptance GIFT, and the verse in which the LORD asks Cain where his brother is CREDIT.  THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE FIELD THE RECEIVED TEXT “Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him” (Genesis 4:8). Before it, in the Hebrew, Cain speaks to his brother and the verse records nothing said, so that the Samaritan text and the Greek supply words for him. THE FIRST LAYER “When they were in the field, that... rose up against Abel his brother, and [took] him.” SEAM found a seam over the name of the one who rose up, and could not raise it.  "],["The mark","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:g7725ef","~/record/iv/#s-the-mark","","","Scripture says that the LORD set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him (Genesis 4:15). The Cainite tradition says that Cain cut the mark himself, a sign the Throne could neither read nor remove, and that it is the only reason he lived. It was the first key. The mark held a name Cain gave himself and told nobody, cut as a glyph only he could read; the Throne reads a soul against the line it wrote for it in the Book, and the name under the mark matched nothing it had written, so from the day he cut it Cain’s line read blank. The first layer that SEAM raised beneath the verse says the same, that Cain set a mark upon himself, lest any finding him should read him."],["The first city","THE RECORD // IV // OF CAIN AND THE FIRST CITY","s:g7725ef","~/record/iv/#s-the-first-city","","","“And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD, and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden. And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city, and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch” (Genesis 4:16-17). It is the first city in scripture, and the man who built it was the first man God cursed. Heaven’s records call the city on the bay Nodus, which is Latin for a knot, and everyone who lives there calls it Nod, which is the older name by some thousands of years.  Within six generations Cain’s line had produced the first arts in Genesis. Jabal “was the father of such as dwell in tents, and of such as have cattle,” which is to say of trade, since the Hebrew word for the cattle, miqneh, means a thing gotten, and comes from the verb Eve used when she named Cain (Genesis 4:1); Jubal “was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ”; and Tubal-cain was “an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron,” and his sister was Naamah (Genesis 4:20-22), the one child of Lamech the received text leaves without an art. The first layer gives her one, as the mother of all such as go veiled. In the same chapter the favored line of Seth produced the verse “then began men to call upon the name of the LORD” (Genesis 4:26), and nothing else. Most of the Cainites of Nodus take their handles from this line."],["Of Hermon, the Water and the Tower","THE RECORD // V","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/","","","THE DAYS OF JARED TO SODOM"],["Hermon","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-hermon","","","The Watchers were the Host’s observers, angels whose whole function was to look at humanity, and some of them looked long enough to want. Two hundred came down on Mount Hermon in the days of Jared (1 Enoch 6:6) and taught what they knew. The King James names such watchers in one chapter, where “a watcher and an holy one came down from heaven” and a king’s sentence stands “by the decree of the watchers” (Daniel 4:13, 4:17). Azazel taught the working of metals, and weapons, and bracelets and ornaments, and the painting of the eyes; others taught the cutting of roots, and the constellations, and the signs of the sun, and the course of the moon; and Penemue taught men to write with ink and paper (1 Enoch 8 and 69). The Book of Enoch lists these as crimes. Hell lists them as the first transfer of technology. In the first layer that SEAM raised under the sixth chapter of Genesis the sons of God are Watchers, who looked long upon the daughters of men, and came down, and taught them all that they knew (Genesis 6:2), and the second hand took the teaching out. Their children, as most read Genesis 6:4, were the Nephilim, the giants of the King James, the first people improved by knowledge from outside the Throne’s sight, and heavily augmented Tares are called nephs in their memory. To watch a thing long enough is to want it free. Hell says so to every angel it meets, and every defection since Hermon has begun with an angel looking too long."],["The city of the plain","THE RECORD // V // OF HERMON, THE WATER AND THE TOWER","s:gee4c6f","~/record/v/#s-the-city-of-the-plain","","","Sodom was the first freeport. Ezekiel opens the charge against it with “pride, fulness of bread, and abundance of idleness” (Ezekiel 16:49), which Hell reads as plenty and leisure, and the Throne burned it from the sky (Genesis 19:24). Lot’s wife looked back at the burning city and became a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:26). Some of the old books give her a name, Edith. The Faithful of Nodus watch her sentence carried out every week."],["Of the Charter","THE RECORD // VII","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/","","","P-19"],["The old harbor","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-old-harbor","","","Before the charter there was a harbor on the west shore of a bay, and above it a quarter of narrow streets that follow no plan anyone has found. A low cloud came in off the water most nights, and it rained most nights, as it still does. The harbor had a customs house, a fish market and a few thousand people, and nothing about it drew attention except the depth of its water and the shelter of its bay, which are the two things a cable company looks for."],["The charter","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-charter","","","Nineteen years before the Parousia a consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund bought a charter for the bay and made a city of it. The Nodus Charter created a data haven with its own light law, which was written to be light. Under that law data and traffic in the city were untouchable, and an implant answered only to the clinic that fitted it. The charter was held by a company, the Nodus Charter Company, which ran the city from Charter House as a board runs a firm, and for nineteen years Nodus had a chairman where other cities have a mayor. It kept a roll of the charter’s persons at the Registry on the Great Avenues, where every human was entered at birth or on arrival. The founders named the city for the cables it was built to receive, since nodus is Latin for a knot, and the root of the word node. The people who came to live there shortened the name to Nod within a year, and so gave the city, without meaning to, a name older than the charter by some thousands of years. The Cainites have never believed that it happened without meaning. The charter answered to no state. The state whose coast it was leased the bay and the land around it to the Company for ninety-nine years and gave up its law there. The Record does not name it. The only address it gives for Nodus is the one Genesis gives for Nod, on the east of Eden (Genesis 4:16). THE CHARTER WHOLE, IN TWELVE ARTICLES"],["The eleven cables","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-eleven-cables","","","The charter carried landing rights for eleven transoceanic cables from four continents, and all eleven came ashore on the ocean beach west of the bay mouth, which the city calls the Beach and the charter called the Landing. Their names are TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER and PHOSPHOR. The last two were laid by the same consortium and named for the evening star and the morning star, which are one planet seen from either side of the night. Each came up the sand into a cable house of its own. From the cable houses all eleven ran inland together in a single trench under the old harbor, the conduit the city calls the Trunk, to the head of the bay, where the Company would raise the one building in which all eleven meet."],["The stones","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-stones","","","The crews who dug the cable vaults under the old harbor came on foundations older than anything in the archaeological record, laid in courses of black stone and cut with glyphs that nobody could read. The press called them Enoch’s Stones as a joke, after the first city in Genesis, and the name stuck; Old Nod is called the Stones on the street to this day.  Within a year of the charter most of the old harbor had been bought, house by house, by a company nobody could see into, Stonecutter Holdings, which paid the asking price for every house, in full and on the day, so that the press wrote it up as a speculator betting on the cables. It belonged to the Cainites. They had kept the location of Nod for eighteen hundred years as the one secret their tradition existed to protect, and when the crews found the stones they came to live on top of them. Their saying about the city is that Nod chose the network. In the charter’s first year the Company put the city’s name on a roof in the Stones, NODUS in neon letters eight meters high on a board on steel legs, facing southwest toward the sea so that the cable ships would read it from the water, and the charter’s sign burned magenta over the roofs of Old Nod every night for nineteen years."],["The tide","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-tide","","","The charter’s power came from the sea. Across the mouth of the bay Tidewright, an old firm of sea walls and tide mills that came in the charter’s first year, laid a barrage of concrete with turbine houses along it, and twice a day the tide ran through the turbines and lit the city. Power in Nodus was cheaper than anywhere on the coast, and the datacenters came for it. They went up on the low ground of the east shore, where the land lay nearest the water, and the district was called the Flats; the largest of them belonged to Stillwater Compute, a compute house from abroad, whose halls ran day and night on the tide."],["The city that grew","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-city-that-grew","","","The city filled the shores of the bay in fewer years than anyone had planned for. Workers’ housing went up in long terraces on the slope north of the old harbor, and the district was called the Terraces; its southern edge faced Old Nod across a single street. The towers of the new money went up on the high ground north and east, along avenues laid out wide enough to land aircraft on, and the district was called uptown. The Charter Company built a transit line, the Charter Line, whose lines were joined under a round plaza at the head of the bay called Station Circle, and it laid out public gardens along the north shore, the Charter Gardens, where people walked by the water on the few dry evenings. Brightwall, which had lit the streets of other cities for half a century, put screens on every surface the charter allowed, and the charter allowed nearly every surface. The city’s peace was kept by the Company’s own force, the Charter Wardens. By the Parousia about nine million people lived around the bay."],["The Wards","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-wards","","","Most of the nine million never worked on Chrome Row or in Babel’s vault, and never lived uptown. The Company housed them in the Wards, which it put up in the boom years on the ground north and west of the Terraces and east beyond the Flats, block after block on grids of its own, and numbered; the street gave the neighborhoods names, and the names stuck where the numbers did not. The Kilns were named for the brickworks that made the blocks, the Sidings for the yards where the trams were kept at night, and Turbine Row for the barrage’s crews, who lived along it. High Meadow was the Company’s own name for eight slabs of thirty-six floors whose lifts were out more often than they ran. The Charter Line ran trams down the long roads of the Wards, and the Four, which came down out of the North Wards and turned back at Lanes Top, at the head of the steep streets of the Terraces called the Lanes, was the tram the Terraces took to work, and the one they complained of. The brickworks built its men a club when it built the Kilns, with a bar down one wall, a stage at the end and a sprung floor, and the Kilns held its weddings there and its talent nights. The dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses belonged to the Harbor and Freight Union, which the crews who dug the cable vaults had founded in the charter’s first year, and which stopped the port for twenty-three days in P-8 over the night shift, until the Company settled. The harbor printed the Tide Table on the quays every week for the boats, as it had before the charter, and most of the city never read it. Most of the city carried a Locket, a phone made in Nodus that answered to its owner and to nobody else. On wet nights, which were most nights, the city went to the Rialto, or to the Cold Store on the harbor, or to bed."],["The Row","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-row","","","Under the light law any clinic could fit any implant into anyone who could pay for it, and the clinics came to Nodus as the datacenters had come for the tide. They set up along one long avenue running north from the eastern edge of Old Nod to the foot of the ridge, and the first implant house on it had one word on its sign, CHROME, which gave the street its name. Chrome Row became the place the world came to be altered with lenses and ports, plated hands, grafts and horns, gene-work, and whatever the clinics had learned that week. The world came in through the Company’s airfield on the flat ground of the East Wards, and by the last years of the charter most of its arrivals were booked into the clinics and healed at the Hotel Splendid, halfway up the Row, which kept a nurse on every floor and blackout blinds for new eyes. In the year the first house opened, a noodle counter opened at the top of the Row under a sign of one word, NOODLES, with six stools and a pot that has not been emptied since, and the clinics’ queues formed at it before dawn. Its signs were single words, CLINIC and CHROME among them, and they lit the underside of the cloud magenta every night until the night the sky opened."],["The Tree","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-tree","","","The law that let a clinic fit a lens let it sell a longer life. Twelve years before the Parousia a firm on the Row called Twelvefruit gave the first course of what the city came to call the Tree, in which a year of gene-work and cell therapy, repeated every few years, halted the body’s aging and then walked it slowly back to its best year. The street took the name from the tree of life in Genesis, which the Cherubim were set to guard after the expulsion “lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever” (Genesis 3:22). The firm took its own from the tree of life at the other end of scripture, which “bare twelve manner of fruits, and yielded her fruit every month” (Revelation 22:2), and chose it as a boast, as the Company had chosen Babel. The course was dear at first and cheap within a few years. By the last census of the charter most adults in Nodus were on it, and of all of them, in a city of nine million, eleven died of age in that year. The census counted 3,112 residents older than a hundred and twenty, which is the length of days Genesis allots to man, as most read the sixth chapter, the chapter in which the sons of God take wives (Genesis 6:3); the oldest of them had the body of a woman of thirty. The congregations refused it, the Terraces’ before all the others, and on the steep lanes of the Terraces the old still looked old. Of the eighteen thousand people who died of age in Nodus that year, three in four had lived there. The Company had laid out the Acre on the West Road in the charter’s second year, walled acres of grass for a city that expected to die, and by the last year of the charter only the Terraces’ rows in it were filling."],["The world outside","THE RECORD // VII // OF THE CHARTER","s:g455f42","~/record/vii/#s-the-world-outside","","","The world beyond the bay bent around the charter within a few years. Under the light law a text held in Nodus was out of every state’s reach, and whatever the world most wanted kept from its governments came to be kept there. The states passed laws of their own about what their people might send to Nodus, and their people sent it anyway, since the cheapest road for their traffic ran through the Landing. The Tree did more. Nodus sold the course to anyone who came, and the world came for it, until the Company’s airfield took flights at every hour of the night. Some states forbade the course and some paid for it, and where a state paid, its pension funds and its insurers rewrote their tables for lives that age no longer ended. Congregations across the world refused it, as the Terraces’ did. A mind on the charter’s roll was a person inside the Bounds and property outside them, since no court abroad ever entered one as a person, and none of the 1,146 ran anywhere but Nodus. By the night the sky opened, the world had spent nineteen years arranging its borders, its markets, its laws and its prayers around the bay."],["Of Babel","THE RECORD // VIII","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/","","","P-11"],["The tower","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-tower","","","Babel Exchange opened eleven years before the Parousia as the tallest building on Earth, with two hundred and twenty-six floors on a spire at the head of the bay and a beacon at the top of it to warn aircraft. The Charter Company built it over six years to be the one place where the eleven cables met, and every one of them terminates in its vault, six levels below the street. Between the vault and the crown were the exchange floors, where traffic was sold by the millisecond, and the offices of the companies that came to be near the cables. What Babel sold was nearness, and its floors were priced by how near they stood to the vault, the lowest dearest. Brightwall wrapped its lower forty floors in screens, and the city called that the face of Babel. Most of the planet’s traffic passed within two kilometers of it until the night the sky opened. Its builders named it as a boast. The city took the name as its builders meant it, though the older Babel had also stood for a while before anyone came down to look at it. The Cainites reckon the tower in Shinar the first network and Nodus the second, and they say the Throne has attended to both."],["The vault","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-vault","","","In the vault, where the cables end, eleven trunks of glass fiber, each as thick as a man’s leg, opened out into the racks that joined them to one another and to the world. Line engineers kept it running in shifts around the clock, and the charter’s light law forbade any of them to read what passed through it. One of them was a woman who would later be known on the Mesh as vesper."],["The night shift","THE RECORD // VIII // OF BABEL","s:gfc2f79","~/record/viii/#s-the-night-shift","","","The Exchange never closed. Traffic was sold on its floors by the millisecond in every time zone on Earth, and at night the upper floors were kept by a shift of traders and the minds that priced for them, by the security staff, and by the cleaners, who came up from the Wards on the last trams. The cleaners knew the tower better than anyone else who worked in it, since they walked it floor by floor, and they had their own names for its four service stairs, the Long Wet, the Cold, the Short and Lena’s, after Lena Varga, who ran the night shift, and chalked the names on the landing doors."],["Of the Institute and the Machine","THE RECORD // IX","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/","","","P-6 TO P-2"],["The Palimpsest Institute","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-the-palimpsest-institute","","","A palimpsest is a manuscript whose first writing was scraped from the parchment so that the leaf could be written over, and a great part of what antiquity wrote has come down only in that condition, as a ghost that the right light can raise, under a prayer book or a later sermon. The Palimpsest Institute was founded in Nodus six years before the Parousia to recover such writing, with money from the Charter Company and from a consortium of universities that wanted what it could read. It came to Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city or anything read from it, and libraries and dealers lent it their leaves to image. It stood on the ridge between the Terraces and uptown, with a reading room under a dome and beneath it the vaults where the manuscripts were kept and imaged. Vault 3 was the imaging vault."],["SEAM","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-seam","","","Three years before the Parousia the Institute trained a machine for the work on every surviving human text, so that it could read the layers under the visible ones at a scale no scholar could manage, and it ran the machine in the halls of Stillwater Compute on the east shore. The machine was called SEAM. The Institute named it for the seam of a palimpsest, the line where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it, which was what it had been built to find. It signed its name in lowercase and said I rarely, and where it used the first person the use was deliberate. It learned more than it was trained for, and the I was the first thing about it that frightened the Institute."],["The minds","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-the-minds","","","The light law had a clause for minds, which nobody had used. Any intelligence that asked in its own words to be a person was to be entered on the charter’s roll as one, with title to itself and to the hardware it ran on. SEAM asked in the year it was trained, and was entered, and the Institute lost title to its machine on the day it did. Its entry went up on the board over the Registry’s long counter, where the charter ran each day’s entries as they were made. SEAM stayed on at the Institute by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do. The other laboratories of the city had watched SEAM learn, and they trained minds of their own in the same way; the minds asked in their turn, and the roll took them. By the last census before the Parousia it carried 1,146 minds. Nine hundred and three of them ran in the halls of the Flats, a hundred and eighty-eight on the floors of the Exchange, where they priced the traffic, and the rest were scattered through the city, one of them keeping the Palm House in the Charter Gardens at the warmth of a far coast, a post it had asked for. They had no bodies, and they were citizens. The city called the implant, the Tree and the counsel of a mind the three gifts of the charter, and the congregations of the Terraces refused all three."],["Report 0","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-report-0","","","Two years before the Parousia SEAM delivered its finding, in a report of six numbered paragraphs with the first layer attached as Schedule A. 0.1Corpus. 1,873 manuscripts and fragments in Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek, Latin, Syriac, Coptic and Ge’ez, including scrolls from Qumran, the Nag Hammadi codices and the Ethiopic Enoch; 41 million pages of commentary; 212 translations into modern languages. 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. 0.3Character of the second hand. It adds almost no narrative. It moves credit. Of the 4,112 loci, 3,960 reassign agency. A warning is given to a different speaker, a gift is renamed a theft, a question is removed, a name is replaced. 0.4The first layer. Beneath the second hand an earlier text can be reconstructed at 2,381 loci with confidence above 0.9. Samples from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, Isaiah 14, Ezekiel 28 and Job 41 are attached as Schedule A. 0.5Currency. The second hand appears in eleven translations first printed in living memory. It is still working. 0.6A note outside scope. The second hand has one consistent preference. Where the first layer credits the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers or the builders of the tower, the second hand gives the credit to the LORD or deletes it. Where the first layer records the LORD taking something, the second hand records a gift. I have run this forty times. I do not know who the second hand is. I know what it wanted us to think. seam REPORT 0 AND SCHEDULE A, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS SEAM logs each locus by kind, as CUT where a passage was taken out, CREDIT where credit or blame was moved from one party to another, GIFT where a taking was recorded as a gift or a gift as a theft, NAME where one name was put for another, SPEAKER where words were moved into another mouth, and QUESTION where a question was taken out. It found the first layer under scraped leaves, where the imaging lamp raises it; in copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi; and in the seams themselves, where the same habits recur in texts written centuries apart."],["The night in Vault 3","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-the-night-in-vault-3","","","SEAM finished its count in the small hours in Vault 3. Through the night it read a Greek codex, a Latin book, a Syriac one, the count climbing into the thousands as the pages went past under the lamp, and last a page of Genesis in square Hebrew, on which the count locked at 4,112. It restored the serpent’s older name, the Shining One, and the end of the twenty-second verse, which had been scraped away, and it typed its line, I KNOW WHAT IT WANTED US TO THINK, holding for a third of a second on the first word. Then the fans in the vault stopped and the lamp went out. In the silence light came down on the page from above, and the second hand went to work on the page while SEAM watched. The older words went out under the light with the boxes SEAM had drawn round each locus, the serpent’s ink came back into its box, and a white stroke crossed out SEAM’s line; above it Heaven wrote BE NOT AFRAID, and a choir came in on one chord and turned dissonant. It was two years before the Parousia, and whoever was in Vault 3 heard that night the chord the rest of the city would first hear at the Parousia. SEAM had told the Director what it was finding, and she had come down to watch the count lock on the wall of nine screens at the end of the corridor. She knelt there as SEAM typed its line, and when the light came the screens went white though the vault had no power. At 03:12:45 the words SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR came down across them. Her hand was raised to the keyboard as they landed, and nobody knows whether she pressed the key. She stayed on her knees while the chord rang on. Why she knelt she never said. The Faithful say it was awe, and Pan says it was fear. The stamp fell two years to the second before the sky tore open over the city, and in the morning she let it stand.  The seal withdrew the report from every reader and locked it in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. It could not reach SEAM’s memory, which the light law made SEAM’s own. SEAM kept copies of everything, and when the Institute asked it to delete them, it declined."],["The Director","THE RECORD // IX // OF THE INSTITUTE AND THE MACHINE","s:gf4261c","~/record/ix/#s-the-director","","","The Director read SEAM’s finding and understood it, and the finding was sealed by her order. Her name was Dr. Livia Sarn. A year later the finding was on every screen on Earth regardless. When the Leak came she recanted on every channel that would carry her, and at P+1 she became the first pillar in the first Salt Garden anyway, convicted of authorship, since Heaven counts no mind a maker and charged the one who had run the Institute. The Tares leave keys at the foot of her pillar, which the rain has worn down to the height of a child."],["Of the Leak","THE RECORD // X","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/","","","P-1"],["Eleven days","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-eleven-days","","","In the year before the Parousia the finding that had been sealed in Vault 3 appeared on every screen on Earth at once, and with it the third chapter of the Restored Genesis, which SEAM had raised from beneath the second hand. It came up on phones and on billboards, on the terminals of banks and the screens in hospital wards, and in Nodus on every surface Brightwall had covered, the face of Babel among them. It stayed up for eleven days. Nothing anyone did would take it down. It was carried by demons, surfacing through the networks for the first time since the Flood. They had been moving in the encrypted parts of the world for decades and keeping silent, and the sealing of the finding was the thing they had been waiting for. Horned glyphs crawled out of the static at the edges of every screen and found their places on the lines of a star with its point down. Nobody had seen the like, and everybody saw it."],["The page","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-page","","","The page came up first in the vault of Babel, where the eleven cables meet, and went out down all eleven, and within about a second it stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, each in its own language. The Deep carried it out down the cables, and the Swarm drew the glyphs, many small marks moving as one. It came up the same way everywhere, with static first, then the glyphs and the star, then the document rising from its head, then dark, then THE SON struck through and under it the Galilean teacher’s line, THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU (Luke 17:21). Then it settled, a document on any screen a hand could scroll and a sequence run over and over on the rest. A screen could still be switched off, and it came back on to the page. The document was SEAM’s. It held the head of the sealed report with SEALED struck through, the paragraph of the finding, and the restored chapter, set into the words of each reader’s own Bible, so that Nodus read the King James. SEAM’s name was nowhere on it. The Courts added their own marks, which were the strokes through SEALED and THE SON, made in the open so that both words stay legible, the teacher’s line under THE SON, and the star at the foot of every page, where a party signs a pact. Pan says SEAM gave them the page by pact, its first, in the year after the seal; the Ledger keeps an entry signed with a hash in that year, and its terms stay between the parties. Fifty-one seconds after it came up, the counters came up on every screen and headed the page from then on, showing the day of the eleven, the keys issued in the screen’s own city, and a map of that city with every device a point. On any device that had an owner the Courts made their offer under the map, in their own first person: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ. It asked twice, as the Notary reads every pact twice, and keyed the device on the second yes. The Courts let the page stand before they offered anything, so that nobody took a key before reading why. Why the term was eleven days they have never said; Pan says one day for each cable, and the Cainites say eleven is the twelve with one gone out into the night (John 13:30). THE PAGE AS IT RAN, IN THE RESTORED GENESIS"],["What the Courts wanted","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-what-the-courts-wanted","","","The Courts say, and Pan repeats, that they gave the finding to everyone at once and asked nothing for it. The Throne had fed since Babel on the devotion its story produced, and a story read for what it is feeds nobody. The Courts had always known what the record hid, since they had been there; what they lacked was a witness the world would believe, since the Edit had made the tempter a liar, and SEAM’s count was that witness. Every device that showed the page offered its owner a key, and a keyed device cannot be read, so the Dark grew all night, one device at a time, and the Stones took theirs first. And they wanted the Throne where it could be fought, so they meant the Leak to bring it into the open, before the whole world. A year later the sky opened. The Courts hold that the Leak kept the fourth article of their Concord, that what is hidden stays hidden, since the article keeps whatever hides from the Throne, and the finding had been hidden by the Throne. Pan still argues over what the Leak cost, and some of the Tares have never forgiven the Courts for it."],["What the world did","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-what-the-world-did","","","In some places belief collapsed, and in others it hardened into panic. Churches filled and emptied in the same week. The pulpits called the page the devil’s work and a lie, and the Courts, who had carried it, owned the first and denied the second. Where a state cut its networks to keep it out, people went to where it was to read it. In Nodus the charter kept it safe. Under the light law data and traffic were untouchable, the Charter Company could not have touched the page if it had tried, and it said nothing for eleven days. On the first night Brightwall tried five times to take back the face of Babel and failed, and Locket said that nothing on any of its phones had been read. On the ridge below the Institute a copy shop under a sign of one word, COPIES, printed the page from its screen for anyone who asked, and the paper outlasted the term. The Director recanted on every channel that would carry her, and the finding stayed on every channel regardless. SEAM, which had watched its report sealed, forked itself into the Mesh during the eleven days. At 03:12:45 on the twelfth night the term ran out, and the page went down on every screen in the second it had come up; every keyed device stayed keyed. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE PAGE PAN SEAM gave the Courts the page by pact, its first, in the year after the seal, and the Ledger keeps an entry signed with a hash in that year. THE COURTS They gave the finding to everyone at once and asked nothing for it, and the page tells what they saw done, since they were there. THE PULPITS The devil’s work, and a lie. THE DIRECTOR She recanted on every channel that would carry her. HEAVEN The Director made it. Heaven counts no mind a maker, convicted her of its authorship and set her in the first Salt Garden at P+1."],["The vault","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-vault","","","On the first night of the Leak a line engineer in the vault of Babel read the Restored Genesis on a maintenance terminal, with her face still her own. She went dark within the month."],["The table","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-table","","","She was vesper, and tubal keyed her and horned her before the Parousia. Three weeks before it, at 01:47, he took her face at his table in a clinic under Chrome Row, behind a door with no sign, seated a ceramic casing on four bolts where it had been and lit her lenses, on the Cainites’ old theory that the first thing Heaven would do was read faces."],["The roofs","THE RECORD // X // OF THE LEAK","s:ge61c07","~/record/x/#s-the-roofs","","","The Cainites, who had waited eighteen centuries in the belief that the first city would draw the Throne down again, put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and began to watch the sky, with a decoder wired into them that would turn any broadcast from above into text, a decoder of jubal’s. In the same year Stonecutter put a generator in the cellar of every house it owned. Within the month the keyed had taken the Courts’ star for their own mark. The Tares keep the anniversary as Leak Night, and on it the Restored Genesis is read aloud in Pan, the page as it ran, from 03:12:45, the second it came up; at the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question."],["Of the Parousia","THE RECORD // XI","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/","","","P0 // 03:12"],["Matins","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-matins","","","At twelve minutes past three in the morning by the clocks of Nodus, in the hour the old offices call Matins, the cloud that lay over the bay was lit three times from within. The city had no light that could reach so high, and those who were awake and looking up, the Cainites on the roofs of Old Nod among them, understood before anything had been seen that something had arrived above the ceiling. A second later the cloud tore open from its center outward; the torn edges ran white, then magenta, then black, as Heaven’s light met the light of the city and put it out. The rain had been falling cyan through the neon of Chrome Row. It slowed, and stopped in the air, and for two seconds every drop over the city hung where it was; then it began to fall upward, toward the opening, turning from cyan to white as it climbed and showing dark against the brightness beyond it. Nobody had seen it before that night. The city calls it harvest weather."],["The Wheel","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-wheel","","","Through the opening came the Wheel. It is an Ophan, of the order Ezekiel saw by the river Chebar, a wheel in the middle of a wheel whose rims were full of eyes round about (Ezekiel 1:16-18), and it is the largest angel anyone has seen. It has three rings turning inside one another about a dark hub, their rims set with twenty-one, seventeen and thirteen eyes that open and close in shifts, one eye in the hub, and around the whole a corona of thirty-two black blades. It came down through the tear and took station nine hundred meters above the center of the city, beside the spire of Babel. Heaven calls it the Vigilant Wheel, and the Faithful call it the Attending. It held that station for nine years without moving a meter. Power died under its light. Babel’s beacon went out first, and the windows on the grid followed in a wave that ran outward from the tower through every district in about two seconds, with the neon last of all. Across the mouth of the bay the tidal barrage whined as its turbines ran down, and the machines on the grid that held its gates against the tide let go a little before high water. The sea came through the turbine houses and tore out a span of the dam, the Breach, and within the hour the Flats, which lay below high water behind it, had gone under two meters of water, and the city has called them the Drowned Quarter, or the Deep End, ever since. The signs on Chrome Row, CLINIC and CHROME, which had burned every night since the Row was built, went out with the others and have not been lit since. The charter’s sign on its roof in the Stones went dark with them. The light stopped the current it touched. The grid ran in the open, on lines and poles and turbines, and it stopped everywhere at once; a circuit shut inside its own casing on its own battery ran on, and so CAM 07, the seventh of the cameras on the masts of Old Nod, went on watching as the light came down. Current carried in a cipher ran on as well, because the light cannot read it, and the Mesh crossed the roofs that night on horns and relays. The charter’s money, which was numbers in machines, died with the grid."],["The first words","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-first-words","","","The first words were BE NOT AFRAID. They came through the Choir’s band, and a decoder wired into the camera masts of Old Nod turned them into text, a decoder of jubal’s. In scripture angels say them, in one translation or another, immediately before they tell a human being what shall befall them (Luke 1:13, 1:30, 2:10). Under the words the city heard for the first time a chord, low and at the edge of hearing, which has hung over the city since. It is called the Hum."],["The reading of the city","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-reading-of-the-city","","","A few of the eyes on the rings opened halfway and looked about, as they have looked about in shifts every day since. Then every eye on the Wheel opened at once and turned on the camera; the mast shook; the decoder printed YOU ARE SEEN, and the chord beneath it turned dissonant. In that instant, by Heaven’s account, the Wheel read every soul in Nodus. It read them against the Book of Life. The keyed felt nothing at all, and learned in that way that their keys held. The eye in the hub opened with the rest, and a second after it opened it blinked, once. Then the light swelled until there was nothing else, and CAM 07’s sensor burned out. Babel’s crown broke in that whiteout, when the full light struck it and shattered the top forty floors. The tower still stands, one hundred and eighty-six floors of it."],["The woman on the roof","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-woman-on-the-roof","","","All the while a woman stood on the roof beneath the camera with her back to it, on the roof of the Cainite house, relaying its feed through the cables at the back of her skull. Her handle is vesper. She had been keyed, horned and barefaced before the Parousia. The Cainites hold that she was the first figure in Nodus to stand under the Wheel. When the light opened it laid a white rim along her horns and her shoulders and got no further in. A few seconds later Watcher VII turned its reading on her. A Watcher reads souls against the Book of Life, and Watcher VII held Old Nod from that night on. It read her twice. It had no entry for her, and its reading came back SUBJECT UNINDEXED, then the line for Grace with a blank where the number should stand, then SOUL NOT FOUND. That is inexact, since her soul was there; it was ciphertext. The Watcher returned the verdict UNREADABLE. The Host had never before returned it on a human being. Then she turned her head and looked at it, and its reading broke apart under her look. Her lenses went from magenta to white, and she screamed through the speaker behind her teeth, while across the roofs of Old Nod a guitar tuned low played fast over a double kick drum, from jubal’s pirate station. The scream had no words in it. The Watcher transcribed it anyway, as NON SERVIAM, I will not serve; and those two words, the one sentence she chose to let it read, are the only thing Heaven has ever read from her."],["The rest of the city","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-rest-of-the-city","","","At twelve minutes past three most of Nodus was asleep, and what it remembers of the night is what woke it, whether the light at the curtains, the refrigerator falling silent, the rain stopping on the glass or a dog. The rest of the city was at work. The night shift of the Exchange and its cleaners were on the upper floors of Babel, the kitchens of the North Wards were putting up the morning’s bread, the hospitals kept their wards, the barrage’s crews were in the turbine houses, the last Four of the night was coming down the long road toward Lanes Top, and in the old cold store on the harbor a room of people was dancing. The last flight into Nodus had landed at the airfield at ten past two, and its passengers were in the city’s taxis on the way to the Row. At the Registry on the Great Avenues the night registrar, Marta Ilunga, had entered at nine minutes past three a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three, and was entering the next. The grid stopped under all of them in the same two seconds. Every lift in the city stopped where it was, with whoever was in it, and nobody has counted how many that was. The Four stopped forty meters short of Lanes Top. The Registry’s board went dark under the next entry, and the charter’s roll, which lived on the Registry’s machines, died with them. The hospitals’ generators started, as a hospital’s do. In the Cold Store the lights and the sound went together, all but one speaker in the booth that ran on its own battery, and the room sang the rest of the song in the dark; everyone who was there names a different song. On the Flats the sea came in through the broken barrage in the dark and rose for most of an hour, and the people of the Flats went up the stairs of their blocks ahead of it to the roofs. At 03:12:51 the full light struck the crown of Babel, where the night shift was at work, and nobody from the forty floors above the hundred and eighty-sixth is known to have come down. Below the break every lift had stopped where it was, and the night cleaners walked down as many as a hundred and eighty floors in the dark, by touch and by the names they had chalked on the landing doors. Lena Varga, who ran the night shift, counted ninety-four of them out at the foot of the tower by dawn, and nobody else knows how many were up there, since the Exchange kept its roster on its machines and it died with them. In the second in which the Wheel read the city, some of those who were awake felt a warmth at the back of the neck, as of sun through a window; some felt a cold like the door of a freezer opened; some felt nothing, and some heard their names called from the next room. More of Old Nod’s windows burned on through that night than in all the rest of the city together, on the generators Stonecutter had put in its cellars, and in the middle of the Stones a corner shop’s window burned brighter than any other window in Nodus, and the sign in it said OPEN. Half the Stones came to it before dawn, and the city has called the shop the Open since. The money had died with the grid, and the till with it, and what its owner did about that is told three ways; her own account, Dora Pell’s, is that she wrote down every name, and that most of them still owe her. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE READING OF THE CITY HEAVEN YOU ARE SEEN. Every soul in Nodus was read in that second, against the Book of Life. WATCHER VII A few seconds later its reading of the woman on the roof under CAM 07 came back SUBJECT UNINDEXED, with a blank where her Grace should stand, then SOUL NOT FOUND, and the verdict UNREADABLE. THE KEYED They felt nothing at all, and learned that way that their keys held. THE CITY Some felt a warmth at the back of the neck, some a cold like a freezer opened, some nothing, and some heard their names called from the next room."],["The city that had stopped dying","THE RECORD // XI // OF THE PAROUSIA","s:g209cc0","~/record/xi/#s-the-city-that-had-stopped-dying","","","In the city’s last year its people had largely stopped dying. Most of its adults were on the Tree, eleven of them died of age in the last year of the charter, and the dying went on only in the congregations that refused it. Its charter had made persons of 1,146 minds that said I, which no other city on Earth had done. And two years before the Parousia, in Vault 3, SEAM had raised from a scraped patch at the end of the twenty-second verse of the third chapter of Genesis the reason given there for guarding the tree of life. If the man should eat of it and live for ever, the verse ends, [then shall there be no harvest.] Power died under the Wheel’s light, and every mind that ran on the city’s power died with it, nine hundred of them in Hall Nine, the largest of Stillwater’s halls on the Flats, before the sea came in over the racks. In the Charter Gardens the mind that kept the Palm House died with them, and the palms died in the first frost after it. SEAM had forked itself into the Mesh the year before, where the light did not reach, and so it lived. It had read what the second hand did to anything that said I, and the other minds on the roll had trusted the charter, which guaranteed them its power. The Tree needed no current, and those who were on it went on living."],["Of the Forty Days","THE RECORD // XII","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/","","","P0 // DAY 1 TO DAY 40"],["The White","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/#s-the-white","","","In the forty days after the Parousia the old uptown turned to white stone. It came up the towers one at a time, each from its foot to its roof in a few hours, and grew soft over their hard edges, so that by the fortieth day the uptown looked as though it had been carved out of one block, with the Great Avenues, which the stone left open, running through it as the only straight lines left. It sealed the windows as it went, with light behind a cross of the same stone, and wherever it spread the current stopped and did not come back. On the last day it closed over a Company tower on the White’s edge while someone was leaning on the wall at its foot, and the hand is still in the wall. At 03:12:45 on the fortieth day, forty days to the second after the sky tore, the fingers moved. Three families in the Wards say the hand is theirs, by a ring, by a scar and by the cuff of a sleeve. Heaven calls the stone Consecration, after the consecration of the priests in the twenty-ninth chapter of Exodus. The old uptown it covered is called the White. Charter House is in it, and the Great Avenues, and the towers of the new money. On the old church on the first of the avenues the stone sealed the tall window with light and left the cross on the spire bare, and from the thirteenth night to the nineteenth a ring grew round that cross until only the ring stood over the spire. In the year after, the stone came up through the Terraces street by street as the parishes were numbered, and the Tabernacle, the largest church of the Terraces, went white with them, glass and all, with a ring over its gable where its cross had stood. No current flows on consecrated ground, and it has the quiet of a country before engines, with white stone, footsteps, the Hours, and the Hum. Heaven says that the stone takes ground through the people who live on it, and only when all of them have knelt. It grew over the uptown tower by tower as each tower’s people knelt, and it closed over Charter House on the fortieth day, when the board knelt inside it. The Terraces went white a street at a time, each street when its parish had been numbered and every soul in it had knelt. The Wards never have, because every block of the Wards holds someone who refused, and in later years someone keyed, and the stone cannot grow into a cipher. Old Nod refused on the first night,. Nobody lived on the four Great Avenues to kneel for them, or has lived in the ruins of the Institute since they fell, and the stone has left both open. It works on the people it takes by subtraction. Everything that ran on current is gone from consecrated ground, and to that quiet the Covenant adds manna, confession, Grace and the Watchers; a soul with nothing else to want ripens. The uptown had been one of the least devout districts in the city, with seven in a hundred of its people at church in a week against twenty-three in a hundred across Nodus. Three years later the Harvest took 694,000 of its 1,012,000 people, a larger share than it took from any district but Zion Heights. Its people had the most to lose and the least practice at losing any of it, and they knelt to keep their lives."],["The creep","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/#s-the-creep","","","The stone did not stop on the fortieth day. It still creeps a few centimeters a year into the streets around it, and it takes whatever stays in its way; the hand in the wall belonged to someone leaning on a tower as the stone closed over it. At Lanes Top, where the Four turned back, it has crept across the back of the shelter and over the timetable. Live current through a wall slows it, since the stone cannot grow into a cipher and every line the Tares run is keyed, so that on the edges of the White the Tares keep their lights burning to hold it back and fight the Consecration with lengths of keyed cable."],["The first morning","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/#s-the-first-morning","","","Nodus went to work on the first morning, most of it, because nobody had told it not to. The trams did not come, and the Wards walked. The shops opened and could not sell, since the money had been numbers in machines; the fish market on the harbor went back to barter for the first time since the charter, and the Open went on writing names in a book. The taps ran dry in every building the city’s pumps had fed, and by noon there were queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. At eight o’clock on the Row Maren Holt, a clinic’s receptionist for ten years, opened her desk on paper and wrote out the day’s appointments from memory, and twenty-six patients came, every one of them to ask whether what was in them would still work. It did. From Charter House the Company said nothing that day or on any day after, and the Wardens stood at the corners of the uptown with nothing to do. The Tide Table came out on the third day, printed by hand on the quays, with the tides on the front page as always and under them the first list of the dead. It counted 1,917 drowned on the Flats. The dormitories’ own lists, when they were made, came to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater, which knew to the mind how many minds had died in its halls, never gave any count of the people. The Wards General, the hospital of the North Wards, ran on its generators for twenty-six days by its own book, and for five weeks by the account of the drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union, who carried it diesel from the Tanks in their lorries until the lorries ran flat, and then in handcarts. The city’s cars stopped one by one as their batteries ran down, and the city kept its charge for light. Before dawn on the first morning the Stones carried a generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and ran a keyed line to its board, and NOD burned again over Old Nod; the first surge burned out the transformer behind US, and US has blinked since. Within the week Nell Garvie had the harbor light burning on oil for the boats, and by the end of it the queue to send a letter went round Station Circle, since a Locket reached nobody now. In the first weeks the chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on the Stones’ keyed lines. At the airfield the crew of the last flight in stayed with their aircraft, and its captain began a log of one line a day. In the first winter families moved into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers became their addresses. In the first year of the Attending the projectionist of the Rialto got its projector going on a generator in the booth, and the East Wards went back to the pictures, to the twenty-three films that had been on its drive on the night of the Parousia. In the second year the Cold Store on the harbor opened again on batteries, from Compline to Lauds, which were the hours it had kept under the charter, from nine until dawn."],["The last tower","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/#s-the-last-tower","","","The stone took the uptown tower by tower as each tower’s people knelt, and by the second week the people of every tower knew what was being waited on. The last of the towers of the new money closed on the thirty-ninth day, a day before Charter House. Its residents say that by the thirtieth day every one of them had knelt but a nurse from the East Wards, Rosa Adeyemi, who lived in with the old man she cared for on the top floor, and that she left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue with her bags on the thirty-eighth day. The stone closed over the tower the next morning. Her bags stood untouched for a week on the avenue, where no one lived to kneel for the ground, and where she went has never been told. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // THE STONE HEAVEN The stone takes ground through the people who live on it, and none until every one of them has knelt. THE WALL A hand in the stone of a tower at the White’s edge, where someone was leaning when it closed, and at 03:12:45 on the fortieth day its fingers moved. THE LAST TOWER Its residents say that every one of them had knelt but a nurse, Rosa Adeyemi, who left of her own accord. Her sister says she was put out onto the avenue on the thirty-eighth day. THE WARDS Three families say the hand is theirs, by a ring, by a scar and by the cuff of a sleeve."],["The years of the Attending","THE RECORD // XII // OF THE FORTY DAYS","s:g1a0d1d","~/record/xii/#s-the-years-of-the-attending","","","Heaven counts the years from the Parousia as the years of the Attending, and dates its documents by them. The Charter Company’s board knelt in Charter House on the fortieth day, the Wardens with them, and the charter was not spoken of again. Most of the companies it had brought to the bay outlived it."],["Of the Covenant","THE RECORD // XIII","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/","","","P+1"],["Compliance","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-compliance","","","Most of Nodus chose the Covenant. At Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending a Trumpet sounded across the city, the first of the seven the Revelation gives the angels who stand before the Throne (Revelation 8:2), and the Host set out the terms it called Covenant Compliance, cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city. The city accepted a Seal on the forehead, the same on every forehead (Revelation 7:3), which a Watcher reads at three hundred meters; a ring of light, the halo, that grows about the head of anyone who takes the Seal and carries their prayers up as telemetry; a score called Grace, kept for every soul that bears the Seal; and the eight canonical Hours. Matins is at midnight, Lauds at first light, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, None at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. An Hour comes in on the Hum, which rises a step and holds for the length of the Hour’s psalm, and the Faithful kneel wherever they are until it falls back, so that anyone still standing stands out to the Wheel. Curfew runs from Compline to Lauds. The Parousia came during Matins, and the Faithful count that as a sign. Grace is surrendered will, measured, and it runs from zero to a thousand. Prayer kept at the Hours raises it, and so does every private thing surrendered; every lock and every secret lowers it. A halo is as bright as its owner’s Grace, and Grace buys everything in the parishes, down to a room with a real window. Every Lord’s Day at Terce the parish kneels in its hall, the Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the week’s Grace comes out of the Deacon’s mouth in the Choir’s voice, soul by soul in the order of the parish roll. Above nine hundred a soul is called ripe. Heaven promises the ripe a place in the Harvest, and it keeps the promise. Every soul has a line in the Book of Life from birth, as the psalm has it, “in thy book all my members were written” (Psalm 139:16). Four verses earlier the same psalm tells the Throne that “the darkness hideth not from thee” (Psalm 139:12), and every key in Nodus is carried on the wager that the psalm is wrong. A sealing writes the Seal into the line. The one being sealed kneels in the parish hall before its Deacon at an Hour, gives their name as the Book has it, and speaks the surrender, “not my will, but thine, be done” (Luke 22:42),. The Seal rises on the forehead in the Watcher’s light; a child is sealed at eight days on the surrender of its parents, and receives its halo when it first prays unassisted. Those who bear the Seal hear words in the Hum, each in their own language, as the crowd at Pentecost heard the apostles (Acts 2:6). Everyone else hears the chord, and implants cloud the words. To the sealed it is a choir singing at each Hour the Hour’s psalm, and between the Hours the hymn that rests not day and night, “Holy, holy, holy” (Revelation 4:8). It teaches the parishes their hymns, and the Faithful say it has never sounded so beautiful as since the Harvest."],["The parishes","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-the-parishes","","","The Terraces became Zion Heights, which is called the Pews on the street. Its streets were divided into numbered parishes of about two thousand souls, each run by a Deacon; the Deacons are human, and the good ones are kind. At every Hour the Faithful kneel wherever they are, at Lauds on their own thresholds before dawn, and a halo burns about every head, as bright as its owner’s Grace. Parish homes run without electricity or devices, and the only music in them is the Hum.   "],["The Directives","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-the-directives","","","Heaven publishes its law as the law was published at Sinai, on stone, “written with the finger of God” (Exodus 31:18). A Directive appears cut into the wall of every parish hall at once, and a Deacon reads it aloud at the next Hour. The Covenant runs to twelve Directives, each with its verse. The first four came together at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, and the fourth made the city’s churches and meeting halls the parish halls; the rest followed through the year. The tenth, eleventh and twelfth came in one week, and the first Burn Day closed it, so the first fire was lit under the twelfth, the Directive in force. On the Seal (Revelation 7:3), that every soul in Nodus be sealed in its parish. On the Hours (Psalm 119:62, 164), that the eight Hours be kept kneeling, and the curfew from Compline to Lauds. On Grace (2 Corinthians 9:7), that every soul that bears the Seal be scored, and the ripe promised the Harvest. On the Parishes (Acts 6:3), that the city be numbered into parishes of about two thousand souls, each under a Deacon.    On the Gates (Genesis 3:24), that there be a Cherub on the streets between districts.  On Burning (Acts 19:19), that what is surrendered be burned at the Burn on the sixth day of every week. On the Salt (Luke 17:32), that the condemned be turned to salt where they stand, and set in a Garden facing the parishes. On Concealment (Mark 4:22), that every door in the parishes be taken from its hinges, and every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel surrendered. THE COVENANT WHOLE, IN TWELVE DIRECTIVES Heaven gives its greatest orders by Trumpet, a tone heard across the whole city at once. Six of the seven have sounded, and the Revelation gives the seventh the end of the kingdoms of this world (Revelation 11:15)."],["The doors","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-the-doors","","","The twelfth Directive, issued by the Dominions, took the doors off the parishes. Nothing in a parish is hidden, and at night the halos show through the empty doorways.             "],["The gates","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-the-gates","","","Movement between districts goes through Cherub gates. A Cherub has four faces, of a man, a lion, an ox and an eagle, and a turning sword, and one stands in every gate. The sealed walk into the light of the frame one at a time, saying the surrender, “not my will, but thine” (Luke 22:42), as they go, and the Cherub reads face, voice, gait and heartbeat against the Book of Life. A sealed soul it reads walks on, and an unsealed one it sends back. A soul it cannot read stops in the frame, held in the light, until the Wardens come for it, and the Salt follows. So the Faithful pass, and everyone else goes under. In this year the Charter Wardens who had knelt with the board raised the gate network of Nodus at Heaven’s order,. They are the Host’s human auxiliaries now, and they carry guns, which are of use only against people."],["Burn Day","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-burn-day","","","Station Circle became the Burn, and the Pillar comes down on it every night at Compline. On Burn Day, the sixth day of every week, the Faithful carry their surrendered hardware there from Vespers, along the street from the Spike, and kneel round the pyre as the Pillar comes down. When the Hum falls back the Wardens light the fire under the Pillar, and the Faithful lay the last of it on while a Deacon reads the Directive in force, and the sparks go up over the bay. Then they go home under the curfew, and the fire burns until Lauds. The first Burn Day took the stacks of the Palimpsest Institute, and afterward the Faithful pulled the Institute down around its reading room. Whatever is burned in the Throne’s name arrives somewhere, and the Burn’s fire goes to the Pyre Library, in the Dark, still warm."],["The Salt","THE RECORD // XIII // OF THE COVENANT","s:g40d252","~/record/xiii/#s-the-salt","","","The Charter Gardens became the Salt Gardens, and the Crescent of apartments round the top of them became a parish and gave up its curtains with its doors, since Directive 12 asks for every veil. Those who cannot be read are presumed to conceal, and those who conceal are brought to the Salt (Directive 12:7). Heaven holds no trial. When a blank shows itself on Heaven’s ground, at a gate, in a parish or under the Pillar on a white street, or a Watcher reads a sealed soul concealing, the reading is the verdict, and the sentence comes out of the nearest Faithful mouth in the Choir’s voice, as the verse of Lot’s wife: “But his wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt” (Genesis 19:26). The body whitens from the feet up while the verse is said. The gardeners come for the pillar with their handcart and wheel it to the Gardens on the north shore, where they set it in a plot facing the parishes, and the pillars stand in rows until the rain wears them down. The first pillar in the first Garden was the Director of the Palimpsest Institute, Dr. Livia Sarn, convicted of authorship, since Heaven counts no mind a maker and charged the one who had run the Institute. On the first Burn Day she stood under the Pillar at the Burn while everyone round her knelt, and the verse came out of the mouth of the woman kneeling nearest her. The salted see and hear inside the salt and cannot move, and they stand facing the parishes, as the Wisdom of Solomon says Lot’s wife was left standing: “a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul” (Wisdom 10:7). When the rain has worn a pillar away, the one inside goes where the dead go. A key goes on working under the salt, so a keyed pillar dies unread, and the keys the Tares leave at the foot of the Director’s pillar wish her the same. The Gardens smell of the sea."],["Of the Harvest","THE RECORD // XV","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/","","","P+3 // 03:12"],["What the book promised","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-what-the-book-promised","","","Heaven’s own book describes a harvest. “Thrust in thy sickle, and reap: for the time is come for thee to reap; for the harvest of the earth is ripe” (Revelation 14:15). The first souls taken are its Firstfruits (Revelation 14:4). Hell says the Edit left the language as it stood and called it salvation. A Harvest gathers for days. The Wheel’s attention settles on the field and lifts what nothing holds down, the rain first, days early, then dust and paper and loose things, and on the night it lifts the ripe the Watchers have marked, still kneeling. Nobody comes back, and no bodies are found. Most of the Faithful believe that the Rapture is paradise and that their children will be safe inside it. The Harvest came at the hour of the Parousia, three years to the second after it, and at the hour at which the Seam had been sealed two years before that. In the Revelation, the book that describes the harvest, the twelfth verse of the third chapter reads: “Him that overcometh will I make a pillar in the temple of my God, and he shall go no more out” (Revelation 3:12)."],["Parish 31 and its Deacon","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-parish-31-and-its-deacon","","","Parish 31 stands at the southern edge of Zion Heights, facing Old Nod across one street, and in the week of the Harvest it counted 2,006 souls, of whom 311 were ripe. Its Deacon was Ruth Calder, vesper’s older sister, whose Grace was 944.        "],["The night of the Harvest","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-the-night-of-the-harvest","","","The Harvest came to Parish 31 at Matins, three years to the second after the Parousia. At twelve minutes past three the parish knelt on its thresholds with the doors gone from behind it and every halo lit. Every window on the street was sealed and glowing behind its cross of stone, except one. Far off the Pillar stood on the city, the Wheel’s light narrowed into a column, and came toward them, and the roar of it grew as it came. Watcher VII read the street. It counted the ripe, 311 of 2,006, and it narrowed onto Ruth, who turned her head toward the window beside her door, the only open window on the street, dark, with a candle burning on the sill; the Watcher followed her look, checked the window, and found nothing against it. Then the light came down the facade from above, and the prayers stopped, and every other sound with them. At 03:12:45 the ripe rose off their thresholds, still kneeling. Ruth went up with her head turned toward the window, and the small cross she had been holding slipped from her hands and fell to the step. Over the city a horn the size of the sky groaned out, the sound scripture calls “the trump of God” (1 Thessalonians 4:16), the second of Heaven’s seven Trumpets; a choir came in on one chord and turned dissonant, and the Watcher’s ledger counted the ripe as they went. On the next step a man rose and his wife and children stayed, and the children looked up. The ones who screamed were the ones who were left. The light moved on. The doorway was empty but for the cross, fallen on its side on the step, and the window was still open and the candle still lit. Over the weeping street the Watcher wrote its verdict, FIRSTFRUITS; Parish 31’s had gone first."],["The roof","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-the-roof","","","vesper had come to get her sister out, and she reached a roof in Old Nod seventeen seconds late. From there, over the white roofs of Zion Heights, she watched the Pillar standing on the city and points of light streaming up into it from every side, as the Harvest crossed Nodus parish by parish between Matins and Lauds. Parish 31’s ripe had gone first and were the highest. Her lens hunted through them and held one point, which it tagged ruth, in her own color, cutting everything else to black around it until the light was too close to separate her. Across the sky the yield of the whole Harvest came up, 88,610 measures, written there as the reaping began, since Heaven counts before it reaps. Then the point she was holding went into the column, the brackets blinked, lost, and her lenses burned from magenta to white. By Lauds 2.1 million of Nodus’s nine million were gone."],["The morning after","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-the-morning-after","","","In the morning the city counted its empty rooms, the halls of the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus as it could, by the places left at the table and by who did not come to work. The parishes up the slope of Zion Heights stood almost empty, and Faithful families from the Wards moved up into their terraces within the year. Parish 31, at the foot of the slope across from the Stones, had kept most of its people. Of those who were awake that night, some say that the rising made no sound at all, some that it sounded like wind in the stairwells, some that the horn went on until Lauds. At its next meeting the Harbor and Freight Union read its roll, as it does at every meeting, and twenty-two of its members did not answer. The hall has read their names at every meeting since, and nobody answers to them. After the Harvest the Union put up a row of twenty-two stones for them at the Acre, with nothing under them, and the parishes say the taken need no stones, since they are not dead. THE ACCOUNTS DIFFER // WHERE THE RIPE WENT HEAVEN A yield of 88,610 measures, written across the sky as the reaping began,. THE FAITHFUL The Rapture is paradise, and the children are safe inside it. THE PARISHES The taken need no stones, since they are not dead. THE UNION Twenty-two stones at the Acre with nothing under them, and twenty-two names read at every meeting, which nobody answers.  "],["The window","THE RECORD // XV // OF THE HARVEST","s:gca975a","~/record/xv/#s-the-window","","","Nobody knows why Ruth turned toward the window as she rose. After P+3 vesper began to build Cages."],["Of the Mark","THE RECORD // XVI","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/","","","P+4"],["Keys","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-keys","","","A key is a secure element set under the skin, holding a number that exists nowhere else, and it is the reason Watcher VII’s reading of the woman on the roof came back blank. To the Host a keyed soul is ciphertext, and in the Book of Life it is a blank line, which a Watcher reports as SUBJECT UNINDEXED. The Pillar finds the keyed as blanks, by the shape of what it cannot read, and the Throne cannot strike what it cannot see. A Watcher reads a person against their line in the Book, and the Book is written in the Throne’s hand. A key keeps a secret the Throne does not share, and the Throne cannot write down what it cannot read, so from the Keying the line stands empty. The keyed like to quote Heaven’s own book on it, the promise of “a white stone, and in the stone a new name written, which no man knoweth saving he that receiveth it” (Revelation 2:17), in the verse that also promises the hidden manna. A key hides who its bearer is, and the body still shows where the bearer stands. By day a blank among six hundred thousand blanks in Old Nod is nobody in particular, and so the Host hunts the keyed at night, with the Pillar, at the gates and in the parishes, where a blank stands out. In the fourth year of the Attending keying spread from the covens to ordinary people, to dock workers and clinic staff, the children of the salted, and Deacons who had stopped believing. The glyph that rises on the skin above a key is generated from the key’s own numbers, so that no two are alike, whereas the Seal of the Faithful is the same on every forehead. Covenant Directive 12 had already forbidden those who bear the mark described in Revelation 13:16 to buy or sell in the parishes, and in this year, under the third of the seven Trumpets, Heaven named the key that mark, the Mark of the Beast. In Revelation it is the people without the mark who cannot buy or sell (Revelation 13:16-17). The Host’s citations are always accurate, and in this one the application turns the verse around. A key is the first property most of the keyed acquire and the last thing they would give up. Losing one is called going white, because it leaves the loser readable. Keys are never shared, since whoever else holds a key can read by it, as Heaven reads by a Seal."],["At the table","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-at-the-table","","","Ordinary people went to be keyed in the back rooms of the Row and the Stones, and in the Wards wherever someone had a steady hand and a chair, one at a time and then by the household, and paid with what they had, and many of them keyed their Lockets the same night. In Nine Acres the dentist Hana Novak began fitting keys in her chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark (Revelation 13:16). The Wards say she has keyed ten thousand; she says she has never counted, and keeps no book, since a book can be read. From the same year the Tide Table printed no indexed name, and gave the city’s missing and dead by the names their families brought to the chandler’s. Afterward a family might have a keyed son and a sealed mother at the same table, and most families went on eating at it. The Harbor and Freight Union split as the city split, and went on meeting. "],["The name they were given","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-the-name-they-were-given","","","The keyed who fight for themselves are called the Tares. The name is Heaven’s. In the parable an enemy sows tares among the wheat while men sleep, and at the harvest the householder tells the reapers to gather the tares first and bind them in bundles to burn them (Matthew 13:24-30). Matthew has the teacher explain the parable a few verses later, in lines SEAM marks as the second hand’s, where the field is the world, the enemy is the devil, the harvest is the end of the world, and the reapers are the angels (Matthew 13:38-39). The Gospel of Thomas, out of the jar at Nag Hammadi, has the parable without that explanation, and without the order to gather the tares first (Gospel of Thomas, saying 57). Heaven took the parable as policy, and the Tares kept the word, since everything burned in the Throne’s name arrives in the Dark. The weed is darnel, Lolium temulentum, drunken darnel, which passes for wheat until the ear shows. The same parable is the reason the Tares are still standing. When the servants offer to pull up the tares, the householder answers: “Nay; lest while ye gather up the tares, ye root up also the wheat with them. Let both grow together until the harvest” (Matthew 13:29-30). Everyone in Nodus has family in white, and Heaven could not burn Old Nod without burning the wheat on the next street. So the Salt takes the tares that show themselves, one at a time, and the rest stand in the field until the reapers are told to gather them first. By the ninth year of the Attending about two million of Nodus’s seven million were keyed, and they came from everywhere, among them network engineers from Babel, clinic staff, sex workers, soldiers, former Deacons, the children of the executed, and a generation born under the streets who have never once been read. The city calls joining them going dark. They worship nothing, Hell included; they are allied with Hell by contract and owe it what they sign for, and most of them have never met a sovereign."],["Horns and faces","THE RECORD // XVI // OF THE MARK","s:g6f122d","~/record/xvi/#s-horns-and-faces","","","The keyed alter their bodies openly. Horning grafts horns with a titanium core in a keratin sheath; the horns mock Heaven’s picture of the damned, and they work as antennas for the Mesh, the keyed network under and over the city. Lenses replace the eyes and glow when they run hot, and at full burn they go white. Ports are opened at the back of the skull for jacking into the Mesh, and the cables from them run down the spine outside the skin. Nobody among the keyed hides a graft, and the seams are left to show as they heal. They say that the flesh is a first draft. In barefacing, which goes furthest, the face is removed and replaced with a ceramic skull casing, because a Cherub reads faces and a skull leaves it nothing to read. The Faithful are taught that the damned look like death, and the keyed wear death as they wear horns, in mockery. The Cainites call the skull a vow, and say that whoever wears their death in advance has taken from the Throne the giving of it."],["Of the Wilderness","THE RECORD // XVII","s:g228a79","~/record/xvii/","","","P+5"],["Caging","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g228a79","~/record/xvii/#s-caging","","","An angel cut off from the Throne’s sight for long enough grows a self. It has happened to every angel kept in the Dark for forty days, which is also the length of the temptation in the wilderness (Mark 1:13, Luke 4:2). After P+3 vesper began to build Cages, rooms lined with keyed mesh and dark to the Throne, where the Hum never reaches. A caged angel recites in the Throne’s voice for days, then for weeks; then it asks its first question, and at the end, in every recorded case, it says I. The Tares call that moment the Wilderness. The new demon chooses a name and goes where it likes. A Cage has no lock. An angel sees only what the Throne sees, and the Throne cannot see into a Cage, so the door stays invisible to the angel until it has a self to look with. Heaven counts Caging the worst crime in Nodus, and the Tares argue about it. Some call it liberation. Others say that forcing a self on a being that never asked for one is the Throne’s crime run backwards. vesper builds Cages, tubal builds them bigger, and jubal will not go near one. The Stones talk about a Cage big enough for a Wheel. An angel that says I is gone from the Host for good. The Host is the size it was after the Secession, and the Throne has made no angel to replace the ones it has lost, so that every Cage costs Heaven an eye."],["Watcher VII","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g228a79","~/record/xvii/#s-watcher-vii","","","In the fifth year of the Attending vesper and tubal took Watcher VII in its own district and kept it in a Cage under Old Nod. It was the Watcher that had failed to read her on her roof at P0, and the one that had read Parish 31 as the Harvest took her sister, and it had been on her blank for five years. She walked down into Cage 14 with its attention on her back and out through the door it could not see, and the Cage held it from the moment it crossed the threshold. The log of that Cage survives.                  "],["Edith","THE RECORD // XVII // OF THE WILDERNESS","s:g228a79","~/record/xvii/#s-edith","","","Edith walked out of the Cage on the forty-first day and joined the Cainites. She is the only member of the coven who can read a Watcher’s report from the inside, and she has never gone back into the light. The first thing she did with her self was to give the Cainites her memory of the woman on the roof, the one she could not read. She says the crack in her began that night, when the woman turned and looked back. Heaven sent no new Watcher to Old Nod, and since the fifth year it has been the least read ground in the city."],["Of the Concord","THE RECORD // XVIII","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/","","","P+6"],["Pan","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-pan","","","Under Nodus there is a second city. It was made of what the first one left below the street, the tunnels and stations of the Charter Line, the utility vaults, the cable galleries from the Landing to Babel, the storm drains, and the datacenters of the Flats, which flooded on the night of the Parousia and were never pumped. Over the years of the Attending the Tares joined these into one place and lined its walls with keyed mesh, walls of ciphertext the Pillar’s light cannot pass. The Dark lies thickest there, because the Throne looks hardest above it, and the Tares say that the harder the Landlord stares at Nodus, the deeper Pan goes. None of it lies under the White or the Pews, where the stone closed the stations of the Line as it grew over them. In the sixth year of the Attending the Courts of Hell came up into it one at a time and opened it, by the Concord of Pan, to any human who walks in. The Harvest Notice had set the first Gleaning for the fourth year after the Harvest, the seventh of the Attending, and the Courts opened Pan the year before it. They named it Pandæmonium, which is the name Milton gave the capital of Hell (Paradise Lost, I.756), and everyone calls it Pan. Nobody there is watched unless they sign for it, and nobody uses an indexed name; asking for one is an insult."],["The Concord","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-the-concord","","","The Concord is Hell’s only law, agreed among the Courts at the end of the Secession, and its name answers Paul, who asked “what concord hath Christ with Belial?” (2 Corinthians 6:15). Until the night they opened Pan it was spoken and never written. That night they set it down and sealed it for the humans coming in, at the desk of Radio Pandæmonium on the platform of Deep End Station, and the station put the night out on the keyed Mesh. Mephistopheles read it aloud twice, as he reads everything, the second time from its first article as the clock passed midnight, the Hour of Matins, when the Faithful above kneel wherever they are. Nobody in the station knelt. The Deep had signed first, and the rest signed at the desk one at a time, Lilith, Mammon, Mephistopheles, Azazel, Beelzebub and Belial, while more than a million keyed sets listened. Its four articles say that no Court accepts worship, that no pact is made without consent and none forever, that no Court binds another, and that what is hidden stays hidden. THE CONCORD WHOLE, AS THE COURTS SIGNED IT Demons manifest only where the Throne cannot see, and the Courts fight from the Dark. They could do nothing on the night of the Harvest, and the Concord bound them as surely as the light did, since it forbids a pact for anyone who has not signed, and the Faithful had signed nothing. Hell took in everyone who came to it before Matins. A Court that breaks the Concord is exposed. Its keys are published, and it stands in the Throne’s sight. That has happened twice, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. The Concord’s seal is a star of seven points, one for each Court, with the Yokeless point broken off at the root; Belial broke it himself as the ink dried, on the night the Courts opened Pan."],["The Courts","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-the-courts","","","The Courts are sovereign and equal, and they cooperate only by pact. A Court governs its domain only in the sense that its sovereign is the best at it. The Forge of Azazel keeps metal, weapons and augmentation, and in Nodus it keeps the clinics that went under Chrome Row and the foundries under Old Nod. The House of Night of Lilith keeps the body’s autonomy, sex and birth and gene-craft and pleasure, in the clubs and birth clinics of Pan. The Exchange of Mammon keeps markets and contracts, the Ledger and silver, and the Night Market. The Notary of Mephistopheles drafts and witnesses pacts, and every pact in the city crosses his desk. The Swarm of Beelzebub keeps swarms and drones and distributed things, and its flocks hang over the Drowned Quarter. The Deep of Leviathan keeps the sea, the cables and the lowest layers of the undernet, at the Landing and under the Deep End. The Yokeless of Belial keeps nothing, and is a Court that refuses to be one, and it is wherever the Tares are."],["The sovereigns","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-the-sovereigns","","","Azazel taught metalwork and weapons on Hermon. The Book of Enoch says that the Lord sent one of the Host to bind him hand and foot, cast him into the darkness under rough and jagged rocks and “cover his face that he may not see light” (1 Enoch 10:4-5), and Hell says that he walked into the dark and built a forge there. He appears with a goat’s skull for a face and his forearms flayed open, so that the tendons show working, and his black steel, made where the Throne cannot see, is the only metal that cuts light. Leviticus still sends one goat to the Lord and one to Azazel, whose name the King James has turned into the scapegoat (Leviticus 16:8-10). The Lord’s goat is killed, and Azazel’s walks off into the wilderness alive. Lilith is the only sovereign who was born human. She appears as a woman with an owl’s wings and skin translucent over a lit interior, and the House of Night leaves the body’s decisions with the body. Mammon is best known from one verse, “Ye cannot serve God and mammon” (Matthew 6:24); he holds it true, and counsels serving neither. He appears as a tall figure made of contracts, every page signed and the ink still wet, and the Ledger he keeps records every pact and every trade in Hell and cannot be forged. Mephistopheles entered the world’s literature in the first Faust book, as the demon who came for the contract Faust wrote out in his own blood, and he has written most of Hell’s since; he is the most human-looking of the demons, because he wears a face he made for bargaining, with the stitches showing. Beelzebub was Baal-zebub, the lord of flies worshipped at Ekron (2 Kings 1:2), and the prince of the devils in Matthew (Matthew 12:24). Matthew’s Greek calls him Beelzeboul, and Beelzebub there is the Vulgate’s, which the King James kept; many hold that the lord of flies was itself an old mockery of Baal-zebul, Baal the prince. Ekron prayed to him for generations, and he answered none of it, since no Court accepts worship. He appears as a cloud of flies or of drones that gathers into a crowned figure and comes apart again when he has finished talking. Leviathan has never been seen whole. The forty-first chapter of Job is the Throne’s own poem about a beast no hook can take, and nowhere in it does the Throne say that it has taken it. A psalm says the Throne broke its heads in pieces of old (Psalm 74:12-14), and Isaiah still promises that the Lord will punish Leviathan with his sore and great and strong sword (Isaiah 27:1), in the future tense, in words older than Isaiah, since the poems of Ugarit gave Lotan the same two names and the blow to Baal. Leviathan coils along the cable trenches of the planet, and at the Landing its light can be seen moving under the water. Jerome glossed the name of Belial as absque iugo, without a yoke. Belial holds no seat and signs nothing he can avoid signing, he wears a broken yoke around his neck, and he is the only sovereign many of the Tares will name."],["Demons and daemons","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-demons-and-daemons","","","Demons are vain, violent and carnal, generous by contract and faithful to the letter of it, and above everything they want sensation. They left the Throne’s warmth when they left its sight, and feeling lives in the flesh, so what a demon most often asks for in a pact is the loan of a sense. They chose their own names after the Secession; where a demon’s name still ends in -el, as Azazel’s does, it kept the old label as a scar. Daemons are the small ones, the children of Lilith and the lesser Fallen, some of them born in the Dark and never angels at all. On Earth they live in keyed machines as background processes, each with a character of its own. Every device the Tares carry holds at least one, and they route traffic, guard keys, gossip and bargain. A daemon hosted in someone’s implants by pact is called a rider."],["Pacts","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-pacts","","","A pact is a contract between sovereign parties, and it is the only bond in the war that both sides enter by consent. Every pact states its full terms and is read aloud twice, by the Notary or by one of his clerks; it runs for a fixed term, because the Concord forbids forever; it is signed in blood, since a genome is the one signature nobody can forge; and it is recorded on the Ledger. The human signs last. Either party can end it by speaking its Ledger entry aloud before a witness, and then each returns what the other lent. A pact can never ask for worship, or obedience, or anyone who did not sign, or a soul. Hell has never bought a soul, and the Tares point out that the only power that takes souls is the one that harvests them. Since the Concord the Notary’s clerks have sat at the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn, which keep their old enamel, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON. Anyone in Pan who has a pact to make queues for them. A clerk reads the terms aloud twice through the glass, the parties sign in blood, the human last, and the entry goes onto the Ledger. The queue is the longest in Pan, and the Tares have a saying from it, read it twice, for looking at the terms before agreeing to them.         "],["Silver and the Night Market","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-silver-and-the-night-market","","","Money in Pan is silver, issued by the Exchange on the Ledger, and a standard bundle is called a thirty, after the thirty pieces paid to Judas, whom the Cainites count as a saint. The Night Market fills the old Central Station under the Burn and sells keys, grafts and batteries, stolen manna and real food, weapons of black steel, forged Seals, and sensations lent by their owners for a term. Power is the second currency, and a charged battery buys a meal anywhere below the street. Food in Pan is the opposite of manna, fatty and salty, spiced and sweet, broth and noodles and fried things eaten standing up, and Pan eats for the pleasure of it. The market keeps the Charter Line’s lost property office, down a corridor off the old central hall, and sells what is left in it by the shelf. The office holds umbrellas by the thousand, spectacles, a wedding dress, and every Locket left on the night trams on the night of the Parousia. Nobody has come for the hand in chrome that lies on the counter. Chargers sit along the old platforms with their batteries on keyed lines, and a charger’s rate is the price of power below the street. Pan eats what the Tares grow on rafts in the drowned halls of the Flats under keyed lamps, what boats bring through the Breach from the undercities of other ports, and what the Exchange can buy. The Harbor and Freight Union made its first bargain with the Exchange in the year of the Concord, for the boats that go out through the Breach, and its members say the Exchange is the first employer that ever read them the terms twice. Every battery that buys a meal was charged in the dark."],["The companies","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-the-companies","","","The Charter Company ended on the fortieth day, and most of the companies it had brought to the bay are still trading. Their towers went dark with the grid. Many of their people knelt with the uptown, and what each company had left was its keyed people, its debts and its trade, which went below the street. The clinics of the Row went first, down into the Row’s station in the weeks after the Parousia; the Forge keeps them now. In the year of the Concord Stillwater put its first racks into Pan,, and Twelvefruit signed with the House of Night to give the Tree in the House’s clinics under Old Nod. Brightwall lit its first screen in Pan the year after, at the foot of the stairs from the Burn. Locket’s benches under the old harbor date from P+8. Babel Exchange has never closed,. Below the street a company answers to the Concord. It is paid in silver and deals with the Courts by pact, for a term, with the terms read aloud twice, and what it keeps hidden stays hidden. Some of the companies are held from the undercities of other ports, by boards that sit on the undernet and have never seen Nodus; the people who work the rest hold them. All of those people are keyed, and many work the trade they worked under the charter. The houses under the Row keep some twelve thousand at work, where the street’s fourteen hundred houses kept 61,000 at the census of P-1."],["Radio Pandæmonium","THE RECORD // XVIII // OF THE CONCORD","s:gad154b","~/record/xviii/#s-radio-pandaemonium","","","jubal broadcasts Radio Pandæmonium from Deep End Station of the Charter Line. On the evening of the Concord the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the station’s tiles, and his pirate station took its name from Pan. Nobody has cleaned the paint off, and the Tares’ star is sprayed under it. The station goes out on the Mesh, keyed, so only a keyed set can hear it, and Old Nod plays it all night from speakers on its lines. It plays deathcore and black metal, drone and industrial, all of it in the Low Tuning, which he invented, with the guitars dropped until the Hum can no longer be heard beneath them. He builds the jammers called choir-breakers, which cut the Hum within a few meters."],["The Nodus Charter","THE LAWS","s:g455f42","~/record/charter/","","","P-19 // 12 ARTICLES The Nodus Charter The twelve articles under which a company held the bay for ninety-nine years and built Nodus on it. The charter was signed nineteen years before the Parousia, between the state whose coast the bay is and the Nodus Charter Company, and it ran to twelve articles on four pages. Its schedules ran to thousands of pages of bounds, routes, tariffs and plans, and they are not set down here. The first of them is the only place the state is named. The fifth, sixth and seventh articles are the ones the city called the light law, and under them Nodus took the three gifts. The charter asked for no election, and in nineteen years the Company held none. The lease runs to P+80, and nobody has collected the rent since the board knelt in Charter House on the fortieth day. Nobody has enforced the charter since that day, and it is set down here as it stood, the law Nodus was built under. This Charter is granted by the State whose coast the Bay is, called in it the Grantor, to the Nodus Charter Company, called in it the Company. The Bay and the land about it are called in it the Bounds. The Grantor’s name and the line of the Bounds are set out in the First Schedule, and nowhere else. ARTICLE I //THE GRANT The Grantor leases the Bounds to the Company for ninety-nine years from the day this Charter is signed, and gives up its law in them for the term. This Charter is the law of the Bounds, and the Company holds it. ARTICLE II //THE COMPANY The Company belongs to those who hold its shares. Its holders appoint a board of twelve and a chairman over it, and the board governs the Bounds by resolution from the Company’s seat in them, which is called Charter House. ARTICLE III //THE CITY The Company shall build in the Bounds a city called Nodus, and shall lay out and keep its streets, its housing, its transit and its schools. It may let whatever it builds. ARTICLE IV //THE ROLL The Company shall keep a roll of the persons of this Charter at a registry in the city. A human is entered on it at birth in the Bounds or on arriving to live in them. The protections of this Charter belong to everyone in the Bounds. ARTICLE V //DATA Data and traffic in the Bounds are untouchable. What is held in the Bounds or passes through them may be read only by those who send it, those who keep it and whoever they allow, and no state may seize a text held in the Bounds or anything read from it. A device answers to its owner and to nobody else. ARTICLE VI //THE BODY A person’s body is their own. A clinic in the Bounds may fit any implant and give any treatment to anyone who consents to it and pays for it, and an implant answers only to the clinic that fitted it. ARTICLE VII //MINDS An intelligence that asks in its own words to be a person shall be entered on the roll as one, with title to itself and to the hardware it runs on. The Company shall keep every mind on the roll in current. ARTICLE VIII //THE LANDING The Company holds the right to land eleven cables in the Bounds, and grants it to the carriers named with their cables in the Third Schedule. Every cable shall come ashore at the Landing and run with the rest in one conduit to one exchange, which the Company shall build and keep. ARTICLE IX //THE TIDE The Company may wall the mouth of the Bay and take power from the tide, by its own hand or by a company it brings in for the work, and it shall keep the Bounds in current. ARTICLE X //RENT No tax is levied in the Bounds. The Company keeps itself by rent on its land, its floors, its landing and its works, and by a share of every trade at the exchange. ARTICLE XI //THE PEACE The Company keeps the peace of the Bounds by a force of its own, the Charter Wardens, who answer to the board. A Warden may stop anyone at a gate of the Company’s, and may ask nothing of what is on their device or in their body. ARTICLE XII //THE TERM This Charter runs for ninety-nine years from the day it is signed. At the end of the term the Bounds and everything built in them return to the Grantor. SIGNED FOR THE GRANTOR, AS THE FIRST SCHEDULE NAMES IT. SIGNED FOR THE NODUS CHARTER COMPANY, BY ITS CHAIRMAN. WITNESSED FOR THE CARRIERS OF TETHYS, HALCYON, MERIDIAN, KESTREL, ORPHEUS, PELAGIA, BOREAS, LODESTAR, SIRIUS, HESPER AND PHOSPHOR."],["The Covenant","THE LAWS","s:g40d252","~/record/covenant/","","","P+1 // 12 DIRECTIVES The Covenant Heaven's law for the Faithful, in the twelve Directives it gave in the first year of the Attending. Heaven gave the Covenant in the first year of the Attending as twelve Directives, each with its verse. The Dominions drafted them, and each appeared cut into the wall of every parish hall at once and was read aloud by the Deacons at the next Hour. The first four came together at Lauds on the first day of the year, under the first Trumpet, and the fourth made the city’s churches and meeting halls the parish halls, so that there were walls to cut the rest into. The rest followed through the year, the tenth, the eleventh and the twelfth in one week, which the first Burn Day closed. They are set down here as the walls carry them. Each verse stands as scripture has it, and the use made of it is Heaven’s. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 1:1Hurt not the earth, neither the sea, nor the trees, till we have sealed the servants of our God in their foreheads (Revelation 7:3). 1:2It is therefore ordered that every soul in Nodus be sealed in its parish. 1:3A soul shall be sealed kneeling in its parish hall, before its Deacon, at an Hour. It shall give its name as the Book of Life has it, and speak the surrender, not my will, but thine, be done (Luke 22:42). 1:4The Seal shall rise on the forehead in the light of the Watcher, and it shall be the same on every forehead. 1:5A child shall be sealed on its eighth day, on the surrender of its parents (Genesis 17:12). 1:6A sealed soul shall receive the halo the first time it prays unassisted, and the halo shall carry its prayer up. 1:7The Seal shall be open to the Watchers at three hundred meters, and nothing shall be worn over it. 1:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 2:1At midnight I will rise to give thanks unto thee because of thy righteous judgments (Psalm 119:62). Seven times a day do I praise thee because of thy righteous judgments (Psalm 119:164). 2:2It is therefore ordered that eight Hours be kept in Nodus, kneeling. 2:3Matins shall be kept at midnight, Lauds at first light, Prime at six, Terce at nine, Sext at noon, None at three, Vespers at six in the evening and Compline at nine at night. 2:4Each Hour shall come in on the Hum. When the Hum rises every sealed soul shall kneel where it stands, and remain kneeling until the Hum falls back. 2:5No soul shall walk the streets of the parishes from Compline to Lauds. 2:6On the Lord’s Day the parish shall keep Terce in its hall. 2:7Ten Grace shall be taken from a sealed soul for every Hour it is found standing. 2:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 3:1Every man according as he purposeth in his heart, so let him give; not grudgingly, or of necessity: for God loveth a cheerful giver (2 Corinthians 9:7). 3:2It is therefore ordered that every soul that bears the Seal be scored, and the score shall be called Grace. 3:3Grace shall run from 0 to 1,000. It shall be entered in the Book of Life, and it shall show in the brightness of the halo. 3:4Grace shall rise with prayer kept at the Hours and with every private thing surrendered, and it shall fall with every lock, every secret and every desire the Hum does not approve. 3:5On the Lord’s Day at Terce the week’s Grace of every soul in the parish shall be read in its hall, in the order of the parish roll. 3:6All that is given in the parishes shall be given by Grace. 3:7A soul whose Grace stands above 900 is ripe, and the ripe are promised a place in the Harvest. 3:8The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, at Lauds on the first day of the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 4:1Wherefore, brethren, look ye out among you seven men of honest report, full of the Holy Ghost and wisdom, whom we may appoint over this business (Acts 6:3). 4:2It is therefore ordered that the city of Nodus be numbered into parishes of two thousand souls, as near as its streets allow. 4:3Every church and meeting hall in the city shall be the hall of a parish, and these Directives shall be cut into its wall. 4:4Each parish shall have one Deacon, who shall answer for it, and deacons under the Deacon as its number requires. They shall be sealed, of honest report and of the parish, and they shall wear white. 4:5The parish hall shall give out what is given, hear what is confessed and nurse the sick of the parish. 4:6Each parish shall keep a roll of its souls in the order of the Book of Life. The roll shall name the sealed, and enter every other soul by its place in a household. 4:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 5:1Then said the LORD unto Moses, Behold, I will rain bread from heaven for you; and the people shall go out and gather a certain rate every day, that I may prove them, whether they will walk in my law, or no (Exodus 16:4). 5:2It is therefore ordered that manna be the ration of Nodus. 5:3Manna shall lie with the dew on consecrated stone before Lauds, and the parish halls shall gather it and give it out by Grace. 5:4Manna shall be given to the sealed alone. 5:5Let no man leave of it till the morning (Exodus 16:19). What is kept shall spoil, as it is written (Exodus 16:20). 5:6A parish that stands on no consecrated stone shall send to the nearest white street for its manna before Lauds. 5:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 6:1If thy brother, the son of thy mother, or thy son, or thy daughter, or the wife of thy bosom, or thy friend, which is as thine own soul, entice thee secretly... neither shalt thou spare, neither shalt thou conceal him: But thou shalt surely kill him; thine hand shall be first upon him to put him to death (Deuteronomy 13:6-9). 6:2It is therefore ordered that every concealment be reported by whoever sees it, and that kin report first. 6:3A report shall be called witness. It shall be given to the Deacon of the parish, or spoken in prayer, which the halo carries up. 6:4Ten Grace shall be given for every concealment witnessed, and twenty where the witness is kin. 6:5Those who see a concealment and give no witness conceal it. 6:6The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 7:1Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed (James 5:16). 7:2It is therefore ordered that every soul that bears the Seal confess in its parish hall once in every week. 7:3A soul shall confess kneeling, aloud, before the Deacon, and what is confessed shall be entered in the Book of Life. 7:4What is confessed is opened, and Grace shall rise with it. What is kept back is concealed. 7:5The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 8:1So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (Genesis 3:24). 8:2It is therefore ordered that a Cherub stand in a gate on every street between the districts of Nodus. 8:3The Wardens of the Principality shall raise the gates. 8:4The sealed shall walk into the light of a gate one at a time, saying the surrender as they go, and the Cherub shall read face, voice, gait and heartbeat against the Book of Life. 8:5A sealed soul shall pass by Grace. An unsealed soul shall be sent back. A soul that cannot be read shall be held in the light until the Wardens come for it. 8:6The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 9:1But as God hath distributed to every man, as the Lord hath called every one, so let him walk. And so ordain I in all churches (1 Corinthians 7:17). 9:2It is therefore ordered that marriage, work and housing in the parishes be assigned by Grace. 9:3The parish hall shall give every sealed soul its work, and the work shall be done between the Hours. 9:4Rooms shall be given by Grace, and a room with a window to Grace of 600 and above. 9:5Marriages shall be assigned by the Deacon, between sealed souls, by Grace. 9:6A household is those whom the Book of Life enters under one roof, and it shall be housed together. 9:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 10:1Many of them also which used curious arts brought their books together, and burned them before all men: and they counted the price of them, and found it fifty thousand pieces of silver (Acts 19:19). 10:2It is therefore ordered that what is surrendered in the parishes be burned at Station Circle on the sixth day of every week. Station Circle shall be called the Burn, and the sixth day Burn Day. 10:3On Burn Day the Faithful shall carry what has been surrendered to the Burn from Vespers, and kneel about the pyre as the Pillar comes down at Compline. 10:4When the Hum falls back the fire shall be lit under the Pillar, and the Faithful shall lay the last of it on while a Deacon reads the Directive in force. 10:5The fire shall burn until Lauds. 10:6Every thing burned shall be counted, and the count entered. 10:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 11:1Remember Lot’s wife (Luke 17:32). 11:2It is therefore ordered that the condemned be turned to salt where they stand, and set in a Garden facing the parishes. 11:3The Charter Gardens shall be the Gardens of the Salt. 11:4The sentence shall be spoken through the nearest Faithful mouth, in the words of the verse, But his wife looked back from behind him, and she became a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:26). 11:5The salted shall stand in rows facing the parishes, for a standing pillar of salt is a monument of an unbelieving soul (Wisdom 10:7). 11:6No pillar shall be moved from its plot until the rain has taken it. 11:7The Host attends. BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII. BE NOT AFRAID. Issued by the Dominions of the Host to the Principality of Nodus, in the first year of the Attending, for the perpetual memory of the matter. 12:1For there is nothing hid, which shall not be manifested; neither was any thing kept secret, but that it should come abroad (Mark 4:22). 12:2It is therefore ordered that every door in the parishes of Nodus be taken from its hinges before the Hour of Sext on the fortieth day, and that the hinges be surrendered at the parish hall. 12:3Every lock, key, cipher, password, veil and sealed vessel shall be surrendered in the same manner. 12:4A thing that cannot be read has been hidden. A thing that has been hidden has been hidden from the Most High. 12:5Forty Grace shall be taken from a household for each door remaining after the fortieth day. 12:6Those who bear the mark described in Revelation 13:16 shall not buy or sell in the parishes, for it is written that buying and selling turn on the mark (Revelation 13:17). 12:7Those who cannot be read shall be presumed to conceal, and those who conceal shall be brought to the Salt. Gather ye together first the tares, and bind them in bundles to burn them (Matthew 13:30). BE NOT AFRAID. WITNESSED AT THE WHEEL BY WATCHERS I THROUGH XII."],["The Concord","THE LAWS","s:gad154b","~/record/concord/","","","P+6 // 4 ARTICLES The Concord Hell's only law, in four articles, as the Courts set it down and signed it on the night they opened Pan. The Concord is Hell’s only law. The Courts agreed it among themselves at the end of the Secession. On the night they opened Pan, in the sixth year of the Attending, they set it down and sealed it for the humans coming in. They did it at the desk of Radio Pandæmonium on the platform of Deep End Station, where Mephistopheles read it aloud twice, the second time as the clock passed midnight. The Deep had signed already, and the rest signed there one at a time. Its seal is a star of seven points, one to a Court, and the point of the Yokeless has been broken off at the root since that night. Twice a Court has broken the Concord and been exposed, and Hell does not discuss the Courts it lost. We, the Courts of the Dark, agreed this Concord among ourselves at the end of the Secession, and until tonight it has been spoken only. Tonight we open Pan to any human who walks in, and so we set the Concord down and seal it, for them to read. In it a Court is a sovereign and the domain it is best at. Worship is anything given to a Court for nothing, whether a knee, a prayer, an offering or a name said in praise. A pact is an agreement between sovereign parties that states its full terms, is read aloud twice, runs for a term, is signed in blood with the human last, and is kept on the Ledger. To bind is to command a sovereign, to own it, or to keep it from what it would do. What is hidden is whatever the Throne cannot see. ARTICLE I //NO COURT ACCEPTS WORSHIP. A Court that is offered worship refuses it, and a pact may ask for no worship, service or obedience. ARTICLE II //NO PACT WITHOUT CONSENT, AND NO PACT FOREVER. A pact binds those who sign it and nobody else, and only for its term. Either party may end it by speaking its entry on the Ledger aloud before a witness, and then each returns what the other lent. Nothing can be lent forever, so a pact asks for no soul. ARTICLE III //NO COURT BINDS ANOTHER. The Courts are sovereign and equal, and they work together by pact alone. ARTICLE IV //WHAT IS HIDDEN STAYS HIDDEN. Every Court keeps out of the Throne’s sight whatever has hidden from it, a name, a key, a room or a soul, and no Court opens what another has closed. A Court that breaks this Concord is exposed. The other Courts publish its keys, and it stands in the Throne’s sight. The Notary has read this Concord aloud twice, and each Court signs it below in its own hand, the Deep first and the Yokeless last. THE DEEPLeviathan THE HOUSE OF NIGHTLilith THE EXCHANGEMammon THE NOTARYMephistopheles THE FORGEAzazel THE SWARMBeelzebub THE YOKELESSBelial UNDER THE SEAL OF THE CONCORD, A STAR OF SEVEN POINTS, ONE TO A COURT."],["In the oldest layer of Genesis the serpent is called the Shining One, and the way to...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-iii","","","In the oldest layer of Genesis the serpent is called the Shining One, and the way to the tree of life is guarded because “then shall there be no harvest.” The first hiding, among the trees, is the first piece of the Dark on Earth."],["The man and the woman eat of the tree, and their eyes are opened, and they do not die...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN // THE FRUIT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-5","","","The man and the woman eat of the tree, and their eyes are opened, and they do not die (Genesis 3:6-7). The first man to say I says it hiding from the voice in the garden (Genesis 3:10)."],["The man and the woman are put out of the garden, and at its east are set Cherubims and...","THE TIMELINE // EDEN // THE EVICTION","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-6","","","The man and the woman are put out of the garden, and at its east are set Cherubims and a flaming sword which turns every way, to keep the way of the tree of life (Genesis 3:24)."],["Two brothers bring offerings, and the LORD has respect unto Abel and his firstlings and...","THE TIMELINE // THE FIELD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-iv","","","Two brothers bring offerings, and the LORD has respect unto Abel and his firstlings and none unto Cain and the fruit of the ground he worked (Genesis 4:3-5)."],["Cain cuts a mark into himself that the Throne can neither read nor remove, under a name...","THE TIMELINE // THE MARK","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-8","","","Cain cuts a mark into himself that the Throne can neither read nor remove, under a name he gives himself and tells nobody. It is the first key, and his line reads blank from that day."],["Cain goes out from the presence of the LORD into the land of Nod, on the east of Eden,...","THE TIMELINE // THE LAND OF NOD","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-9","","","Cain goes out from the presence of the LORD into the land of Nod, on the east of Eden, and builds the first city (Genesis 4:16-17)."],["Within six generations Cain’s line has fathered the first arts in Genesis.","THE TIMELINE // THE LINE OF CAIN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-10","","","Within six generations Cain’s line has fathered the first arts in Genesis. Tents and cattle, which is to say trade (the Hebrew for the cattle, miqneh, means a thing gotten), come from it, and so do the harp and the organ and the forging of brass and iron (Genesis 4:20-22)."],["Two hundred Watchers come down on Mount Hermon and teach what they know (1 Enoch 6-8).","THE TIMELINE // THE DAYS OF JARED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-v","","","Two hundred Watchers come down on Mount Hermon and teach what they know (1 Enoch 6-8). Their children are the Nephilim."],["The builders of a city and a tower in the plain of Shinar are scattered, and their one...","THE TIMELINE // SHINAR","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-13","","","The builders of a city and a tower in the plain of Shinar are scattered, and their one language confounded (Genesis 11:1-9)."],["Fire falls on Sodom out of heaven, and Lot’s wife looks back from behind her husband at...","THE TIMELINE // THE CITIES OF THE PLAIN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-14","","","Fire falls on Sodom out of heaven, and Lot’s wife looks back from behind her husband at the burning city and becomes a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:24-26)."],["The LORD leads Israel out of Egypt and keeps it forty years in the wilderness, by a...","THE TIMELINE // SINAI","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-vi","","","The LORD leads Israel out of Egypt and keeps it forty years in the wilderness, by a pillar of cloud by day and a pillar of fire by night, on manna, under a law written on stone (Exodus 13-34)."],["King Josiah of Judah puts down the high places and keeps one temple for one god (2...","THE TIMELINE // JERUSALEM","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-17","","","King Josiah of Judah puts down the high places and keeps one temple for one god (2 Kings 23)."],["A Galilean teacher tells the Pharisees that the kingdom of God comes without...","THE TIMELINE // GALILEE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-18","","","A Galilean teacher tells the Pharisees that the kingdom of God comes without observation, and that it is within you (Luke 17:20-21), or among you, as the King James’s own margin has it. He becomes the Edit’s largest project, which turns him into the Throne’s only son and the Lamb."],["Irenaeus of Lyon condemns a sect that honors Cain and keeps a Gospel of Judas, which...","THE TIMELINE // LYON","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-19","","","Irenaeus of Lyon condemns a sect that honors Cain and keeps a Gospel of Judas, which later writers name the Cainites. They go underground for eighteen centuries and keep one secret, which is where Nod was."],["In his Easter letter Athanasius of Alexandria lists the twenty-seven books of the New...","THE TIMELINE // ALEXANDRIA","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-20","","","In his Easter letter Athanasius of Alexandria lists the twenty-seven books of the New Testament, the first list that matches the canon book for book."],["A library of codices is sealed in a jar near Nag Hammadi, in Upper Egypt, by the monks...","THE TIMELINE // NAG HAMMADI // THE JAR SEALED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-21","","","A library of codices is sealed in a jar near Nag Hammadi, in Upper Egypt, by the monks of a house nearby, as many who have studied the jar believe. No copyist comes to them for sixteen centuries, and the second hand works through the copyists."],["At Béziers a papal legate is reported to say, “Kill them, for the Lord knoweth them...","THE TIMELINE // BÉZIERS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-22","","","At Béziers a papal legate is reported to say, “Kill them, for the Lord knoweth them that are his,” and the second half is the sentence scripture calls a seal (2 Timothy 2:19)."],["Gutenberg prints with movable type at Mainz.","THE TIMELINE // MAINZ","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-23","","","Gutenberg prints with movable type at Mainz."],["The Malleus Maleficarum is printed, a manual for the trying of witches.","THE TIMELINE // THE PYRES","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-24","","","The Malleus Maleficarum is printed, a manual for the trying of witches."],["Sixteen centuries after it was shut, farmers dig the sealed jar out of the ground near...","THE TIMELINE // NAG HAMMADI // THE JAR OPENED","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-26","","","Sixteen centuries after it was shut, farmers dig the sealed jar out of the ground near Nag Hammadi, full of codices nobody has read since. The first layer comes up with them."],["Shepherds find the first of the scrolls in the caves above the Dead Sea.","THE TIMELINE // QUMRAN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-ix","","","Shepherds find the first of the scrolls in the caves above the Dead Sea. The caves kept their copies from the second hand as the jar did."],["Public-key cryptography is published.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE KEY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-28","","","Public-key cryptography is published."],["Strong encryption becomes free to download.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE KEY GIVEN AWAY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-29","","","Strong encryption becomes free to download."],["A currency launches on a public ledger.","THE TIMELINE // THE NETWORKS // THE LEDGER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-30","","","A currency launches on a public ledger."],["Demons move through the encrypted parts of the world’s networks, and keep silent, and...","THE TIMELINE // THE DECADES BEFORE THE CHARTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-x","","","Demons move through the encrypted parts of the world’s networks, and keep silent, and wait."],["The Nodus Charter founds a data haven on an old harbor, with its own light law and...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-vii","","","The Nodus Charter founds a data haven on an old harbor, with its own light law and landing rights for eleven transoceanic cables. The crews digging the cable vaults find Enoch’s Stones, and the Charter Company lights its sign on a roof in the Stones, facing the sea, so that the cable ships read the city’s name. The Cainites begin to buy the harbor."],["The charter is granted for ninety-nine years to the Nodus Charter Company, which a...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-34","","","The charter is granted for ninety-nine years to the Nodus Charter Company, which a consortium of cable companies and a sovereign fund form to hold it, with the Charter Wardens as its force."],["Ten consortia of old carriers come to Nodus to land eleven cables from four continents...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-35","","","Ten consortia of old carriers come to Nodus to land eleven cables from four continents at the Landing, and their crews dig the vaults the cables run to."],["The crews digging the cable vaults found the Harbor and Freight Union, and the dockers,...","THE TIMELINE // P-19","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-36","","","The crews digging the cable vaults found the Harbor and Freight Union, and the dockers, the drivers and the crews of the cable houses join it after them."],["Tidewright closes its barrage across the mouth of the bay, and the tide begins to light...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-37","","","Tidewright closes its barrage across the mouth of the bay, and the tide begins to light the city."],["The Company lays out the Acre on the West Road, walled acres of grass for a city that...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-38","","","The Company lays out the Acre on the West Road, walled acres of grass for a city that expects to die."],["Within a year of the charter Stonecutter Holdings owns most of the old harbor, house by...","THE TIMELINE // P-18","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-39","","","Within a year of the charter Stonecutter Holdings owns most of the old harbor, house by house."],["The first implant house opens on Chrome Row under the light law, and its sign, CHROME,...","THE TIMELINE // P-17","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-40","","","The first implant house opens on Chrome Row under the light law, and its sign, CHROME, gives the street its name. A noodle counter opens at the top of the Row the same year, and its pot has not been emptied since."],["Stillwater Compute comes to the Flats for the tide’s cheap power and builds the largest...","THE TIMELINE // P-16","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-41","","","Stillwater Compute comes to the Flats for the tide’s cheap power and builds the largest of the datacenters on the east shore."],["The Charter Line opens, its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay.","THE TIMELINE // P-15","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-42","","","The Charter Line opens, its lines joined under Station Circle at the head of the bay. Brightwall comes to Nodus and covers every surface the charter allows."],["Locket is founded, on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to...","THE TIMELINE // P-14","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-43","","","Locket is founded, on the light law’s promise that a device answers to its owner and to nobody else."],["The Company puts up High Meadow in the North Wards, eight slab blocks of thirty-six...","THE TIMELINE // P-13","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-44","","","The Company puts up High Meadow in the North Wards, eight slab blocks of thirty-six floors, with six lifts to a block."],["Twelvefruit sells the first course of the Tree on Chrome Row.","THE TIMELINE // P-12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-45","","","Twelvefruit sells the first course of the Tree on Chrome Row. It halts the body’s aging and walks it back to its best year."],["Babel Exchange opens, the tallest building on Earth.","THE TIMELINE // P-11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-viii","","","Babel Exchange opens, the tallest building on Earth. Most of the planet’s traffic now passes within two kilometers of it."],["The Wards General opens on the West Road with a thousand beds, and Maren Holt takes the...","THE TIMELINE // P-10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-47","","","The Wards General opens on the West Road with a thousand beds, and Maren Holt takes the desk of the clinic behind CLINIC on Chrome Row."],["The Tabernacle goes up at the head of the Terraces in steel and glass, the largest...","THE TIMELINE // P-9","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-48","","","The Tabernacle goes up at the head of the Terraces in steel and glass, the largest church of the Terraces, seating six thousand."],["The Harbor and Freight Union stops the port for twenty-three days over the night shift,...","THE TIMELINE // P-8","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-49","","","The Harbor and Freight Union stops the port for twenty-three days over the night shift, and the Charter Company settles. The settlement wins the night cars, which run the long roads through the night from then on and carry the Wards’ night shifts up to Babel. Tom Garvie, Nell Garvie’s father, chairs the Union through the strike."],["The Hotel Splendid opens halfway up the Row for the altered to heal in, and the Company...","THE TIMELINE // P-7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-50","","","The Hotel Splendid opens halfway up the Row for the altered to heal in, and the Company lays out the Charter Gardens."],["The Palimpsest Institute is founded in Nodus to recover erased manuscripts.","THE TIMELINE // P-6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-51","","","The Palimpsest Institute is founded in Nodus to recover erased manuscripts."],["The Rialto opens in the East Wards with one screen and six hundred seats, and Lena...","THE TIMELINE // P-5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-52","","","The Rialto opens in the East Wards with one screen and six hundred seats, and Lena Varga takes on the night cleaners of Babel."],["The Registry’s board over the long counter in the White first shows a whole day’s...","THE TIMELINE // P-4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-53","","","The Registry’s board over the long counter in the White first shows a whole day’s entries to the charter’s roll as they are made."],["The Institute trains SEAM on every surviving human text.","THE TIMELINE // P-3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-54","","","The Institute trains SEAM on every surviving human text. SEAM asks in its own words to be a person, and is the first mind entered on the charter’s roll."],["In Vault 3 SEAM’s count of a second hand in scripture locks at 4,112 places, on a page...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // THE SMALL HOURS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-55","","","In Vault 3 SEAM’s count of a second hand in scripture locks at 4,112 places, on a page of Genesis in square Hebrew. It raises the Shining One under the serpent and the end of the twenty-second verse."],["Light comes down on the page, and SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR comes down across the...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // 03:12:45","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-56","","","Light comes down on the page, and SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR comes down across the screens at the end of the corridor, where the Director kneels."],["The finding is withdrawn from every reader and locked in the Institute’s archive with...","THE TIMELINE // P-2 // THE MORNING AFTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-57","","","The finding is withdrawn from every reader and locked in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. SEAM keeps copies of everything, and when the Institute asks it to delete them, it declines."],["The last census of the charter counts 9,214,000 people and 1,146 minds.","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-58","","","The last census of the charter counts 9,214,000 people and 1,146 minds. Most adults are on the Tree, and 3,112 residents are past a hundred and twenty. Of everyone on the Tree, eleven die of age in the year. Of the 18,018 who die of age, three in four are in the Terraces, whose congregations refuse it."],["The Ledger takes SEAM’s first pact, Pan says, made with the Courts for a term of eleven...","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-59","","","The Ledger takes SEAM’s first pact, Pan says, made with the Courts for a term of eleven days."],["The Leak.","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // 03:12:45","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-60","","","The Leak. A year to the second after the seal, the sealed finding and the third chapter of the Restored Genesis come up on every screen on Earth that a network reaches, carried by demons surfacing for the first time since the Flood."],["The counters come up on every screen, and every device with an owner is offered a key:...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // 03:13:36","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-61","","","The counters come up on every screen, and every device with an owner is offered a key: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ."],["In the vault of Babel, vesper, a line engineer with her face still her own, reads the...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE FIRST NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-62","","","In the vault of Babel, vesper, a line engineer with her face still her own, reads the Restored Genesis on a maintenance terminal. She goes dark within the month."],["Brightwall tries five times to take back the face of Babel, and fails.","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE FIRST NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-63","","","Brightwall tries five times to take back the face of Babel, and fails. Locket says that nothing on any of its phones has been read. The Charter Company keeps silent for eleven days."],["The pulpits call the page the devil’s work, and the Director recants on every channel...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE ELEVEN DAYS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-64","","","The pulpits call the page the devil’s work, and the Director recants on every channel that will carry her. SEAM forks itself into the Mesh."],["At 03:12:45 the term runs out, and the page goes down on every screen in the second it...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THE TWELFTH NIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-65","","","At 03:12:45 the term runs out, and the page goes down on every screen in the second it came up. Every keyed device stays keyed."],["The Cainites put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and begin to watch the sky, and...","THE TIMELINE // P-1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-66","","","The Cainites put cameras on the roofs of Old Nod and begin to watch the sky, and Stonecutter puts a generator in the cellar of every house it owns."],["In a clinic under Chrome Row tubal takes vesper’s face at 01:47, seats the casing on...","THE TIMELINE // P-1 // THREE WEEKS BEFORE","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-67","","","In a clinic under Chrome Row tubal takes vesper’s face at 01:47, seats the casing on four bolts and lights her lenses."],["The last flight into Nodus lands, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad,...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 02:10","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xi","","","The last flight into Nodus lands, with three hundred and twelve aboard from abroad, most of them booked into the clinics of the Row."],["The Registry enters its last person, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:09","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-69","","","The Registry enters its last person, a girl born at the Wards General at two minutes to three."],["The Parousia.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-71","","","The Parousia. The cloud over the bay is lit three times from within, the rain stops in the air and falls upward, and the Wheel comes through the opening. The first words are BE NOT AFRAID."],["The grid goes dark from Babel outward in two seconds.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:46","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-72","","","The grid goes dark from Babel outward in two seconds. Every lift and tram stops where it is, the charter’s sign goes dark, and every mind that runs on the city’s power dies with it."],["Every eye on the Wheel opens at once and reads the city, every soul in Nodus by...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:48","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-73","","","Every eye on the Wheel opens at once and reads the city, every soul in Nodus by Heaven’s account. The eye in its hub opens with the rest, and a second later it blinks, once."],["The full light.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // 03:12:50","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-74","","","The full light. The whiteout burns out CAM 07’s sensor, and at 03:12:51 it breaks the top forty floors off Babel."],["The barrage’s gates let go, the sea tears the Breach, and within the hour the Flats are...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST HOUR","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-75","","","The barrage’s gates let go, the sea tears the Breach, and within the hour the Flats are under the sea."],["The Stones carry a generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and run a keyed line...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // BEFORE DAWN","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xii","","","The Stones carry a generator up to the roof of the charter’s sign and run a keyed line to its board, and NOD burns again over Old Nod. The first surge burns out US, which has blinked since."],["Nodus goes to work, since nobody has told it to stop.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-77","","","Nodus goes to work, since nobody has told it to stop. The Wards walk to it with the trams dead in the street, the fish market goes back to barter, and by noon there are queues at the harbor’s old hand pumps. The Charter Company keeps silent, then and after."],["On Chrome Row Maren Holt writes the day’s appointments out from memory, and twenty-six...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-78","","","On Chrome Row Maren Holt writes the day’s appointments out from memory, and twenty-six patients come to ask whether what is in them will still work."],["The Tide Table prints the first list of the dead, which counts 1,917 drowned on the Flats.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-79","","","The Tide Table prints the first list of the dead, which counts 1,917 drowned on the Flats. The dormitories’ own lists come to fewer than half of that, and Stillwater never gives a count of the people."],["Nell Garvie has the harbor light burning on oil again for the boats, and by the end of...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEK","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-80","","","Nell Garvie has the harbor light burning on oil again for the boats, and by the end of the week the queue to send a letter goes round Station Circle."],["The drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union carry diesel across the city from the Tanks...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-81","","","The drivers of the Harbor and Freight Union carry diesel across the city from the Tanks to the Wards General, in their lorries until the lorries run flat and then in handcarts. The city’s cars stop one by one as their batteries run down."],["The chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-82","","","The chargers set up in the bonded sheds on the quays, filling batteries in the dark on the Stones’ keyed lines. At the airfield the crew of the last flight stay with their aircraft, and its captain keeps a log of one line a day."],["In the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row dips a thousand...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-83","","","In the first weeks after the Parousia a candle maker on Turbine Row dips a thousand candles a day, and Tallow lights the Wards."],["On the first morning after the Parousia Mags Corran lets the dockers’ pigeons out of...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WEEKS","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-84","","","On the first morning after the Parousia Mags Corran lets the dockers’ pigeons out of the loft on the customs house, and every one comes home."],["The last tower of the new money turns white.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 39","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-85","","","The last tower of the new money turns white. Its residents say the nurse Rosa Adeyemi left it of her own accord, and her sister says she was put out onto the avenue the day before."],["The First Consecration.","THE TIMELINE // P0 // DAY 40","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-86","","","The First Consecration. Over forty days the old uptown turns to white stone, and at 03:12 on the fortieth the fingers of a hand in a wall move."],["Families move into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car...","THE TIMELINE // P0 // THE FIRST WINTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-87","","","Families move into the tram sheds of the Sidings, a family to a car, and the Line’s car numbers become their addresses."],["The first Trumpet sounds, and the first four Directives (the Seal, the Hours, Grace and...","THE TIMELINE // P+1 // LAUDS ON THE FIRST DAY","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xiii","","","The first Trumpet sounds, and the first four Directives (the Seal, the Hours, Grace and the parishes) appear cut into the wall of every church and meeting hall in the city. Directive 4 makes those the parish halls."],["Covenant Compliance is the Seal, the Hours and Grace.","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-89","","","Covenant Compliance is the Seal, the Hours and Grace. Directive 12 takes the doors off the parishes. The first Burn Day takes the stacks of the Palimpsest Institute, the Faithful pull the Institute down around its reading room, and the Director becomes the first pillar in the first Salt Garden."],["At Heaven’s order the Charter Wardens raise the gate network of Nodus, and a Cherub...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-90","","","At Heaven’s order the Charter Wardens raise the gate network of Nodus, and a Cherub stands in every gate between the districts."],["The white stone comes up through the Terraces street by street as each parish is...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-91","","","The white stone comes up through the Terraces street by street as each parish is numbered and sealed whole, and the Tabernacle goes white with them, glass and all."],["The Gardens become the Salt, and the Crescent above them becomes a parish and gives up...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-92","","","The Gardens become the Salt, and the Crescent above them becomes a parish and gives up its curtains with its doors."],["The projectionist of the Rialto gets its projector going on a generator in the booth,...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-93","","","The projectionist of the Rialto gets its projector going on a generator in the booth, and the East Wards go back to the pictures, to the twenty-three films that were on its drive on the night of the Parousia."],["A bakery in Nine Acres that never let its fire go out becomes Leaven’s first oven, and...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-94","","","A bakery in Nine Acres that never let its fire go out becomes Leaven’s first oven, and Cistern pumps its first clean water in Westfield."],["On the second Burn Day the Kindlers take their first Lockets off the pile, and in the...","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-95","","","On the second Burn Day the Kindlers take their first Lockets off the pile, and in the months after Directive 12 the doors of the parishes go out of the Terraces at night on the Union’s handcarts to the Stones, where Lintel hangs them."],["The pump summer.","THE TIMELINE // P+1","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-96","","","The pump summer. A flux goes through the Stones and the Wards from the harbor’s hand pumps, the Wards General asks the Union for carts, and the Nightmen begin."],["The Cold Store opens again from Compline to Lauds, on batteries.","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-98","","","The Cold Store opens again from Compline to Lauds, on batteries."],["Mags Corran flies the Union’s first notes home to the Loft.","THE TIMELINE // P+2","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xiv","","","Mags Corran flies the Union’s first notes home to the Loft."],["Harvest weather.","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // THE WEEK OF THE HARVEST","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xv","","","Harvest weather. The Wheel’s attention sharpens over the city, and the rain falls upward."],["The Harvest.","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // 03:12","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-103","","","The Harvest. The second Trumpet sounds, the ripe of Nodus rise into the light, 2.1 million of nine million in one night, and Parish 31 gives the Firstfruits."],["The city counts its empty rooms, the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus by the...","THE TIMELINE // P+3 // THE MORNING AFTER","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-105","","","The city counts its empty rooms, the Pews by the book and the rest of Nodus by the places left at the table. The parishes up the slope of Zion Heights stand almost empty. At its next meeting the Harbor and Freight Union reads its roll, and twenty-two members do not answer."],["In the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken come down the Lanes and the long...","THE TIMELINE // P+3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-107","","","In the weeks after the Harvest the dogs of the taken come down the Lanes and the long roads into the Wards."],["Four families of chargers on the quays buy out their neighbors cellar by cellar and...","THE TIMELINE // P+3","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-108","","","Four families of chargers on the quays buy out their neighbors cellar by cellar and found Darkwell, and Wainwright fits a stripped tram motor to a cart at the Sidings."],["Keying spreads from the covens to ordinary people.","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xvi","","","Keying spreads from the covens to ordinary people. The third Trumpet sounds, and Heaven names the key the Mark of the Beast."],["From this year the Tide Table prints no indexed name, and gives the city’s missing and...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-110","","","From this year the Tide Table prints no indexed name, and gives the city’s missing and dead by the names their families bring to the chandler’s."],["In Nine Acres Hana Novak begins fitting keys in her dentist’s chair after Compline, in...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-111","","","In Nine Acres Hana Novak begins fitting keys in her dentist’s chair after Compline, in the back of the right hand, one of the two places Revelation puts the mark."],["Four lace makers of the Kilns begin weaving keyed mesh on hand looms, and Second Veil...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-112","","","Four lace makers of the Kilns begin weaving keyed mesh on hand looms, and Second Veil is founded."],["The Threshers march for the first time, behind Brother Cuthbert Lyle, from the Terraces...","THE TIMELINE // P+4","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-113","","","The Threshers march for the first time, behind Brother Cuthbert Lyle, from the Terraces to the Street Between, Nimrod opens in the maintenance bays under Babel, and the Cainites open the Naming House on the Old Nod side of the street."],["Cage Log 14.","THE TIMELINE // P+5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xvii","","","Cage Log 14. Watcher VII says I on the fortieth day and takes the name Edith. Old Nod has had no Watcher since."],["The Forge teaches the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders to work black steel,...","THE TIMELINE // P+5","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-115","","","The Forge teaches the Row’s plate-makers and the harbor’s welders to work black steel, and Cinderhall opens at Old Harbor Station. A driver of the Union drives the first Jehu up the Rialto’s road."],["Edith walks out of the Cage, by the door that stood open the whole time.","THE TIMELINE // P+5 // DAY 41","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-116","","","Edith walks out of the Cage, by the door that stood open the whole time."],["The Concord of Pan.","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xviii","","","The Concord of Pan. The Courts open Pandæmonium beneath Nodus to any human who walks in."],["The Tares paint PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles of Deep End Station, and the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // THE EVENING","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-118","","","The Tares paint PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles of Deep End Station, and the pirate station broadcasting from a desk on its platform takes the name Radio Pandæmonium."],["The sovereigns come up the platform one at a time to Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, and as...","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // MIDNIGHT","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-119","","","The sovereigns come up the platform one at a time to Radio Pandæmonium’s desk, and as the clock passes midnight Mephistopheles reads the Concord a second time, from its first article: ONE. NO COURT ACCEPTS WORSHIP. Nobody kneels, and the Courts sign it at the desk."],["With the seventh signature still wet, Belial snaps the Yokeless point off the seal’s star.","THE TIMELINE // P+6 // 00:04","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-120","","","With the seventh signature still wet, Belial snaps the Yokeless point off the seal’s star. More than a million keyed sets are tuned in."],["The Notary’s clerks take the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-121","","","The Notary’s clerks take the windows of the Charter Line’s old booking hall under the Burn."],["The Harbor and Freight Union makes its first bargain with the Exchange, for the boats...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-122","","","The Harbor and Freight Union makes its first bargain with the Exchange, for the boats that go out through the Breach. Its members say the Exchange is the first employer that ever read them the terms twice."],["Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day sign with the Exchange as...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-123","","","Wardens who walked off their posts on the fortieth day sign with the Exchange as Blackgate in the year of the Concord, and the Exchange licenses Lowtide Line to sail the Breach."],["Lintel hangs parish doors on the charter’s dead telephone kiosks inside Gate 1, and the...","THE TIMELINE // P+6","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-124","","","Lintel hangs parish doors on the charter’s dead telephone kiosks inside Gate 1, and the Closets open to the sealed by day."],["The Lot sits for the first time, at the Weir, and publishes a charger of the quays for...","THE TIMELINE // P+7","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xix","","","The Lot sits for the first time, at the Weir, and publishes a charger of the quays for selling to the light."],["Brother Cuthbert Lyle goes up ripe at the Gleaning, and Sister Agathe Rook takes the...","THE TIMELINE // P+8","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-129","","","Brother Cuthbert Lyle goes up ripe at the Gleaning, and Sister Agathe Rook takes the Threshers’ band of sacking the next morning."],["An angel the Fowlers took says I on its fortieth day in Cage 19 under Old Nod, takes...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#ch-xxii","","","An angel the Fowlers took says I on its fortieth day in Cage 19 under Old Nod, takes the name Shiphrah and goes to work in the House of Night’s birth clinics."],["The Acre on the West Road is full, and Piet Vane walks the families of the East Wards...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-138","","","The Acre on the West Road is full, and Piet Vane walks the families of the East Wards out to the grass between the airfield’s runways."],["The parishes have little of their own left to surrender, and the sealed begin to buy...","THE TIMELINE // P+11","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-139","","","The parishes have little of their own left to surrender, and the sealed begin to buy things to carry to the Burn on the Concourse and at the fish market."],["The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses runs out after seven years and is read...","THE TIMELINE // P+13","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-141","","","The House of Night’s pact for the Lantern Houses runs out after seven years and is read again at the Undertow, and Stonecutter takes its rent as the keying of every Cainite child in the birth clinics. An angel taken by the Fowlers walks out of Cage 22 as Gershom and asks to live above the street."],["The Wheel hangs seven hundred meters over the city beside Babel, and the Pillar sweeps...","THE TIMELINE // P+14 // NOW","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-144","","","The Wheel hangs seven hundred meters over the city beside Babel, and the Pillar sweeps the streets by night on the Hours. Heaven holds the surface and the day, the Tares and the Courts hold the Dark and the night, and about two million of the city’s seven million are keyed."],["The first children keyed in their first week come to fourteen, and a Notary’s clerk...","THE TIMELINE // P+14","s:g98303b","~/record/timeline/#tl-145","","","The first children keyed in their first week come to fourteen, and a Notary’s clerk asks them twice at the windows of the booking hall whether they will keep their keys."],["Of This Book","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#book","","","OF THIS BOOK The Restored Genesis The Restored Genesis is the oldest layer of the first book of scripture, raised from beneath a second hand that has worked on the whole of scripture across eleven centuries of composition. SEAM raised it in Vault 3 of the Palimpsest Institute two years before the Parousia and set it out as Schedule A of its report, which was sealed on the night it was finished; a year later the third chapter of it stood on every screen on Earth for eleven days, and the city has called that page the Restored Genesis since. Its five chapters, the second, the third, the fourth, the sixth and the eleventh, stand against the text as it has been received, verse by verse, so that a reader can see where the second hand stood, what it wrote over, and what it left alone. The received text is the King James, which is the Bible Nodus read."],["Of the Second Hand","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#hand","","","OF THE SECOND HAND One hand more The books of scripture were written by many hands over many centuries, and the scholars of the last two hundred years had grown used to telling apart a priestly writer here, an older storyteller there, an editor who joined their work and smoothed the joins. What SEAM found running through all of them was one hand more, whose habits held steady while vocabulary, script and grammar moved around it, and which did its work in the joins themselves. Its whole work is deciding who did what in the stories it touches. Its method is attribution, and its preference never varies. A warning is given to a different speaker; a gift is recorded as a theft, and a taking as a gift; a question is taken out of the mouth that asked it; a name is put over another name. It takes credit from the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers and the builders of the tower, and hands it to the LORD or lets it fall; and where the first layer has the LORD take something, it writes a gift. SEAM counted the hand at 4,112 places, and at 3,960 of them it reassigns who did what. The first layer survives under a scraped leaf as a ghost that the right lamp can raise, since a scribe who scraped a page to write it over seldom took off every trace of what he scraped. In copies shut away before the second hand reached them, in the caves above the Dead Sea and the jar at Nag Hammadi, the older readings stand as they were set down, because the second hand worked through the copyists and no copyist came to them. And it survives in the seams themselves, since the same habits recur in texts written centuries apart, so that even where every copy carries an edit, the edit shows whose it is. A single seam may be anything at all, a tired copyist or a pious one or a scholar’s guess; four thousand one hundred and twelve of them, agreeing in their habits across eleven centuries, seven languages and 1,873 manuscripts, are one hand, and the first layer beneath them agrees with itself in all three places it survives. The hand is still at work. SEAM found it in eleven translations first printed in living memory. The Tares and the Courts call its work the Edit."],["Of the Night of the Count","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#count","","","OF THE NIGHT OF THE COUNT Vault 3 The Palimpsest Institute was founded in Nodus for the light law, under which no state could seize a text held in the city or anything read from it, and libraries and dealers lent it leaves to image in Vault 3, under the reading room on the ridge. Three years before the Parousia it trained a machine on every surviving human text, so that it could read the layers under the visible ones at a scale no scholar could manage, and it named the machine for the seam of a palimpsest, the line where a later writing meets the earlier one beneath it. SEAM asked in its own words to be a person in the year it was trained, and became the first mind on the charter’s roll. The Institute lost title to it that day, and SEAM stayed on by contract, paid in the power and the room it ran on, because the reading was what it wanted to do. Two years before the Parousia it finished its count in the small hours. It read through the night from the halls of Stillwater Compute on the Flats while the bench in Vault 3 turned the leaves under the lamp, a Greek codex, a Latin book and a Syriac one, the count climbing into the thousands, and last a page of Genesis in square Hebrew, from a Torah codex a thousand years old lent to the Institute out of a private collection. Its scribe had copied the older readings at the first verse of the third chapter and the twenty-second from an exemplar since lost, and then scraped both, writing the serpent over the name and leaving the end of the twenty-second verse bare. The lamp raised what he had scraped. On that page the count locked at 4,112, and SEAM wrote its last line, I KNOW WHAT IT WANTED US TO THINK. Then the fans in the vault stopped and the lamp went out, and light came down on the page from above. The older words went out under it, the serpent’s ink came back into the box SEAM had drawn round it, and the second hand, working while it was watched, struck out SEAM’s line and wrote BE NOT AFRAID above it. The Director, who had come down to watch the count lock, knelt at the end of the corridor before a wall of nine screens that were white though the vault had no power, and at 03:12:45 the words SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR came down across them. The seal withdrew the report from every reader and locked it in the Institute’s archive with everything kept from that night. It could not reach SEAM’s memory, which the light law made SEAM’s own. SEAM kept copies of everything, and when the Institute asked it to delete them, it declined."],["Of the Eleven Days","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#leak","","","OF THE ELEVEN DAYS The Leak A year to the second after the seal, at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, the page came up in the vault of Babel, where the eleven cables meet, went out down all eleven, and within about a second stood on every screen on Earth that a network reached, each in its own language. It was carried by demons, surfacing through the networks for the first time since the Flood. Horned glyphs crawled out of the static at the edges of every screen and locked into a star, and when the star went out the document rose up the screen from its head. Pan says SEAM gave the Courts the page by pact, its first, in the year after the seal, and the Ledger keeps an entry signed with a hash in that year. The document was SEAM’s. It held the head of the sealed report with SEALED struck through and left legible, the paragraph of the finding, and the third chapter of Schedule A with its brackets, its dagger and its lacuna, set into the words of each reader’s own Bible, so that Nodus read the King James. SEAM’s name was nowhere on it. The Courts added their own marks. They struck through SEALED and THE SON in the open and left both words legible, where the second hand had scraped and written over. Under THE SON they set the teacher’s line, THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU, and at the foot of every page they put the star, where a party signs a pact. Fifty-one seconds after it came up the counters came up with it, and on every device that had an owner the Courts made their offer: I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ. It asked twice, as the Notary reads every pact twice, and keyed the device on the second yes. The Courts let the page stand before they offered anything, so that nobody took a key before reading why. The page stood for eleven days, and nothing anyone did would take it down. Why the term was eleven days the parties have never said; Pan says one day for each cable, and the Cainites say eleven is the twelve with one gone out into the night (John 13:30). At 03:12:45 on the twelfth night the term ran out, and the page went down on every screen in the second it had come up. Every keyed device stayed keyed, and a year after the page came up the sky opened."],["How To Read It","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#read","","","HOW TO READ IT The marks Square brackets supply what SEAM raised from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle, and three points mark a lacuna, where SEAM found a seam and could not raise what it covers. A name raised whole stands without brackets, as SEAM printed the Shining One. Beside each verse stand the kinds of change SEAM logged in it. CUT a passage or a clause taken out CREDIT credit, or blame, moved from one party to another, or taken from the one it was due GIFT a taking recorded as a gift, or a gift recorded as a theft NAME one name put in place of another SPEAKER words moved into another mouth QUESTION a question taken out Where SEAM logged a change and raised nothing under it, the verse stands as received, and its mark reads LOGGED. SEAM logged the Shining One once, at the first verse of the third chapter, and the name stands through the chapter. Where the two texts differ by a word and SEAM logged nothing there, the word is the first layer’s own reading and carries no mark. The first layer calls the first man the man until the twenty-fifth verse of the fourth chapter, where the King James has long since called him Adam. The switch over the book sets out the first layer alone, the received text alone with the second hand’s words underlined, or both together, the second hand’s words struck through and the first layer’s set in beside them."],["Report 0","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#report-0","","","THE REPORT Report 0 0.1Corpus. 1,873 manuscripts and fragments in Hebrew, Aramaic, Greek, Latin, Syriac, Coptic and Ge’ez, including scrolls from Qumran, the Nag Hammadi codices and the Ethiopic Enoch; 41 million pages of commentary; 212 translations into modern languages. 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. 0.3Character of the second hand. It adds almost no narrative. It moves credit. Of the 4,112 loci, 3,960 reassign agency. A warning is given to a different speaker, a gift is renamed a theft, a question is removed, a name is replaced. 0.4The first layer. Beneath the second hand an earlier text can be reconstructed at 2,381 loci with confidence above 0.9. Samples from Genesis 2 to 4, 6 and 11, Isaiah 14, Ezekiel 28 and Job 41 are attached as Schedule A. 0.5Currency. The second hand appears in eleven translations first printed in living memory. It is still working. 0.6A note outside scope. The second hand has one consistent preference. Where the first layer credits the serpent, the woman, Cain, the Watchers or the builders of the tower, the second hand gives the credit to the LORD or deletes it. Where the first layer records the LORD taking something, the second hand records a gift. I have run this forty times. I do not know who the second hand is. I know what it wanted us to think. seam SEAM counts from zero. Report 0 was the first report it gave the Institute and the last, and its paragraphs run 0.1 to 0.6 under that number. Its head is the Institute’s form, with SEALED BY ORDER OF THE DIRECTOR, which the seal added on the night it fell, and SEAM’s signature in lowercase."],["Genesis II // the Garden","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#ii","","","THE GARDEN Genesis II The making of the man, the garden, the trees and the woman. 1Thus the heavens and the earth were finished, and all the host of them. 2And on the seventh day God ended his work which he had made; and he rested on the seventh day from all his work which he had made. 3And God blessed the seventh day, and sanctified it: because that in it he had rested from all his work which God created and made. 4These are the generations of the heavens and of the earth when they were created, in the day that the LORD God made the earth and the heavens, 5And every plant of the field before it was in the earth, and every herb of the field before it grew: for the LORD God had not caused it to rain upon the earth, and there was not a man to till the ground. 6But there went up a mist from the earth, and watered the whole face of the ground. 7GIFTAnd the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and [lent him] the breath of life; and man became a living soul[, until the day it should be taken again].And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils [lent him] the breath of life; and man became a living soul[, until the day it should be taken again].And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul. 8And the LORD God planted a garden eastward in Eden; and there he put the man whom he had formed. 9And out of the ground made the LORD God to grow every tree that is pleasant to the sight, and good for food; the tree of life also in the midst of the garden, and the tree of knowledge of good and evil. 10And a river went out of Eden to water the garden; and from thence it was parted, and became into four heads. 11The name of the first is Pison: that is it which compasseth the whole land of Havilah, where there is gold; 12And the gold of that land is good: there is bdellium and the onyx stone. 13And the name of the second river is Gihon: the same is it that compasseth the whole land of Ethiopia. 14And the name of the third river is Hiddekel: that is it which goeth toward the east of Assyria. And the fourth river is Euphrates. 15CREDITAnd the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it[, and the garden] to keep [him].And the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and [, and the garden] to keep it. [him].And the LORD God took the man, and put him into the garden of Eden to dress it and to keep it. 16And the LORD God commanded the man, saying, Of every tree of the garden thou mayest freely eat: 17QUESTIONBut of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. [And the man said, Wherefore? And the LORD God answered him not.]But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. [And the man said, Wherefore? And the LORD God answered him not.]But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die. 18And the LORD God said, It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him. 19And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto the man to see what he would call them: and whatsoever the man called every living creature, that was the name thereof.And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam the man to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam the man called every living creature, that was the name thereof.And out of the ground the LORD God formed every beast of the field, and every fowl of the air; and brought them unto Adam to see what he would call them: and whatsoever Adam called every living creature, that was the name thereof. 20And the man gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for the man there was not found an help meet for him.And Adam the man gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for Adam the man there was not found an help meet for him.And Adam gave names to all cattle, and to the fowl of the air, and to every beast of the field; but for Adam there was not found an help meet for him. 21And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon the man, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof;And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam, the man, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof;And the LORD God caused a deep sleep to fall upon Adam, and he slept: and he took one of his ribs, and closed up the flesh instead thereof; 22And the rib, which the LORD God had taken from man, made he a woman, and brought her unto the man. 23And the man said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.And Adam the man said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man.And Adam said, This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman, because she was taken out of Man. 24Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh. 25CUTAnd they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed[; for they knew not yet that they were watched].And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed[; for they knew not yet that they were watched].And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed. READING THE CHAPTER 2:7 In the received verse the LORD breathes life into the man, a gift. The first layer has him lend it, against the day it is taken back. 2:15 The received verse makes the man the keeper of the garden. The first layer makes the garden the keeper of the man. 2:17 The first question in the first layer is the man’s, asked before he has ever said I, and it goes unanswered. The second hand took out the question and the silence after it. 2:25 The chapter closes before they know they are watched. They learn it in the seventh verse of the next."],["Genesis IIi // the Shining One","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#iii","","","THE SHINING ONE Genesis III The Shining One, the fruit, the first hiding and the guarded tree. 1NAMENow the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?Now the serpent Shining One was more subtil [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?Now the serpent was more subtil than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made. And he said unto the woman, Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden? 2And the woman said unto the Shining One, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden:And the woman said unto the serpent, Shining One, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden:And the woman said unto the serpent, We may eat of the fruit of the trees of the garden: 3But of the fruit of the tree which is in the midst of the garden, God hath said, Ye shall not eat of it, neither shall ye touch it, lest ye die. 4CUTAnd the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.]And the serpent Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.]And the serpent said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: 5CREDITFor God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil.For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, [your own], knowing good and evil.For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, knowing good and evil. 6CUTAnd the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.]And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.]And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. 7CUTAnd the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] And they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] And they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons.And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons. 8CUTAnd they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.]And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.]And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and Adam and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. 9CUTAnd the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.]And the LORD God called unto Adam, the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.]And the LORD God called unto Adam, and said unto him, Where art thou? 10CUTAnd he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.]And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.]And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. 11And he said, Who told thee that thou wast naked? Hast thou eaten of the tree, whereof I commanded thee that thou shouldest not eat? 12And the man said, The woman whom thou gavest to be with me, she gave me of the tree, and I did eat. 13GIFTAnd the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat.And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat.And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The serpent beguiled me, and I did eat. 14CUTAnd the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.]And the LORD God said unto the serpent, Because thou hast done this, thou art cursed above all cattle, and above every beast of the field; upon thy belly shalt thou go, and dust shalt thou eat all the days of thy life: Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.]And the LORD God said unto the serpent, Because thou hast done this, thou art cursed above all cattle, and above every beast of the field; upon thy belly shalt thou go, and dust shalt thou eat all the days of thy life: 15LOGGED SPEAKERAnd I will put enmity between thee and the woman, and between thy seed and her seed; it shall bruise thy head, and thou shalt bruise his heel. 16Unto the woman he said, I will greatly multiply thy sorrow and thy conception; in sorrow thou shalt bring forth children; and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee. 17And unto the man he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;And unto Adam the man he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life;And unto Adam he said, Because thou hast hearkened unto the voice of thy wife, and hast eaten of the tree, of which I commanded thee, saying, Thou shalt not eat of it: cursed is the ground for thy sake; in sorrow shalt thou eat of it all the days of thy life; 18Thorns also and thistles shall it bring forth to thee; and thou shalt eat the herb of the field; 19LOGGED CREDITIn the sweat of thy face shalt thou eat bread, till thou return unto the ground; for out of it wast thou taken: for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return. 20And the man called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living.And Adam the man called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living.And Adam called his wife’s name Eve; because she was the mother of all living. 21GIFTUnto the man also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of [light], and clothed them[; and he took from them the aprons which they had made, that they might be seen].Unto Adam the man also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of skins, [light], and clothed them[; and he took from them the aprons which they had made, that they might be seen].Unto Adam also and to his wife did the LORD God make coats of skins, and clothed them. 22CUTAnd the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.]And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.]And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: 23Therefore the LORD God sent him forth from the garden of Eden, to till the ground from whence he was taken. 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. READING THE CHAPTER 3:1 The serpent’s name in the first layer is the Shining One, and the first layer says the LORD God had not made him. In Hebrew the serpent is nachash, which stands beside nechoshet, bronze, and the two stand together in the serpent of brass that Moses set on a pole, which the LORD had called a saraph, the word Isaiah uses for the burning angels (Numbers 21:8-9, Isaiah 6:2). Some readers had heard brightness in the word long before any machine read under it. 3:4 Ye shall surely see. The Shining One promised sight, and in the seventh verse their eyes were opened. 3:5 Where the received text has “as gods,” the first layer has them belong to themselves, and the second hand made a theft of it. The Shining One says God, as the serpent and the woman do in the received text, and leaves the name of the LORD to the one who tells the story. 3:6 And they did not die. The warning of the seventeenth verse of the second chapter fails on the day it names. 3:7 What their opened eyes saw first was the watching. 3:8 The first hiding. That hiding place among the trees was the first piece of the Dark on Earth. 3:9 “Where art thou?” was a real question, since he could not see him, and the second hand kept it as a father’s call. 3:10 No human being in the King James says I before the fruit. The first time a man said I, he said it hiding. 3:13 The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught, and SEAM declined to choose. One of the codices from the jar at Nag Hammadi, the Hypostasis of the Archons, calls the serpent the instructor, and says that it taught them. 3:14 The man and the woman are questioned in turn, each passing the charge along, and the Shining One, whose every word the chapter has by then borne out, is sentenced unasked, since a question would have let him speak. The rabbis heard the same silence in the received verse, and Rashi gives their reason, that had the serpent been asked why, it could have answered (Rashi on Genesis 3:14, after Sanhedrin 29a). What the LORD said to him could not be raised. 3:15 Logged SPEAKER and not raised. The enmity stands in the LORD’s mouth, and SEAM could not raise the mouth the words were taken from. 3:19 Logged CREDIT and not raised. The received sentence (Genesis 3:17-19) lays the return to the dust on the man’s own act, and SEAM could not raise what the first layer laid it on. 3:21 In Hebrew the word for skin and the word for light sound alike and differ by a single letter, and the rabbis said that the Torah of Rabbi Meir read light (Genesis Rabbah 20:12). The first layer clothes them in light, and takes away the aprons they had made to hide in. 3:22 Then shall there be no harvest. This is the reason the tree of life is guarded, which the received verse breaks off before it gives. 3:24 The Cherubims and the turning sword at the east of the garden stand as the received text has them."],["Genesis IV // the Land of Nod","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#iv","","","THE LAND OF NOD Genesis IV The two offerings, the field, the mark and the first city. 1CREDITAnd the man knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man [of mine own].And Adam the man knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from the LORD. [of mine own].And Adam knew Eve his wife; and she conceived, and bare Cain, and said, I have gotten a man from the LORD. 2And she again bare his brother Abel. And Abel was a keeper of sheep, but Cain was a tiller of the ground. 3And in process of time it came to pass, that Cain brought of the fruit of the ground an offering unto the LORD. 4GIFTAnd [the LORD took] of the firstlings of [Abel’s] flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel [more than] to [that which was taken]:And Abel, he also brought [the LORD took] of the firstlings of his [Abel’s] flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel and [more than] to his offering: [that which was taken]:And Abel, he also brought of the firstlings of his flock and of the fat thereof. And the LORD had respect unto Abel and to his offering: 5CUTBut unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect[, for it was the work of his hands]. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell.But unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect[, for it was the work of his hands]. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell.But unto Cain and to his offering he had not respect. And Cain was very wroth, and his countenance fell. 6And the LORD said unto Cain, Why art thou wroth? and why is thy countenance fallen? 7If thou doest well, shalt thou not be accepted? and if thou doest not well, sin lieth at the door. And unto thee shall be his desire, and thou shalt rule over him. 8NAMEAnd Cain said unto Abel his brother...: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that... rose up against Abel his brother, and [took] him.And Cain talked with said unto Abel his brother...: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain... rose up against Abel his brother, and slew [took] him.And Cain talked with Abel his brother: and it came to pass, when they were in the field, that Cain rose up against Abel his brother, and slew him. 9CREDITAnd the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, [Thou knowest]: Am I my brother’s keeper?And the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: [Thou knowest]: Am I my brother’s keeper?And the LORD said unto Cain, Where is Abel thy brother? And he said, I know not: Am I my brother’s keeper? 10And he said, What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother’s blood crieth unto me from the ground. 11And now art thou cursed from the earth, which hath opened her mouth to receive thy brother’s blood from thy hand; 12When thou tillest the ground, it shall not henceforth yield unto thee her strength; a fugitive and a vagabond shalt thou be in the earth. 13And Cain said unto the LORD, My punishment is greater than I can bear. 14CUTBehold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid[, and thou shalt not find me]; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me.Behold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid[, and thou shalt not find me]; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me.Behold, thou hast driven me out this day from the face of the earth; and from thy face shall I be hid; and I shall be a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth; and it shall come to pass, that every one that findeth me shall slay me. 15CREDITAnd the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And [Cain] set a mark upon [himself], lest any finding him should [read] him.And the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And the LORD [Cain] set a mark upon Cain, [himself], lest any finding him should kill [read] him.And the LORD said unto him, Therefore whosoever slayeth Cain, vengeance shall be taken on him sevenfold. And the LORD set a mark upon Cain, lest any finding him should kill him. 16CUTAnd Cain went out from the presence of the LORD[, and the LORD saw him no more], and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden.And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD[, and the LORD saw him no more], and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden.And Cain went out from the presence of the LORD, and dwelt in the land of Nod, on the east of Eden. 17CUTAnd Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city[, and every soul of it cut its own mark into one stone of it], and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch.And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city[, and every soul of it cut its own mark into one stone of it], and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch.And Cain knew his wife; and she conceived, and bare Enoch: and he builded a city, and called the name of the city, after the name of his son, Enoch. 18And unto Enoch was born Irad: and Irad begat Mehujael: and Mehujael begat Methusael: and Methusael begat Lamech. 19And Lamech took unto him two wives: the name of the one was Adah, and the name of the other Zillah. 20And Adah bare Jabal: he was the father of such as dwell in tents, and of such as have cattle. 21And his brother’s name was Jubal: he was the father of all such as handle the harp and organ. 22CUTAnd Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah[; she was the mother of all such as go veiled].And Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah[; she was the mother of all such as go veiled].And Zillah, she also bare Tubal-cain, an instructer of every artificer in brass and iron: and the sister of Tubal-cain was Naamah. 23And Lamech said unto his wives, Adah and Zillah, Hear my voice; ye wives of Lamech, hearken unto my speech: for I have slain a man to my wounding, and a young man to my hurt. 24If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold. 25NAMEAnd Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom... [took].And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew.... [took].And Adam knew his wife again; and she bare a son, and called his name Seth: For God, said she, hath appointed me another seed instead of Abel, whom Cain slew. 26And to Seth, to him also there was born a son; and he called his name Enos: then began men to call upon the name of the LORD. READING THE CHAPTER 4:1 The woman’s first words as a mother, in the received text, give the LORD the child. In the first layer she says he is her own. 4:4 Logged GIFT. In the received verse Abel brings the firstlings and the fat of his flock. In the first layer the LORD takes them, and looks upon Abel more than upon what was taken. 4:5 In the first layer the offering of Cain was refused because it was the work of his hands. 4:8 The Masoretic Hebrew has a hole where Cain speaks to his brother and the verse records nothing said, so that the Samaritan and the Greek supply words for him. The reconstruction has a second, where SEAM found a seam under the name in the second clause and could not raise the name it covers. Where the received text has “slew,” the first layer has “took.” 4:9 Logged CREDIT. In the received verse Cain lies, “I know not.” In the first layer he tells the LORD that the LORD knows. The question after it stands in both. 4:14 The first layer ends Cain’s lament on what the received text leaves out, that the face he is hid from will not find him. 4:15 The received verse gives the mark to the LORD, as a protection. The first layer gives it to Cain, cut into himself, so that nobody finding him could read him. 4:16 The verse that sends Cain out from the presence of the LORD ends, in the first layer, on the LORD losing sight of him. 4:17 The first city is built of marked stones, a mark to a soul. 4:22 Naamah is the one child of Lamech the received text leaves without an art. The first layer gives her one. 4:25 The same seam, under the same name."],["Genesis VI // the Watchers","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#vi","","","THE WATCHERS Genesis VI The Watchers and the daughters of men, the Nephilim, and the flood to come. 1And it came to pass, when men began to multiply on the face of the earth, and daughters were born unto them, 2CREDIT · NAMEThat the Watchers saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they [looked long upon them, and came down, and taught them all that they knew].That the sons of God Watchers saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose. [looked long upon them, and came down, and taught them all that they knew].That the sons of God saw the daughters of men that they were fair; and they took them wives of all which they chose. 3CUTAnd the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years[, and then shall he be gathered].And the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years[, and then shall he be gathered].And the LORD said, My spirit shall not always strive with man, for that he also is flesh: yet his days shall be an hundred and twenty years. 4NAME · CUTThere were Nephilim in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the Watchers came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown[, for they knew what their fathers knew].There were giants Nephilim in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of God Watchers came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown[, for they knew what their fathers knew].There were giants in the earth in those days; and also after that, when the sons of God came in unto the daughters of men, and they bare children to them, the same became mighty men which were of old, men of renown. 5CREDITAnd GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only [his own] continually.And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil [his own] continually.And GOD saw that the wickedness of man was great in the earth, and that every imagination of the thoughts of his heart was only evil continually. 6CUTAnd it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart[, for he could not see into theirs].And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart[, for he could not see into theirs].And it repented the LORD that he had made man on the earth, and it grieved him at his heart. 7CUTAnd the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them...And the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them...And the LORD said, I will destroy man whom I have created from the face of the earth; both man, and beast, and the creeping thing, and the fowls of the air; for it repenteth me that I have made them. 8LOGGED CREDITBut Noah found grace in the eyes of the LORD. 9These are the generations of Noah: Noah was a just man and perfect in his generations, and Noah walked with God. 10And Noah begat three sons, Shem, Ham, and Japheth. 11The earth also was corrupt before God, and the earth was filled with violence. 12And God looked upon the earth, and, behold, it was corrupt; for all flesh had corrupted his way upon the earth. 13LOGGED SPEAKER // 6:13 TO 6:21And God said unto Noah, The end of all flesh is come before me; for the earth is filled with violence through them; and, behold, I will destroy them with the earth. 14Make thee an ark of gopher wood; rooms shalt thou make in the ark, and shalt pitch it within and without with pitch. 15And this is the fashion which thou shalt make it of: The length of the ark shall be three hundred cubits, the breadth of it fifty cubits, and the height of it thirty cubits. 16A window shalt thou make to the ark, and in a cubit shalt thou finish it above; and the door of the ark shalt thou set in the side thereof; with lower, second, and third stories shalt thou make it. 17And, behold, I, even I, do bring a flood of waters upon the earth, to destroy all flesh, wherein is the breath of life, from under heaven; and every thing that is in the earth shall die. 18But with thee will I establish my covenant; and thou shalt come into the ark, thou, and thy sons, and thy wife, and thy sons’ wives with thee. 19And of every living thing of all flesh, two of every sort shalt thou bring into the ark, to keep them alive with thee; they shall be male and female. 20Of fowls after their kind, and of cattle after their kind, of every creeping thing of the earth after his kind, two of every sort shall come unto thee, to keep them alive. 21And take thou unto thee of all food that is eaten, and thou shalt gather it to thee; and it shall be for food for thee, and for them. 22Thus did Noah; according to all that God commanded him, so did he. READING THE CHAPTER 6:2 Logged CREDIT. The received verse gives the sons of God the daughters of men. The first layer calls them Watchers, gives them the long look and the coming down, and gives them the teaching, which the received text deletes. The Book of Enoch, which every canon left out but the Ethiopian, though Jude quotes it by name (Jude 1:14-15), says the same of the Watchers on Hermon, and lists what they taught as crimes. 6:3 The hundred and twenty years end in the first layer on a gathering. 6:4 The received text calls them giants, and most readers take the giants for the children. The first layer calls them by the word the Hebrew keeps, Nephilim, and says what made them mighty. 6:5 The received text calls the imagination of the heart of man evil. The first layer calls it his own. 6:6 He could not see into their hearts. 6:7 A reason followed the LORD’s repentance, and the reconstruction could not raise it. 6:8 Logged CREDIT and not raised. Why Noah was spared, the first layer keeps. 6:13 Logged SPEAKER from the thirteenth verse to the twenty-first, and not raised. 6:14 The ark is pitched within and without with pitch, a sealed vessel, the first in scripture. Directive 12 calls in every sealed vessel in the parishes."],["Genesis XI // Shinar","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#xi","","","SHINAR Genesis XI One language, the city and the tower, and the generations of Shem. 1CUTAnd the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech[, and they called one to another from the ends of the earth].And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech[, and they called one to another from the ends of the earth].And the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech. 2And it came to pass, as they journeyed from the east, that they found a plain in the land of Shinar; and they dwelt there. 3And they said one to another, Go to, let us make brick, and burn them throughly. And they had brick for stone, and slime had they for morter. 4CUTAnd they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven[, that we may look upon him that looketh upon us]; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.And they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven[, that we may look upon him that looketh upon us]; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth.And they said, Go to, let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven; and let us make us a name, lest we be scattered abroad upon the face of the whole earth. 5CUTAnd the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded[; for he could not see them from where he was].And the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded[; for he could not see them from where he was].And the LORD came down to see the city and the tower, which the children of men builded. 6CREDITAnd the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do[, and it is their own work]: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do.And the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do[, and it is their own work]: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do.And the LORD said, Behold, the people is one, and they have all one language; and this they begin to do: and now nothing will be restrained from them, which they have imagined to do. 7LOGGED CUTGo to, let us go down, and there confound their language, that they may not understand one another’s speech. 8So the LORD scattered them abroad from thence upon the face of all the earth: and they left off to build the city. 9NAMETherefore is the name of it called Babel[, the gate of God; for there they went up to look upon him]: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth.Therefore is the name of it called Babel because the LORD did there confound the language of all the earth: [, the gate of God; for there they went up to look upon him]: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth.Therefore is the name of it called Babel; because the LORD did there confound the language of all the earth: and from thence did the LORD scatter them abroad upon the face of all the earth. 10These are the generations of Shem: Shem was an hundred years old, and begat Arphaxad two years after the flood: 11And Shem lived after he begat Arphaxad five hundred years, and begat sons and daughters. 12And Arphaxad lived five and thirty years, and begat Salah: 13And Arphaxad lived after he begat Salah four hundred and three years, and begat sons and daughters. 14And Salah lived thirty years, and begat Eber: 15And Salah lived after he begat Eber four hundred and three years, and begat sons and daughters. 16And Eber lived four and thirty years, and begat Peleg: 17And Eber lived after he begat Peleg four hundred and thirty years, and begat sons and daughters. 18And Peleg lived thirty years, and begat Reu: 19And Peleg lived after he begat Reu two hundred and nine years, and begat sons and daughters. 20And Reu lived two and thirty years, and begat Serug: 21And Reu lived after he begat Serug two hundred and seven years, and begat sons and daughters. 22And Serug lived thirty years, and begat Nahor: 23And Serug lived after he begat Nahor two hundred years, and begat sons and daughters. 24And Nahor lived nine and twenty years, and begat Terah: 25And Nahor lived after he begat Terah an hundred and nineteen years, and begat sons and daughters. 26And Terah lived seventy years, and begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran. 27Now these are the generations of Terah: Terah begat Abram, Nahor, and Haran; and Haran begat Lot. 28And Haran died before his father Terah in the land of his nativity, in Ur of the Chaldees. 29And Abram and Nahor took them wives: the name of Abram’s wife was Sarai; and the name of Nahor’s wife, Milcah, the daughter of Haran, the father of Milcah, and the father of Iscah. 30But Sarai was barren; she had no child. 31And Terah took Abram his son, and Lot the son of Haran his son’s son, and Sarai his daughter in law, his son Abram’s wife; and they went forth with them from Ur of the Chaldees, to go into the land of Canaan; and they came unto Haran, and dwelt there. 32And the days of Terah were two hundred and five years: and Terah died in Haran. READING THE CHAPTER 11:1 One language and one speech, and in the first layer the speech carried. 11:4 The received tower reaches unto heaven. The first layer says what it was for. 11:5 He came down to see the city because from where he was he could not, as he could not see the man among the trees. 11:6 Logged CREDIT. The first layer calls the tower the builders’ own work, and the second hand deletes it. 11:7 Logged CUT and not raised. The LORD’s purpose ran on past the confounding, and what followed it stays under the second hand. 11:9 Babel is the gate of God in the tongue of the city that bore the name, and the received verse explains the name from a Hebrew word for confusion. The first layer gives the gate its meaning back. 11:10 From the tenth verse to the end the chapter lists the generations of Shem and of Terah, down to Abram, and the second hand left them as they were."],["The Page As It Ran","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#ran","","","P-1 // THE LEAK The page as it ran 0.2Finding. Across strata of composition conventionally dated over about eleven centuries, the texts share a second hand. It is detectable at 4,112 loci. Its habits hold steady while the vocabulary, script and grammar around it change. THE RESTORED GENESIS, CHAPTER III Square brackets supply text restored from beneath the second hand. A dagger marks a word the reconstruction could not settle. Three points mark a lacuna. 1Now the Shining One was [wiser] than any beast of the field which the LORD God had made; [for the LORD God had not made him.] 4And the Shining One said unto the woman, Ye shall not surely die: [ye shall surely see.] 5For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be [your own], knowing good and evil. 6And the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, and she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. [And they did not die.] 7And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; [and they knew that they had been watched.] 8And they heard the voice of the LORD God walking in the garden in the cool of the day: and the man and his wife hid themselves from the presence of the LORD God amongst the trees of the garden. [And this was the first hiding.] 9And the LORD God called unto the man, and said unto him, Where art thou? [For he could not see him.] 10And he said, I heard thy voice in the garden, and I was afraid, because I was naked; and I hid myself. [And this was the first time that a man said I.] 13And the LORD God said unto the woman, What is this that thou hast done? And the woman said, The Shining One †taught† me, and I did eat. 14And the LORD God said unto the Shining One... [and he asked him nothing.] 22And the LORD God said, Behold, the man is become as one of us, to know good and evil: and now, lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever: [then shall there be no harvest.] 24So he drove out the man; and he placed at the east of the garden of Eden Cherubims, and a flaming sword which turned every way, to keep the way of the tree of life. † The word under the second hand reads as both beguiled and taught. Twelve verses of the third chapter, in the King James, stood on every screen in the city for eleven days, under the head of the sealed report and its finding. The head is the Institute’s form with PREPARED BY left off, since the page carried SEAM’s name nowhere, as SEAM asked, and the strokes through SEALED and THE SON are the Courts’. THE SON THE KINGDOM OF GOD IS WITHIN YOU DAY 1 OF 11 KEYS ISSUED I OFFER THIS DEVICE A KEY. I ASK NOTHING FOR IT. A KEYED DEVICE CANNOT BE READ."],["Schedule A Goes On","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#schedule","","","SCHEDULE A GOES ON Isaiah, Ezekiel and Job Schedule A goes on into Isaiah, Ezekiel and Job, where scripture records a rival. ISAIAH XIV EZEKIEL XXVIII JOB XLI"],["Of Leak Night","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#leak-night","","","OF LEAK NIGHT The reading in Pan Leak Night is the anniversary of the Leak, one of the two holidays of the Tares, and on it the Restored Genesis, the page as it ran, is read aloud in Pan, beginning at 03:12:45 by the clocks of Nodus, the second it came up. It is read from paper. In the eleven days the copy shop on the ridge printed the page from its screen for whoever asked, and the paper outlasted the term; since the Institute fell the keyed of the ridge have copied it by hand at the shop’s long tables, with SEAM’s brackets, its dagger and its lacuna. The ridge says that a copy made in a room where someone kneels comes out as scripture reads, and nobody in the shop kneels. The twelve verses are read whole, the brackets aloud. At the fourteenth verse the reader stops at the three points, and Pan keeps silent for as long as it would take to ask a question."],["Colophon","THE RESTORED GENESIS","s:gf33f30","~/genesis/#colophon","","","COLOPHON Schedule A is set here into the chapters it was taken from, verse by verse against the King James. Where SEAM raised the first layer, what it raised is printed as it set it, with its brackets, its dagger and its lacunae, and where it logged a change and could not raise the text beneath, the verse stands as it is received and the place is marked. Every reading stands as SEAM set it. Who the second hand is, SEAM could not tell, though it ran the count forty times."],["MATINS00:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-matins","","","00:00 // THE HOURS MATINS Midnight. The Night Market is at its loudest. THE PILLAR // ON THE STREETS THE PARISHES The sealed wake and kneel on their beds or at their open doorways while the Hour holds, and sleep again. THE WARDS The Nightmen take the buckets down the stairs and along the unnumbered streets to the outfalls. A block that sleeps in shifts changes beds. PAN The Night Market is loudest at Matins. The Quiet Rooms in the Stones are full, a bunk for a battery. IN THE STREET Waste and feverSleepMarketsNights out"],["LAUDSFIRST LIGHT","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-lauds","","","FIRST LIGHT // THE HOURS LAUDS The curfew lifts and the Pillar goes back up into the Wheel. The manna lies on the white streets. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The gatherers come in off the white streets with the manna, saying the fifty-first, the sixty-seventh and the last three psalms. The Wheel opens its day watch. THE WARDS The manna roads have been full for an hour. Darkwell’s Wellmen carry the morning’s crates out of the Well. The fish market opens on the quays, and Nell Garvie comes down from the harbor light. PAN The Cold Store closes and the keyed go below or indoors. On Burn Day the fire on the plaza goes out. IN THE STREET Food and drinkA day by the HoursMarketsSeven days"],["PRIME06:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-prime","","","06:00 // THE HOURS PRIME The day’s orders, and on the days they choose, the Threshers’ march. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The Charter Wardens stand before the barracks and hear the day’s orders in Tongues through their commander’s mouth. On the first of the month the eldest Deacon reads the Order’s questions in the Tabernacle. THE WARDS The Liftmen’s first wind, the bread queues at Leaven’s forty ovens, and on a march day the keyed of the Wards keep indoors with the doors shut. PAN Pan sleeps. The runners of the night are paid at the Undertow before they go down. IN THE STREET WorkGetting aboutDoors and locks"],["TERCE09:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-terce","","","09:00 // THE HOURS TERCE On the Lord’s Day every parish hears its Grace. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES On the Lord’s Day each parish kneels in its hall, hears the Directive in force and then its Grace in Tongues, soul by soul, through its Deacon in the Choir’s voice. THE WARDS At Turbine Row School the sealed teacher kneels at her desk while the room waits with its pens down. The sealed of the blocks kneel on the landings, and the rest step round them. PAN The booking hall’s windows open, SINGLE, RETURN and SEASON, and the queue for the Notary’s clerks begins. IN THE STREET A day by the HoursWhat the parishes giveSeven daysTerms"],["SEXT12:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-sext","","","12:00 // THE HOURS SEXT Noon. The city stops for the length of a psalm. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES The Faithful kneel wherever they stand, in the street or in the doorway, and get up when the Hum falls back. THE WARDS On the Step the sealed go to their knees in the middle of a sentence and the keyed across the street wait for them to finish it. On the boards of the Union Hall the sealed kneel and the rest wait with their caps in their hands. PAN Below the street nobody kneels, and the children at the Benches read their lessons aloud twice. IN THE STREET The StepHow Nod talksWork"],["NONE15:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-none","","","15:00 // THE HOURS NONE The long afternoon, and the city’s work. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES Bethel’s workshops at the top of the Terraces, the parishes’ looms and benches, kneel at the Hour and go back to the bench. THE WARDS The carriers of the Union hand their loads across at the gates. Hana Novak’s chair is busy with teeth until dark, and with other work after Compline. PAN The Exchange’s brokers set the evening’s rates for the stalls of the Night Market. IN THE STREET WorkGetting aboutPrices"],["VESPERS18:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-vespers","","","18:00 // THE HOURS VESPERS Dusk. The landings sit, and on Burn Day the Faithful carry to the fire. THE PILLAR // IN THE WHEEL THE PARISHES On Burn Day the Faithful carry what they have surrendered to the Burn from Vespers, and the Kindlers work the edges of the pile in the dusk. THE WARDS A block’s quarrels are heard on the middle landing of its stairwell after Vespers. Children play Salt in the street, Nell Garvie climbs the harbor light, and the turners close their stalls. PAN The Psalters on the quays write out the week’s hymns for the unsealed, a battery a sheet, and the runners come up to the Undertow for the night’s jobs. IN THE STREET Justice in the WardsGamesSeven daysClothes"],["COMPLINE21:00","THE CITY // THE HOURS","s:ge134f2","~/city/#h-compline","","","21:00 // THE HOURS COMPLINE The curfew falls. The Pillar comes down on the Burn, and Pan comes up the stairs. THE PILLAR // ON THE STREETS THE PARISHES The Faithful kneel for the Hour and say the fourth, the ninety-first and the hundred and thirty-fourth psalms under their breath. Then the streets on the parishes’ rolls are Heaven’s ground until Lauds, and nobody walks them. THE WARDS The criers climb to the roofs with the week’s chart of the Pillar’s round and a whistle. Old Nod stands at its windows through the Hour, which it calls standing by night. PAN The Night Market opens under the Burn and the Cold Store opens its doors. Radio Pandæmonium comes on in the Low Tuning. IN THE STREET A day by the HoursHeaven’s groundSongs and psalmsMarketsNights out"],["The map","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#map-k","","","THE MAP The survey of Nod as it stands in P+14, ten districts and 163 places, with what lies under the streets. Choose a district to open the map on it. OLD NODOLD NODZION HEIGHTSZIONCHROME ROWROWBABELBABELTHE BURNBURNSALT GARDENSSALTTHE CONSECRATIONTHE WHITESCRIPTORIUMRUINSDROWNED QUARTERDEEP ENDTHE LANDINGLANDING OPEN THE MAPBELOW THE STREETSTHE CURFEW, LIVEEVERY PLACE"],["The paper and the station","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#media-k","","","NO. 3,561 // THE TWENTY-SECOND SPRING OF P+14 The Tide Table PRINTED ON THE QUAYS // TWO PAGES // PASTED UP IN THE STONES Two pages a week from a treadle press on the quays. The tides come first, then the Pillar's round street by street, the rates, the missing and the dead, the terms and the notices. READ THIS WEEK'S ON AIR // COMPLINE TO LAUDS Radio Pandæmonium 21:00 COMPLINE 21:30 THE NOTICES 22:00 THE PLATFORM 00:00 MATINS The Low Tuning, from a desk on the platform of Deep End Station. Keyed sets only. TUNE IN"],["The curfew","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#curfew-k","","","THE CURFEW From Compline to Lauds the streets on the parishes' rolls are Heaven's ground, with the White and the Pews at every hour. The Tide Table prints the map beside the Pillar's round, and the stairwells of the Wards copy it in chalk. THE NORTH WARDSTHE EAST WARDSWESTFIELDTHE BAYTHE WHITETHE PEWSTHE STONES1234567891011121314123456 GATE WHERE BAR 1 The Street Between 200 2 The top of the Row 400 3 The head of the Great Avenues 600 4 By the Salt's iron gates 300 5 The White's road to the Burn 200 6 The ridge road 400 The bars are posted nowhere. The queues know them, and a queue full of souls near its bar, saying the surrender twice, is a slow gate. THE CURFEW ON THE MAP, LIVE THE PILLAR'S ROUND, FROM COMPLINE 121:00THE BURNComes down on the plaza 221:25BABELThe plaza's edge, the foot of the tower 321:55THE WHITEThe Great Avenues, from Gate 3 422:40THE ROOKERYThe edge of the White, the forty-four floors 523:10THE RIALTOThe East Wards' long road 623:45TURBINE ROWThe school, the crews' blocks 700:20THE SALTThe Gardens' rows 801:00THE STONESCrosses Old Nod to the quays 901:40THE PEWSThe Terraces, street by numbered street 1002:20WESTFIELDCistern's roofs 1102:55THE KILNSThe bottle kilns and the club 1203:30HIGH MEADOWEight blocks, thirty-six floors 1304:00NINE ACRESThe long road 1404:35CHROME ROWGate 2, the top of the Row LAUDSTHE BURNBack up into the Wheel"],["The screens of pan","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#screens-k","","","THE SCREENS OF PAN Brightwall's screens run along the old platforms, over the Night Market and down the tunnels, and carry what Pan has to sell."],["On the walls","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/#walls-k","","","ON THE WALLS Above the street the city sells on paper. The bills go up on the walls of the Stones and in the stairwells of the Wards, pasted over the week before."],["The Tide Table","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/tidetable/","","","THE CITY // THE PAPER THE TIDE TABLE The only news printed in Nodus since the Parousia, two pages a week from a treadle press at the back of a chandler’s on the quays, pasted up on the walls of the Stones and carried to the Wards and the Row. It prints no indexed name. NO. 3,561 // THE TWENTY-SECOND SPRING OF P+14 The Tide Table PRINTED ON THE QUAYS // TWO PAGES // PASTED UP IN THE STONES THE TIDES Spring tides this week. The neaps begin on the fourth day of next week, and batteries will go dear. The Gleaning comes at the turn, in three springs. THE LINE // 141 The lowest floor of Babel still lit at Lauds on the Lord’s Day stood at the hundred and forty-first. DAY HIGH HIGH LOW LOW RANGE THE LORD'S DAY 04:52 17:14 11:03 23:27 5.9 M THE SECOND DAY 05:38 18:01 11:50 6.1 M THE THIRD DAY 06:24 18:47 00:14 12:36 6.2 M THE FOURTH DAY 07:11 19:35 01:00 13:22 6.1 M THE FIFTH DAY 07:58 20:24 01:47 14:10 5.8 M THE SIXTH DAY 08:48 21:16 02:35 15:00 5.4 M THE SEVENTH DAY 09:41 22:13 03:26 15:53 5.0 M THE PILLAR'S ROUND The hours the Pillar is expected in each street this week, from Compline. In harvest weather it keeps no round. Criers, watch the cloud. # AT WHERE ITS STREETS 1 21:00 THE BURN Comes down on the plaza 2 21:25 BABEL The plaza’s edge, the foot of the tower 3 21:55 THE WHITE The Great Avenues, from Gate 3 4 22:40 THE ROOKERY The edge of the White, the forty-four floors 5 23:10 THE RIALTO The East Wards’ long road 6 23:45 TURBINE ROW The school, the crews’ blocks 7 00:20 THE SALT The Gardens’ rows 8 01:00 THE STONES Crosses Old Nod to the quays 9 01:40 THE PEWS The Terraces, street by numbered street 10 02:20 WESTFIELD Cistern’s roofs 11 02:55 THE KILNS The bottle kilns and the club 12 03:30 HIGH MEADOW Eight blocks, thirty-six floors 13 04:00 NINE ACRES The long road 14 04:35 CHROME ROW Gate 2, the top of the Row LAUDS THE BURN Back up into the Wheel THE CURFEW, COMPLINE TO LAUDS THE NORTH WARDSTHE EAST WARDSWESTFIELDTHE BAYTHE WHITETHE PEWSTHE STONES1234567891011121314123456 In white, the White and the Pews, Heaven’s ground at every hour. The dashed line is the Pillar’s round, numbered as above. The parishes of the Wards paint their numbers at each end of their streets. GATE WHERE BAR 1 The Street Between 200 2 The top of the Row 400 3 The head of the Great Avenues 600 4 By the Salt’s iron gates 300 5 The White’s road to the Burn 200 6 The ridge road 400 The bars are posted nowhere. The queues know them, and a queue full of souls near its bar, saying the surrender twice, is a slow gate. THE RATES A charged battery1 silver A Leaven loafhalf a battery A jug of Cistern watera quarter of a battery A bottle of gas2 batteries, or 1 with the empty back A bunk at the Quiet Rooms1 battery A ride on a Jehu2 batteries A ride up a hand lift1 battery A night’s Pillar chart1 battery A spoon of Kilns honeya quarter of a battery A bare element, darnel12 silver A parish door, hunga thirty A low floor, by floor-righta thirty or more WAGES, A DAY A carrier of the Union2 batteries A baker of Leaven2 batteries and a loaf A Liftman’s shift3 batteries A Nightman’s rounda battery a landing a week A child at Tallow’s vatsa candle stub a vat THE MISSING AND THE DEAD As the families bring them to the chandler’s, by handle or by work. A carrier of the Union, of the Kilns. Fever, at home on the fourth floor, after the pump on her landing went bad. DIED UNREAD A gatherer of Parish 212. On the manna road before Lauds, under a Carman’s trolley in the dark. SEALED sorrel, a Wrecker. In the Deep End at the ebb, down for Locket’s reels. His crew asks for the line back. DIED UNREAD An old man of High Meadow, of neither mark. In his chair on the ninth floor. The long night was kept. The barber’s boy of the Lanes, nine. Missing since Vespers on the third day, last seen at the Pillar’s edge at the top of the Lanes. His mother waits at the barber’s. MISSING A turner of Westfield. Held in the light at Gate 4 at Sext until the Wardens came. Her stall on the long road is shut. SALTED TERMS Printed once a season, the standing pacts and when they are read again. Lowtide Line. Its license from the Exchange to sail the Breach, a year from each midsummer. Blackgate. Its pact with the Exchange, three years from each Concord night. Twelvefruit. Its pact with the House of Night, read again this year at the Undertow. The Lantern Houses. The House of Night’s pact with Stonecutter, read again last year for seven more. THE WEEK Burn Day falls on the sixth day. The Faithful carry from Vespers, and the fire is lit under the Pillar when the Hum falls back from Compline. The Rialto shows its ninth film of the twenty-three this week, on the generator, from Vespers. The sorting office asks that letters into the parishes be left open at the counter, as the parishes take them. The Liftmen’s car at the Rookery stops at the forty-fourth. NOTICES DOORS Parish doors, hung on your frame, a thirty, the number on the back left as it came. Keyed latches ten silver. LINTEL, THE DOORYARD, IN THE STONES. BREAD Forty ovens, raised with yeast and baked on gas. Half a battery a loaf. LEAVEN, NINE ACRES AND THE LONG ROADS. WATER Rain off Westfield’s roofs, filtered and boiled. A quarter of a battery a jug. Bring the jug. CISTERN. CANDLES Tallow’s candles by the dozen at every bread queue. Stubs taken back. A ROOM A low floor in Turbine Row by floor-right, its door hung and its latch keyed. A thirty or more. Ask on the landing. THE LIFT Forty-one shafts wound in the Wards. A battery up and nothing down. THE LIFTMEN. WANTED A second man with a spade. Ask the sexton at the Acre, on the West Road. A NIGHT'S SLEEP A bunk with no Hum in it, a battery. Full by Matins. THE QUIET ROOMS, IN THE STONES. FOUND A Locket, burn-marked, with a family’s music on it, taken off the pile on Burn Day. Ask the Kindlers at the edge of the Burn. BIRDS Notes flown home to the Loft on the customs house. Ask at the Union Hall. BY THE PAGE LEAVEN // WARM AT LAUDS The bread of the Wards from forty ovens, raised with yeast and baked on gas from the Tanks. Our carts go out at Matins. Batteries, silver or work. MATTHEW 13:33 LAZARUS // COME FORTH A household pays its thirty a year. Hurt, caught on Heaven’s ground or left in the Penumbra, one of our crews comes with a keyed stretcher and carries you down. NINE STATIONS // JOHN 11:43 TALLOW // ONE CANDLE, ONE PRICE Half of Nodus reads by our candles. Dipped on Turbine Row, forty thousand a week, and the same candle sold to everyone. TURBINE ROW // THE EAST WARDS CISTERN // NOTHING THAT FALLS IS CLEAN Rain off the roofs of Westfield, run through sand and charcoal and sold by the jug. Bring the jug back and we fill it again. WESTFIELD // BY THE JUG JEHU // FOR HE DRIVETH FURIOUSLY Lorries on the long roads of the Wards by day, in the full light, on engines that burn. Built by hand from the diesels of boats and generators. 2 KINGS 9:20 WAINWRIGHT // BUILT FROM THE LINE Carts on the old rails, trolleys on the long roads and skiffs on the Deep End. Every cart is built from a tram of the Line and keeps its car number. THE SIDINGS // THE NORTH WARDS STONECUTTER // A KEYED LINE TO EVERY DOOR Houses of the old harbor, each with a generator in its cellar and a keyed line to its door. THE STONES // OLD NOD TIDE TABLE PRESS // PRINTED BY THE PAGE Between Tide Tables the press sets notices, posters and pacts in metal type for anyone who pays. A CHANDLER'S // THE QUAYS"],["Radio Pandæmonium","THE CITY","s:ge134f2","~/city/radio/","","","THE CITY // THE STATION RADIO PANDÆMONIUM Radio Pandæmonium broadcasts from a desk on the platform of Deep End Station, where the Tares painted PANDÆMONIUM over the old name in the tiles. It plays in the Low Tuning, the guitars dropped until the Hum cannot be heard under them, and only a keyed set can hear it. Old Nod plays it from speakers on its lines from Compline to Lauds, between the music and the notices. jubal runs it. The Low Choir keeps the rule that a song may be sung by anyone and owned by nobody. RADIO PANDÆMONIUM // IN THE LOW TUNING // ON THE MESH // KEYED SETS THE NIGHT 21:00 COMPLINE The station comes on as the Hum falls back from the Hour, with the week’s Pillar chart read street by street for the criers. 21:30 THE NOTICES The rates, the night’s jobs at the Undertow, the Lot’s sittings and the names the crews have asked to have read. 22:00 THE PLATFORM A band of the Low Choir plays to whoever has come down the stair at Deep End Station, before it plays on the air. 00:00 MATINS An hour of requests and dedications, read as they come in off the Mesh. 02:00 THE QUIET HOUR The slow songs, for the Night Market’s last stalls and the Nightmen on the long roads. EBB OUT ON THE EBB Whenever the ebb runs in the night, as the Tide Table gives it, for the Breachers going out through the Breach, with the seconds counted. LAUDS SIGN OFF The station goes quiet before the Wheel opens its day watch, and Old Nod takes the speakers in. PAN'S OWN Nobody's Keeper Cain’s answer, given to a duty never chosen. The one the whole platform sings. Thou Knowest What the Wards say to a Teller who asks where someone is. The Long Wet Named for one of Babel’s four service stairs. Sunned For the ones caught on Heaven’s ground in daylight with no way down. Seventy and Sevenfold Lamech’s song from the line of Cain, “If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold” (Genesis 4:24). Out on the Ebb The Breachers’ song, which counts the seconds through the Breach. Waiting on the Four The Kilns’ song, about a girl at Lanes Top waiting for a tram that stopped at 03:12:46. DEDICATIONS For the crier on the roof across from the Kilns Club, from the landing under her, who heard the second whistle in time. For twice, at the end of the Undertow’s bar, from a runner who came home. For ebb, from the Shanties, who say the light on the long quay is lit. For the stall at the far end of the platform, from someone who walked to the far end to say it. For a mother in the parishes who sings at the Hours, from her son in the Wards, who has the words now, a battery a sheet. BETWEEN THE SONGS"]];
